<header class="topbar"><div class="frame bar"><a href="#home" class="brand" aria-label="Maaden Navigator home"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Maaden"></a><span class="brand-rule"></span><a class="product" href="#home">MSHEM <b>Navigator</b> · Offline</a><nav id="topnav" aria-label="Main navigation"></nav></div></header>
<main id="content" tabindex="-1"><div class="frame loading">Opening the Navigator…</div></main>
<footer><div class="frame foot"><div><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Maaden"><span>Corporate EHSS</span></div><a href="#about">Source &amp; document control</a></div></footer>
<nav id="mobilenav" aria-label="Mobile navigation"></nav><div id="toast" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
<script id="field-edition" type="application/json">{"index":{"processes":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Purpose","page":7,"purpose":"The Maaden Safety, Security, Health and Environment Management System (MSHEM) establishes a unified Maaden wide framework for managing environmental, health, safety and security risks throughout the lifecycle of Maaden activities. MSHEM defines the minimum mandatory requirements, controls and required outcomes that enable Maaden to protect employees, contractors, communities, environment, assets and operations, manage EHSS risks, fulfil applicable legal and regulatory obligations, and achieve safe, responsible and sustainable business performance. MSHEM provides a consistent Maaden wide approach to: • Establish EHSS governance, leadership, accountability and responsibilities; • Engage leaders, employees and contractors in managing EHSS risks and improving performance; • Identify, assess and manage EHSS risks and applicable obligations; • Establish, implement and maintain effective preventive and mitigative risk controls; • Integrate applicable EHSS requirements into operations, projects and contractor activities; • Maintain emergency preparedness and response capability for credible emergency situations; • Monitor, evaluate and assure EHSS performance; and • Promote learning, incident prevention and continual improvement. MSHEM establishes consistent Maaden wide requirements and outcomes for managing EHSS risks and performance, while enabling Business Units and Corporate Functions to implement those requirements in a manner that reflects their activities, risks and operating context.","scope":"","clauses":[],"area":"Framework","part":"MSHEM Framework","keywords":"Purpose; purpose","trigger":"Use where the section purpose and applicability statement apply.","refs":[]},{"id":"1.2","title":"Scope","page":7,"purpose":"MSHEM applies to Maaden businesses, functions, sites, operations, projects and activities over which Maaden has management or operational control. MSHEM addresses four integrated EHSS domains: Environment, Health, Safety and Security. The Safety domain encompasses both Occupational Safety and Process Safety. It applies throughout the relevant asset and activity lifecycle, including exploration, project development, design, construction, commissioning, operations, maintenance, modification, closure, post-closure and rehabilitation. MSHEM requirements shall be implemented by Business Units and Corporate Functions through detailed procedures, work instructions, plans, controls and other implementation arrangements. Contractors and other parties performing activities under Maaden's control shall comply with applicable MSHEM requirements relevant to their work.","scope":"","clauses":[],"area":"Framework","part":"MSHEM Framework","keywords":"Scope; scope","trigger":"Use where the section purpose and applicability statement apply.","refs":[]},{"id":"1.3","title":"MSHEM Structure","page":7,"purpose":"MSHEM is an integrated management system that enables Maaden to manage EHSS risks, fulfil applicable obligations and support continual improvement in EHSS performance. The system is organized into six integrated Parts that collectively define the framework, governance, processes and performance requirements for effective EHSS management: Part 1 | MSHEM Framework Defines the Purpose, Scope and Structure of MSHEM. Part 2 | EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability Defines the governance, leadership, risk management and organizational capabilities required to support effective EHSS management. Part 3 | EHSS Processes Defines the core EHSS risk control requirements across: • Environment; • Health; • Safety, comprising Occupational Safety and Process Safety; and • Security. Part 4 | Contractor and Project EHSS Management Defines requirements for managing EHSS risks associated with contractor activities and projects. Part 5 | Emergency Management and Response Defines requirements for emergency preparedness, response capability and emergency response resources. Part 6 | EHSS Performance, Assurance and Improvement Defines requirements for incident management, performance evaluation, assurance, management review and continual improvement.","scope":"","clauses":[],"area":"Framework","part":"MSHEM Framework","keywords":"MSHEM Structure; mshem structure; Part 1 | MSHEM Framework","trigger":"Use where the section purpose and applicability statement apply.","refs":[]},{"id":"2.1","title":"MSHEM Governance","page":8,"purpose":"To ensure MSHEM is effectively governed, implemented, maintained, evaluated and continually improved through defined accountabilities, responsibilities, consistent implementation and systematic oversight.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units, Corporate Functions and activities subject to MSHEM requirements.","clauses":[{"id":"2.1.1","page":8,"heading":"MSHEM Implementation Principles","text":"MSHEM shall establish the minimum Maaden wide EHSS requirements applicable to Business Units and Corporate Functions."},{"id":"2.1.2","page":8,"heading":"MSHEM Implementation Principles","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions may establish additional or more stringent EHSS requirements and controls based on their activities, risks, operating conditions and applicable obligations, provided that applicable MSHEM requirements continue to be met."},{"id":"2.1.3","page":8,"heading":"MSHEM Implementation Principles","text":"MSHEM shall not replace or reduce compliance with applicable laws, regulations, permits, regulatory directives or other binding obligations. Where multiple applicable requirements address the same matter, the more stringent requirement shall apply."},{"id":"2.1.4","page":9,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Executive leadership shall be accountable for the implementation and effectiveness of applicable MSHEM requirements within their areas of responsibility."},{"id":"2.1.5","page":9,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall designate responsible people for coordinating and monitoring the implementation of applicable MSHEM requirements."},{"id":"2.1.6","page":9,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Roles, responsibilities and authorities relevant to MSHEM implementation shall be defined, documented and communicated by Business Units and Corporate Functions."},{"id":"2.1.7","page":9,"heading":"Implementation, Applicability and Deviations","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement applicable MSHEM requirements within their areas of responsibility and maintain proportionate documented information sufficient to demonstrate implementation, verify controls, support compliance and inform decision making, without creating duplicate records where equivalent controlled evidence is available."},{"id":"2.1.8","page":9,"heading":"Implementation, Applicability and Deviations","text":"Where a MSHEM requirement is determined to be not applicable, the decision and supporting justification shall be documented and approved by the highest EHSS authority of the relevant Business Unit or Corporate Function."},{"id":"2.1.9","page":9,"heading":"Implementation, Applicability and Deviations","text":"Where an applicable MSHEM requirement cannot be met, the proposed deviation shall be documented, justified and risk assessed, with alternative controls identified where necessary, and approved by the appropriate management authority in accordance with the assessed risk level."},{"id":"2.1.10","page":9,"heading":"Implementation, Applicability and Deviations","text":"A deviation from MSHEM shall not authorize non-compliance with applicable law, regulation, permit, license, approval or regulatory directive."},{"id":"2.1.11","page":9,"heading":"Implementation, Applicability and Deviations","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain action plans for identified MSHEM implementation and compliance gaps. Actions shall be assigned, prioritized according to risk where appropriate, tracked and closed within established completion dates."},{"id":"2.1.12","page":9,"heading":"Governance Review","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically review the implementation status, effectiveness and compliance of applicable MSHEM requirements within their areas of responsibility in accordance with section 6.3 EHSS Audit and Assurance."},{"id":"2.1.13","page":9,"heading":"Governance Review","text":"Corporate EHSS shall periodically review the implementation status, effectiveness and compliance of MSHEM across Maaden in accordance with section 6.3 EHSS Audit and Assurance."},{"id":"2.1.14","page":9,"heading":"MSHEM Ownership, Review and Control","text":"Corporate EHSS shall be the owner and custodian of MSHEM and shall establish, maintain, approve, issue, communicate and control MSHEM documentation."},{"id":"2.1.15","page":9,"heading":"MSHEM Ownership, Review and Control","text":"Corporate EHSS, supported by relevant Subject Matter Experts, shall evaluate MSHEM requirements at least annually to confirm their continued suitability and effectiveness and identify opportunities for continual improvement."},{"id":"2.1.16","page":9,"heading":"MSHEM Ownership, Review and Control","text":"The annual evaluation shall consider changes in Maaden activities and risks, applicable obligations, incidents and lessons learned, audit and assurance findings, performance trends, operating experience, and relevant technical or industry developments."},{"id":"2.1.17","page":9,"heading":"MSHEM Ownership, Review and Control","text":"MSHEM shall be revised where annual evaluations or other significant changes identify a need for modification."},{"id":"2.1.18","page":10,"heading":"MSHEM Ownership, Review and Control","text":"MSHEM shall undergo a comprehensive review at least every three years to confirm its continued suitability, effectiveness and alignment with Maaden requirements."},{"id":"2.1.19","page":10,"heading":"MSHEM Ownership, Review and Control","text":"MSHEM records shall be retained for at least three years unless a longer or shorter retention period is required by applicable legal, regulatory, permit, or specific MSHEM requirements."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"MSHEM Governance; mshem governance; MSHEM Implementation Principles; Governance and Accountability; Implementation, Applicability and Deviations; Governance Review; MSHEM Ownership, Review and Control","trigger":"if MSHEM scope, interfaces, controlled entities or applicability must be defined","refs":["6.3"]},{"id":"2.2","title":"EHSS Leadership and Accountability","page":10,"purpose":"Leadership at all levels visibly demonstrates commitment to EHSS, establishes clear accountabilities, provides resources, promotes worker engagement and ensures EHSS considerations are integrated into business decisions and operations.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions, and to Maaden employees and contractor personnel working under Maaden’s control.","clauses":[{"id":"2.2.1","page":10,"heading":"Leadership and Commitment","text":"Executive leadership, management and supervisors shall visibly demonstrate commitment to EHSS and lead by example in the implementation of applicable MSHEM requirements."},{"id":"2.2.2","page":10,"heading":"Leadership and Commitment","text":"EHSS considerations shall be integrated into business planning, decision-making, change management and operational activities."},{"id":"2.2.3","page":10,"heading":"Leadership and Commitment","text":"Leadership shall ensure that resources necessary to implement, maintain and improve MSHEM are available."},{"id":"2.2.4","page":10,"heading":"Leadership and Commitment","text":"Leadership shall promote a culture in which personnel are empowered to report hazards, incidents, concerns and improvement opportunities, and to exercise authorized stop-work authority without fear of retaliation, and shall ensure that reported matters are evaluated and addressed."},{"id":"2.2.5","page":10,"heading":"Leadership and Commitment","text":"Leadership shall promote and support the recognition of employees and contractor personnel who demonstrate positive EHSS behaviors, contributions or performance that support the objectives and intended outcomes of MSHEM."},{"id":"2.2.6","page":10,"heading":"EHSS Accountabilities","text":"EHSS accountabilities, responsibilities and authorities shall be clearly assigned to relevant roles, communicated and periodically reviewed."},{"id":"2.2.7","page":10,"heading":"EHSS Accountabilities","text":"Accountability for EHSS performance shall remain with line management and shall not be delegated to EHSS personnel."},{"id":"2.2.8","page":10,"heading":"EHSS Accountabilities","text":"EHSS responsibilities applicable to a role shall be incorporated into relevant position descriptions and communicated to affected personnel."},{"id":"2.2.9","page":10,"heading":"Worker Participation and Engagement","text":"Employees and contractor personnel shall be provided with opportunities to participate in EHSS activities, consultations, hazard identification, risk assessments, incident investigations and continual improvement initiatives relevant to their work activities."},{"id":"2.2.10","page":11,"heading":"Worker Participation and Engagement","text":"Employees and contractor personnel shall comply with applicable EHSS requirements, report hazards, incidents, unsafe acts or unsafe conditions, and shall be authorized and expected to stop or not start work where required controls are absent or ineffective, or where conditions present an unacceptable EHSS risk."},{"id":"2.2.11","page":11,"heading":"Visible EHSS Leadership","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain a visible EHSS leadership program that defines leadership engagement activities, participation expectations, monitoring and follow-up requirements."},{"id":"2.2.12","page":11,"heading":"Visible EHSS Leadership","text":"Management shall undertake visible EHSS leadership activities appropriate to their roles and areas of responsibility to engage with personnel, verify the effectiveness of EHSS controls, reinforce desired behaviors, and identify issues and improvement opportunities."},{"id":"2.2.13","page":11,"heading":"Visible EHSS Leadership","text":"Visible EHSS leadership activities shall be risk based and consider material EHSS risks, control effectiveness, EHSS performance, serious injury and fatality prevention, and workforce concerns. Significant observations, issues and resulting actions shall be documented, communicated and addressed."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"EHSS Leadership and Accountability; ehss leadership and accountability; Leadership and Commitment; EHSS Accountabilities; Worker Participation and Engagement; Visible EHSS Leadership","trigger":"if leadership accountability, authority, approval or escalation is required","refs":[]},{"id":"2.3","title":"EHSS Policy, Objectives and Planning","page":11,"purpose":"Maaden establishes and maintains an approved EHSS Policy and translates its commitments into aligned and measurable EHSS objectives supported by adequately resourced plans that support compliance, EHSS risk reduction and continual improvement of EHSS performance and MSHEM.","scope":"This process applies to the development, approval, communication, implementation and revision of the Maaden EHSS Policy, EHSS objectives and associated EHSS objective plans across Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions.","clauses":[{"id":"2.3.1","page":11,"heading":"Policy Establishment and Approval","text":"Maaden shall establish, implement and maintain an EHSS Policy that is appropriate to Maaden’s purpose and context, and to the nature and scale of its activities and associated EHSS risks and opportunities."},{"id":"2.3.2","page":11,"heading":"Policy Establishment and Approval","text":"The EHSS Policy shall be approved by the Chief Executive Officer and shall provide the framework for establishing EHSS objectives and performance improvement initiatives."},{"id":"2.3.3","page":11,"heading":"Policy Establishment and Approval","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall apply the approved Maaden EHSS Policy and shall not establish or use a separate EHSS policy."},{"id":"2.3.4","page":11,"heading":"Policy Commitments","text":"EHSS Policy shall include, as a minimum, commitments to:\na. protect people and provide healthy and safe working conditions;\nb. protect the environment, including the prevention of pollution and minimization of adverse environmental impacts;\nc. eliminate hazards and manage EHSS risks;\nd. fulfil applicable legal requirements and other obligations;\ne. consult and enable the participation of workers; and\nf. continually improve EHSS performance and MSHEM."},{"id":"2.3.5","page":12,"heading":"Policy Communication and Review","text":"EHSS Policy shall be maintained as documented information, communicated to employees and contractor personnel, and made available to relevant interested parties."},{"id":"2.3.6","page":12,"heading":"Policy Communication and Review","text":"Corporate EHSS shall review the EHSS Policy at least every three years and revise it where necessary to ensure it remains current, effective and aligned with Maaden's strategic direction, activities, risks and obligations."},{"id":"2.3.7","page":12,"heading":"EHSS Objectives Establishment and Alignment","text":"Corporate EHSS shall establish Maaden wide EHSS objectives that support the commitments of the EHSS Policy, align with Maaden’s strategic and business planning processes, and contribute to the intended outcomes of MSHEM."},{"id":"2.3.8","page":12,"heading":"EHSS Objectives Establishment and Alignment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish EHSS objectives appropriate to their respective EHSS responsibilities, risks, and operating contexts that:\na. are relevant to their activities and operating context;\nb. support applicable Maaden wide EHSS objectives; and\nc. address identified EHSS performance and improvement priorities."},{"id":"2.3.9","page":12,"heading":"Objective Setting Criteria and Communication","text":"EHSS objectives shall include relevant baselines, measurable targets, defined timeframes, and criteria for evaluating progress and achievement."},{"id":"2.3.10","page":12,"heading":"Objective Setting Criteria and Communication","text":"When establishing and revising EHSS objectives, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall consider:\na. EHSS risks and opportunities;\nb. applicable legal and regulatory requirements;\nc. Maaden’s strategic and business priorities;\nd. previous and current EHSS performance;\ne. significant incidents and relevant lessons learned;\nf. audit, assurance and management review findings."},{"id":"2.3.11","page":12,"heading":"Objective Setting Criteria and Communication","text":"EHSS objectives shall be documented and communicated to personnel responsible for their implementation, monitoring and achievement."},{"id":"2.3.12","page":12,"heading":"Resources and Improvement Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain EHSS objective plans to achieve their EHSS objectives."},{"id":"2.3.13","page":12,"heading":"Resources and Improvement Planning","text":"Each EHSS objective plan shall define, as a minimum, the required actions, accountable owner, required resources, target completion dates and applicable milestones, and the indicators or other criteria for monitoring progress and evaluating achievement."},{"id":"2.3.14","page":12,"heading":"Resources and Improvement Planning","text":"Accountable leaders shall ensure that the human, technical, physical and financial resources necessary to implement approved EHSS objective plans are identified and addressed through applicable business planning and resource allocation processes."},{"id":"2.3.15","page":12,"heading":"Resources and Improvement Planning","text":"EHSS objectives and associated EHSS objective plans shall be integrated into applicable strategic, business, project and operational planning processes."},{"id":"2.3.16","page":13,"heading":"Monitoring, Review and Revision","text":"Progress against EHSS objectives and EHSS objective plans shall be monitored and reported at the frequency defined in the applicable objective plan, using the indicators, milestones and completion criteria established in the plan."},{"id":"2.3.17","page":13,"heading":"Monitoring, Review and Revision","text":"EHSS objectives and plans shall be formally reviewed at least once annually."},{"id":"2.3.18","page":13,"heading":"Monitoring, Review and Revision","text":"EHSS objectives and EHSS objective plans shall be reviewed and, where justified, revised when necessary to address:\na. material changes to activities, assets, organization or operating conditions;\nb. changes to material EHSS risks or opportunities;\nc. new or changed legal requirements, regulatory requirements, permit conditions or other obligations;\nd. significant incidents, findings or lessons learned;\ne. inadequate progress or failure to achieve an objective;\nf. changes to Maaden’s strategic or business priorities; or\ng. identified opportunities to improve EHSS performance or MSHEM."},{"id":"2.3.19","page":13,"heading":"Monitoring, Review and Revision","text":"Changes to EHSS objectives, EHSS objective plans, responsibilities, resources or target dates shall be appropriately justified and approved, documented and communicated to affected personnel."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"EHSS Policy, Objectives and Planning; ehss policy, objectives and planning; Policy Establishment and Approval; Policy Commitments; Policy Communication and Review; EHSS Objectives Establishment and Alignment; Objective Setting Criteria and Communication; Resources and Improvement Planning; Monitoring, Review and Revision","trigger":"if policy commitments, objectives, plans or resources are being established or reviewed","refs":[]},{"id":"2.4","title":"EHSS Legal and Regulatory Requirements","page":13,"purpose":"Applicable EHSS legal and regulatory requirements are identified, understood, communicated, implemented and periodically evaluated to support and demonstrate compliance.","scope":"This process applies to the identification, evaluation, implementation and review of EHSS legal and regulatory requirements applicable to Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions.","clauses":[{"id":"2.4.1","page":13,"heading":"Identification and Evaluation of Legal and Regulatory Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and maintain access to EHSS legal and regulatory requirements applicable to their activities, facilities, projects and operations."},{"id":"2.4.2","page":13,"heading":"Identification and Evaluation of Legal and Regulatory Requirements","text":"EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be evaluated to determine their applicability and resulting compliance obligations."},{"id":"2.4.3","page":13,"heading":"Identification and Evaluation of Legal and Regulatory Requirements","text":"The identification and evaluation of EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be undertaken by competent personnel and supported by specialist expertise where required."},{"id":"2.4.4","page":13,"heading":"Identification and Evaluation of Legal and Regulatory Requirements","text":"EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be reviewed and updated at least annually, and when new requirements are introduced, existing requirements are amended, or activities or operations change."},{"id":"2.4.5","page":13,"heading":"Identification and Evaluation of Legal and Regulatory Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain arrangements to monitor relevant sources for new and amended EHSS legal and regulatory requirements and assess their applicability within defined timeframes that consider effective dates, compliance deadlines and associated EHSS risks."},{"id":"2.4.6","page":13,"heading":"Legal and Regulatory Requirements Register","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, maintain and review at least annually a register of applicable EHSS legal and regulatory requirements, including permit conditions, approvals, licenses and regulatory authorizations, identifying their sources or references, associated activities and compliance obligations, responsible functions or roles, established compliance controls, and validity or renewal requirements, as applicable."},{"id":"2.4.7","page":14,"heading":"Implementation and Communication","text":"Responsibilities and authorities for implementing and maintaining compliance with applicable EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be assigned and communicated to relevant personnel and stakeholders."},{"id":"2.4.8","page":14,"heading":"Implementation and Communication","text":"Applicable EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be integrated, as applicable, into:\na. EHSS risk assessments;\nb. Operational controls;\nc. Management of change;\nd. EHSS objectives and plans;\ne. Competency and training requirements."},{"id":"2.4.9","page":14,"heading":"Evaluation of Compliance","text":"Compliance with applicable EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be monitored at intervals appropriate to the nature of the obligation, associated risks and applicable permit conditions."},{"id":"2.4.10","page":14,"heading":"Evaluation of Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall, as part of the annual review of EHSS legal and regulatory requirements, evaluate compliance with applicable obligations, maintain documented evidence of the evaluation, and identify actions necessary to address any compliance gaps."},{"id":"2.4.11","page":14,"heading":"Evaluation of Compliance","text":"Non-compliance with applicable EHSS legal and regulatory requirements shall be documented, evaluated, appropriately escalated and addressed through corrective actions that are tracked to closure. Applicable regulatory notification and reporting requirements shall be fulfilled."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"EHSS Legal and Regulatory Requirements; ehss legal and regulatory requirements; Identification and Evaluation of Legal and Regulatory Requirements; Legal and Regulatory Requirements Register; Implementation and Communication; Evaluation of Compliance","trigger":"if a law, licence, permit, approved study, reporting or compliance obligation applies","refs":[]},{"id":"2.5","title":"EHSS Risk Management","page":14,"purpose":"EHSS risks associated with Maaden activities, facilities, projects, products, services and changes are systematically identified, assessed, treated, monitored and reviewed so that risks are eliminated where reasonably practicable or otherwise reduced and maintained at a level that is As Low As Reasonably Practicable (ALARP).","scope":"This process applies to Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions that manage activities, facilities, projects, products, services or changes that may create or modify EHSS risk.","clauses":[{"id":"2.5.1","page":14,"heading":"EHSS Risk Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain processes to identify, assess, treat, monitor and review EHSS risks associated with their activities, facilities, projects, products, services and changes."},{"id":"2.5.2","page":14,"heading":"EHSS Risk Management Framework","text":"EHSS risks shall be assessed using the approved Maaden EHSS Risk Matrix and a risk assessment methodology selected according to the nature, complexity and credible consequence potential of the matter being assessed."},{"id":"2.5.3","page":14,"heading":"EHSS Risk Management Framework","text":"Risk assessment methodologies shall be used in accordance with section 3.47 Process Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment."},{"id":"2.5.4","page":15,"heading":"Risk Identification and Assessment","text":"Risk assessments shall consider, as applicable:\na. Routine and non-routine activities;\nb. Normal, abnormal and emergency conditions;\nc. Employees, contractors, visitors and other affected people;\nd. Relevant facility, project, product and service lifecycle stages;\ne. Planned and reasonably foreseeable changes; and\nf. Environmental aspects, occupational health exposures, safety hazards, process hazards and security threats.\ng. Existing controls and safeguards; and\nh. Relevant incidents, findings, operating experience and changes in applicable obligations."},{"id":"2.5.5","page":15,"heading":"Risk Identification and Assessment","text":"Risk assessments shall identify and evaluate:\na. The hazard, exposure, aspect, threat or risk source;\nb. Credible unwanted events and consequence scenarios;\nc. People, the environment, assets or operations that may be affected;\nd. Potential consequences and their severity;\ne. Likelihood of occurrence;\nf. Existing controls and safeguards;\ng. Initial risk and residual risk."},{"id":"2.5.6","page":15,"heading":"Risk Identification and Assessment","text":"Initial risk shall be assessed before credit is given to controls or safeguards intended to prevent the unwanted event or reduce its consequences."},{"id":"2.5.7","page":15,"heading":"Risk Identification and Assessment","text":"Residual risk shall be assessed after crediting only those controls and safeguards that meet applicable Maaden requirements for effectiveness, independence and reliability."},{"id":"2.5.8","page":15,"heading":"Risk Identification and Assessment","text":"Risk assessments shall be documented in sufficient detail to support review, approval, implementation, monitoring and reassessment."},{"id":"2.5.9","page":15,"heading":"Risk Assessment Triggers","text":"A risk assessment shall be conducted or reviewed when new EHSS risks arise or existing risks change, including:\na. facility and plant design, construction, commissioning and operation;\nb. management of Change activities;\nc. unresolved Pre-Start-Up Safety Review actions;\nd. abnormal test, inspection or monitoring results;\ne. deferred inspections, tests or maintenance activities associated with EHSS critical asset or systems;\nf. bypasses, impairments or disabling of a critical control, protective device or emergency system;\ng. significant findings from incidents, audits, inspections, assessments or investigations; and\nh. unsafe conditions that cannot be immediately eliminated or controlled.\ni. evidence that an existing control or safeguard is degraded, unavailable or ineffective."},{"id":"2.5.10","page":15,"heading":"Risk Assessment Triggers","text":"Where findings are not immediately corrected, or where findings may affect similar activities, systems, facilities or operations, a risk assessment shall be performed and risk reduction actions identified."},{"id":"2.5.11","page":16,"heading":"Risk Assessment Triggers","text":"An approved Medium, Significant, High or Critical risk shall be reassessed before the approval validity period expires, and earlier where the scope, operating conditions, assumptions, affected people, consequence potential or effectiveness of a credited control change."},{"id":"2.5.12","page":16,"heading":"Risk Treatment and Interim Controls","text":"Risk control measures shall be selected and implemented in accordance with the hierarchy of controls to eliminate risks where reasonably practicable or otherwise reduce risks to a level that is As Low As Reasonably Practicable (ALARP)."},{"id":"2.5.13","page":16,"heading":"Risk Treatment and Interim Controls","text":"Where risks cannot immediately be reduced to ALARP, interim controls shall be identified, implemented and monitored until permanent risk reduction measures are implemented."},{"id":"2.5.14","page":16,"heading":"Risk Treatment and Interim Controls","text":"The risk assessment shall specify the performance requirements, verification method and verification frequency for each interim control."},{"id":"2.5.15","page":16,"heading":"Risk Treatment and Interim Controls","text":"Where interim controls are not identified, or where interim controls do not reduce the initial risk, risk approval shall be escalated to a higher approval authority."},{"id":"2.5.16","page":16,"heading":"Risk Treatment and Interim Controls","text":"EHSS Risks, approved interim controls, operating restrictions, required actions and escalation conditions shall be communicated to affected employees, contractors and other people responsible for implementing or verifying them."},{"id":"2.5.17","page":16,"heading":"Competence and Participation","text":"EHSS risk assessments shall be led or facilitated by personnel competent in the selected risk assessment methodology, the use of the Maaden EHSS Risk Matrix and the activities, processes or hazards being assessed."},{"id":"2.5.18","page":16,"heading":"Competence and Participation","text":"Risk assessment participants shall collectively possess the operational, technical and EHSS knowledge necessary to identify credible scenarios, evaluate controls and assess risk within the defined scope."},{"id":"2.5.19","page":16,"heading":"Risk Approval and Acceptance","text":"Immediately after a Medium, Significant, High or Critical risk is identified, verbal authorization for continued operation or activity shall be obtained from the relevant management authority specified in Table 2.5-1, followed by documented review and approval within the applicable timeframe. No mitigating actions are required to be taken or approved for Low risks."},{"id":"2.5.20","page":16,"heading":"Risk Approval and Acceptance","text":"Where immediate verbal authorization is permitted, the decision, approving authority, basis for continuation, interim controls and time of authorization shall be recorded and subsequently completed through the documented approval process."},{"id":"2.5.21","page":16,"heading":"Risk Approval and Acceptance","text":"The minimum approval authority, approval timeframe, and maximum validity period shall be as specified in Table\n2.5-1:\n[Table table-16-1.png]\n\nTable 2.5-1. Risk Approval for Continued Operation."},{"id":"2.5.22","page":17,"heading":"Risk Approval and Acceptance","text":"Risk approvals shall only be granted after the risk assessment, interim controls, mitigation actions and required endorsements have been reviewed and determined to be suitable for continued operation or activity."},{"id":"2.5.23","page":17,"heading":"Risk Approval and Acceptance","text":"All approved risks shall have defined risk treatment actions, responsible people and target completion dates established to reduce the risk to an acceptable level."},{"id":"2.5.24","page":17,"heading":"Risk Approval and Acceptance","text":"Risks that remain open at the end of the approved validity period shall be reassessed, reviewed and reapproved prior to renewal of the risk acceptance."},{"id":"2.5.25","page":17,"heading":"Risk Approval and Acceptance","text":"Following implementation of all reasonably practicable controls, residual risk shall be evaluated using the approved Maaden EHSS Risk Matrix and accepted in accordance with the criteria and approval authorities specified in Table\n2.5-2.\n[Table table-17-1.png]\n\nTable 2.5-2. Residual Risk Acceptance Requirements."},{"id":"2.5.26","page":17,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assign a competent EHSS Risk Management Focal Point, or equivalent role, responsible for supporting the quality, maintenance, review and reporting of EHSS risk management outputs."},{"id":"2.5.27","page":17,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain risk registers for Medium, Significant, High and Critical EHSS risks through HEMAYA."},{"id":"2.5.28","page":17,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Risk registers shall identify, as a minimum:\na. The hazard or risk source;\nb. The unwanted event;\nc. The risk level;\nd. Existing controls and safeguards;\ne. Interim controls;\nf. Required mitigation actions;\ng. Responsible people;\nh. Target completion dates; and\ni. Review and verification requirements."},{"id":"2.5.29","page":17,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review the status of Medium, Significant, High and Critical risks and the effectiveness of associated controls, interim controls and risk treatment actions on monthly basis."},{"id":"2.5.30","page":18,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall validate at least quarterly the implementation and effectiveness of interim controls and mitigation actions for Critical and High risks, and retain documented evidence supporting decisions to close, extend or renew the risk approval."},{"id":"2.5.31","page":18,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that risks from their EHSS risk registers, including risks arising from the failure or degradation of critical controls associated with identified material unwanted events in accordance with section 2.6 Critical Control Management, are reflected in the Business Units or Corporate Functions ERM risk registers ."},{"id":"2.5.32","page":18,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Critical and High Risk status shall be periodically reported to the applicable Maaden executive EHSS governance forum in accordance with section 6.4 Management Review and Continual Improvement."},{"id":"2.5.33","page":18,"heading":"Risk Registers and Stewardship","text":"Business Units shall provide quarterly reporting of Critical and High Risks to Corporate EHSS for periodic review and verification."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"EHSS Risk Management; risk register, HIRA, TRA, bowtie, risk assessment; EHSS Risk Management Framework; Risk Identification and Assessment; Risk Assessment Triggers; Risk Treatment and Interim Controls; Competence and Participation; Risk Approval and Acceptance; Risk Registers and Stewardship","trigger":"if hazards, exposures, aspects, threats or changes require assessment and treatment","refs":["2.6","3.47","6.4"]},{"id":"2.6","title":"Critical Control Management","page":18,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for identifying, implementing, verifying and improving critical controls for fatal hazards and associated material unwanted events.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden controlled activities, facilities, projects and operations where hazards have the potential to result in one or more fatalities or life altering occupational illness or impairment.","clauses":[{"id":"2.6.1","page":18,"heading":"Critical Control Management Framework and Accountability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain a Critical Control Management process with reference to the ICMM Critical Control Management Good Practice Guide and applicable Maaden risk management requirements."},{"id":"2.6.2","page":18,"heading":"Critical Control Management Framework and Accountability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and define applicable fatal hazards and associated material unwanted events, including their scope, credible causes and maximum reasonable consequences. Applicability shall be based on the maximum reasonable consequence, considering credible failure of existing controls, and shall not be excluded solely because the likelihood of the material unwanted event is assessed as rare."},{"id":"2.6.3","page":18,"heading":"Critical Control Management Framework and Accountability","text":"Critical Control Management shall be led by operational line management. EHSS, Risk and relevant technical functions shall provide governance, facilitation, technical support and assurance without replacing operational accountability."},{"id":"2.6.4","page":18,"heading":"Critical Control Management Framework and Accountability","text":"A Risk Owner shall be assigned for each fatal hazard or defined group of related material unwanted events, and a Control Owner shall be assigned for each critical control. Responsibilities for implementing, verifying, evaluating and reporting critical controls shall be defined."},{"id":"2.6.5","page":18,"heading":"Fatal Hazards, Material Unwanted Events and Critical Controls","text":"Controls for applicable material unwanted events shall be identified using an appropriate hazard and control analysis methodology, considering the nature and complexity of the hazard."},{"id":"2.6.6","page":18,"heading":"Fatal Hazards, Material Unwanted Events and Critical Controls","text":"A control shall only be designated as a critical control where:\na. it directly prevents a material unwanted event or mitigates its consequences;\nb. its absence, degradation or failure would significantly increase the likelihood or severity of the material unwanted event despite other controls; and\nc. its required performance can be defined and verified. Critical control selection shall also consider the importance, reliability and contribution of the control relative to other controls."},{"id":"2.6.7","page":19,"heading":"Fatal Hazards, Material Unwanted Events and Critical Controls","text":"Supporting activities, including management plans, procedures, risk assessments, training, maintenance and monitoring, shall not be designated as critical controls unless they independently meet the critical control criteria."},{"id":"2.6.8","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Performance Requirements","text":"Each critical control shall have an approved Critical Control Performance Specification, Performance Standard or equivalent documented requirement."},{"id":"2.6.9","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Performance Requirements","text":"The performance specification shall establish specific and verifiable performance requirements and define, as applicable:\na. the control objective and associated material unwanted event;\nb. required performance and applicable operating limits;\nc. factors that could degrade or compromise the control and supporting activities required to maintain its performance;\nd. verification requirements and acceptance criteria;\ne. performance triggers, including applicable Go/No-Go criteria and required response; and\nf. the accountable Control Owner."},{"id":"2.6.10","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Performance Requirements","text":"Performance requirements shall reflect the nature of the critical control and, where applicable, the approved design basis, technical standards, operating limits and required human actions."},{"id":"2.6.11","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Implementation","text":"Critical controls and their performance requirements shall be integrated into the applicable processes through which work is designed, planned, authorized, executed, maintained and reviewed."},{"id":"2.6.12","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Implementation","text":"Personnel and contractors exposed to fatal hazards shall understand the applicable material unwanted events, critical controls, their responsibilities, Go/No-Go criteria and required actions where a critical control is absent, degraded or ineffective."},{"id":"2.6.13","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Implementation","text":"Before exposure to a fatal hazard, applicable task dependent critical controls shall be confirmed as in place and meeting the defined Go/No-Go criteria."},{"id":"2.6.14","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Implementation","text":"Work shall not commence or continue where an applicable Go/No-Go criterion is not met. Any temporary substitution, impairment or alternative arrangement affecting a critical control shall be formally assessed and authorized through the applicable MSHEM Risk Management, EHSS Critical Systems Bypass or Management of Change process, as applicable."},{"id":"2.6.15","page":19,"heading":"Critical Control Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish a risk-based verification program to determine whether critical controls are in place and meet their approved performance requirements."},{"id":"2.6.16","page":20,"heading":"Critical Control Verification","text":"Verification methods, frequencies and responsibilities shall be appropriate to the control type, failure mechanism, consequence of failure and applicable technical, legal, regulatory or manufacturer requirements."},{"id":"2.6.17","page":20,"heading":"Critical Control Verification","text":"Critical control verification shall form part of operational line management accountability. Technical specialists shall participate where specialist competence is required, and verification shall not be treated solely as an EHSS, audit or assurance activity."},{"id":"2.6.18","page":20,"heading":"Critical Control Verification","text":"Where a critical control is absent, degraded, bypassed, ineffective or does not meet its approved performance requirements, the deficiency shall be addressed and escalated according to its significance. Exposure shall be prevented or stopped where applicable Go/No-Go criteria are not met, and required corrective actions shall be tracked to verified closure."},{"id":"2.6.19","page":20,"heading":"Critical Control Verification","text":"Critical control deficiencies shall be assessed for broader applicability where equivalent controls, equipment, locations or activities may be affected."},{"id":"2.6.20","page":20,"heading":"Critical Control Verification","text":"Personnel implementing or verifying critical controls shall meet the required competency requirements established as part of section 2.7 EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication process."},{"id":"2.6.21","page":20,"heading":"Evaluation, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Risk Owners, with input from Control Owners and relevant technical specialists, shall periodically evaluate critical control effectiveness considering whether the control is adequately designed to achieve its objective; and whether the control is effectively implemented and operating as required."},{"id":"2.6.22","page":20,"heading":"Evaluation, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Critical control evaluation shall consider relevant verification results, incidents and High Potential Incidents, recurring deficiencies, inspection and maintenance results, assurance findings, changes and operating experience."},{"id":"2.6.23","page":20,"heading":"Evaluation, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall monitor and report the health and effectiveness of critical controls, including significant or recurring deficiencies and corrective actions."},{"id":"2.6.24","page":20,"heading":"Evaluation, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Significant or systemic critical control deficiencies shall be escalated through the applicable Business Unit and Corporate governance arrangements, and relevant lessons shall be communicated to personnel and contractors who implement, verify or rely on the affected controls."},{"id":"2.6.25","page":20,"heading":"Review and Management of Change","text":"Fatal hazards, material unwanted events, critical controls and associated performance requirements shall be reviewed periodically and following relevant incidents or High Potential Incidents, recurring critical control deficiencies, material changes, or relevant changes in legal, technical or operating knowledge."},{"id":"2.6.26","page":20,"heading":"Review and Management of Change","text":"Changes to the approved Critical Control Management scope, fatal hazards, material unwanted events, critical controls or performance requirements shall be controlled through the Management of Change process."},{"id":"2.6.27","page":20,"heading":"Review and Management of Change","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain controlled documented information sufficient to demonstrate implementation and effectiveness of Critical Control Management."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"Critical Control Management; critical control, MUE, material unwanted event, control verification; Critical Control Management Framework and Accountability; Fatal Hazards, Material Unwanted Events and Critical Controls; Critical Control Performance Requirements; Critical Control Implementation; Critical Control Verification; Evaluation, Reporting and Improvement; Review and Management of Change","trigger":"if a fatal or material unwanted event or associated critical control is relevant","refs":["2.7"]},{"id":"2.7","title":"EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication","page":20,"purpose":"Personnel are competent, trained, informed and engaged in EHSS matters relevant to their roles and activities through effective competency management, training, awareness and communication processes.","scope":"This process applies to the identification, development, implementation, verification, review and maintenance of EHSS competency, training, awareness and communication requirements for employees, contractor personnel and other relevant people undertaking activities on behalf of Maaden.","clauses":[{"id":"2.7.1","page":21,"heading":"Competency Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify the EHSS competencies required for roles that can affect EHSS performance, considering, as a minimum, assigned responsibilities, applicable legal and other requirements, EHSS risks, and operational and emergency response requirements."},{"id":"2.7.2","page":21,"heading":"Competency Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain role based EHSS competency requirements, including for safety critical roles, defining applicable competency criteria, required training, assessment or verification methods, proficiency or acceptance criteria, and revalidation requirements commensurate with the risks associated with the role."},{"id":"2.7.3","page":21,"heading":"Competency Management","text":"Personnel performing activities that can affect EHSS performance shall be competent based on appropriate education, training, qualifications, experience, or a combination thereof."},{"id":"2.7.4","page":21,"heading":"Competency Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess the competency of relevant personnel against established requirements and implement appropriate actions to address identified competency gaps."},{"id":"2.7.5","page":21,"heading":"Competency Management","text":"Competency shall be verified before personnel perform critical risk or role specific work and revalidated at defined intervals, with required certifications, licenses and authorizations kept current and valid."},{"id":"2.7.6","page":21,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and maintain applicable mandatory EHSS training requirements for employees and contractor personnel, and establish EHSS training programs based on competency requirements, legal obligations, EHSS risks and operational needs."},{"id":"2.7.7","page":21,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"Required EHSS training shall be completed before personnel undertake activities for which specific training or competency is required."},{"id":"2.7.8","page":21,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"The suspension, deferment or significant interruption of mandatory EHSS training or required EHSS awareness programs shall be formally authorized, risk assessed and documented. Interim controls and recovery plans shall be established, including affected priority roles and training requirements, defined responsibilities, completion or reinstatement criteria, monitoring and escalation of outstanding requirements."},{"id":"2.7.9","page":21,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"All EHSS training interventions shall comply with applicable Maaden Learning Center policies, procedures, standards and governance requirements, including its governance and oversight of learning compliance, quality assurance and training record integrity across Maaden."},{"id":"2.7.10","page":21,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"The Maaden Learning Center shall review and endorse, before contract signing, all scopes of work involving external EHSS training providers, consultants, certification bodies, or training interventions to verify alignment with corporate learning standards, competency requirements, quality assurance processes, and governance requirements."},{"id":"2.7.11","page":21,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"Completion and attendance for mandatory EHSS training shall be supported by verifiable evidence. Missed, overdue or expired mandatory training, and approved exceptions, shall be managed through defined arrangements including refresher or makeup training, escalation, monitoring of repeated non-attendance and documented closure."},{"id":"2.7.12","page":22,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"Employees, contractor personnel and visitors shall receive applicable EHSS induction and awareness information before accessing or undertaking activities within Maaden controlled workplaces."},{"id":"2.7.13","page":22,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"The effectiveness of EHSS training shall be evaluated and verified using methods appropriate to the training objectives and associated risks."},{"id":"2.7.14","page":22,"heading":"EHSS Training","text":"EHSS training and competency assessments shall be conducted by personnel competent in the applicable subject matter and training or assessment method."},{"id":"2.7.15","page":22,"heading":"EHSS Awareness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide employees, contractor personnel and other relevant people with EHSS awareness based on their roles, activities and exposure to risk, including, as applicable, the Maaden EHSS Policy, relevant EHSS risks and controls, individual responsibilities and accountabilities, applicable obligations, incident and emergency reporting requirements, and stop-work authority and responsibilities."},{"id":"2.7.16","page":22,"heading":"EHSS communication","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain processes to communicate relevant EHSS information to employees, contractor personnel and other interested parties, as applicable, in a manner and language understood by the intended audience."},{"id":"2.7.17","page":22,"heading":"EHSS communication","text":"EHSS communication processes shall support consultation, participation, feedback and the sharing of relevant EHSS information, including incidents, lessons learned, changes in requirements and improvement opportunities."},{"id":"2.7.18","page":22,"heading":"Records and Review","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain accurate, complete and current records of required EHSS competency, training, attendance, verification, revalidation, exceptions and certifications. Required records shall be maintained in the designated Maaden Learning Center platforms, with missing, overdue, expired or non-compliant requirements identified, escalated and addressed."},{"id":"2.7.19","page":22,"heading":"Records and Review","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically review competency, training, awareness and communication requirements and associated compliance. Requirements shall be updated following significant changes to activities, processes, EHSS risks, applicable obligations, technology, organizational responsibilities, or relevant lessons from incidents and assurance findings."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication; ehss competency, awareness and communication; Competency Management; EHSS Training; EHSS Awareness; EHSS communication; Records and Review","trigger":"if competency, authorization, consultation, awareness or communication is required","refs":[]},{"id":"2.8","title":"Life Saving Rules","page":22,"purpose":"The Maaden Life Saving Rules establish clear and consistent non-negotiable requirements for activities and conditions associated with fatal or other high consequence risks and provide a common basis for identifying, reporting and managing Life Saving Rule violations across Maaden.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden employees, contractor personnel and other people performing applicable activities under Maaden operational control, with the Life Saving Rules forming part of the applicable MSHEM risk management, critical control, work planning and operational requirements.","clauses":[{"id":"2.8.1","page":23,"heading":"Maaden Life Saving Rules","text":"Maaden shall establish and maintain the following nine Life Saving Rules:\na. Permit to Work\nb. Confined Space\nc. Energy Isolation\nd. Line of fire\ne. Working at height\nf. Mobile equipment\ng. Geotechnical Ground Control and Stability\nh. Hazardous Materials and Explosives\ni. EHSS Critical Systems Bypass"},{"id":"2.8.2","page":23,"heading":"Maaden Life Saving Rules","text":"Each Life Saving Rule shall have approved Maaden trigger criteria defining the acts, omissions or conditions that constitute an LSR violation."},{"id":"2.8.3","page":23,"heading":"Maaden Life Saving Rules","text":"An event shall only be classified as an LSR violation where the applicable approved Maaden LSR trigger criteria are met."},{"id":"2.8.4","page":23,"heading":"Maaden Life Saving Rules","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall integrate applicable Life Saving Rules and associated controls into relevant work planning, risk assessment, permit to work, contractor management and other operational arrangements."},{"id":"2.8.5","page":23,"heading":"Maaden Life Saving Rules","text":"Personnel and contractors undertaking work to which an LSR applies shall be informed of the applicable Life Saving Rule and its trigger criteria."},{"id":"2.8.6","page":23,"heading":"Maaden Life Saving Rules","text":"Where an applicable LSR trigger condition exists, affected work shall not commence or continue until the condition has been made safe and applicable control requirements are satisfied. Life Saving Rules support the Critical Control Management framework and do not replace applicable critical control performance, verification or Go/No-Go requirements under Section 2.6."},{"id":"2.8.7","page":23,"heading":"Maaden Life Saving Rules","text":"LSR violations shall be reported, recorded, classified and managed in accordance with Section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship."}],"area":"Governance","part":"EHSS Governance, Risk and Capability","keywords":"Life Saving Rules; LSR, life saving rule, lifesaving rule, stop work; Maaden Life Saving Rules","trigger":"if the activity falls within a Maaden Life Saving Rule or associated stop-work condition","refs":["6.1"]},{"id":"3.1","title":"Environmental Risk and Impact Management","page":23,"purpose":"Environmental risks, impacts and obligations arising from Maaden activities, products, services and projects are systematically identified, assessed, maintained within acceptable risk tolerance, and considered in operational, project and closure decision making throughout the asset lifecycle.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions. It applies across the lifecycle of an asset including exploration, feasibility, design, construction, operation, closure and rehabilitation.","clauses":[{"id":"3.1.1","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risk and Impact Identification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify environmental aspects, impacts, environmental hazards and environmental risks associated with activities, products, services, projects and facilities under their control or influence, in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.1.2","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risk and Impact Identification","text":"Environmental risk and impact identification shall consider all routine activities, non-routine activities, abnormal operating conditions, reasonably foreseeable emergency situations, planned changes and projects, closure and rehabilitation activities, and legacy environmental conditions where applicable."},{"id":"3.1.3","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risk and Impact Identification","text":"Environmental risk and impact identification shall consider the full range environmental hazards and receptors, including land, water, air, acid rock drainage, heavy metals and mercury, hazardous chemicals, biodiversity, waste and tailings, energy, climate, cultural heritage and others."},{"id":"3.1.4","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risk and Impact Identification","text":"Environmental risk and impact identification shall consider information from applicable studies, monitoring data, incidents, audit findings, inspections, permit requirements, stakeholder concerns, regulatory observations, scientific information and operational experience."},{"id":"3.1.5","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risk and Impact Assessment","text":"Environmental aspects and associated impacts shall be evaluated to determine their significance, taking into account applicable legal and permit conditions, the nature and severity of the impact, likelihood, affected environmental receptors, community concerns and relevant stakeholder expectations."},{"id":"3.1.6","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risk and Impact Assessment","text":"Environmental risks arising from identified aspects and impacts shall be assessed and managed in accordance with the section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.1.7","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risk and Impact Assessment","text":"Environmental aspects and associated impacts shall be prioritized for avoidance, minimization, or elimination."},{"id":"3.1.8","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risks and Impacts","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish criteria to determine environmental risks and impacts."},{"id":"3.1.9","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risks and Impacts","text":"Environmental risks and impacts shall be documented and maintained in a controlled register."},{"id":"3.1.10","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risks and Impacts","text":"Environmental risks and impacts shall be considered when establishing objectives, plans, operational controls, monitoring programs and improvement initiatives. All critical, high and significant risks and the risk treatment plan shall be communicated to the relevant Business Unit management as well as Corporate environment."},{"id":"3.1.11","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Risks and Impacts","text":"Environmental controls shall be maintained for all environmental risks and impacts throughout the asset lifecycle stages."},{"id":"3.1.12","page":24,"heading":"Environmental Obligations and Decision Making","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and maintain environmental legal, regulatory, permit and other obligations relevant to identified environmental risks and impacts in accordance with section 2.4 EHSS Legal and Regulatory Requirements process."},{"id":"3.1.13","page":25,"heading":"Environmental Obligations and Decision Making","text":"Environmental risks, impacts and obligations shall be considered during project development, operational planning, modification, expansion, closure and rehabilitation decisions."},{"id":"3.1.14","page":25,"heading":"Environmental Obligations and Decision Making","text":"Environmental risk assessments shall support permitting, environmental studies, environmental management plans and operational control arrangements where required."},{"id":"3.1.15","page":25,"heading":"Environmental Obligations and Decision Making","text":"Where environmental risks or impacts may affect external stakeholders, ecosystems, protected areas or shared resources, the Business Unit shall consider applicable stakeholders and regulatory requirements."},{"id":"3.1.16","page":25,"heading":"Review Cycle and Change Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review and, where necessary, update environmental risk and impact assessments at defined intervals and whenever any of the following occurs:\na. significant change to activities, processes, products, services, facilities or operating conditions;\nb. new information becomes available that may affect the assessment outcomes;\nc. a significant environmental incident occurs;\nd. a change to applicable legal, regulatory, permit or other compliance obligations occurs;\ne. an audit, assurance, inspection or review activities identify the need for reassessment."},{"id":"3.1.17","page":25,"heading":"Review Cycle and Change Management","text":"Changes that may alter environmental risks, impacts, obligations or controls shall be evaluated through the applicable Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.1.18","page":25,"heading":"Review Cycle and Change Management","text":"Environmental risk and impact information shall be maintained current and reflective of actual operating conditions."},{"id":"3.1.19","page":25,"heading":"Monitoring and Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall monitor the effectiveness of controls implemented for significant environmental risks and impacts."},{"id":"3.1.20","page":25,"heading":"Monitoring and Verification","text":"Monitoring and assurance activities shall verify that identified environmental risks and impacts remain understood, controlled and compliant with applicable obligations."},{"id":"3.1.21","page":25,"heading":"Monitoring and Verification","text":"Deficiencies identified through monitoring, audits, inspections, incidents or reviews shall be evaluated and addressed."},{"id":"3.1.22","page":25,"heading":"Records and Evidence","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain documented information demonstrating environmental risk and impact assessments, significant environmental risk and impact registers, environmental obligations relevant to identified risks and impacts, reassessments and reviews, and actions arising from identified environmental risks and impacts."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Environmental Risk and Impact Management; environmental risk and impact management; Environmental Risk and Impact Identification; Environmental Risk and Impact Assessment; Significant Environmental Risks and Impacts; Environmental Obligations and Decision-Making; Review cycle and Change Management; Monitoring and Verification; Records and Evidence","trigger":"if the activity creates or changes a significant environmental aspect or impact","refs":["2.4","2.5"]},{"id":"3.2","title":"Environmental Permitting and Compliance","page":25,"purpose":"Environmental legal and regulatory obligations, permits and approvals applicable to Maaden are identified, obtained, maintained, implemented, monitored, verified and managed throughout their lifecycle to maintain compliance and prevent permit breaches, enforcement actions and environmental non-compliance.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions where Maaden activities are subject to environmental permits and approvals or authorizations, legal and regulatory obligations, environmental approval requirements associated with exploration, projects, construction, operation, modification, expansion, closure, rehabilitation or post-closure activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.2.1","page":26,"heading":"Governance and Lifecycle Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define accountability and responsibility for obtaining, maintaining and complying with applicable environmental permits, approvals and compliance obligations."},{"id":"3.2.2","page":26,"heading":"Governance and Lifecycle Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and integrate applicable environmental permit and approval requirements throughout the lifecycle of activities, projects and assets."},{"id":"3.2.3","page":26,"heading":"Governance and Lifecycle Management","text":"Required environmental permits, licenses and approvals shall be obtained before the applicable activity begins and maintained throughout their period of applicability."},{"id":"3.2.4","page":26,"heading":"Governance and Lifecycle Management","text":"Projects shall follow sustainability requirements provided in the Maaden Capital Investment System (MCIS_TK_0387_Sustainability Requirements Toolkit )for consideration at different stages of the project."},{"id":"3.2.5","page":26,"heading":"Governance and Lifecycle Management","text":"Constraints, permitting delays, regulatory actions, or compliance risks shall be escalated through the applicable governance processes and incorporated into the relevant operational and project risk registers."},{"id":"3.2.6","page":26,"heading":"Identification and Management of Compliance Obligations","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify applicable environmental laws, regulations, permit and approval conditions, monitoring and reporting requirements, regulatory directions and formally adopted environmental commitments in accordance with section 2.4 EHSS Legal and Regulatory Requirements process."},{"id":"3.2.7","page":26,"heading":"Identification and Management of Compliance Obligations","text":"Operational, engineering and administrative controls shall be implemented and maintained to comply with applicable environmental obligations, permit conditions and regulatory commitments. Obligations and related actions shall be assigned to responsible functions and tracked to completion through Hemaya system."},{"id":"3.2.8","page":26,"heading":"Change Management and Permit Maintenance","text":"Proposed changes shall be evaluated before implementation to determine their effect on environmental permits, approvals and compliance obligations in accordance with the section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.2.9","page":26,"heading":"Change Management and Permit Maintenance","text":"Environmental compliance obligations, permit requirements and related controls shall be reviewed and updated following relevant legal, regulatory, permit, project, operational or organizational changes."},{"id":"3.2.10","page":26,"heading":"Regulatory Monitoring and Reporting","text":"Environmental monitoring required by environmental permits, regulatory approvals or legal obligations shall be defined and implemented in accordance with section 3.3 Environmental Monitoring process."},{"id":"3.2.11","page":26,"heading":"Regulatory Monitoring and Reporting","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that required environmental reports and regulatory submissions, including environmental monitoring reports, permit application packages, environmental impact assessment reports, environmental audit reports and associated studies, are reviewed to identify and address compliance concerns before submission to the relevant authority."},{"id":"3.2.12","page":27,"heading":"Regulatory Compliance Verification and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically evaluate compliance with applicable environmental obligations using documented evidence, including permits, approvals, monitoring results, regulatory reporting and adopted commitments."},{"id":"3.2.13","page":27,"heading":"Regulatory Compliance Verification and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall be prepared in advance and maintain readiness as per Maaden Regulatory Environmental Inspection Violation Governance Protocols and fully co-operate with regulatory inspections, audits, assessments and compliance verification activities."},{"id":"3.2.14","page":27,"heading":"Non-Compliance, Permit Breaches and Corrective Action","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify, report and manage, regulatory requirement deviations, environmental permit breaches, non-compliance events, regulatory findings and observations, regulatory notices of violations, penalties, or other enforcement actions and major noncompliance risks."},{"id":"3.2.15","page":27,"heading":"Non-Compliance, Permit Breaches and Corrective Action","text":"Actual or potential environmental non-compliance shall be investigated to determine its causes and required corrective actions, with actions assigned, tracked and verified for effectiveness by the relevant Business Unit."},{"id":"3.2.16","page":27,"heading":"Non-Compliance, Permit Breaches and Corrective Action","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall notify and respond to the relevant regulatory authorities within the timeframes and in the manner required by applicable laws, permit conditions or regulatory directions for environmental non-compliance, major environmental incidents, shutdowns, emergencies and other reportable events."},{"id":"3.2.17","page":27,"heading":"Records, Review and Improvement","text":"Environmental permitting and compliance records shall be controlled, retained and available in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory and corporate requirements."},{"id":"3.2.18","page":27,"heading":"Records, Review and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically review and report environmental permitting and compliance performance in accordance with section 6.4 Management Review and Continual Improvement."},{"id":"3.2.19","page":27,"heading":"Records, Review and Improvement","text":"Environmental permitting and compliance arrangements shall be updated following changes, compliance evaluations, regulatory inspections, non-compliance events, findings and relevant lessons learned."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Environmental Permitting and Compliance; environmental permit, consent, licence, compliance; Governance and Lifecycle Management; Identification and Management of Compliance Obligations; Change Management and Permit Maintenance; Regulatory Monitoring and Reporting; Regulatory Compliance Verification and Assurance; Non-Compliance, Permit Breaches and Corrective Action; Records, Review and Improvement","trigger":"if an environmental permit, approval, licence, consent or compliance condition applies","refs":["2.4","3.3","3.52","6.4"]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Environmental Monitoring","page":27,"purpose":"Environmental monitoring is established and maintained to verify environmental performance, demonstrate compliance with legal, regulatory and permit obligations, and other commitments, and to detect actual or potential environmental harm, and support timely corrective action and continual improvement throughout the lifecycle of applicable sources and assets.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions where environmental monitoring is required by applicable obligations, environmental risk assessments, environmental commitments or Maaden requirements, throughout the relevant lifecycle phases.","clauses":[{"id":"3.3.1","page":28,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Applicability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and document environmental monitoring requirements arising from applicable legal and regulatory obligations, permits, approvals, authorizations, environmental assessments, commitments and Maaden internal requirements."},{"id":"3.3.2","page":28,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Applicability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine and document applicable environmental monitoring requirements for relevant project, operational, closure and post-closure lifecycle phases and establish environmental monitoring programs to address those requirements."},{"id":"3.3.3","page":28,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Applicability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assign competent personnel to implement, review and report on environmental monitoring programs."},{"id":"3.3.4","page":28,"heading":"Environmental Monitoring Planning and Implementation","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, maintain and implement a documented environmental monitoring plan to demonstrate compliance with applicable obligations, commitments and Maaden requirements."},{"id":"3.3.5","page":28,"heading":"Environmental Monitoring Planning and Implementation","text":"Environmental monitoring plans shall be based on significant environmental aspects and associated risks, relevant receptors and exposure pathways, applicable obligations, operational controls and lifecycle requirements. The plans shall define, as applicable, monitoring locations, parameters, methods, frequencies, limits and trigger levels, and data quality, reporting and escalation requirements."},{"id":"3.3.6","page":28,"heading":"Environmental Monitoring Planning and Implementation","text":"Environmental monitoring plans shall be reviewed and updated following significant changes affecting their basis, scope or requirements."},{"id":"3.3.7","page":28,"heading":"Environmental Monitoring Planning and Implementation","text":"Environmental monitoring shall be performed using approved or accepted methods and equipment, qualified personnel, authorized service providers and competent laboratories."},{"id":"3.3.8","page":28,"heading":"Environmental Monitoring Planning and Implementation","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain the monitoring locations, infrastructure, equipment and supporting systems necessary to implement the environmental monitoring plan."},{"id":"3.3.9","page":28,"heading":"Data Quality, Validation and Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish quality assurance and quality control measures to ensure the integrity, traceability and reliability of environmental monitoring data. Records demonstrating monitoring data quality and validation shall be retained."},{"id":"3.3.10","page":28,"heading":"Data Quality, Validation and Control","text":"Environmental monitoring data shall be reviewed and validated by competent Business Unit environmental personnel before use for decision-making, performance evaluation or external reporting. Invalid, unreliable or incomplete data shall be investigated and addressed before use."},{"id":"3.3.11","page":28,"heading":"Exceedance, Escalation and Corrective Action","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish documented criteria for identifying exceedances of applicable limits or internal trigger levels, adverse environmental trends and monitoring program failures."},{"id":"3.3.12","page":28,"heading":"Exceedance, Escalation and Corrective Action","text":"Identified exceedances and actual or potential environmental non-compliance shall be reported, classified, investigated and escalated in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory, permit and section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship requirements."},{"id":"3.3.13","page":28,"heading":"Exceedance, Escalation and Corrective Action","text":"Corrective actions addressing the identified causes of exceedances, adverse environmental trends and environmental monitoring program failures shall be implemented and their effectiveness verified."},{"id":"3.3.14","page":29,"heading":"Reporting and Regulatory Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify the completeness and accuracy of required environmental monitoring reports and submit them in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory and permit requirements, including content, format and submission timeframes."},{"id":"3.3.15","page":29,"heading":"Reporting and Regulatory Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall communicate significant environmental monitoring results, exceedances and compliance issues to relevant internal and external stakeholders, along with justifications and corrective action plans where applicable."},{"id":"3.3.16","page":29,"heading":"Verification and Review","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically analyze environmental monitoring results and review monitoring programs to identify trends, emerging risks and improvement opportunities and confirm their continued applicability and effectiveness."},{"id":"3.3.17","page":29,"heading":"Verification and Review","text":"Environmental monitoring programs shall be updated where review findings or changes in applicable obligations, environmental risks, operating or lifecycle conditions affect monitoring requirements or program effectiveness."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Environmental Monitoring; environmental monitoring; Governance, Accountability and Applicability; Environmental Monitoring Planning; Environmental Monitoring Program Implementation; Data Quality, Validation and Control; Exceedance, Escalation and Corrective Action; Reporting and Regulatory Compliance; Verification and Review","trigger":"if environmental sampling, monitoring, calibration or data reporting is required","refs":["6.1"]},{"id":"3.4","title":"Environmental Footprint Management","page":29,"purpose":"Maaden environmental footprint data are complete, accurate, validated, traceable, and consistently consolidated to support internal management, investor reporting, external sustainability disclosures, performance evaluation, target setting, and strategic decision making.","scope":"This process applies to all Business Units, Corporate Functions, contractors and third parties that generate or report data contributing to Maaden’s environmental footprint reporting, including greenhouse gas emissions, energy, water, materials and waste data.","clauses":[{"id":"3.4.1","page":29,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain an Environmental Footprint Management process for the collection, validation, consolidation, analysis and reporting of environmental footprint data."},{"id":"3.4.2","page":29,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Governance and Accountability","text":"Corporate Sustainability shall maintain and periodically review the approved Environmental Footprint Technical Guidance Document, including environmental footprint methodologies, indicator definitions, reporting requirements, and reporting protocols."},{"id":"3.4.3","page":29,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Aspects and Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and assess aspects that may affect the integrity, accuracy, completeness, consistency or reliability of environmental footprint data."},{"id":"3.4.4","page":29,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Aspects and Controls","text":"The environmental footprint aspect assessment shall consider, where applicable:\na. unknown reporting boundaries;\nb. excluded data sources and Inaccurate source data;\nc. metering equipment deficiencies and failures;\nd. inconsistent methodologies and unauthorized calculation factors;\ne. non verified estimates; and\nf. calculation errors and Disclosure oversight."},{"id":"3.4.5","page":30,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Aspects and Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify, implement and periodically verify controls to prevent, detect and correct environmental footprint data errors and reporting deficiencies that could result in materially incorrect environmental performance information or disclosures."},{"id":"3.4.6","page":30,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Aspects and Controls","text":"Environmental footprint risks and controls shall be reviewed following significant changes to activities, assets, reporting obligations, organizational arrangements or reporting methodologies."},{"id":"3.4.7","page":30,"heading":"Reporting Boundaries and Data Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented environmental reporting boundaries consistent with corporate requirements and applicable reporting obligations provided in the Environmental Footprint Technical Guidance Document."},{"id":"3.4.8","page":30,"heading":"Reporting Boundaries and Data Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that environmental footprint data are obtained from identified and authorized sources, remain traceable to supporting source information, and are reported using documented and consistently applied indicators, definitions, units of measure, responsibilities, reporting frequencies and calculation methodologies. Material changes to reporting boundaries, methodologies, inventories or indicators shall be reviewed, approved and documented."},{"id":"3.4.9","page":30,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Data Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall collect, maintain and submit environmental footprint data relevant to their activities, products and services in accordance with approved reporting requirements."},{"id":"3.4.10","page":30,"heading":"Environmental Footprint Data Management","text":"Missing, incomplete, inconsistent or inaccurate environmental footprint data shall be identified, investigated, corrected and documented."},{"id":"3.4.11","page":30,"heading":"Calculation, Estimation and Methodology Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall use approved methodologies, verified emission factors, conversion factors and calculation methods specified in Environmental Footprint Technical Guidance Document."},{"id":"3.4.12","page":30,"heading":"Calculation, Estimation and Methodology Control","text":"Estimates or data substitutions shall be used only when valid source data are unavailable or do not meet defined data quality criteria, with their use justified, documented and traceable."},{"id":"3.4.13","page":30,"heading":"Calculation, Estimation and Methodology Control","text":"Changes to calculation methodologies, factors, assumptions or estimation methods shall be assessed, approved and documented, including evaluation of the effects of significant changes on reported inventories, historical performance, baselines and targets."},{"id":"3.4.14","page":30,"heading":"Data Validation, Quality Assurance and Consolidation","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall validate environmental footprint data and consolidated information before finalization or reporting to confirm completeness, accuracy, consistency and traceability, and investigate, resolve and document identified discrepancies, anomalies or validation failures."},{"id":"3.4.15","page":30,"heading":"Performance Indicators, Baselines and Targets","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain applicable environmental footprint performance indicators, baselines and targets aligned with corporate requirements, Maaden strategic objectives and applicable business specific commitments, and monitor performance and progress against them."},{"id":"3.4.16","page":31,"heading":"Performance Indicators, Baselines and Targets","text":"Material deviations from approved baselines or targets shall be investigated, documented and escalated in accordance with defined criteria."},{"id":"3.4.17","page":31,"heading":"Reporting, Disclosure and Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide environmental footprint information and data required for corporate reporting and external disclosures in accordance with Environmental Footprint Technical Guidance Document."},{"id":"3.4.18","page":31,"heading":"Reporting, Disclosure and Verification","text":"Environmental footprint data, performance metrics and material environmental disclosures shall be reviewed for completeness, consistency and accuracy before external release and shall be supported by documented evidence, traceable source data and Maaden approved methodologies aligned with applicable GRI disclosure requirements and other applicable reporting criteria."},{"id":"3.4.19","page":31,"heading":"Reporting, Disclosure and Verification","text":"Independent verification or assurance shall be undertaken where required by legal obligations, adopted standards, certification schemes, stakeholder commitments or corporate requirements."},{"id":"3.4.20","page":31,"heading":"Monitoring, Escalation and Corrective Action","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate, at defined intervals, the implementation and effectiveness of environmental footprint management requirements and controls, and escalate legal non-compliance, material misstatements, control failures, overdue actions and significant reporting deficiencies in accordance with section 6.4 Management Review and Continual Improvement."},{"id":"3.4.21","page":31,"heading":"Monitoring, Escalation and Corrective Action","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and implement environmental footprint reduction and performance improvement plans where environmental footprint performance evaluation identifies adverse trends, unmet objectives or targets, or opportunities for improvement."},{"id":"3.4.22","page":31,"heading":"Monitoring, Escalation and Corrective Action","text":"Environmental footprint management findings and deficiencies shall be investigated, with corrective actions tracked to verified closure and lessons learned communicated and used to improve controls, competency, methodologies and reporting processes."},{"id":"3.4.23","page":31,"heading":"Monitoring, Escalation and Corrective Action","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain environmental footprint management records that are controlled, traceable, retrievable and protected from loss, unauthorized alteration or destruction, and shall retain them in accordance with applicable legal and Maaden requirements."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Environmental Footprint Management; environmental footprint management; Environmental Footprint Governance and Accountability; Environmental Footprint Aspects and Controls; Reporting Boundaries and Data Framework; Environmental Footprint Data Management; Calculation, Estimation and Methodology Control; Data Validation, Quality Assurance and Consolidation; Performance Indicators, Baselines and Targets; Reporting, Disclosure and Verification; Monitoring, Escalation and Corrective Action; Records and Retention","trigger":"if greenhouse-gas, resource, material, water, energy or waste footprint data are managed","refs":["6.4"]},{"id":"3.5","title":"Air Quality and Emissions Management","page":31,"purpose":"Air quality and emissions are systematically identified, assessed, prevented, controlled, monitored and continually improved to protect environment and community receptors; prevent pollution and adverse impacts on health and ecosystems; maintain compliance with applicable legal and regulatory obligations and sustain responsible environmental stewardship throughout the assets lifecycle.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business units and Corporate Functions with activities that may generate air emissions from construction, mining, crushing, screening, conveying, stockpiling, material handling, transporting, manufacturing and materials processing, refining, smelting, power generation, storing, treatment and other operational activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.5.1","page":32,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Applicability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain an Air Quality and Emissions Management process, aligned with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, permit obligations and other agreed commitments."},{"id":"3.5.2","page":32,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Applicability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine and document the applicability and boundaries of air quality and emissions management for their activities, projects, facilities and assets, including relevant emission sources and categories, air pollutants and potential receptors."},{"id":"3.5.3","page":32,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Applicability","text":"Air quality and emissions management requirements shall be integrated into project development, design, construction, operation, modification, closure and post-closure activities."},{"id":"3.5.4","page":32,"heading":"Source Identification and Inventory Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify, document and maintain an inventory of applicable air emission sources, including point sources, flares, mobile sources, fugitive sources, emergency emission sources, wastewater- related sources, storage sources and temporary construction sources."},{"id":"3.5.5","page":32,"heading":"Source Identification and Inventory Management","text":"The up-to-date air inventory shall include, but not limited to, location of the emission source, emission flow, air pollutants in emission stream, emission quantities, concentrations of air pollutants, EPO requirements, monitoring requirements and calculation methods, applicable receptors, pollution or emission control equipment."},{"id":"3.5.6","page":32,"heading":"Source Identification and Inventory Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain and report inventory of regulated pollutants including particulate matter, sulfur oxides, nitrogen oxides, carbon monoxide, hazardous air pollutants, volatile organic compounds and other pollutants required by regulatory obligations."},{"id":"3.5.7","page":32,"heading":"Source Identification and Inventory Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and evaluate air emission sources, pollutants, receptors, applicable obligations and associated risks in accordance with section 3.1 Environmental Risk and Impact Management process, and implement and maintain controls to manage significant air emission risks."},{"id":"3.5.8","page":32,"heading":"Air Emissions and Air Quality Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify air emission sources and activities that could affect air quality, people, communities or environmental receptors, and establish and maintain controls to comply with applicable obligations and emission limits and to prevent or minimize adverse air quality impacts."},{"id":"3.5.9","page":32,"heading":"Air Emissions and Air Quality Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain programs to monitor, assess and verify air emissions and air quality performance where required by applicable obligations, permits, environmental risks or management system requirements."},{"id":"3.5.10","page":32,"heading":"Fugitive Emissions and Dust Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and control applicable sources of fugitive emissions and dust, including volatile organic compounds, hazardous air pollutants and other relevant pollutants, to prevent or minimize adverse impacts."},{"id":"3.5.11","page":32,"heading":"Fugitive Emissions and Dust Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement monitoring, inspection, verification and leak detection and repair programs where required by applicable obligations, permits or risk assessments, and investigate and address significant fugitive emission and dust events and odor complaints."},{"id":"3.5.12","page":33,"heading":"Monitoring, Verification and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain air quality and emissions monitoring programs, evaluate results and trends against applicable limits, permit conditions and internal criteria, and address identified exceedances and control failures."},{"id":"3.5.13","page":33,"heading":"Monitoring, Verification and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify and submit required air quality and emissions reports, retain associated records in accordance with applicable regulatory requirements, and evaluate at defined intervals the effectiveness of air quality and emissions management to determine required updates to assessments, inventories, controls, monitoring programs and plans."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Air Quality and Emissions Management; air quality, emissions, stack, fugitive dust; Governance, Accountability and Applicability; Source Identification and Inventory Management; Air Emissions and Air Quality Management; Fugitive Emissions and Dust Management; Monitoring, Verification and Improvement","trigger":"if air emissions, fugitive dust, stack releases or ambient-air impacts may occur","refs":["3.1"]},{"id":"3.6","title":"Waste Management","page":33,"purpose":"Manage all applicable waste throughout its lifecycle from generation to final recovery, treatment or disposal in a manner that prevents environmental harm, supports resource efficiency and circularity, maintains legal compliance, and provides demonstrable traceability and control.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions that generate or manage waste throughout its lifecycle, including storage, handling, transport, recovery, recycling, treatment and disposal. It covers industrial, municipal, medical, laboratory, construction and demolition wastes, contaminated materials, wastewater residues and other applicable waste streams. This process does not apply to Tailings, Waste rock, Ore Rejects, Phospho-gypsum, Red mud or bauxite residue, managed under section 3.10 Tailings Management process.","clauses":[{"id":"3.6.1","page":33,"heading":"Establish and Define Waste Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain a waste management process covering waste generation, classification, storage, collection, transport, recovery, treatment and disposal."},{"id":"3.6.2","page":33,"heading":"Establish and Define Waste Management","text":"Business Unit and Corporate Function management shall ensure that responsibilities, competent personnel, resources and waste management arrangements are established and implemented within their areas of control."},{"id":"3.6.3","page":33,"heading":"Establish and Define Waste Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify applicable waste management obligations and maintain documented procedures, waste inventories, registers and records appropriate to their waste profile."},{"id":"3.6.4","page":33,"heading":"Waste Identification, Characterization and Classification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify, characterize and classify waste streams generated by activities, products, services, projects and facilities under their control using applicable requirements, approved methodologies, process knowledge and analytical data where required."},{"id":"3.6.5","page":33,"heading":"Waste Identification, Characterization and Classification","text":"Hazardous waste and waste of uncertain classification shall be managed as hazardous in accordance with applicable requirements until its classification has been verified."},{"id":"3.6.6","page":33,"heading":"Waste Identification, Characterization and Classification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain a waste inventory and register that identifies waste types, classifications, quantities, sources and management pathways."},{"id":"3.6.7","page":33,"heading":"Waste Identification, Characterization and Classification","text":"Waste characterization and classification shall be reviewed whenever changes occur that may affect waste properties, classification or management requirements."},{"id":"3.6.8","page":34,"heading":"Waste Identification, Characterization and Classification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions that generate or control radioactive waste shall establish and maintain a radioactive waste management program in accordance with applicable Nuclear and Radiological Regulatory Commission (NRRC) requirements, including inventory, traceability, recordkeeping and regulatory reporting."},{"id":"3.6.9","page":34,"heading":"Waste Impact Assessment and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and assess waste management impacts associated with generation, handling, storage, transport, recovery, treatment and disposal activities."},{"id":"3.6.10","page":34,"heading":"Waste Impact Assessment and Planning","text":"Waste impact assessments shall consider, where applicable; misclassification, incompatible mixing, container and secondary containment failure, labelling deficiencies, storage failures, uncontrolled accumulation, fire and explosion, hazard exposure, waste transport incidents, spill and release events, contamination, unauthorized disposal, biodiversity impacts and contract absence or failures."},{"id":"3.6.11","page":34,"heading":"Waste Impact Assessment and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall develop and implement waste management plans that apply the waste hierarchy in the following order of preference: avoid, reduce, reuse, recycle, recover, treat and dispose."},{"id":"3.6.12","page":34,"heading":"Waste Impact Assessment and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall minimize waste generation through project development, procurement, operational planning, process improvement, material efficiency initiatives, shutdowns, decommissioning and closure, and establish objectives and actions for significant waste streams."},{"id":"3.6.13","page":34,"heading":"Waste Impact Assessment and Planning","text":"Waste shall be treated or disposed of only where it cannot reasonably be avoided, reduced, reused, recycled or recovered, using methods compatible with its classification and applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.6.14","page":34,"heading":"Waste Segregation, Handling and Storage","text":"Waste shall be segregated at or as close as reasonably practicable to the point of generation, with hazardous waste separated from non-hazardous waste and incompatible waste streams to prevent reactions, releases, contamination or exposure."},{"id":"3.6.15","page":34,"heading":"Waste Segregation, Handling and Storage","text":"Waste shall be handled and stored in identified and labelled containers and designated storage areas that are compatible with the waste characteristics and intended management pathway and provide controls against releases, unauthorized access and other applicable risks, including secondary containment, inspection access and emergency response provisions where required."},{"id":"3.6.16","page":34,"heading":"Waste Segregation, Handling and Storage","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall control waste accumulation and apply additional hazard specific and regulatory controls to medical, laboratory, chemical and other waste streams requiring special management. Anticipated accumulation beyond applicable storage limits or capacity shall be subject to contingency arrangements and required regulatory authorization."},{"id":"3.6.17","page":34,"heading":"Waste Transport, Transfer and Service Providers","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall use approved and legally authorized service providers for waste transport, transfer, recovery, treatment and disposal and verify that the providers and receiving facilities are authorized to manage the relevant waste streams."},{"id":"3.6.18","page":34,"heading":"Waste Transport, Transfer and Service Providers","text":"Waste movements shall be traceable from the point of generation to confirmed receipt, recovery, treatment or disposal using applicable manifests, transportation documents or equivalent tracking systems, with waste service providers managed in accordance with section 4.1 Contractor EHSS Management."},{"id":"3.6.19","page":35,"heading":"Monitoring, Verification and Records","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall inspect and monitor waste management activities, including waste storage areas, at defined intervals and against established criteria to verify the implementation and effectiveness of controls and compliance with applicable obligations and permit requirements."},{"id":"3.6.20","page":35,"heading":"Monitoring, Verification and Records","text":"Waste management performance indicators, and targets shall be established and periodically reviewed, tracked and reported."},{"id":"3.6.21","page":35,"heading":"Monitoring, Verification and Records","text":"Legal breaches, failed controls and overdue corrective actions shall be escalated through defined governance arrangements."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Waste Management; waste management; Establish and Define Waste Management; Waste Identification, Characterization and Classification; Waste Impact Assessment and Planning; Waste Segregation, Handling and Storage; Waste Transport, Transfer and Service Providers; Monitoring, Verification and Records","trigger":"if waste is generated, classified, stored, transported, treated or disposed","refs":["4.1"]},{"id":"3.7","title":"Water Management","page":35,"purpose":"Water resources, water related infrastructure and water interactions are managed throughout the asset lifecycle to protect people, the environment, communities and operations, comply with obligations, maintain water security, and prevent water depletion, contamination and uncontrolled releases.","scope":"This process applies to all Business Units and Corporate Functions involved in water management across the asset lifecycle, from exploration and feasibility through design, construction, operation, closure and rehabilitation, including water abstraction, mine dewatering, receipt, storage, transfer, treatment, use, recycling and discharge.","clauses":[{"id":"3.7.1","page":35,"heading":"Governance and Water Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall use this process as the framework for water management and establish, implement and maintain water management procedures aligned with applicable legal, regulatory and permit requirements and the Maaden Corporate Water Management Standard (CWMS)"},{"id":"3.7.2","page":35,"heading":"Governance and Water Management Framework","text":"Water related projects and water management infrastructure shall be planned, assessed, designed, constructed, commissioned and transferred to operations in accordance with applicable Maaden Capital Investment System (MCIS) requirements."},{"id":"3.7.3","page":35,"heading":"Governance and Water Management Framework","text":"Where an applicable requirement of the CWMS or MCIS exceeds or provides additional detail to this process, the applicable CWMS or MCIS requirement shall apply."},{"id":"3.7.4","page":35,"heading":"Governance and Water Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain a water management framework that addresses; water abstraction and supply, mine dewatering and groundwater management, process and potable water systems, wastewater treatment and discharge, stormwater and seepage management, water reuse and recycling, tailings water management, surface water and groundwater protection and closure and post-closure water obligations."},{"id":"3.7.5","page":35,"heading":"Governance and Water Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and apply applicable water management obligations and commitments, obtain and maintain required licenses, permits, approvals and authorizations, comply with their conditions, and verify compliance at defined intervals."},{"id":"3.7.6","page":35,"heading":"Governance and Water Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define, document and communicate responsibilities and authorities for water management and ensure that assigned personnel are competent to fulfil their responsibilities."},{"id":"3.7.7","page":36,"heading":"Governance and Water Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and track controls and actions proportionate to identified water related risks and sufficient to meet applicable obligations."},{"id":"3.7.8","page":36,"heading":"Water Risk and Impact Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify, assess, manage and review water related risks and impacts across the asset lifecycle in accordance with the Corporate Water Management Standard, including following significant changes affecting water risks, obligations or performance."},{"id":"3.7.9","page":36,"heading":"Water Stewardship and Catchment Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions operating in shared catchments or water stressed regions shall identify and evaluate the catchments, groundwater systems and shared water resources that may influence, or be influenced by, their activities."},{"id":"3.7.10","page":36,"heading":"Water Stewardship and Catchment Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify water sources and dependent receptors, groundwater and surface water interactions, other significant water users, community water dependencies and catchment level water risks."},{"id":"3.7.11","page":36,"heading":"Water Stewardship and Catchment Management","text":"Where water related risks are associated with shared water resources, the assessments shall consider existing water users, foreseeable future water users, water allocation arrangements, sensitive receptors, ecosystem water requirements, high-value water resources, cumulative impacts, and major stakeholder concerns."},{"id":"3.7.12","page":36,"heading":"Water Stewardship and Catchment Management","text":"Catchment related assessments and controls shall be reviewed when significant changes occur in water availability, water demand, catchment conditions, regulatory requirements, or foreseeable cumulative impacts."},{"id":"3.7.13","page":36,"heading":"Water Stewardship and Catchment Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate actual and potential impacts on communities, ecosystems, other water users and long-term water availability where their operations may materially influence shared water resources."},{"id":"3.7.14","page":36,"heading":"Water Stewardship and Catchment Management","text":"Water related stakeholder concerns, commitments and identified shared water risks shall be considered in water management planning."},{"id":"3.7.15","page":36,"heading":"Water Planning and Balance Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain water balances covering material water inputs, outputs, transfers, storage, consumption, reuse, recycling and losses."},{"id":"3.7.16","page":36,"heading":"Water Planning and Balance Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall integrate water balances into operational planning, risk management, water stewardship, regulatory compliance and asset lifecycle planning, and review and update them following significant changes affecting water supply, demand, infrastructure, operations or water related risks."},{"id":"3.7.17","page":36,"heading":"Operational Water Management Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and implement controls to manage water related activities, infrastructure and facilities throughout the asset lifecycle to achieve compliance with applicable obligations, protect water resources, prevent adverse impacts to people and the environment, and manage water related emergencies."},{"id":"3.7.18","page":36,"heading":"Operational Water Management Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that water abstraction, collection, storage, transfer, treatment, use, reuse, recycling, injection and discharge activities comply with applicable legal, regulatory, permit and Maaden requirements."},{"id":"3.7.19","page":37,"heading":"Operational Water Management Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain emergency preparedness and response plans for credible water related emergency scenarios, including loss of water supply, pond overtopping, pipeline rupture, water contamination, tailings, phosphogypsum or red mud water releases, extreme weather events, and water treatment system failures scenarios in accordance with section 5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning."},{"id":"3.7.20","page":37,"heading":"Operational Water Management Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions that abstract, dewater, use or may impact groundwater shall establish and implement groundwater protection, abstraction management and aquifer monitoring controls to manage groundwater quantity, groundwater quality, regulatory obligations and potential impacts on environmental receptors."},{"id":"3.7.21","page":37,"heading":"Water Quality and Environment Protection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify water resources, receiving environments, groundwater and surface-water receptors, environmentally sensitive areas, water-dependent ecosystems, people and other water users potentially affected by their activities, and implement controls to prevent or minimize adverse impacts."},{"id":"3.7.22","page":37,"heading":"Water Quality and Environment Protection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify, assess and manage risks associated with wastewater, contaminated water, stormwater, seepage and other water releases, and ensure that authorized discharges comply with applicable legal, regulatory, permit and Maaden requirements."},{"id":"3.7.23","page":37,"heading":"Water Monitoring and Performance Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain risk based water monitoring programs to evaluate water related risks, control effectiveness, compliance and performance."},{"id":"3.7.24","page":37,"heading":"Water Monitoring and Performance Management","text":"Water related monitoring information and records shall be maintained and reported in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory, permit and Maaden requirements."},{"id":"3.7.25","page":37,"heading":"Water Monitoring and Performance Management","text":"Monitoring programs shall be developed based on Corporate Water Management Standard and shall provide minimum information to evaluate; water abstraction, water consumption, water losses, water use efficiency, water reuse and recycling, water quality, water discharge performance, groundwater and surface water impacts, effectiveness of water controls."},{"id":"3.7.26","page":37,"heading":"Water Monitoring and Performance Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish measurable water-intensity performance indicators and water efficiency targets, taking into account water availability, operational requirements, water-related risks, significant water uses, legal obligations and opportunities to improve water stewardship."},{"id":"3.7.27","page":37,"heading":"Water Monitoring and Performance Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement and periodically review water efficiency and water consumption reduction plans to improve performance and support achievement of established objectives and targets."},{"id":"3.7.28","page":37,"heading":"Closure, Post-Closure and Long-Term Water Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify foreseeable long-term water related risks, liabilities and obligations and incorporate applicable water quantity, water quality, treatment, storage, discharge, monitoring and maintenance requirements into closure and post-closure planning throughout the asset lifecycle."},{"id":"3.7.29","page":37,"heading":"Closure, Post-Closure and Long-Term Water Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall incorporate ongoing post-closure water management requirements, including associated operation, maintenance, monitoring and treatment requirements, into closure plans and financial provisions."},{"id":"3.7.30","page":38,"heading":"Verification, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify, at defined intervals, the implementation and effectiveness of water management requirements and controls and maintain evidence demonstrating compliance with applicable water related obligations."},{"id":"3.7.31","page":38,"heading":"Verification, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall report, escalate, investigate and address water related incidents, non- compliances, permit breaches, significant exceedances, control failures and findings in accordance with applicable MSHEM processes."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Water Management; water management; Governance and Water Management Framework; Water Risk and Impact Assessment; Water Stewardship and Catchment Management; Water Planning and Balance Management; Operational Water Management Controls; Water Quality and Environment Protection; Water Monitoring and Performance Management; Closure, Post-Closure and Long-Term Water Management; Verification, Reporting and Improvement","trigger":"if water is abstracted, treated, stored, transferred, discharged or monitored","refs":["5.1"]},{"id":"3.8","title":"Energy Management","page":38,"purpose":"This process defines Maaden’s minimum corporate requirements for establishing, implementing, maintaining and continually improving energy management across applicable Business Units and Corporate Functions to achieve measurable improvement in energy performance, including energy efficiency, energy use and energy consumption.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions under Maaden’s operational control in Saudi Arabia where activities, assets or decisions affect energy use, consumption, efficiency or performance.","clauses":[{"id":"3.8.1","page":38,"heading":"Governance, Roles, and Accountability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall be accountable for energy performance, regulatory compliance, resource allocation, implementation of this process, and continual improvement of energy management within their respective areas of control."},{"id":"3.8.2","page":38,"heading":"Governance, Roles, and Accountability","text":"Business Unit and Corporate Functions shall appoint competent energy management personnel with clearly defined roles, responsibilities, authority, and reporting lines. Appointed personnel shall have the required competence to implement ISO 50001 requirements and applicable SEEC Framework obligations."},{"id":"3.8.3","page":38,"heading":"Governance, Roles, and Accountability","text":"For Growth Projects, Maaden Capital Investment System (MCIS) minimum requirements shall be followed, including the applicable sustainability toolkit requirements under MCIS_TK_0387, to ensure energy efficiency, metering, monitoring, regulatory compliance, and sustainability considerations are embedded during project development, design review, procurement, and approval stages."},{"id":"3.8.4","page":38,"heading":"Governance, Roles, and Accountability","text":"Corporate Sustainability shall act as the corporate process custodian for energy management and define minimum requirements, reporting expectations and performance review requirements for Business Units, sites, operations and growth projects."},{"id":"3.8.5","page":38,"heading":"Energy Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall integrate energy management into applicable business planning, significant energy use assessment, operational control, performance monitoring and continual improvement processes, in accordance with ISO 50001 principles.\na. Sites that collectively represent at least 90% of the respective Business Unit’s total energy consumption shall be prioritized for ISO 50001 implementation, certification and re-certification.\nb. For other sites within each Business Unit, management shall identify Significant Energy Users in accordance with ISO 50001, establish monitoring arrangements, and implement documented action plans to improve energy efficiency and energy performance."},{"id":"3.8.6","page":39,"heading":"Energy Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify, obtain, maintain, and comply with all applicable energy-related permits, regulatory authorizations, SEEC requirements, utility conditions, and other legally binding obligations relevant to their operations, projects, fuels, utilities, and energy consuming assets."},{"id":"3.8.7","page":39,"heading":"Energy Management Framework","text":"New projects, major modifications, replacement equipment, and procurement decisions shall consider best available technology, life-cycle energy performance, energy efficiency specifications, design alternatives, and total cost of ownership, as applicable to the project scope and technical feasibility."},{"id":"3.8.8","page":39,"heading":"Energy Management Framework","text":"Energy performance, compliance status, action plan progress, savings verification, key risks, and corrective actions shall be reported at defined intervals to Site, Business Unit, and Corporate Sustainability governance forums, in accordance with section 6.4 Management Review and Continual Improvement process."},{"id":"3.8.9","page":39,"heading":"Energy Management Framework","text":"Records shall be retained as auditable evidence, including permits and approvals, legal and regulatory obligation registers, energy reviews, energy baselines, energy performance indicators, metering data, calibration records, operational-control evidence, project design reviews, procurement evaluations, training records, management reviews, internal audits, nonconformities, and corrective actions."},{"id":"3.8.10","page":39,"heading":"Energy Management Framework","text":"Internal audits shall be planned and performed periodically to verify conformance with this process, ISO 50001, applicable SEEC requirements, MCIS sustainability toolkit requirements for growth projects, operational controls, monitoring arrangements, reporting obligations, and recordkeeping requirements."},{"id":"3.8.11","page":39,"heading":"Energy Management Framework","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically review energy performance, including progress against objectives, targets, action plans, regulatory obligations, and improvement opportunities."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Energy Management; energy management; Governance, Roles, and Accountability; Energy Management Framework","trigger":"if significant energy is consumed, generated, recovered or optimized","refs":["6.4"]},{"id":"3.9","title":"Biodiversity Management","page":39,"purpose":"Biodiversity and ecosystem service dependencies, impacts, risks and opportunities are identified, assessed and managed throughout the asset lifecycle to prevent or minimize adverse impacts on biodiversity, support ecosystem restoration, realize biodiversity commitments, and achieve agreed biodiversity objectives.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden controlled activities, projects and operations that may affect biodiversity, ecosystems or ecosystem services throughout relevant lifecycle stages, from exploration and design through operation, rehabilitation, closure and post-closure. It also applies to contractors and other value chain parties where Maaden has control or material influence over the relevant biodiversity risks or outcomes.","clauses":[{"id":"3.9.1","page":39,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Maaden shall establish corporate biodiversity governance arrangements defining accountability for biodiversity strategy and biodiversity performance across Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions."},{"id":"3.9.2","page":39,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assign competent personnel and integrate biodiversity risks, opportunities, objectives and controls into business and asset planning and decision making."},{"id":"3.9.3","page":40,"heading":"Biodiversity Obligations and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify applicable biodiversity related legal requirements, regulatory requirements, approvals and agreed commitments, and incorporate the resulting obligations and actions into applicable plans and controls."},{"id":"3.9.4","page":40,"heading":"Biodiversity Baseline Development and LEAP (Locate, Evaluate, Assess and Prepare) Approach","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall apply the Locate, Evaluate, Assess and Prepare phases to identify biodiversity sensitive locations, evaluate dependencies, assess impacts and risks, and establish actions required to achieve defined biodiversity outcomes."},{"id":"3.9.5","page":40,"heading":"Locate","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify relevant national and regional biodiversity plans, legally protected areas and recognized conservation priorities and consider them in project siting, mine planning and design."},{"id":"3.9.6","page":40,"heading":"Locate","text":"Maaden shall not conduct exploration or mining within properties inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List. Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and manage potential impacts on legally protected areas and other recognized biodiversity sensitive areas affected by their activities."},{"id":"3.9.7","page":40,"heading":"Locate","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain biodiversity baselines where biodiversity values, dependencies, impacts or risks could be material. Baselines shall be developed or validated by competent personnel and shall establish measurable reference conditions for evaluating biodiversity losses, gains, rehabilitation and restoration outcomes, and biodiversity objectives."},{"id":"3.9.8","page":40,"heading":"Locate","text":"Biodiversity baselines shall include, habitat inventory, flora and fauna inventory, protected species, threatened species, critical habitats, ecosystem services, invasive species, habitat condition protected areas, and community biodiversity values, where applicable."},{"id":"3.9.9","page":40,"heading":"Locate","text":"Business Units shall identify and spatially record priority biodiversity locations using Maaden approved geographic information systems or equivalent controlled mapping systems. Priority locations shall include, where applicable, protected areas, key biodiversity areas, critical habitats, biodiversity sensitive ecosystems, biodiversity sensitive catchments, restoration priority areas and areas of biodiversity importance to affected communities."},{"id":"3.9.10","page":40,"heading":"Locate","text":"Biodiversity baselines and priority location information shall be reviewed at intervals defined in the applicable Biodiversity Action Plan or monitoring program and when material changes to activities, environmental conditions, available knowledge or applicable obligations occur."},{"id":"3.9.11","page":40,"heading":"Evaluate","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify biodiversity and ecosystem service dependencies associated with operations and relevant activities."},{"id":"3.9.12","page":40,"heading":"Evaluate","text":"Dependency assessments shall consider water availability, groundwater recharge, climate changes, soil stability and erosion control, closure performance and nature Impacts."},{"id":"3.9.13","page":41,"heading":"Assess","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and assess direct, indirect and cumulative biodiversity impacts, including, where applicable, habitat loss, degradation and fragmentation, vegetation clearing, species disturbance, invasive alien species, hydrological alteration and pollution."},{"id":"3.9.14","page":41,"heading":"Assess","text":"Assessment outputs shall be incorporated into biodiversity management planning and action plans. Material biodiversity risks shall be managed and escalated in accordance with the section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.9.15","page":41,"heading":"Prepare","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and implement a Biodiversity Action Plan where significant biodiversity values or sensitive receptors are present, material biodiversity impacts or risks are identified, or an applicable obligation or commitment requires coordinated biodiversity action."},{"id":"3.9.16","page":41,"heading":"Prepare","text":"Biodiversity Action Plans shall define, as applicable, relevant baseline and reference conditions; significant biodiversity values and priority risks; applicable obligations and stakeholder commitments; avoidance, minimization, rehabilitation, restoration and offset actions; No Net Loss and Net Gain objectives where required or formally adopted; measurable indicators and success criteria; monitoring and reporting requirements; milestones, responsibilities and resources; and review and adaptive-management arrangements."},{"id":"3.9.17","page":41,"heading":"Prepare","text":"Where activities could affect critical habitat, applicable Business Units shall establish specific avoidance, protection and mitigation measures and measurable outcomes for the affected critical biodiversity values."},{"id":"3.9.18","page":41,"heading":"Prepare","text":"Where an approved project or expansion is permitted to affect critical habitat, the applicable Business Unit shall establish and monitor measurable Net Gain objectives for the affected critical biodiversity values."},{"id":"3.9.19","page":41,"heading":"Prepare","text":"Where No Net Loss, Net Gain or offset outcomes are required or claimed, the applicable Business Unit shall use a documented methodology to quantify and verify biodiversity losses, gains and progress against defined objectives."},{"id":"3.9.20","page":41,"heading":"Mitigation Hierarchy and Operational Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall apply the biodiversity mitigation hierarchy by avoiding impacts, minimizing unavoidable impacts, restoring affected biodiversity and considering offsets only for significant residual impacts after the preceding measures have been evaluated."},{"id":"3.9.21","page":41,"heading":"Mitigation Hierarchy and Operational Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish controls for land disturbance, vegetation clearing, wildlife interactions, invasive species, habitat protection, erosion and sediment, and topsoil conservation, including limiting disturbance to approved boundaries."},{"id":"3.9.22","page":41,"heading":"Restoration, Rehabilitation and Biodiversity Outcomes","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall integrate biodiversity rehabilitation and restoration into project design, development, operation and closure planning and implement progressive rehabilitation where practicable."},{"id":"3.9.23","page":41,"heading":"Restoration, Rehabilitation and Biodiversity Outcomes","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish measurable rehabilitation and restoration criteria linked to biodiversity and closure objectives and monitor effectiveness against applicable baseline or reference conditions."},{"id":"3.9.24","page":41,"heading":"Restoration, Rehabilitation and Biodiversity Outcomes","text":"New projects and expansions shall establish and implement measures to achieve No Net Loss of significant biodiversity values by closure."},{"id":"3.9.25","page":42,"heading":"Restoration, Rehabilitation and Biodiversity Outcomes","text":"Existing operations with material biodiversity impacts shall establish measurable pathways toward No Net Loss where required by applicable obligations or formally adopted Maaden biodiversity objectives."},{"id":"3.9.26","page":42,"heading":"Biodiversity Monitoring, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement biodiversity monitoring programs using indicators relevant to identified biodiversity values, impacts and objectives. Indicators shall be quantitative where practicable and may include habitat disturbance and restoration, rehabilitation success, species abundance and distribution, invasive species occurrence, ecosystem condition and water-related biodiversity indicators."},{"id":"3.9.27","page":42,"heading":"Biodiversity Monitoring, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate biodiversity performance and control effectiveness against baseline conditions, defined objectives and success criteria, and use the results to support adaptive management."},{"id":"3.9.28","page":42,"heading":"Biodiversity Monitoring, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall manage biodiversity incidents, failures, significant deviations and corrective actions in accordance with the section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship process, and escalate material biodiversity issues through applicable management and risk processes."},{"id":"3.9.29","page":42,"heading":"Biodiversity Monitoring, Reporting and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain verifiable evidence supporting material biodiversity performance claims and applicable regulatory and sustainability reporting, including Taskforce for Nature Related Financial Disclosures (TNFD) aligned disclosures where adopted by Maaden."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Biodiversity Management; biodiversity management; Governance and Accountability; Biodiversity Obligations and Planning; Biodiversity Baseline Development and LEAP (Locate, Evaluate, Assess and Prepare) Approach; Locate; Evaluate; Assess; Prepare; Mitigation Hierarchy and Operational Controls; Restoration, Rehabilitation and Biodiversity Outcomes; Biodiversity Monitoring, Reporting and Improvement","trigger":"if land disturbance, water use or other activity may affect biodiversity or ecosystem services","refs":["2.5","6.1"]},{"id":"3.10","title":"Tailings Management","page":42,"purpose":"Tailings storage facilities and associated systems are governed under accountable leadership and are designed, constructed, operated, monitored, modified, closed and managed post-closure throughout their lifecycle to prevent or control credible failure modes, maintain facility integrity and critical controls, protect workers, communities, infrastructure and the environment, and comply with applicable legal, regulatory and approved Maaden requirements.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units responsible for tailings storage facilities or associated systems throughout their lifecycle, from concept and site selection through design, construction, commissioning, operation, modification, care and maintenance, closure and post-closure. It covers engineered facilities used to contain tailings or mineral-processing residues, including tailings dams, red mud storage facilities, gypsum stack facilities and associated water management, seepage control, diversion, monitoring and containment systems.","clauses":[{"id":"3.10.1","page":42,"heading":"Tailings Management Framework and Application","text":"Business Units shall establish, implement and maintain a performance-based Tailings Management System for each applicable facility in accordance with this process, applicable GISTM requirements, the approved Maaden Corporate Tailings Management Standard (CTMS), and applicable legal, regulatory and permit requirements."},{"id":"3.10.2","page":42,"heading":"Tailings Management Framework and Application","text":"Corporate Sustainability shall maintain the CTMS and Maaden’s corporate tailings governance framework and oversee implementation and conformance. Material tailings risks and performance shall be reported through approved governance channels."},{"id":"3.10.3","page":42,"heading":"Tailings Management Framework and Application","text":"Critical decisions affecting tailings facility design, construction, operation, expansion, monitoring, risk reduction, closure or post-closure performance shall be supported by documented risk-based decision-making processes consistent with the CTMS and applicable GISTM requirements."},{"id":"3.10.4","page":43,"heading":"Tailings Management Framework and Application","text":"The CTMS shall define detailed applicability, roles, competency criteria, technical and operating requirements, review frequencies, mandatory deliverables, conformance evidence and disclosure requirements."},{"id":"3.10.5","page":43,"heading":"Tailings Management Framework and Application","text":"Tailings management shall be integrated with applicable MSHEM processes and the Maaden Capital Investment System (MCIS). Capital projects shall apply CTMS requirements from concept and option selection through design, construction, commissioning and handover to operations."},{"id":"3.10.6","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Maaden shall maintain a Board approved and publicly available commitment to safe tailings management, emergency preparedness and response, and recovery following failure, consistent with GISTM."},{"id":"3.10.7","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"An Accountable Executive (AE) shall be designated for each Business Unit with accountability for tailings facility safety, environmental and social performance."},{"id":"3.10.8","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Business Units shall appoint a site-specific Responsible Tailings Facility Engineer. The Engineer of Record and Independent Tailings Review Board or senior independent technical reviewer shall also be appointed as required by GISTM and the CTMS and subject to Accountable Executive approval."},{"id":"3.10.9","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"The Engineer of Record shall be empowered through a written agreement defining the role’s authority and responsibilities and Maaden’s obligations to support its effective performance. Procurement may inform but shall not determine the Engineer of Record selection."},{"id":"3.10.10","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Personnel performing tailings safety critical roles shall have defined authority, appropriate competency, sufficient time and resources, independence where required, and succession arrangements."},{"id":"3.10.11","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Relevant performance objectives, performance reviews and incentive arrangements shall incorporate public safety and tailings facility integrity. They shall not be compromised by production, schedule, cost or procurement considerations."},{"id":"3.10.12","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Tailings facilities and associated systems shall be subject to layered assurance comprising Business Unit and site verification, Corporate assurance and competent independent technical review. Assurance shall include Engineer of Record reviews, Dam Safety Reviews and Independent Tailings Review Board or senior independent technical review, as applicable, at frequencies defined by GISTM and the CTMS."},{"id":"3.10.13","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Business Units shall conduct documented assessments of conformance with GISTM, the CTMS and this process. Corporate Sustainability shall arrange independent validation where required. Findings and corrective actions shall be risk-ranked, tracked to closure and verified for effectiveness, with material issues reported through appropriate governance channels."},{"id":"3.10.14","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Business Units shall promote early problem recognition, cross-functional collaboration and learning. Workers’ experience-based knowledge and lessons from incidents, non-conformances, reviews, exercises and relevant external events shall be incorporated into planning, design, operations and the TMS. Personnel shall be trained for their responsibilities."},{"id":"3.10.15","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"The Accountable Executive shall ensure that a formal, confidential and documented process is maintained to receive, investigate and promptly address tailings-related concerns from employees and contractors. People reporting concerns in good faith shall be protected from retaliation."},{"id":"3.10.16","page":43,"heading":"Management and Governance","text":"Tailings information and records shall be controlled, retained and accessible throughout the facility lifecycle and post-closure period in accordance with the CTMS and Maaden information-management requirements. Knowledge continuity shall be maintained during organizational changes, changes of Engineer of Record and transfers of ownership or responsibility."},{"id":"3.10.17","page":44,"heading":"Integrated Knowledge Base","text":"Each applicable facility shall maintain an interdisciplinary knowledge base covering its technical, environmental, social, human-rights and local economic context. The knowledge base shall include site and material characterization, downstream conditions, climate change and material uncertainties and shall be updated at GISTM and CTMS defined intervals and following material changes."},{"id":"3.10.18","page":44,"heading":"Integrated Knowledge Base","text":"Credible failure scenarios shall be supported by documented breach and inundation analyses and assessments of potential human exposure, vulnerability and downstream impacts. These assessments shall be updated following material changes to the facility, its surroundings or the knowledge base."},{"id":"3.10.19","page":44,"heading":"Integrated Knowledge Base","text":"Consequence assessments shall consider potential impacts on people, the environment, infrastructure, communities and business continuity and be updated following changes to facility conditions or consequence exposure."},{"id":"3.10.20","page":44,"heading":"Integrated Knowledge Base","text":"New facilities shall be supported by documented multi-criteria alternatives and impact assessments. The assessments shall use the knowledge base to select sites, technologies and management strategies that minimize lifecycle risks to people and the environment and minimize the volume of tailings and water placed in external tailings facilities. Impact mitigation shall follow the mitigation hierarchy and consider climate change and safe closure."},{"id":"3.10.21","page":44,"heading":"Integrated Knowledge Base","text":"Existing facilities and expansions shall periodically review opportunities to improve tailings technologies, designs and management strategies. Impact assessments shall be updated following material changes."},{"id":"3.10.22","page":44,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Business Units shall maintain an inventory of applicable facilities and associated systems and their current consequence classifications. Classifications shall be determined in accordance with GISTM and the CTMS and reviewed following material changes and at CTMS-defined intervals."},{"id":"3.10.23","page":44,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Business Units shall maintain multidisciplinary lifecycle risk assessments using FMEA to identify credible failure modes, consequences, uncertainties, control dependencies and required risk-reduction measures under normal, abnormal and extreme conditions, including care and maintenance, closure and post-closure. The assessments shall be reviewed at intervals defined by the CTMS and following material changes."},{"id":"3.10.24","page":44,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Risks shall be reduced to ALARP and managed in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management and as defined in CTMS."},{"id":"3.10.25","page":44,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"For High, Very High and Extreme consequence facilities, the Accountable Executive shall confirm and document that applicable risks have been reduced to ALARP. Reasonable measures to further reduce potential consequences shall be identified and implemented as required by GISTM and the CTMS."},{"id":"3.10.26","page":44,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Critical controls associated with catastrophic or other material unwanted events shall be managed in accordance with section 2.6 Critical Control Management process. Their performance requirements shall be defined, monitored and verified, and failures or material deterioration shall be promptly escalated and addressed."},{"id":"3.10.27","page":44,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Tailings facilities and associated systems shall be designed by competent people using robust design criteria appropriate to the consequence classification, credible failure modes and all lifecycle stages."},{"id":"3.10.28","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"The Engineer of Record shall prepare and maintain a controlled Design Basis Report. The report shall address the knowledge base, site conditions, geotechnical, hydrogeological, seismic, climate and water-management considerations, operating constraints, staged construction, and closure and post-closure requirements. It shall be reviewed by the Independent Tailings Review Board or senior independent technical reviewer, as required by GISTM and the CTMS."},{"id":"3.10.29","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Where an existing facility cannot meet an applicable GISTM or CTMS design or upgrade requirement, the Engineer of Record shall document the basis. The Independent Tailings Review Board or senior independent technical reviewer shall review the determination."},{"id":"3.10.30","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"The Accountable Executive shall approve measures and timeframes for reducing both the probability and consequences of failure to ALARP."},{"id":"3.10.31","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Construction and commissioning shall comply with approved designs and the CTMS. Appropriate Quality Control, Construction Quality Assurance and Construction versus Design Intent Verification shall be implemented, and controlled construction and as-built records shall be maintained. A facility or construction stage shall not be placed into service until design conformance and readiness for safe operation have been verified and approved."},{"id":"3.10.32","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Each facility shall be operated, maintained and surveilled within its design intent and approved operating limits under a current Operations, Maintenance and Surveillance Manual. Controls shall address, as applicable, tailings deposition, storage capacity, water balance and water management, seepage, containment and prevention of overtopping or uncontrolled releases."},{"id":"3.10.33","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Monitoring and surveillance programs shall be implemented, aligned with facility risks, consequence classification and identified credible failure modes. The Integrated, risk-based monitoring and surveillance shall verify design assumptions, consequence classification, identified credible failure modes, operating controls, critical controls and environmental and social performance."},{"id":"3.10.34","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Monitoring data shall be evaluated for trends and deterioration against defined performance criteria. Trigger Action Response Plans shall be used to manage exceedances and abnormal conditions. Monitoring results shall be reviewed and approved as required by the CTMS."},{"id":"3.10.35","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Changes that could affect facility integrity or risk shall be managed through the Management of Change process and the CTMS. This includes changes to design, construction, operating parameters, deposition, water management, monitoring, closure arrangements or organization. Reviews shall consider cumulative effects and require corresponding updates to risk assessments, designs and facility documentation."},{"id":"3.10.36","page":45,"heading":"Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring","text":"Tailings Storage Facilities and associated structure closure plans and cost estimates shall be integrated into overall mine closure planning and address progressive closure, long-term stability, environmental and social objectives, post-mining land use, performance criteria, monitoring, maintenance and assigned responsibilities."},{"id":"3.10.37","page":45,"heading":"Emergency Response and Long-Term Recovery","text":"Each applicable facility shall maintain a site-specific Emergency Preparedness and Response Plan based on credible failure scenarios, breach analysis and potential consequences. The plan shall be in accordance with section\n5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning process."},{"id":"3.10.38","page":45,"heading":"Emergency Response and Long-Term Recovery","text":"Emergency arrangements shall be developed, communicated, resourced, tested and updated with relevant workers, contractors, project-affected people, authorities and emergency services in accordance with the CTMS. External emergency-response capabilities and gaps shall be assessed, and community-focused preparedness measures shall be established."},{"id":"3.10.39","page":46,"heading":"Emergency Response and Long-Term Recovery","text":"Arrangements shall address immediate life-saving and environmental response following catastrophic failure. They shall also address medium- and long-term reconstruction, restoration and recovery."},{"id":"3.10.40","page":46,"heading":"Affected Communities","text":"Business Units shall respect human rights and conduct human-rights due diligence in accordance with the United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights. The due diligence shall inform tailings decisions and address risks arising from credible failure scenarios."},{"id":"3.10.41","page":46,"heading":"Affected Communities","text":"Project-affected people shall be meaningfully engaged throughout the facility lifecycle, including in developing the knowledge base and making decisions affecting public safety or tailings facility integrity."},{"id":"3.10.42","page":46,"heading":"Affected Communities","text":"Effective grievance and remedy mechanisms shall be maintained for project-affected people. Where applicable, Free, Prior and Informed Consent requirements affecting indigenous or tribal peoples and internationally recognized involuntary-resettlement requirements shall be applied in accordance with GISTM, the CTMS and relevant Maaden processes."},{"id":"3.10.43","page":46,"heading":"Public Disclosure and Access to Information","text":"Public and regulatory disclosures and responses to requests for tailings information shall be complete, accurate, timely and authorized in accordance with GISTM, the CTMS, applicable legal and permit requirements and Maaden disclosure requirements. Where a request for information is denied, the reason shall be communicated to the requesting party, subject to applicable legal, security and confidentiality restrictions."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Tailings Management; tailings management; Tailings Management Framework and Application; Management and Governance; Integrated Knowledge Base; Design, Construction, Operation and Monitoring; Emergency Response and Long-Term Recovery; Affected Communities; Public Disclosure and Access to Information","trigger":"if a tailings or residue storage facility or supporting system is involved","refs":["2.5","2.6","5.1"]},{"id":"3.11","title":"Closure and Land Rehabilitation","page":46,"purpose":"Maaden mines and assets are planned, operated, rehabilitated, closed, monitored and relinquished in a manner that protects human health and the environment, fulfils legal and regulatory obligations, supports community transition, maintains financial assurance, achieves the agreed post-closure land use, and leaves sites safe, stable and non-polluting.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units and relevant Corporate Functions and covers closure, rehabilitation and asset retirement obligations for mines, plants, infrastructure, mineral waste facilities, other assets and disturbed land throughout their lifecycle, from exploration and project development through operation, decommissioning, closure, post- closure monitoring and relinquishment.","clauses":[{"id":"3.11.1","page":46,"heading":"Closure Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units shall establish closure governance with defined accountability and multidisciplinary involvement for closure planning, implementation, monitoring and relinquishment."},{"id":"3.11.2","page":46,"heading":"Closure Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units shall integrate closure requirements, risks, costs and activities into Life of Asset planning, operational planning, investment plans and other relevant business decisions."},{"id":"3.11.3","page":46,"heading":"Closure Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units shall provide the technical capability, personnel and financial resources necessary to fulfil applicable closure, rehabilitation, post-closure and relinquishment obligations."},{"id":"3.11.4","page":47,"heading":"Closure Planning and Life of Mine Integration","text":"Business Units shall establish a Mine Closure Plan for each applicable operation during project development, maintain it throughout the Life of Mine, and use the Maaden Closure Planning Toolbox to support closure planning, closure criteria development, progressive closure, closure execution planning, costing, closure assurance and continual improvement."},{"id":"3.11.5","page":47,"heading":"Closure Planning and Life of Mine Integration","text":"Mine Closure Plans shall be integrated with mine and asset design, operational planning, mineral waste management, water management, biodiversity management and social performance planning, and shall address progressive closure, temporary closure, unplanned closure, final closure and post-closure requirements."},{"id":"3.11.6","page":47,"heading":"Closure Planning and Life of Mine Integration","text":"Business Units shall review and update Mine Closure Plans following material operational, regulatory, environmental, social, financial or closure-risk changes that may affect closure outcomes, liabilities, post-closure land use or closure assumptions."},{"id":"3.11.7","page":47,"heading":"Closure Knowledge Base and Studies","text":"Business Units shall establish and maintain the environmental, social, operational, technical, financial and regulatory information, and undertake and maintain the technical studies, necessary to support closure planning, decision- making, closure feasibility and the achievement of closure objectives. Studies shall be reviewed and updated when new information, operational changes or closure performance may affect their outcomes."},{"id":"3.11.8","page":47,"heading":"Closure Knowledge Base and Studies","text":"Business Units shall identify, assess and manage material knowledge gaps and uncertainties that may affect closure risks, costs, objectives, post-closure land use or outcomes."},{"id":"3.11.9","page":47,"heading":"Closure Vision, Objectives and Post-Closure Land Use","text":"Business Units shall document and maintain the closure vision, closure objectives and intended post-closure land use for each applicable operation or asset, considering applicable legal and regulatory obligations, Maaden commitments and relevant stakeholder input."},{"id":"3.11.10","page":47,"heading":"Closure Vision, Objectives and Post-Closure Land Use","text":"Closure objectives shall define the environmental, social, community-transition, safety and financial outcomes to be achieved through closure and post-closure."},{"id":"3.11.11","page":47,"heading":"Closure Vision, Objectives and Post-Closure Land Use","text":"Business Units shall demonstrate that the intended post-closure land use is legally permissible, technically feasible, environmentally sustainable and compatible with relevant stakeholder and regional land-use considerations."},{"id":"3.11.12","page":47,"heading":"Closure Vision, Objectives and Post-Closure Land Use","text":"Business Units shall document the implications of material changes to the intended post-closure land use and update the Mine Closure Plan, closure objectives and closure cost estimate, as applicable."},{"id":"3.11.13","page":47,"heading":"Risk and Opportunity Management","text":"Business Units shall identify, assess, record and manage closure related risks and opportunities throughout the asset lifecycle in accordance with Section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management, considering safety, environmental, social, legal and regulatory, financial and reputational consequences, including climate change, cumulative impacts and long-term residual risks."},{"id":"3.11.14","page":47,"heading":"Risk and Opportunity Management","text":"Closure risk assessment outcomes shall determine the prioritization of closure actions, rehabilitation activities, technical studies and resources, and the measures included in closure plans to manage material long-term and residual risks."},{"id":"3.11.15","page":48,"heading":"Closure Design and Rehabilitation Requirements","text":"Business Units shall ensure that closure and rehabilitation designs achieve long-term physical and chemical stability, protect human health and the environment, and support the intended post-closure land use and closure success criteria."},{"id":"3.11.16","page":48,"heading":"Closure Design and Rehabilitation Requirements","text":"Closure and rehabilitation designs shall address applicable mine features, mineral waste facilities, processing facilities, contaminated areas, water management infrastructure and associated infrastructure."},{"id":"3.11.17","page":48,"heading":"Closure Design and Rehabilitation Requirements","text":"Closure and rehabilitation designs shall assess and manage material long-term risks, including acid rock drainage, metal leaching, contaminant migration, surface water, groundwater and other sources of long-term pollution, where applicable."},{"id":"3.11.18","page":48,"heading":"Progressive Closure","text":"Business Units shall identify, plan and implement progressive closure opportunities throughout operations and integrate them into Life of Mine plans and operational schedules."},{"id":"3.11.19","page":48,"heading":"Progressive Closure","text":"Business Units shall select and prioritize progressive closure activities based on their contribution to risk reduction, closure objectives, post-closure land use, value creation and reduction of closure costs and liabilities."},{"id":"3.11.20","page":48,"heading":"Progressive Closure","text":"The funding approach for progressive closure activities shall be determined with consideration of the expected reduction in closure liabilities, closure costs and financial assurance requirements."},{"id":"3.11.21","page":48,"heading":"Progressive Closure","text":"Business Units shall monitor progressive closure performance and evaluate its effect on closure outcomes, costs, liabilities and financial assurance requirements, where applicable."},{"id":"3.11.22","page":48,"heading":"Social Transition and Stakeholder Engagement","text":"Business Units shall identify stakeholders affected by closure and engage them during relevant stages of closure planning and execution. Relevant stakeholder input shall be documented and considered in closure planning and decision-making."},{"id":"3.11.23","page":48,"heading":"Social Transition and Stakeholder Engagement","text":"Business Units shall assess material social transition impacts, risks and opportunities associated with closure, including those relating to the workforce, affected communities, local economies and cultural heritage, where applicable."},{"id":"3.11.24","page":48,"heading":"Social Transition and Stakeholder Engagement","text":"Closure plans shall define measures to manage identified material social transition risks and impacts and to support beneficial transition opportunities, where practicable."},{"id":"3.11.25","page":48,"heading":"Closure Costing and Financial Assurance","text":"Business Units shall maintain lifecycle closure cost estimates, developed using the support from Maaden Closure Planning Toolbox, covering all material closure obligations, including decommissioning, rehabilitation, post-closure monitoring and maintenance, social transition and residual risk management, where applicable."},{"id":"3.11.26","page":48,"heading":"Closure Costing and Financial Assurance","text":"Business Units shall maintain and periodically reassess financial provisions and any required financial assurance to cover closure liabilities in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory, permit and corporate requirements."},{"id":"3.11.27","page":48,"heading":"Closure Execution Planning","text":"Before final closure activities commence, Business Units shall establish and maintain a Closure Execution Plan defining the scope, schedule, resources, monitoring and completion requirements, and take necessary approvals."},{"id":"3.11.28","page":49,"heading":"Closure Execution Planning","text":"Business Units shall manage closure execution through established project management processes."},{"id":"3.11.29","page":49,"heading":"Monitoring, Success Criteria and Adaptive Management","text":"Business Units shall establish measurable success criteria linked to closure objectives, activities and the agreed post-closure land use, covering applicable physical, environmental, social and safety outcomes."},{"id":"3.11.30","page":49,"heading":"Monitoring, Success Criteria and Adaptive Management","text":"Business Units shall monitor and evaluate closure implementation and performance against applicable obligations and success criteria at frequencies determined by legal, regulatory, permit and corporate requirements."},{"id":"3.11.31","page":49,"heading":"Monitoring, Success Criteria and Adaptive Management","text":"Business Units shall use monitoring and evaluation results to address performance deviations, implement corrective actions and update closure plans, controls and monitoring programs where required."},{"id":"3.11.32","page":49,"heading":"Temporary or Sudden Closure","text":"Business Units shall identify credible temporary suspension and unplanned closure scenarios and incorporate associated preparedness, response, care and maintenance, and cost requirements into closure plans and closure cost estimates."},{"id":"3.11.33","page":49,"heading":"Temporary or Sudden Closure","text":"During temporary suspension or transition to unplanned closure, Business Units shall implement care and maintenance arrangements that protect human health and safety, maintain environmental and regulatory compliance, manage security and asset-integrity risks, and support either the safe resumption of operations or progression to final closure."},{"id":"3.11.34","page":49,"heading":"Decommissioning, Dismantling and Asset Retirement","text":"Business Units shall identify assets requiring retirement, decommissioning, dismantling, decontamination, remediation or rehabilitation and integrate the applicable requirements and costs into closure plans."},{"id":"3.11.35","page":49,"heading":"Decommissioning, Dismantling and Asset Retirement","text":"Business Units shall ensure that retired assets and affected areas are safe, physically and chemically stable, non- polluting and compatible with the agreed post-closure land use and completion criteria, with technically feasible and legally permitted opportunities to reuse, repurpose, recycle or recover assets and materials evaluated before disposal."},{"id":"3.11.36","page":49,"heading":"Relinquishment and Liability Transfer","text":"Business Units shall identify, document and maintain the applicable requirements and pathway for closure certification, relinquishment and any associated liability transfer."},{"id":"3.11.37","page":49,"heading":"Relinquishment and Liability Transfer","text":"Relinquishment or liability transfer shall not be completed until the Business Unit has demonstrated achievement of applicable closure success criteria, obtained all required legal and regulatory approvals, and retained evidence supporting certification, relinquishment and liability transfer."}],"area":"Environment","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Closure and Land Rehabilitation; closure and land rehabilitation; Closure Governance and Accountability; Closure Planning and Life-of-Mine Integration; Closure Knowledge Base and Studies; Closure Vision, Objectives and Post-Closure Land Use; Risk and Opportunity Management; Closure Design and Rehabilitation Requirements; Progressive Closure; Social Transition and Stakeholder Engagement; Closure Costing and Financial Assurance; Closure Execution Planning; Monitoring, Success Criteria and Adaptive Management; Temporary or Sudden Closure; Decommissioning, Dismantling and Asset Retirement; Relinquishment and Liability Transfer","trigger":"if temporary closure, decommissioning, demolition, rehabilitation or post-closure applies","refs":["2.5"]},{"id":"3.12","title":"Occupational Health Risk Assessment and Industrial Hygiene Management","page":49,"purpose":"Identify, assess and prioritize occupational health hazards and exposures throughout the asset lifecycle, establishing a reliable basis for selecting and verifying controls, and uses exposure and health information to prevent occupational illness.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units, Corporate Functions and controlled workplaces, including employees, contractor personnel and others who may be exposed to occupational health hazards during routine, non-routine, abnormal or foreseeable emergency work throughout the asset lifecycle.","clauses":[{"id":"3.12.1","page":50,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Competence","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain occupational health risk assessment and industrial hygiene arrangements commensurate with occupational health hazards and exposures arising from their activities, including provision of the resources, information, access and personnel required to implement them."},{"id":"3.12.2","page":50,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Competence","text":"Line management shall ensure occupational health risks, controls and required actions are managed and escalated in accordance with the EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.12.3","page":50,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Competence","text":"Personnel conducting or reviewing occupational health risk or exposure assessments shall be competent for the assessment scope. Workers, contractor representatives and other personnel knowledgeable about the work shall participate where relevant."},{"id":"3.12.4","page":50,"heading":"Hazard Identification and Exposure Profiling","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify chemical, physical, biological, ergonomic and psychosocial or work organization hazards; their sources; affected areas; exposed people; exposure routes; and potential acute, chronic or long-latency health effects under routine, non-routine, abnormal and foreseeable emergency conditions."},{"id":"3.12.5","page":50,"heading":"Hazard Identification and Exposure Profiling","text":"Similar Exposure Groups (SEGs) shall be established and maintained for workers with comparable exposure profiles, with the grouping basis, material changes, relevant Safety Data Sheets and process specific exposure information documented."},{"id":"3.12.6","page":50,"heading":"Hazard Identification and Exposure Profiling","text":"Occupational health profiles and assessments shall be completed before commissioning new or modified facilities, equipment or processes; reviewed at intervals not exceeding five years; and revalidated following change, new hazards or information, abnormal results, control failure, occupational illness or health incidents, or substantiated worker concerns."},{"id":"3.12.7","page":50,"heading":"Hazard Identification and Exposure Profiling","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall register and assess workplace agents classified by the current International Agency for Research on Cancer Monographs as Group 1, 2A or 2B. Diesel particulate matter shall be treated as a carcinogenic airborne contaminant in underground mining. Exposure shall be eliminated where reasonably practicable and otherwise minimized in accordance with Airborne Contaminants and Chemical Exposure Control."},{"id":"3.12.8","page":50,"heading":"Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain baseline occupational health hazard and exposure profiles for applicable activities and Similar Exposure Groups and complete targeted assessments where exposure is material or uncertain, results are abnormal, or required controls are absent, degraded or failed."},{"id":"3.12.9","page":50,"heading":"Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment","text":"Assessments shall evaluate exposure characteristics, health consequences, inherent and residual risk, control effectiveness and uncertainty using Maaden EHSS Risk Management Process. Hazards capable of fatal, irreversible or disabling harm shall be evaluated for inclusion in Critical Control Management."},{"id":"3.12.10","page":51,"heading":"Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement and maintain a risk based exposure assessment strategy that defines assessment priorities, methodologies, assessment criteria, data quality requirements and response actions."},{"id":"3.12.11","page":51,"heading":"Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment","text":"Qualitative assessments shall characterize potential exposure, identify uncertainty and information gaps, determine interim controls and establish priorities for quantitative monitoring."},{"id":"3.12.12","page":51,"heading":"Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment","text":"Quantitative monitoring shall be conducted when required by law, an applicable MSHEM process, assessment uncertainty, abnormal results or trends, or control-verification needs"},{"id":"3.12.13","page":51,"heading":"Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment","text":"Exposure sampling shall represent relevant workers, tasks and operating conditions. Combined exposures, extended work schedules and incomplete sampling coverage shall be evaluated where relevant, and associated uncertainty shall be documented."},{"id":"3.12.14","page":51,"heading":"Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment","text":"Assessment results shall inform Similar Exposure Groups, monitoring and control plans, competency and training, health surveillance, emergency preparedness, design and change-management decisions."},{"id":"3.12.15","page":51,"heading":"Exposure Criteria and Assessment Quality","text":"Occupational exposure assessments shall apply the ACGIH Threshold Limit Values (TLVs) and Biological Exposure Indices (BEIs)."},{"id":"3.12.16","page":51,"heading":"Exposure Criteria and Assessment Quality","text":"Occupational health assessments shall identify credible acute exposure and oxygen displacement scenarios requiring detection, alarms or emergency response and shall provide relevant emergency response requirements to the applicable Emergency Preparedness and Response process."},{"id":"3.12.17","page":51,"heading":"Exposure Criteria and Assessment Quality","text":"Occupational health assessments shall identify hazards and Similar Exposure Groups requiring referral to Medical Services and Health Surveillance. Health surveillance and biological monitoring shall reflect the hazard, exposure route, exposure profile and applicable requirements and shall not replace hazard prevention or exposure controls. Relevant exposure information shall be made available to Medical Services as required for health surveillance. Aggregated health-surveillance findings shall be used to review occupational health risks and control effectiveness while maintaining medical confidentiality."},{"id":"3.12.18","page":51,"heading":"Exposure Criteria and Assessment Quality","text":"Affected workers shall receive understandable information on occupational health hazards, exposure results, associated health significance, required controls and actions following control failure, in a manner that addresses applicable language, literacy and accessibility needs and protects personal and medical information."},{"id":"3.12.19","page":51,"heading":"Contractors, Projects, Performance and Records","text":"Contractor occupational health requirements shall be managed through Contractor EHSS Management. Where Maaden and contractor personnel share or influence an exposure profile, contractor personnel shall be included in Similar Exposure Groups and exposure assessments, and relevant information and controls shall be maintained."},{"id":"3.12.20","page":51,"heading":"Contractors, Projects, Performance and Records","text":"Projects shall apply occupational health criteria, assessments and controls through Projects EHSS Management and transfer outstanding occupational health risks and actions through the applicable handover process."},{"id":"3.12.21","page":51,"heading":"Contractors, Projects, Performance and Records","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review occupational health performance at least annually through EHSS Performance Monitoring, Measurement and Evaluation, considering exposure trends, aggregated health findings, occupational illnesses, control effectiveness and outstanding actions. Exposure exceedances, occupational illnesses and material control failures shall be reported through Incident Management and Stewardship when the applicable criteria are met."},{"id":"3.12.22","page":52,"heading":"Contractors, Projects, Performance and Records","text":"Occupational health assessment, exposure monitoring and control verification records shall be retained for the life of the facility and for any longer period required by Maaden or applicable law to enable reconstruction of long-latency exposure histories. Exposure records shall be maintained separately from confidential medical records, with access, disclosure and disposal controlled to protect personal and medical information."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Occupational Health Risk Assessment and Industrial Hygiene Management; occupational health risk assessment and industrial hygiene management; Governance, Accountability and Competence; Hazard Identification and Exposure Profiling; Occupational Health Risk and Exposure Assessment; Exposure Criteria and Assessment Quality; Contractors, Projects, Performance and Records","trigger":"if workplace health hazards require industrial-hygiene assessment, monitoring or control","refs":[]},{"id":"3.13","title":"Airborne Contaminants and Chemical Exposure Control","page":52,"purpose":"Prevent occupational illness and acute harm arising from airborne contaminants and hazardous chemicals by assessing exposures, applying effective controls and responding to exceedances or control failures.","scope":"This process applies where employees, contractors or other people may be exposed to airborne contaminants or hazardous chemicals by inhalation, skin or eye contact, ingestion or injection during routine, non-routine, abnormal or emergency conditions.","clauses":[{"id":"3.13.1","page":52,"heading":"Exposure Hazard Identification and Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify airborne contaminant and chemical exposure hazards arising from materials, processes, equipment, tasks and workplace conditions, considering all credible exposure routes and relevant normal, abnormal, non-routine, maintenance, upset and foreseeable emergency conditions."},{"id":"3.13.2","page":52,"heading":"Exposure Hazard Identification and Assessment","text":"Before disturbing materials, deposits or residues of unknown composition, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify or sample the material and apply precautionary controls until its hazards and required controls are established under sections 3.12 Occupational Health Risk Assessment and Industrial Hygiene Management and 3.20 Hazardous Materials Management."},{"id":"3.13.3","page":52,"heading":"Exposure Hazard Identification and Assessment","text":"Hazard identification, Similar Exposure Group determination, exposure assessment, monitoring and control verification shall comply with Process 3.12. Airborne contaminant sampling and analysis shall be performed using validated methods, calibrated equipment and competent laboratories, consistent with applicable requirements of ISO 7708, ISO 13137, ISO 15767, relevant parts of ISO 16000 and the NIOSH Manual of Analytical Methods."},{"id":"3.13.4","page":52,"heading":"Exposure Hazard Identification and Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate airborne contaminant exposures and establish control and response actions against the applicable exposure criteria determined through the section 3.12 Occupational Health Risk Assessment and Industrial Hygiene Management process."},{"id":"3.13.5","page":52,"heading":"Exposure Hazard Identification and Assessment","text":"Exposure evaluations shall apply relevant time-weighted average, short-term exposure and ceiling limits, together with applicable skin, sensitizer and biological exposure criteria, and account for extended shifts and combined exposures. The airborne exposure action level is 50% of the applicable occupational exposure limit. Exposure at or above the action level shall trigger reassessment, representative monitoring, implementation or improvement of controls, and verification of control effectiveness. Biological monitoring shall apply the relevant BEI, determinant, sampling time and background notation."},{"id":"3.13.6","page":52,"heading":"Exposure Hazard Identification and Assessment","text":"Affected people shall be withdrawn or their exposure stopped where an applicable OEL, short-term exposure limit, ceiling limit or withdrawal criterion is exceeded or likely to be exceeded, with affected work resumed only after controls are implemented and verified as effective and confirmed exceedances reported and investigated in accordance with Process 6.1 Incident Management."},{"id":"3.13.7","page":53,"heading":"Exposure Prevention and Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall apply the hierarchy of controls to eliminate or minimize contaminant generation, release, spread and exposure through all credible routes, prioritizing elimination, substitution and engineering controls over administrative controls and personal protective equipment."},{"id":"3.13.8","page":53,"heading":"Exposure Prevention and Control","text":"Agent specific controls shall be established for serious, irreversible or uncertain health hazards, including carcinogens, mutagens, reproductive toxicants, sensitizers, respirable crystalline silica, asbestos and other fibrous minerals, toxic metals, welding fumes, diesel particulate matter, acid mists and toxic gases. Work affecting asbestos- containing or other regulator controlled material shall not start until required identification, authorization, competent people, isolation, decontamination, waste and clearance arrangements are confirmed."},{"id":"3.13.9","page":53,"heading":"Exposure Prevention and Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that required engineering controls, including suppression, extraction and enclosure systems, have defined performance criteria, are operational before exposure generating work begins, and are commissioned, verified, inspected, tested and maintained based on risk and applicable specifications, with work stopped or restricted whenever a required control is absent, ineffective or impaired until equivalent protection is implemented and verified as effective."},{"id":"3.13.10","page":53,"heading":"Airborne Contaminant Controls","text":"Dust generated by drilling, blasting, crushing, screening, cutting, grinding, loading, tipping, conveyor transfer, muck piles, stockpiles and haul roads shall be controlled at source using wet methods, extraction, enclosure, isolation, remote operation, reduced drop heights, stabilized surfaces or other effective controls to prevent hazardous loss of visibility, secondary exposure and re-entrainment."},{"id":"3.13.11","page":53,"heading":"Airborne Contaminant Controls","text":"Ventilation and local extraction shall have defined airflow and capture criteria, collect contaminants at or near their source and prevent contaminated air from reaching other work areas or air intakes. Contaminated air shall not be recirculated unless contaminant removal, monitoring and failure controls are designed and verified for the hazard."},{"id":"3.13.12","page":53,"heading":"Airborne Contaminant Controls","text":"Operator enclosures used for particulate exposure control shall conform to ISO 23875, maintain the required protective performance and be monitored, inspected and maintained. Use for gases or vapors requires specific design and verification, and loss of protective performance requires affected work to stop until equivalent protection is verified as effective."},{"id":"3.13.13","page":53,"heading":"Airborne Contaminant Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall control diesel particulate matter exposure using the hierarchy of controls and the ICMM Diesel Particulate Matter Good Practice Guide, prioritizing electric or low-emission equipment, low-sulfur fuel, engine maintenance and exhaust after-treatment, followed by ventilation, filtered enclosures, remote operation and work planning, with respiratory protection used only as a supplementary or interim control. Underground ventilation shall comply with section 3.36 Underground Mines Ventilation, Fire and Refuge Safety."},{"id":"3.13.14","page":53,"heading":"Respiratory Protection and Acute Exposure Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement a documented respiratory protection program in accordance with Process 3.31 and applicable ISO/TS 16975-1 requirements where respiratory protection is necessary for residual exposure, temporary control impairment, emergency response or specialized work, and not as a substitute for feasible higher-order controls."},{"id":"3.13.15","page":53,"heading":"Respiratory Protection and Acute Exposure Controls","text":"Respiratory protective equipment shall be certified and selected for the applicable contaminant, exposure level, oxygen concentration, task, duration and required protection factor. The respiratory protection program shall address medical suitability, training, equipment issue, inspection, cleaning, storage, maintenance and filter or cartridge replacement. Supplied breathing air shall meet at least Grade D breathing-air quality in accordance with ANSI/CGA G-7.1 or an equivalent adopted standard."},{"id":"3.13.16","page":54,"heading":"Respiratory Protection and Acute Exposure Controls","text":"Tight-fitting respirators shall be fit-tested in accordance with ISO 16975-3 before initial use, at least every 12 months and following changes that could affect the seal, with wearers completing a seal check before each use and remaining free from facial hair or other equipment that interferes with the seal."},{"id":"3.13.17","page":54,"heading":"Respiratory Protection and Acute Exposure Controls","text":"Respiratory protection areas shall be identified, signposted and access controlled where necessary. Air-purifying respirators shall not be used where oxygen is below 19.5%, the contaminant or concentration is unknown, or the atmosphere is or may become immediately dangerous to life or health (IDLH)."},{"id":"3.13.18","page":54,"heading":"Respiratory Protection and Acute Exposure Controls","text":"Credible acute exposure scenarios shall have monitoring, alarms, withdrawal, escape, communication and emergency response controls. IDLH entry shall require positive pressure self-contained breathing apparatus or positive pressure supplied air with auxiliary escape."},{"id":"3.13.19","page":54,"heading":"Contamination Control and Exposure Response","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall prevent ingestion, skin contact and cross contamination through risk- based hygiene and decontamination arrangements, including changing, washing, laundering, equipment cleaning and contaminated-waste management. Dry sweeping and compressed air shall not be used where they could disperse contamination; wet cleaning or HEPA-filtered vacuum equipment shall be used instead. Eating, drinking, smoking and food storage shall be prohibited in contaminated areas."},{"id":"3.13.20","page":54,"heading":"Contamination Control and Exposure Response","text":"Emergency eyewash and shower facilities provided where harmful chemicals could contact the eyes or skin shall comply with ANSI/ISEA Z358.1, be accessible by an unobstructed route within 10 seconds or closer where required, and undergo weekly activation and annual performance testing."},{"id":"3.13.21","page":54,"heading":"Contamination Control and Exposure Response","text":"Actual or suspected harmful exposure or failure of a required exposure control shall trigger immediate work controls, decontamination, first aid or medical evaluation, as applicable, and reporting and investigation through Process 6.1 Incident Management."},{"id":"3.13.22","page":54,"heading":"Information, Records and Improvement","text":"Affected employees and contractors shall receive understandable information and training covering hazards, exposure routes and criteria, controls, respiratory protection limitations, symptoms, emergency actions and hygiene. Workers shall be consulted during exposure assessment and control review and informed of monitoring conclusions and required actions. Personal exposure information shall be accessible to the individual, and medical information shall remain confidential."},{"id":"3.13.23","page":54,"heading":"Information, Records and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain retrievable exposure, monitoring, control verification, exceedance and respiratory protection records for the longest applicable legal, regulatory, permit or Maaden period. As minimum, dust, gas, mist and fume survey records shall be retained for at least 10 years and made available to SAIS when required."},{"id":"3.13.24","page":54,"heading":"Information, Records and Improvement","text":"Exposure assessments and controls shall be reviewed, as minimum, following relevant changes, action level or OEL exceedances, control failures, health concerns, new technical information or adverse trends."},{"id":"3.13.25","page":54,"heading":"Information, Records and Improvement","text":"Performance monitoring, corrective action and continual improvement shall comply with section 6.2 EHSS Performance Monitoring, Measurement and Evaluation."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Airborne Contaminants and Chemical Exposure Control; dust, silica, fumes, vapour, gas, chemicals, exposure; Exposure Hazard Identification and Assessment; Exposure Prevention and Control; Airborne Contaminant Controls; Respiratory Protection and Acute Exposure Controls; Contamination Control and Exposure Response; Information, Records and Improvement","trigger":"if dust, fumes, vapours, gases, fibres or hazardous chemicals may expose personnel","refs":["3.12","3.36","6.2"]},{"id":"3.14","title":"Noise and Hearing Conservation","page":55,"purpose":"Maaden identifies, assess, control and monitor occupational noise exposure to prevent noise induced hearing loss and tinnitus and to maintain personnel ability to communicate and respond to alarms, warnings and operational hazards.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden controlled activities, facilities and work where employees, contractors, visitors or other personnel may be exposed to occupational noise that could damage hearing or interfere with communication, alarms or warnings, including applicable activities, areas, equipment, tasks, routine and non-routine work, and relevant lifecycle stages.","clauses":[{"id":"3.14.1","page":55,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assign accountability and establish a risk-based Noise and Hearing Conservation Program proportionate to its occupational noise exposure profile."},{"id":"3.14.2","page":55,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Line management shall implement workplace noise controls, while Industrial Hygiene shall provide technical ownership of noise hazard identification, exposure assessment and control performance verification."},{"id":"3.14.3","page":55,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Occupational Health shall manage audiometric surveillance, medical interpretation, confidential follow-up and work- relatedness review through the Medical Services and Health Surveillance process."},{"id":"3.14.4","page":55,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Contractor work under Maaden control shall meet the applicable requirements of this process."},{"id":"3.14.5","page":55,"heading":"Noise criteria","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall apply the occupational noise exposure limits and action levels in Table\n3.14-1 as minimum Maaden requirements, or lower limits where required by applicable legal, regulatory, permit or authority requirements:\n[Table table-55-1.png]\n\nTable 3.14-1. Occupational Noise Exposure Limits and Action levels."},{"id":"3.14.6","page":55,"heading":"Noise criteria","text":"Occupational noise exposure shall remain below 100 dBA over 15 minutes and 140 dB linear peak sound pressure level, with exposure at or above the applicable Action Level evaluated and managed through Process 3.12 Industrial Hygiene Management."},{"id":"3.14.7","page":55,"heading":"Noise Hazard Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify occupational noise sources, areas, equipment, occupations and tasks and assess representative exposure scenarios for applicable Personnel, Similar Exposure Groups, shifts, operating conditions and routine and non-routine activities through the Occupational Health Risk Assessment and Industrial Hygiene Management processes."},{"id":"3.14.8","page":55,"heading":"Noise Hazard Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Exposure assessment shall consider continuous, intermittent, variable and impulsive noise; exposure duration and shift length; representative operating conditions; and combined exposure to noise, ototoxic substances and vibration where interaction is credible."},{"id":"3.14.9","page":56,"heading":"Noise Hazard Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Personal noise exposure results shall be evaluated and reported without deducting attenuation provided by hearing protection used by the worker. Exposure characterization, Similar Exposure Group (SEG) classification, and Hearing Conservation Program enrollment shall be based on unprotected noise exposure."},{"id":"3.14.10","page":56,"heading":"Noise Hazard Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Exposure shall be reassessed at the interval established by the controlled Industrial Hygiene strategy and following a change, complaint, incident, abnormal surveillance trend or control failure that could materially alter exposure."},{"id":"3.14.11","page":56,"heading":"Noise Hazard Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Affected personnel and accountable line management shall be informed of the assessment outcome and the controls applicable to their work."},{"id":"3.14.12","page":56,"heading":"Noise Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall eliminate or minimize occupational noise exposure by applying the hierarchy of controls, prioritizing elimination, substitution and engineering controls."},{"id":"3.14.13","page":56,"heading":"Noise Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish a documented noise control plan where occupational noise exposure exceeds the applicable exposure limit specified in 3.14.5, required controls are not fully implemented, or existing controls are ineffective."},{"id":"3.14.14","page":56,"heading":"Noise Control","text":"The plan shall identify material exposure scenarios, permanent and interim controls, accountable owners, target dates and verification requirements, with interim controls maintained until permanent controls are implemented and verified as effective."},{"id":"3.14.15","page":56,"heading":"Noise Control","text":"Noise controls shall be verified under representative operating conditions against defined performance criteria, with failed, degraded, bypassed or ineffective controls reported, risk-assessed and corrected, and unresolved or material failures escalated through the applicable MSHEM process."},{"id":"3.14.16","page":56,"heading":"Hearing Conservation Program (HCP) and Noise Hazard Areas","text":"Personnel or Similar Exposure Groups (SEGs) whose occupational noise exposure meets or exceeds the applicable action level specified in 3.14.5, or the applicable 100 dBA or 140 dB peak criterion, shall be enrolled in the Hearing Conservation Program."},{"id":"3.14.17","page":56,"heading":"Hearing Conservation Program (HCP) and Noise Hazard Areas","text":"The Hearing Conservation Program shall identify enrolled personnel or exposure groups, designated areas and tasks, exposure information, required controls, hearing protection requirements, occupational health surveillance interfaces, communication requirements and performance measures."},{"id":"3.14.18","page":56,"heading":"Hearing Conservation Program (HCP) and Noise Hazard Areas","text":"Areas, equipment and tasks meeting the applicable noise-hazard designation criteria, including those with noise levels of 85 dBA or greater, shall be identified and communicated to affected personnel through applicable warning signs, access controls and hearing-protection requirements."},{"id":"3.14.19","page":56,"heading":"Hearing Conservation Program (HCP) and Noise Hazard Areas","text":"Noise area and task designations shall be reviewed and revised when changes to noise exposure, work activities, equipment, controls or applicable criteria could affect the designation."},{"id":"3.14.20","page":56,"heading":"Hearing Protection","text":"Hearing protectors shall be selected to maintain protected exposure below the applicable exposure limit, considering measured noise exposure, noise characteristics, device attenuation, compatibility with other personal protective equipment, communication and alarm requirements, task demands and wearer factors."},{"id":"3.14.21","page":57,"heading":"Hearing Protection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall manage hearing protectors through the section 3.31 Personal Protective Equipment process and provide affected personnel with approved, individually suitable devices and instruction on their fitting, inspection, use, care and limitations."},{"id":"3.14.22","page":57,"heading":"Hearing Protection","text":"Hearing protectors shall comply with applicable requirements of the SASO Technical Regulation for Personal Protective Equipment and Clothing, have their performance individually verified where required by controlled technical criteria, and be used as dual hearing protection where the 8-hour noise exposure is 110 dBA or greater."},{"id":"3.14.23","page":57,"heading":"Hearing Protection","text":"Hearing protection shall be used without replacing feasible and effective higher-order controls, with failed performance verification requiring refitting or replacement, review of exposure controls, and successful reverification before continued reliance on the hearing protector."},{"id":"3.14.24","page":57,"heading":"Hearing Protection","text":"Where hearing protection cannot reduce exposure below the applicable exposure limit, additional controls and exposure duration restrictions shall be implemented before work continues."},{"id":"3.14.25","page":57,"heading":"Occupational Health Surveillance and Medical Follow-up","text":"Personnel meeting the exposure criteria specified in 3.14.16 or other approved occupational health surveillance criteria shall participate in audiometric surveillance."},{"id":"3.14.26","page":57,"heading":"Occupational Health Surveillance and Medical Follow-up","text":"Medical Services and Health Surveillance shall define and maintain requirements for baseline, periodic, job- transfer, exit, repeat and event triggered audiometry, specialist referral, evaluation criteria, follow-up actions and notification timeframes."},{"id":"3.14.27","page":57,"heading":"Occupational Health Surveillance and Medical Follow-up","text":"A hearing change meeting approved referral criteria shall trigger Occupational Health review and evaluation of relevant workplace noise exposure, noise controls and hearing protection effectiveness. Suspected work-related hearing conditions meeting the applicable reporting or investigation criteria shall be managed through section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship process."},{"id":"3.14.28","page":57,"heading":"Occupational Health Surveillance and Medical Follow-up","text":"Medical information disclosed outside Occupational Health shall be limited to fitness, restrictions, required workplace controls, legally required information and de-identified or aggregated trends."},{"id":"3.14.29","page":57,"heading":"Competency, Information and Communication","text":"Personnel conducting, interpreting, approving or supervising occupational noise assessments, exposure controls or hearing protection evaluations shall be competent for their assigned responsibilities."},{"id":"3.14.30","page":57,"heading":"Competency, Information and Communication","text":"Personnel exposed to occupational noise shall receive information and training appropriate to their exposure risks, including health effects, workplace noise hazards, required controls, designated noise areas and tasks, hearing protector selection and use, health surveillance, and reporting of concerns or symptoms."},{"id":"3.14.31","page":57,"heading":"Competency, Information and Communication","text":"Changes to noise hazards, controls, designated areas or work requirements shall be communicated to affected personnel before work begins under the changed conditions."},{"id":"3.14.32","page":57,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Lifecycle Assurance","text":"Projects, procurement activities and changes that could affect occupational noise exposure shall define noise performance criteria, evaluate practicable lower noise options, and require suppliers of noise-significant equipment to provide noise emission data sufficient to verify compliance."},{"id":"3.14.33","page":57,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Lifecycle Assurance","text":"Project and commissioning assurance shall verify, under representative operating conditions and before operational acceptance, that specified noise controls and contractual noise performance criteria have been achieved."},{"id":"3.14.34","page":58,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Lifecycle Assurance","text":"Changes that could materially affect noise generation, occupational exposure, safety-critical communication, alarms or noise controls shall be managed through section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.14.35","page":58,"heading":"Performance Review and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate the effectiveness of the Noise and Hearing Conservation Program at a risk-based frequency, considering exposure results, control verification results, hearing protector performance, aggregated health surveillance trends, incidents, complaints, findings, competency status and overdue actions."},{"id":"3.14.36","page":58,"heading":"Performance Review and Improvement","text":"The evaluation shall identify ineffective controls and program arrangements, assign corrective or improvement actions with accountable owners and due dates, and escalate material adverse trends, occupational noise exposure limit exceedances, control failures and overdue risk reduction actions through applicable MSHEM processes."},{"id":"3.14.37","page":58,"heading":"Performance Review and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain occupational noise and hearing conservation records in accordance with applicable legal requirements."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Noise and Hearing Conservation; noise, hearing, audiometry; Governance and Accountability; Noise criteria; Noise Hazard Identification and Exposure Assessment; Noise Control; Hearing Conservation Program (HCP) and Noise Hazard Areas; Hearing Protection; Occupational Health Surveillance and Medical Follow-up; Competency, Information and Communication; Design, Procurement and Lifecycle Assurance; Performance Review and Improvement","trigger":"if noise exposure may exceed applicable criteria or hearing protection is required","refs":["3.31","3.52","6.1"]},{"id":"3.15","title":"Thermal Stress and Physical Hazards","page":58,"purpose":"Thermal stress, vibration and workplace illumination exposures shall be identified, assessed and controlled to prevent acute and chronic harm, maintain defined operating limits and verify control effectiveness.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden controlled activities and workplaces where employees, contractors, visitors or other people may be exposed to thermal stress, hand-arm or whole-body vibration, inadequate or disruptive illumination, or other physical agents assigned to this process. It covers thermal stress associated with weather, sunlight, radiant or process heat, cold or refrigerated environments, wet conditions, workload, clothing and PPE.","clauses":[{"id":"3.15.1","page":58,"heading":"Assessment Governance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that physical hazard assessments and monitoring are conducted or technically reviewed by personnel competent in the physical agent and applicable assessment method."},{"id":"3.15.2","page":58,"heading":"Assessment Governance","text":"Physical hazard assessments shall comply with applicable legal requirements and technical standards, including ISO 7243 for thermal stress, ISO 5349 for hand-arm vibration, ISO 2631-1 for whole-body vibration and ISO/CIE 8995 for workplace illumination, as applicable."},{"id":"3.15.3","page":58,"heading":"Assessment Governance","text":"Instruments used for quantitative assessments shall be suitable for the applicable method, maintained, calibrated or verified at specified intervals, and function checked before use where required."},{"id":"3.15.4","page":58,"heading":"Physical Agent Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify physical agent sources, affected people and foreseeable exposure scenarios and assess exposure magnitude, frequency, duration and variability, considering operating and environmental conditions, existing controls, combined exposures and foreseeable higher exposure conditions."},{"id":"3.15.5","page":58,"heading":"Physical Agent Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Quantitative monitoring shall be conducted where required by applicable obligations or Maaden adopted criteria, or where qualitative assessment cannot reliably exclude overexposure, using sampling strategies representative of affected people, tasks and operating conditions."},{"id":"3.15.6","page":59,"heading":"Physical Agent Identification and Exposure Assessment","text":"Assessment and monitoring results shall be evaluated against applicable exposure limits, action levels and technical criteria, considering exposure variability and measurement uncertainty, and reviewed following material exposure changes, exceedances, control failures, reported symptoms, incidents or occupational illnesses."},{"id":"3.15.7","page":59,"heading":"Exposure Control","text":"Physical hazard exposure shall be eliminated where reasonably practicable or otherwise reduced to within applicable exposure criteria using the hierarchy of controls, prioritizing higher order controls over administrative controls and PPE."},{"id":"3.15.8","page":59,"heading":"Exposure Control","text":"Where exposure limits or action levels are exceeded, permanent controls are delayed, or exposure may change, documented control and trigger action arrangements shall define interim and permanent controls, monitored conditions, trigger levels, responsibilities, work restrictions, verification requirements and restart criteria."},{"id":"3.15.9","page":59,"heading":"Exposure Control","text":"Work shall be stopped or restricted where applicable exposure limits are exceeded, required controls or monitoring are unavailable or ineffective, or changed conditions have not been assessed, and can resume only after the conditions are reassessed and required controls are verified as effective."},{"id":"3.15.10","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"Thermal stress assessments shall consider heat and cold sources, environmental conditions, metabolic workload, exposure duration, clothing and PPE, acclimatization and foreseeable changes in work or environmental conditions."},{"id":"3.15.11","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"Heat stress screening shall use Wet Bulb Globe Temperature (WBGT) measurements representative of actual workplace conditions and consider workload, clothing, PPE and acclimatization in accordance with ISO 7243 criteria and recognized assessment methods. Weather or forecast data may support planning but shall not replace workplace measurements where local conditions materially affect exposure."},{"id":"3.15.12","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"Thermal controls shall be established and adjusted before and during work based on documented assessments, forecasts, workplace measurements and observed conditions, using, as applicable, source isolation, shielding, ventilation, cooling or heating, workload reduction, scheduling, work-rest arrangements, hydration, recovery facilities, clothing, personal protective equipment, worker observation and emergency arrangements."},{"id":"3.15.13","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"Work rest arrangements shall be based on the thermal exposure assessment under 3.15.11 and include specified rest periods, hygienic potable water and suitable recovery facilities. Direct-sun work shall comply with applicable Saudi requirements, including any authorized exemptions or alternative arrangements."},{"id":"3.15.14","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"New, returning or non-acclimatized personnel shall complete a supervised acclimatization or re-acclimatization process with defined progression, monitoring and completion criteria before full thermal exposure."},{"id":"3.15.15","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"Cold-stress controls shall address wet, refrigerated, cryogenic and adverse weather conditions and foreseeable loss of heating, shelter, protective clothing or other required controls."},{"id":"3.15.16","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"Work shall be stopped or restricted when thermal strain symptoms or defined stop-work conditions occur, with affected personnel removed from exposure and referred for site medical response where heat- or cold-related illness is suspected."},{"id":"3.15.17","page":59,"heading":"Thermal Stress Assessment and Control","text":"High risk, lone or remote thermal work shall provide appropriate worker monitoring, reliable communication, emergency access and a means to summon assistance."},{"id":"3.15.18","page":60,"heading":"Vibration Assessment and Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify sources and tasks exposing people to hand-arm (HAV) or whole-body vibration (WBV)."},{"id":"3.15.19","page":60,"heading":"Vibration Assessment and Control","text":"Vibration exposure assessments shall consider the vibration characteristics, actual exposure duration, operating conditions and all significant sources during the work period, with daily exposure evaluated over an eight-hour reference period, A(8), in accordance with ISO 5349 for HAV and ISO 2631-1 for WBV."},{"id":"3.15.20","page":60,"heading":"Vibration Assessment and Control","text":"A documented vibration control plan shall be implemented where an applicable exposure action value is reached or exceeded, and exposure shall not exceed the applicable exposure limit value. Unless more stringent applicable legal requirements apply, the following criteria shall be used:\na. Hand-arm vibration exposure action value: 2.5 m/s² A(8).\nb. Hand-arm vibration exposure limit value: 5.0 m/s² A(8).\nc. Whole-body vibration exposure action value: 0.5 m/s² A(8).\nd. Whole-body vibration exposure limit value: 1.15 m/s² A(8)."},{"id":"3.15.21","page":60,"heading":"Vibration Assessment and Control","text":"Vibration exposure shall be eliminated or reduced using the hierarchy of controls, including equipment selection or substitution, isolation, damping, maintenance, surface and roadway improvement, vehicle or seating improvements, operating practices and exposure duration controls. Equipment with defective, missing or ineffective vibration controls shall be removed from use unless verified interim controls prevent unacceptable exposure."},{"id":"3.15.22","page":60,"heading":"Vibration Assessment and Control","text":"Design, procurement, hire, inspection and maintenance of vibration generating equipment, tools and vehicles shall apply defined vibration performance and acceptance criteria. Where manufacturer vibration data is used for exposure assessment, its applicability to actual Maaden operating conditions shall be technically verified."},{"id":"3.15.23","page":60,"heading":"Vibration Assessment and Control","text":"Vibration controls and exposure conditions shall be reassessed following changes in equipment, vehicles, road or work surface conditions, work practices, exposure duration, reported symptoms or evidence that existing controls are no longer be effective."},{"id":"3.15.24","page":60,"heading":"Illumination Assessment and Control","text":"Workplace illumination shall meet task- and location-specific requirements established in accordance with applicable Saudi requirements and ISO/CIE 8995. The minimum maintained illuminance values specified below shall apply unless a higher level is required by an applicable obligation, task assessment or specific visual condition.\na. Access and movement areas – 100 lux: walkways, corridors, stairs, circulation routes and areas primarily used for movement.\nb. General operational areas – 200 lux: plant areas, equipment rooms, warehouses and general operating locations where limited visual detail is required.\nc. Routine work and monitoring – 300 lux: routine operational checks, surveillance or monitoring activities, reading clear displays and similar activities.\nd. Detailed work – 500 lux: maintenance activities, instrument or panel reading, normal inspection, workshops, reading, writing and similar detailed tasks.\ne. Fine or precision work – 750 to 1,000 lux: fine inspection, precision activities or tasks involving small or low- contrast detail, unless a task-specific assessment determines a higher requirement. Where a task does not clearly fall within these categories or requires specialized visual performance, the required illumination shall be determined using ISO/CIE 8995 standard and task-specific assessment."},{"id":"3.15.25","page":61,"heading":"Illumination Assessment and Control","text":"Illumination assessments shall consider the task and operating conditions, illuminance at the relevant work or travel surface, lighting type, contrast, shadows, reflections, glare, transitions between lighting levels, color recognition, emergency routes and foreseeable deterioration, with measurements conducted where compliance has not been verified, conditions have changed, lighting contributed to an incident or task error, or visual difficulty has been reported."},{"id":"3.15.26","page":61,"heading":"Illumination Assessment and Control","text":"Lighting deficiencies affecting safe work, access, vehicle interaction or emergency egress shall be corrected before affected activities continue, unless verified interim controls provide an acceptable level of safety, with areas remaining restricted until suitable illumination is restored."},{"id":"3.15.27","page":61,"heading":"Illumination Assessment and Control","text":"Lighting systems shall be inspected and maintained, and their performance verified after installation, modification or repair and thereafter at intervals based on deterioration, contamination, environmental conditions, task criticality and operating experience."},{"id":"3.15.28","page":61,"heading":"Information, Health and Worker Participation","text":"Affected people shall be informed of applicable physical hazards, exposure criteria, controls, work restrictions, reportable symptoms, stop-work conditions and emergency actions, and consulted on exposure scenarios and the practicality and effectiveness of controls."},{"id":"3.15.29","page":61,"heading":"Information, Health and Worker Participation","text":"Personnel requiring medical evaluation, health surveillance, fitness for work assessment or work restrictions following physical agent exposure shall be referred through the Medical Services and Health Surveillance process. Only necessary work restrictions and de-identified health information shall be communicated to relevant functions, with findings indicating possible control failure used to review the applicable exposure assessment and controls."},{"id":"3.15.30","page":61,"heading":"Design, Change and Monitoring","text":"Projects, designs, modifications and procurement shall identify foreseeable thermal stress, vibration and illumination exposures and incorporate higher order controls and defined performance criteria before approval or acquisition."},{"id":"3.15.31","page":61,"heading":"Design, Change and Monitoring","text":"Changes that could affect physical agent exposure shall be managed in accordance with Section 3.52 Management of Change, with required controls and monitoring verified through applicable project, commissioning or pre-startup reviews before startup, return to service or exposure."},{"id":"3.15.32","page":61,"heading":"Design, Change and Monitoring","text":"Risk-based verification shall confirm that physical hazard controls remain effective, applicable exposure criteria are met and affected people understand required controls and actions. Deficiencies shall be corrected or escalated in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management."},{"id":"3.15.33","page":61,"heading":"Design, Change and Monitoring","text":"Exposure limit or trigger-level exceedances shall result in immediate controls, communication to affected personnel and supervisors, and management of related incidents, suspected occupational illnesses and other reportable events in accordance with Section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Thermal Stress and Physical Hazards; heat stress, cold, vibration, illumination; Assessment Governance; Physical Agent Identification and Exposure Assessment; Exposure Control; Thermal Stress Assessment and Control; Vibration Assessment and Control; Illumination Assessment and Control; Information, Health and Worker Participation; Design, Change, Monitoring and Improvement","trigger":"if heat, cold, vibration, illumination or other physical agents may affect personnel","refs":["2.5","3.52","6.1"]},{"id":"3.16","title":"Radiation Management","page":62,"purpose":"Radiation sources and exposures are identified, authorized, controlled, monitored and managed throughout their lifecycle to protect workers, the public and the environment, prevent loss of control, and meet applicable regulatory requirements.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions whose activities, facilities, equipment, materials or contracted services involve, or could result in exposure to, ionizing radiation or radioactive material. This includes radiation sources and radiation producing equipment, naturally occurring radioactive material (NORM), technologically enhanced naturally occurring radioactive material (TENORM), radioactive contamination and waste, materials with credible radiological potential, contracted radiation services, and radioactive or nuclear materials subject to applicable regulatory controls. It applies to employees, contractors, visitors, outside workers and members of the public who could be affected by Maaden controlled activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.16.1","page":62,"heading":"Governance and Authorization","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify radiation sources, facilities and activities subject to regulatory control, determine the applicable Nuclear and Radiological Regulatory Commission requirements, and obtain and maintain the required authorization, permit, approval or exemption before commencement and throughout the applicable lifecycle."},{"id":"3.16.2","page":62,"heading":"Governance and Authorization","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall remain accountable for radiation protection, source security and regulatory compliance, including activities performed by contractors."},{"id":"3.16.3","page":62,"heading":"Governance and Authorization","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain a risk proportionate Radiation Protection Program covering applicable responsibilities, authorizations, source control and security, exposure management, monitoring, source integrity, radioactive waste management, emergency preparedness and response, competency, incident management, reporting and records."},{"id":"3.16.4","page":62,"heading":"Governance and Authorization","text":"Where required by regulation or authorization conditions, an NRRC-certified Radiation Safety Officer shall be appointed with documented responsibilities and authority, including liaison with regulators and the authority to stop unsafe or unauthorized radiation activities."},{"id":"3.16.5","page":62,"heading":"Governance and Authorization","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall comply with applicable NRRC safeguards requirements for nuclear material and uranium- or thorium-bearing ore."},{"id":"3.16.6","page":62,"heading":"Source Inventory and Lifecycle Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain a current, controlled and periodically verified inventory of radiation sources and generators, with their acquisition, movement, transfer, use and disposal authorized, documented and traceable throughout the lifecycle in accordance with NRRC requirements."},{"id":"3.16.7","page":62,"heading":"Source Inventory and Lifecycle Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish authorized arrangements for the return, transfer or disposal of radioactive sources at end of life and manage changes affecting radiation sources, facilities, activities or controls through the section 3.52 Management of Change process, obtaining required NRRC approvals before implementation."},{"id":"3.16.8","page":63,"heading":"Radiation Risk Assessment and Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess and manage radiation risks in accordance with Section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management before introducing or modifying radiation sources, facilities, activities, materials or services and following changes, including risks from radon, contamination, NORM and TENORM."},{"id":"3.16.9","page":63,"heading":"Radiation Risk Assessment and Control","text":"Radiation risk assessments and controls shall address applicable exposures, affected people, materials, operating conditions, security considerations and lifecycle stages; apply the principles of justification, dose limitation and optimization; and prioritize elimination and engineering controls over administrative controls and personal protective equipment. Assessments shall be performed or approved by a qualified person."},{"id":"3.16.10","page":63,"heading":"Dose Constraints and Dose Limits","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish dose constraints, operational limits, investigation levels and work controls, where required, to support dose optimization and prevent exceedance of applicable dose limits in accordance with NRRC requirements.\n[Table table-63-1.png]\n\nTable 3.16-1. NRRC-R-01 dose limits for planned exposure situations."},{"id":"3.16.11","page":63,"heading":"Dose Constraints and Dose Limits","text":"For declared pregnancy or breastfeeding, the embryo, fetus or infant dose shall not exceed 1 mSv."},{"id":"3.16.12","page":63,"heading":"Dose Constraints and Dose Limits","text":"People under 16 years of age shall not be occupationally exposed to ionizing radiation. People under 18 years of age can enter controlled areas only under supervision for approved training or study."},{"id":"3.16.13","page":63,"heading":"Dose Constraints and Dose Limits","text":"Dose compliance assessments shall include external exposure and committed intake dose using NRRC specified criteria, factors and reference values."},{"id":"3.16.14","page":63,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Commissioning","text":"New or modified radiation facilities, equipment and installations shall incorporate radiological protection, security, maintainability and failure prevention requirements."},{"id":"3.16.15","page":63,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Commissioning","text":"Radiation sources, equipment and associated services shall be procured from authorized suppliers and shall meet applicable technical, quality, safety, security and lifecycle management requirements, including end of life arrangements where applicable."},{"id":"3.16.16","page":63,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Commissioning","text":"New, relocated or significantly modified radiation sources, equipment and installations shall be tested and verified before operation or return to service, including verification of radiological conditions, area classification and affected safety functions, as applicable."},{"id":"3.16.17","page":64,"heading":"Area Classification, Access and Work Control","text":"Areas where radiological hazards exist shall be classified, documented and maintained as controlled or supervised areas based on radiological risk and applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.16.18","page":64,"heading":"Area Classification, Access and Work Control","text":"Controlled and supervised areas shall have defined boundaries, signage, access controls, monitoring arrangements and other protective measures commensurate with the level of radiological risk."},{"id":"3.16.19","page":64,"heading":"Area Classification, Access and Work Control","text":"Area classifications and associated controls shall be reviewed periodically and following changes that affect radiological conditions or exposure risks."},{"id":"3.16.20","page":64,"heading":"Area Classification, Access and Work Control","text":"Radiation work shall be performed only by authorized and competent personnel under documented work authorization and controls proportionate to the radiological risks, including for temporary and field activities where applicable."},{"id":"3.16.21","page":64,"heading":"Workplace and Individual Monitoring","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain risk-based workplace and individual radiation monitoring arrangements that define the objectives, methods, frequencies, action levels and records required by the NRRC and applicable authorization conditions."},{"id":"3.16.22","page":64,"heading":"Workplace and Individual Monitoring","text":"Radiological surveys and monitoring shall be conducted before initial operation and following changes that affect radiological conditions, occupational exposure or control effectiveness, using workplace monitoring, individual monitoring or a combination of both based on radiological risk and applicable NRRC requirements."},{"id":"3.16.23","page":64,"heading":"Workplace and Individual Monitoring","text":"Workers shall be subject to individual dose monitoring where required by the NRRC or applicable authorization conditions, using NRRC recognized dosimetry services and methods commensurate with the nature, magnitude and pathways of potential exposure. Where individual monitoring is not feasible, dose assessment shall use methods approved by the NRRC and representative workplace or worker exposure information."},{"id":"3.16.24","page":64,"heading":"Workplace and Individual Monitoring","text":"Monitoring equipment shall be fit for purpose and maintained, calibrated or verified, with monitoring results and exposure records made available to affected workers and abnormal results, limit or action level exceedances and monitoring deficiencies investigated and corrected."},{"id":"3.16.25","page":64,"heading":"Public Exposure and Radiological Environmental Protection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess actual and potential public exposure from Maaden activities in accordance with applicable NRRC requirements, considering the representative person, relevant exposure pathways, foreseeable changes, future exposures and uncertainties, and demonstrate compliance with applicable dose limits and authorization conditions."},{"id":"3.16.26","page":64,"heading":"Public Exposure and Radiological Environmental Protection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement engineering, operational and access controls to protect the public from radiological hazards and ensure that visitors entering radiologically controlled areas receive applicable information, precautions, supervision and access control based on the radiological risk."},{"id":"3.16.27","page":64,"heading":"Public Exposure and Radiological Environmental Protection","text":"Source and environmental monitoring shall be implemented where required by the NRRC or the public-exposure assessment to verify radiological conditions and control effectiveness, with abnormal results, unexpected increases and actual or potential limit exceedances assessed, recorded and reported in accordance with applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.16.28","page":64,"heading":"Public Exposure and Radiological Environmental Protection","text":"New installations and significant modifications that could affect public exposure shall obtain required NRRC approval before commissioning or operation."},{"id":"3.16.29","page":65,"heading":"Health Surveillance and Special Protection","text":"Radiation workers shall participate in health surveillance and fitness assessments in accordance with NRRC requirements and applicable Occupational Health requirements."},{"id":"3.16.30","page":65,"heading":"Health Surveillance and Special Protection","text":"Suspected abnormal exposure, dose limit exceedance, pregnancy or breastfeeding considerations shall be managed through medical assessment and follow-up actions in accordance with NRRC requirements."},{"id":"3.16.31","page":65,"heading":"Health Surveillance and Special Protection","text":"An exposure investigation shall be initiated if an occupationally exposed individual receives, during a single dosimetry reporting period: an effective dose greater than 2 mSv, an equivalent dose to the lens of the eye greater than 1.5 mSv; or an equivalent dose to the skin, hands or feet greater than 40 mSv."},{"id":"3.16.32","page":65,"heading":"Source Integrity and Control Verification","text":"Radiation sources, equipment and critical controls shall be inspected, tested, maintained, calibrated and verified at defined frequencies and following acquisition, installation, relocation, maintenance or other activities that could affect integrity, with traceable records maintained."},{"id":"3.16.33","page":65,"heading":"Source Integrity and Control Verification","text":"Defective, damaged, leaking, compromised or missing radiation sources and failed critical radiation controls shall trigger immediate cessation of affected activities, source security, assessment, escalation and applicable regulatory notification until an authorized disposition or approved alternative controls are implemented."},{"id":"3.16.34","page":65,"heading":"Radioactive Material Security and Waste Management","text":"Radioactive sources and materials shall be protected throughout their lifecycle against unauthorized access, removal, loss, theft, damage, misuse, sabotage and other malicious acts through security controls proportionate to the associated risks and in accordance with applicable NRRC requirements."},{"id":"3.16.35","page":65,"heading":"Radioactive Material Security and Waste Management","text":"Radioactive waste and disused sources shall be minimized and managed, stored, transferred, transported, released, discharged or disposed of only in accordance with applicable NRRC authorizations and requirements."},{"id":"3.16.36","page":65,"heading":"Radioactive Material Security and Waste Management","text":"Suspected orphaned, abandoned or uncontrolled radioactive material shall be isolated, secured, assessed and reported to the NRRC before further handling."},{"id":"3.16.37","page":65,"heading":"Competence and Radiation Work Coordination","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that personnel and contractors performing radiation related activities are appropriately authorized and competent for their assigned duties, with competence verified before independent work and maintained through applicable education, training, qualifications and experience."},{"id":"3.16.38","page":65,"heading":"Competence and Radiation Work Coordination","text":"Radiation work involving contractors or outside workers shall be planned and coordinated to define responsibilities and maintain applicable exposure controls, dose management and exposure records in accordance with Maaden and applicable legal, regulatory and permit requirements."},{"id":"3.16.39","page":65,"heading":"Radiation Emergency Preparedness and Response","text":"Radiation emergency scenarios shall be identified and addressed through applicable emergency preparedness and response arrangements, including response actions, responsibilities, communication, NRRC notification, medical support, radiation monitoring, security, recovery, testing and applicable external contacts."},{"id":"3.16.40","page":65,"heading":"Radiation Emergency Preparedness and Response","text":"Radiation events shall be promptly controlled, escalated, investigated and reported in accordance with applicable NRRC requirements and section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship process."},{"id":"3.16.41","page":66,"heading":"Radiation Emergency Preparedness and Response","text":"Emergency worker exposures shall be planned, controlled, assessed and recorded in accordance with NRRC-R-01, with exposure above 50 mSv limited to circumstances permitted by NRRC-R-01 and involving informed, trained and consenting volunteers, applicable medical evaluation and communication of dose information."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Radiation Management; radiation, source, gauge, NORM, TENORM, radon; Governance and Authorization; Source Inventory and Lifecycle Control; Radiation Risk Assessment and Control; Dose Constraints and Dose Limits; Design, Procurement and Commissioning; Area Classification, Access and Work Control; Workplace and Individual Monitoring; Public Exposure and Radiological Environmental Protection; Health Surveillance and Special Protection; Source Integrity and Control Verification; Radioactive Material Security and Waste Management; Competence and Radiation Work Coordination; Radiation Emergency Preparedness and Response","trigger":"if ionizing radiation, radiation equipment, sources, NORM/TENORM or radon is involved","refs":["2.5","3.52","6.1"]},{"id":"3.17","title":"Ergonomics and Work Design","page":66,"purpose":"Maaden work systems, tasks, equipment, workplaces and organizational work arrangements are designed, selected, operated and reviewed to eliminate or reduce ergonomic risks and prevent work-related musculoskeletal disorders and other adverse health effects arising from poor work design.","scope":"This process applies throughout relevant Maaden lifecycle stages to activities and workplaces where ergonomic risks can arise from work demands, work organization, workplace or equipment design, tool or vehicle use, vibration, or changes that can affect ergonomic exposure.","clauses":[{"id":"3.17.1","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomics Governance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain a process to identify, assess, control and review ergonomic risks associated with activities, roles and work environments that can cause work-related musculoskeletal disorders or other ergonomic related adverse health outcomes."},{"id":"3.17.2","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomics Governance","text":"Ergonomic risks shall be considered in applicable risk assessments, work and project design, operational planning and management of change, with changes assessed before implementation where they could introduce or alter ergonomic risks in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.17.3","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomics Governance","text":"Contractors and other people performing work on behalf of Maaden shall comply with the ergonomic risk management requirements applicable to their work."},{"id":"3.17.4","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomic Hazard Identification and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall conduct ergonomic assessment where identified risk factors, injury or illness information, personnel reports, occupational health findings, design reviews, workplace observations, risk screening or assurance findings indicate that ergonomic risk require further evaluation."},{"id":"3.17.5","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomic Hazard Identification and Risk Assessment","text":"Ergonomic assessments shall consider relevant task, exposure, equipment, environmental and individual factors, including force, frequency, duration, posture, manual handling, vibration and human-machine interaction."},{"id":"3.17.6","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomic Hazard Identification and Risk Assessment","text":"Ergonomic assessments shall be reviewed when changes, incidents, injuries, illnesses, occupational health findings, personnel reports, workplace observations or assurance findings indicate that the assessment, risk level or controls are no longer be valid or effective."},{"id":"3.17.7","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomic Hazard Identification and Risk Assessment","text":"Personnel performing or familiar with the assessed work shall be consulted during ergonomic assessments, workplace design reviews and the evaluation of ergonomic controls."},{"id":"3.17.8","page":66,"heading":"Ergonomic Design and Work Design Requirements","text":"Ergonomic requirements shall be incorporated into the design, selection, procurement and modification of workplaces, facilities, tools, equipment, workstations and vehicles before acquisition, installation or implementation."},{"id":"3.17.9","page":67,"heading":"Ergonomic Design and Work Design Requirements","text":"Work shall be designed or modified to eliminate or reduce ergonomic risks associated with manual handling, repetitive work, force, sustained or awkward postures, excessive reach and vibration."},{"id":"3.17.10","page":67,"heading":"Ergonomic Design and Work Design Requirements","text":"New facilities, projects and major modifications shall evaluate ergonomic risks sufficiently early in the project and engineering lifecycle to influence design, procurement and commissioning decisions and reduce lifecycle ergonomic risk."},{"id":"3.17.11","page":67,"heading":"Ergonomic Design and Work Design Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review and modify workload, staffing, task allocation and work-rest arrangements where they contribute to ergonomic risk."},{"id":"3.17.12","page":67,"heading":"Ergonomic Risk Control","text":"Ergonomic risks shall be eliminated or, where elimination is not reasonably practicable, reduced through the hierarchy of controls, giving priority to substitution, engineering and design solutions that control exposure at source and avoiding sole reliance on administrative controls where higher order controls are reasonably practicable."},{"id":"3.17.13","page":67,"heading":"Manual Handling and Physical Work Demands","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall screen manual handling activities and assess tasks involving potential musculoskeletal injury risks."},{"id":"3.17.14","page":67,"heading":"Manual Handling and Physical Work Demands","text":"Manual handling risks shall be eliminated or reduced through work design and the hierarchy of controls, including mechanical assistance, team handling and improvements to workplaces, storage, equipment, work processes and material flows."},{"id":"3.17.15","page":67,"heading":"Equipment, Tools, Vehicles and Workplace Design","text":"Workplaces, equipment and workstations shall be designed, selected or adjustable to accommodate the expected user population and applicable accessibility requirements."},{"id":"3.17.16","page":67,"heading":"Equipment, Tools, Vehicles and Workplace Design","text":"Tools and equipment shall be selected and maintained to minimize force requirements, awkward postures and hand-arm vibration exposure throughout their intended use."},{"id":"3.17.17","page":67,"heading":"Equipment, Tools, Vehicles and Workplace Design","text":"Mobile equipment and vehicle operator environments shall be designed and maintained to reduce ergonomic risks associated with posture, visibility, access and egress, seating, control layout and whole-body vibration."},{"id":"3.17.18","page":67,"heading":"Equipment, Tools, Vehicles and Workplace Design","text":"Control rooms, offices, laboratories and other fixed workstations shall be designed to support safe posture, visibility, reach, accessibility and effective human-machine interaction."},{"id":"3.17.19","page":67,"heading":"Reporting and Early Intervention","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate reports and health findings indicating potential work-related musculoskeletal or other ergonomic health effects, assess contributing workplace factors, and implement corrective actions."},{"id":"3.17.20","page":67,"heading":"Competency and Awareness","text":"Personnel exposed to ergonomic risks shall receive relevant information and training before starting work and following changes affecting the risks or controls."},{"id":"3.17.21","page":67,"heading":"Competency and Awareness","text":"Ergonomic assessments shall be conducted by personnel competent in the methods used."},{"id":"3.17.22","page":68,"heading":"Performance Monitoring and Continuous Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall monitor ergonomic performance using relevant assessment results, health and incident information, exposure data, personnel feedback, corrective actions and assurance findings, and use identified trends and lessons to improve ergonomic risk management."},{"id":"3.17.23","page":68,"heading":"Performance Monitoring and Continuous Improvement","text":"Ergonomic controls shall be verified after implementation and reviewed at risk-based intervals and following changes, incidents, health findings or other indications of reduced effectiveness, to confirm the intended risk reduction and identify any additional risks."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Ergonomics and Work Design; ergonomics, manual handling, repetitive, posture; Ergonomics Governance; Ergonomic Hazard Identification and Risk Assessment; Ergonomic Design and Work Design Requirements; Ergonomic Risk Control; Manual Handling and Physical Work Demands; Equipment, Tools, Vehicles and Workplace Design; Reporting and Early Intervention; Competency and Awareness; Performance Monitoring and Continuous Improvement","trigger":"if manual handling, repetitive work, force, posture or workstation design creates ergonomic risk","refs":["3.52"]},{"id":"3.18","title":"Fitness for Work, Fatigue and Wellbeing Management","page":68,"purpose":"Ensure workers are fit to safely perform assigned work, with fatigue, impairment and work-related psychosocial risks effectively managed to protect health, safety and wellbeing.","scope":"This process applies to all people working under Maaden’s operational control where being unfit for work, fatigue, impairment or work-related psychosocial risks could affect health, safety, the environment or operational integrity. Includes safety critical roles, physically or cognitively demanding work, and other roles or circumstances where fitness for work determination is required by applicable obligations or documented risk-based criteria.","clauses":[{"id":"3.18.1","page":68,"heading":"Governance and Responsibilities","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define responsibilities and authorities for managing fitness for work, fatigue, impairment and work-related psychosocial risks, including authority to apply interim controls and stop, restrict or reassign work, with clinical fitness determinations made by the authorized medical function."},{"id":"3.18.2","page":68,"heading":"Governance and Responsibilities","text":"People assigning or supervising work shall ensure assigned work is consistent with the worker’s current fitness status and functional restrictions."},{"id":"3.18.3","page":68,"heading":"Governance and Responsibilities","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide workers with access to confidential methods for reporting fatigue, impairment, fitness for work or wellbeing concerns and shall not discourage good faith reporting."},{"id":"3.18.4","page":68,"heading":"Governance and Responsibilities","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall consult affected workers when assessing fatigue and work-related psychosocial risks and reviewing related controls."},{"id":"3.18.5","page":68,"heading":"Safety Critical Work and Fitness Criteria","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify safety critical roles and tasks where reduced fitness could contribute to fatality, serious injury, significant environmental impact, loss of operational control or failure of emergency response."},{"id":"3.18.6","page":68,"heading":"Safety Critical Work and Fitness Criteria","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define fitness criteria for safety critical roles and tasks based on the physical, sensory, psychological and cognitive capabilities required for safe performance and provide the authorized medical function with current information on relevant work demands, hazards, exposures and operating conditions."},{"id":"3.18.7","page":68,"heading":"Safety Critical Work and Fitness Criteria","text":"Safety critical roles, tasks and fitness criteria shall be reviewed following changes that could affect fitness requirements or applicable obligations."},{"id":"3.18.8","page":69,"heading":"Fitness Assessment and Work Status","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that workers requiring formal fitness determination are assessed through medical services established under section 3.19 Medical Services and Health Surveillance."},{"id":"3.18.9","page":69,"heading":"Fitness Assessment and Work Status","text":"Fitness determinations shall specify the worker’s fitness status, applicable work restrictions and review date, where required, and be communicated to the worker and relevant persons responsible for assigning or controlling work without disclosing diagnoses or clinical records unless authorized by applicable legal and Maaden privacy requirements."},{"id":"3.18.10","page":69,"heading":"Fitness Assessment and Work Status","text":"Workers shall be assigned only to work consistently with their current fitness status and restrictions, with restricted work arrangements reviewed by the specified date and following changes affecting the worker’s ability to perform the assigned work. A worker assessed as Not Fit can resume affected work only after a revised fitness determination permits it."},{"id":"3.18.11","page":69,"heading":"Fitness Assessment and Work Status","text":"Contractors shall verify that their personnel meet applicable fitness requirements before assignment and provide Maaden with only the fitness status and restrictions necessary to verify compliance."},{"id":"3.18.12","page":69,"heading":"Fitness for Work and Impairment Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define triggers, responsibilities and actions for responding to reported or observed potential impairment, including immediate removal from affected safety critical work and control of associated driving or travel risks until an authorized assessment permits the work or suitable alternative duties are assigned."},{"id":"3.18.13","page":69,"heading":"Fitness for Work and Impairment Controls","text":"Potential impairment and fitness determinations shall be managed with respect for safety, dignity, privacy and medical confidentiality and consider fatigue, acute illness or injury, medication, alcohol or other substances, and physical, psychological or cognitive factors affecting safe performance."},{"id":"3.18.14","page":69,"heading":"Fitness for Work and Impairment Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide workers with confidential access to authorized medical advice where medication could affect safe performance, requiring workers to seek advice before performing affected safety critical work and preventing supervisors from diagnosing medical conditions or advising changes to medication."},{"id":"3.18.15","page":69,"heading":"Fitness for Work and Impairment Controls","text":"Alcohol and other drug controls shall comply with applicable Saudi legal and regulatory obligations and approved Maaden Human Resources, privacy and medical requirements."},{"id":"3.18.16","page":69,"heading":"Fatigue Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess fatigue risks arising from work arrangements, task demands, operating conditions, travel and accommodation, including before changes that could affect alertness, sleep opportunity, recovery or safe performance."},{"id":"3.18.17","page":69,"heading":"Fatigue Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish risk-based work-hour, roster, rest and recovery requirements that comply with the Saudi Labor Law and implementing requirements, define fatigue escalation and stop-work criteria, and prioritize work design, staffing, scheduling, engineering and organizational controls over reliance on worker self-management or training."},{"id":"3.18.18","page":69,"heading":"Fatigue Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"Overtime, call-outs, shutdowns, turnarounds and other extended or unplanned work shall be assessed and controlled, with safety critical work reassigned, restricted or stopped where fatigue risk remains uncontrolled and necessary rest, recovery or safe transport provided."},{"id":"3.18.19","page":70,"heading":"Fatigue Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"Contractor fatigue arrangements and exceptions to work-hour, roster, rest or recovery requirements shall be risk- assessed, documented, approved by the designated authority, time-limited and monitored, without overriding applicable Saudi Labor Law, other legal requirements or a worker’s fitness status."},{"id":"3.18.20","page":70,"heading":"Wellbeing and Psychosocial Risk Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and assess work-related psychosocial hazards, considering work design and workload, role clarity, organizational change, remote or isolated work, critical or traumatic events, harmful interpersonal conduct, organizational support and fatigue-related work arrangements."},{"id":"3.18.21","page":70,"heading":"Wellbeing and Psychosocial Risk Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall manage work-related psychosocial risks through work design, work organization, leadership practices and operational controls, without relying primarily on individual awareness, resilience or wellbeing initiatives."},{"id":"3.18.22","page":70,"heading":"Wellbeing and Psychosocial Risk Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide workers with confidential health and wellbeing support and implement needs-based initiatives, with participation and health information protected and effectiveness evaluated using worker feedback and aggregated information."},{"id":"3.18.23","page":70,"heading":"Wellbeing and Psychosocial Risk Controls","text":"Workers exposed to critical or traumatic events shall be offered confidential support and, where determined by the authorized medical function, have their fitness reviewed before returning to safety critical work."},{"id":"3.18.24","page":70,"heading":"Wellbeing and Psychosocial Risk Controls","text":"Contractors shall assess and manage work-related psychosocial risks affecting their personnel and subcontractors and provide confidential health and wellbeing support consistent with applicable Maaden and contractual requirements."},{"id":"3.18.25","page":70,"heading":"Competence, Awareness and Communication","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that workers exposed to fatigue or impairment risks, or subject to fitness requirements, can recognize and report concerns that could affect safe performance and apply the controls relevant to their work."},{"id":"3.18.26","page":70,"heading":"Competence, Awareness and Communication","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that supervisors and other people with work-control authority can respond to reported or observed concerns, apply required interim controls and escalate matters to the authorized medical or management function."},{"id":"3.18.27","page":70,"heading":"Competence, Awareness and Communication","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that people whose planning or scheduling decisions could affect fatigue risk can assess and control that risk within their authority."},{"id":"3.18.28","page":70,"heading":"Monitoring and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall monitor and document the implementation and effectiveness of fitness for work, fatigue, psychosocial risk and wellbeing arrangements at defined risk-based intervals, considering compliance, approved exceptions, interventions, contractor conformance, worker feedback, field verification findings, and relevant incident and health trends."},{"id":"3.18.29","page":70,"heading":"Monitoring and Improvement","text":"Incident investigations shall consider fatigue, reduced fitness for work and other impairment as possible causal or contributing factors where relevant to the event."},{"id":"3.18.30","page":70,"heading":"Monitoring and Improvement","text":"Monitoring and reporting information shall be aggregated or de-identified unless individual identification is necessary and authorized for a defined purpose, with identifiable information limited and handled in accordance with applicable obligations and Maaden privacy requirements."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Fitness for Work, Fatigue and Wellbeing Management; fatigue, fitness for work, impairment, wellbeing; Governance and Responsibilities; Safety-Critical Work and Fitness Criteria; Fitness Assessment and Work Status; Fitness-for-Work and Impairment Controls; Fatigue Risk Assessment and Controls; Wellbeing and Psychosocial Risk Controls; Competence, Awareness and Communication; Monitoring and Improvement","trigger":"if fitness, fatigue, impairment, shift work or worker wellbeing may affect safe work","refs":["3.19"]},{"id":"3.19","title":"Medical Services and Health Surveillance","page":71,"purpose":"Define the requirements for risk-based medical services and health surveillance to protect worker health, support medical and emergency response, identify potentially work-related health effects, evaluate exposure control effectiveness and protect confidential medical information.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions requiring medical services or health surveillance based on occupational health risks, workforce needs, operating conditions, emergency scenarios or applicable obligations. It covers medical needs assessment, emergency medical care at Maaden workplaces, determination of service levels and coverage, occupational health surveillance, and protection of confidential medical information.","clauses":[{"id":"3.19.1","page":71,"heading":"Medical Services Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assign accountability for medical services and health surveillance."},{"id":"3.19.2","page":71,"heading":"Medical Services Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall complete and maintain a needs assessment for medical services, health surveillance and medical emergency response before starting applicable activities or operations and following changes that could affect these needs, considering:\na. workforce and contractor characteristics;\nb. occupational health and communicable disease risks;\nc. credible medical emergencies;\nd. location, remoteness and response times;\ne. managed accommodation;\nf. external healthcare and public health capabilities;\ng. operating experience; and\nh. applicable obligations"},{"id":"3.19.3","page":71,"heading":"Medical Services Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define the required medical services, health-surveillance programs, coverage, resources, emergency arrangements, patient referral and transport, contingencies, readiness verification, and coordination with relevant internal and external services based on the needs assessment."},{"id":"3.19.4","page":71,"heading":"Medical Service Capability and Access","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide access to medical services with the availability, coverage and capability determined by the needs assessment."},{"id":"3.19.5","page":71,"heading":"Medical Service Capability and Access","text":"Medical services shall assess, treat and, where required, stabilize occupational injuries and acute illnesses within their authorized clinical scope, and arrange referral, transport and clinical handover when required care exceeds available capability."},{"id":"3.19.6","page":71,"heading":"Medical Service Capability and Access","text":"Medical services shall provide or support applicable occupational examinations, health surveillance and work- related case management, and contribute medical expertise to emergency planning and exercises."},{"id":"3.19.7","page":71,"heading":"Medical Service Capability and Access","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify the authorization, capability, availability and response times of external medical providers and establish arrangements for their activation, patient referral and transfer."},{"id":"3.19.8","page":72,"heading":"Medical Service Capability and Access","text":"Remote or access constrained operations shall establish medical first-response, communication, stabilization, patient transport and escalation arrangements based on the needs assessment."},{"id":"3.19.9","page":72,"heading":"Medical Service Capability and Access","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall inform personnel how to obtain medical assistance and report occupational injuries, illnesses and exposure concerns through applicable reporting arrangements."},{"id":"3.19.10","page":72,"heading":"Medical Facilities, Personnel and Service Providers","text":"Medical facilities and clinical work areas shall be authorized, equipped, maintained and operated within their approved service scope by personnel who hold the qualifications, licenses, registrations, clinical privileges and competencies required under applicable MOH regulations for their assigned duties and credible medical emergencies."},{"id":"3.19.11","page":72,"heading":"Medical Facilities, Personnel and Service Providers","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall manage contracted medical service providers through the Contractor Management process and verify required provider and personnel authorizations before service delivery and before their expiry."},{"id":"3.19.12","page":72,"heading":"Medical Facilities, Personnel and Service Providers","text":"Medical services shall maintain clinical governance and quality assurance arrangements that define clinical decisions, referral, escalation, emergency response, health surveillance and medical information authorities and address clinical protocols, patient safety, diagnostic and laboratory quality, adverse events, complaints, provider performance and corrective actions."},{"id":"3.19.13","page":72,"heading":"Medical Facilities, Personnel and Service Providers","text":"Medical equipment, medicines, consumables and patient transport resources shall be available, secured where required, maintained and controlled for functionality, authorization, inspection, testing, calibration, storage conditions, expiry, damage and recall, with authorized contingencies established for unavailable or defective medical-emergency resources and affected work stopped unless the contingency meets the needs-assessment criteria."},{"id":"3.19.14","page":72,"heading":"Medical Facilities, Personnel and Service Providers","text":"Medical facilities shall control infection risks, segregate medical waste at the point of generation, and manage medical waste in accordance with applicable obligations and section 3.6 Waste Management process."},{"id":"3.19.15","page":72,"heading":"Health Surveillance Program","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish health surveillance where required by applicable obligations or indicated by occupational health risk or exposure assessments, work-related symptoms, suspected occupational illness, exposure control failure or an occupational exposure event."},{"id":"3.19.16","page":72,"heading":"Health Surveillance Program","text":"Health-surveillance protocols shall be linked to identified hazards, relevant exposures and applicable Similar Exposure Groups and use exposure information to define worker inclusion, assessment timing, result interpretation, follow-up, delayed health effects and hazard specific obligations."},{"id":"3.19.17","page":72,"heading":"Health Surveillance Program","text":"An authorized occupational health physician shall approve a protocol for each applicable hazard or exposure group that defines covered people and inclusion criteria; baseline, periodic and exit assessments, including applicable tests and frequencies; authorized providers and laboratories; interpretation and communication of results; clinical follow- up and referral; and work restrictions and return-to-exposure criteria."},{"id":"3.19.18","page":72,"heading":"Health Surveillance Program","text":"Health surveillance shall be implemented in accordance with the approved protocol and applicable obligations and not replace hazard elimination or exposure controls."},{"id":"3.19.19","page":72,"heading":"Health Surveillance Program","text":"Workers included in health surveillance shall be informed of the relevant hazard, surveillance purpose and requirements, confidentiality protections, results and required follow-up."},{"id":"3.19.20","page":73,"heading":"Health Surveillance Program","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define responsibilities for contractor health surveillance, maintain current surveillance coverage and completion status, review surveillance requirements following changes to hazards, exposures or applicable obligations, and prevent or restrict exposure when required surveillance is incomplete."},{"id":"3.19.21","page":73,"heading":"Management of Health Surveillance Findings","text":"An authorized occupational health physician shall interpret health surveillance results and obtain specialist occupational medicine advice where required."},{"id":"3.19.22","page":73,"heading":"Management of Health Surveillance Findings","text":"Workers shall be informed of their health surveillance results, their significance and required follow-up."},{"id":"3.19.23","page":73,"heading":"Management of Health Surveillance Findings","text":"When results are abnormal or indicate a potentially work-related health effect, the applicable Business Unit or Corporate Function shall arrange clinical follow-up, implement required work restrictions, review relevant exposures and controls, assess the need for surveillance of similarly exposed workers, and complete applicable internal and regulatory reporting."},{"id":"3.19.24","page":73,"heading":"Management of Health Surveillance Findings","text":"Medical recommendations provided to management shall state work restrictions, review conditions and reassessment requirements without disclosing the diagnosis or other medical information not required to control work-related risk. Further medical information shall be disclosed only when authorized or required by applicable obligations."},{"id":"3.19.25","page":73,"heading":"Management of Health Surveillance Findings","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall analyze de-identified health-surveillance and exposure data, identify trends, implement and verify preventive actions, and provide workers with a process to review or appeal health- surveillance findings, work restrictions and return to exposure decisions."},{"id":"3.19.26","page":73,"heading":"Medical Emergency Interface","text":"Medical response arrangements shall be integrated with the applicable emergency response plan and credible emergency scenarios."},{"id":"3.19.27","page":73,"heading":"Medical Emergency Interface","text":"Medical response arrangements shall address activation, communication, escalation, triage, stabilization, casualty collection, patient transport and evacuation, clinical handover, remote medical advice, specialist consultation, receiving facility coordination, alternative referral and transport arrangements and multiple-casualty contingencies, as applicable to the needs assessment."},{"id":"3.19.28","page":73,"heading":"Medical Emergency Interface","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall test medical response arrangements through exercises and following changes that can affect medical response capability, evaluate findings and track resulting actions to verified closure."},{"id":"3.19.29","page":73,"heading":"Medical Emergency Interface","text":"Responders shall have access to the emergency medical information and resources required for their roles while protecting personal medical information from unnecessary disclosure."},{"id":"3.19.30","page":73,"heading":"Medical Information, Records and Reporting","text":"Medical and occupational health records shall be accurate, traceable, protected from unauthorized access or alteration, and include relevant work and exposure history, surveillance results, clinical recommendations, work restrictions, and the identity and assessment date of the authorized healthcare professional."},{"id":"3.19.31","page":73,"heading":"Medical Information, Records and Reporting","text":"Medical information shall be collected, accessed, used, disclosed and transferred securely in accordance with applicable privacy, consent and legal requirements."},{"id":"3.19.32","page":74,"heading":"Medical Information, Records and Reporting","text":"Medical and occupational health records shall be retained for the longest period required by applicable obligations, exposure specific requirements, and, for radiation exposed workers, applicable NRRC regulations and authorization conditions, with authorized disposal preventing unauthorized access or recovery."},{"id":"3.19.33","page":74,"heading":"Medical Information, Records and Reporting","text":"Workers shall have access to their medical information as legally permitted, while management reporting shall use aggregated or de-identified information unless identifiable information is legally required or necessary to implement a work restriction."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Medical Services and Health Surveillance; medical services and health surveillance; Medical Services Governance and Planning; Medical Service Capability and Access; Medical Facilities, Personnel and Service Providers; Health Surveillance Program; Management of Health Surveillance Findings; Medical Emergency Interface; Medical information, records and reporting","trigger":"if medical services, emergency medical support or exposure-based health surveillance is required","refs":["3.6"]},{"id":"3.20","title":"Hazardous Material Management","page":74,"purpose":"Hazardous materials are identified, approved and controlled throughout their lifecycle to prevent harmful exposure, fire, explosion, incompatible reactions, loss of containment and environmental release.","scope":"This process applies to hazardous materials that Maaden procures, introduces, generates, stores, handles, uses or transfers, including raw materials, products, process intermediates, by-products and residual materials. Explosives, radioactive materials, hazardous waste and the transportation of dangerous goods are governed by their respective MSHEM processes. Relevant hazardous material requirements continue to apply where these materials or activities interface with this process.","clauses":[{"id":"3.20.1","page":74,"heading":"Hazardous Material Governance and Lifecycle Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assign accountability and authority for hazardous material management throughout the applicable lifecycle and coordinate controls at interfaces with related MSHEM processes."},{"id":"3.20.2","page":74,"heading":"Hazardous Material Governance and Lifecycle Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine and control hazardous materials subject to regulatory thresholds, approvals, notifications, declarations or reporting requirements and ensure required submissions are complete, accurate and made within the applicable timeframes."},{"id":"3.20.3","page":74,"heading":"Hazardous Material Governance and Lifecycle Control","text":"Hazardous materials shall be introduced, generated, stored, handled, used, transferred, discontinued and removed only when authorized and when the applicable lifecycle controls have been established."},{"id":"3.20.4","page":74,"heading":"Identification, Classification and Hazard Information","text":"Hazardous materials, including process intermediates and by-products, shall be identified and classified for applicable physical, health and environmental hazards using applicable classification requirements and documented, traceable information. The classification shall be reviewed when the material’s composition, properties or hazard information changes."},{"id":"3.20.5","page":74,"heading":"Identification, Classification and Hazard Information","text":"Material whose identity or hazards cannot be reliably confirmed shall be secured against use, transfer or unintended interaction and treated as hazardous until its identity, hazards and classification are established through documented technical evaluation, supported by testing or analysis where available information is insufficient."},{"id":"3.20.6","page":74,"heading":"Approval and Introduction of Hazardous Materials","text":"Hazardous materials shall be evaluated and approved before first procurement, introduction to a site, generation by a new or modified process, or use for a new or modified purpose."},{"id":"3.20.7","page":75,"heading":"Approval and Introduction of Hazardous Materials","text":"The evaluation shall address intended use, quantity, location, restrictions, hazards, elimination or substitution, exposure and release pathways, lifecycle controls, competency and applicable process safety and change management interfaces."},{"id":"3.20.8","page":75,"heading":"Approval and Introduction of Hazardous Materials","text":"Hazardous materials shall not be accepted for storage or use unless their identity, hazards and controls are established, approval and receipt requirements are verified, and a current compliant safety data sheet is available."},{"id":"3.20.9","page":75,"heading":"Approval and Introduction of Hazardous Materials","text":"Prohibited materials shall not be procured, introduced or used unless legally authorized and formally approved by Maaden. Temporary, trial, sample, research and vendor supplied materials shall have risk-based approval defining quantity, location, duration, accountability and disposition."},{"id":"3.20.10","page":75,"heading":"Approval and Introduction of Hazardous Materials","text":"Damaged, leaking, rejected, unidentified or otherwise nonconforming hazardous-material deliveries shall be isolated and controlled pending safe return, transfer or disposition."},{"id":"3.20.11","page":75,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Operational Control","text":"People who handle hazardous materials shall be competent and, where required, authorized for their assigned activities."},{"id":"3.20.12","page":75,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Operational Control","text":"Hazardous material risks shall be assessed before handling or use and reassessed following a relevant change, new hazard information, an incident or evidence that a control is ineffective."},{"id":"3.20.13","page":75,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Operational Control","text":"The assessment shall consider, as applicable, the material hazards, incompatibilities and potential hazardous reactions or decomposition; quantity, concentration and physical form; operating conditions and states; potential exposure and release pathways; people, the environment and assets that may be affected; and credible failures and their consequences."},{"id":"3.20.14","page":75,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Operational Control","text":"Controls shall be selected and implemented in accordance with the hierarchy of controls, prioritizing elimination, substitution, inherently safer design and engineering controls. Controls identified as critical under section 2.6 Critical Control Management shall be managed in accordance with that process."},{"id":"3.20.15","page":75,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Operational Control","text":"Hazardous material activities shall not begin or continue unless the applicable hazards, operating conditions and limits have been assessed and the required controls are available and effective."},{"id":"3.20.16","page":75,"heading":"Inventory, Safety Data Sheets and Hazard Communication","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain an accurate, current and traceable hazardous material inventory identifying each material’s identity, classification, source, location, quantity, Safety Data Sheet, restriction status and responsible area, with discrepancies investigated and corrected or controlled."},{"id":"3.20.17","page":75,"heading":"Inventory, Safety Data Sheets and Hazard Communication","text":"Current Safety Data Sheets and control information shall be available at or near hazardous material storage and use areas and accessible to personnel and emergency responders, including during electronic system outages, with obsolete information removed from use and retained where required."},{"id":"3.20.18","page":75,"heading":"Inventory, Safety Data Sheets and Hazard Communication","text":"Containers, tanks, vessels, pipelines and hazardous material storage areas shall be legibly labelled to identify their contents and hazards, using material identification and NFPA 704 markings for tanks and vessels and ASME A13.1 identification for pipelines, including direction of flow."},{"id":"3.20.19","page":75,"heading":"Inventory, Safety Data Sheets and Hazard Communication","text":"Hazard information shall be communicated to affected personnel in an understandable form and language and updated following changes affecting Safety Data Sheets, inventories, labels, risk assessments or controls."},{"id":"3.20.20","page":76,"heading":"Storage, Containment and Transfer","text":"Hazardous material storage and transfer systems shall be compatible with their contents and operated to prevent loss of containment, incompatible interaction, fire, explosion, exposure and environmental release. Materials shall be segregated and maintained within defined storage conditions, with access for inspection and emergency response."},{"id":"3.20.21","page":76,"heading":"Storage, Containment and Transfer","text":"Secondary containment, leak detection, overfill protection and drainage isolation shall be provided where required by applicable obligations, permits, approved design criteria or risk assessment. Containment shall be compatible with the materials present, sized for the credible release and maintained with sufficient available capacity in accordance with SAF-08 requirements."},{"id":"3.20.22","page":76,"heading":"Storage, Containment and Transfer","text":"Hazardous material transfers shall begin only after the material, source, destination, available receiving capacity, transfer route, connections, containment and emergency arrangements are verified, with engineered controls or independent verification used where transfer errors could result in serious consequences."},{"id":"3.20.23","page":76,"heading":"Storage, Containment and Transfer","text":"Ignition sources shall be controlled where hazardous materials could create a fire or explosion risk, and containers shall remain closed when not in use unless equivalent protection is provided."},{"id":"3.20.24","page":76,"heading":"Storage, Containment and Transfer","text":"Temporary hazardous material storage shall meet the applicable control requirements for permanent storage."},{"id":"3.20.25","page":76,"heading":"Contractors, Transportation and Lifecycle Disposition","text":"Contractors and other external parties shall disclose hazardous materials before site introduction and obtain the approvals required by this process."},{"id":"3.20.26","page":76,"heading":"Contractors, Transportation and Lifecycle Disposition","text":"Current hazard, handling and emergency information shall be provided to parties receiving hazardous materials from Maaden. Transportation classified as dangerous goods shall comply with section 3.43 Transportation of Dangerous Goods and Materials process."},{"id":"3.20.27","page":76,"heading":"Contractors, Transportation and Lifecycle Disposition","text":"Obsolete, expired, deteriorated, surplus or prohibited hazardous materials, and containers or equipment with residual hazardous material contamination, shall remain identified and controlled until approved for reuse, returned to the supplier, decontaminated or managed in accordance with section 3.6 Waste Management process."},{"id":"3.20.28","page":76,"heading":"Contractors, Transportation and Lifecycle Disposition","text":"The potential presence of residual hazardous materials shall be assessed before decommissioning, demolition, excavation or other intrusive work. Identified hazards and required controls shall be communicated before disturbance."},{"id":"3.20.29","page":76,"heading":"Asbestos Containing Materials","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain an accessible asbestos register and management plan covering known and presumed asbestos containing materials throughout their lifecycle."},{"id":"3.20.30","page":76,"heading":"Asbestos Containing Materials","text":"Work that could disturb known or presumed asbestos containing material shall not begin until the asbestos register has been checked, the material’s status has been confirmed or conservatively presumed, and the required controls have been established."},{"id":"3.20.31","page":76,"heading":"Asbestos Containing Materials","text":"Asbestos risks, including risks from naturally occurring asbestiform minerals, shall be identified, assessed and controlled during applicable activities, including inspection, maintenance, excavation, disturbance, emergency response, removal, transportation and disposal, in accordance with applicable requirements and section 3.12 Occupational Health Risk Assessment and Industrial Hygiene Management process."},{"id":"3.20.32","page":77,"heading":"Asbestos Containing Materials","text":"New asbestos containing materials shall not be procured or installed."},{"id":"3.20.33","page":77,"heading":"Emergency Readiness, Verification and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide current hazardous material inventory, location, hazard and response information for the development and maintenance of emergency arrangements in accordance with section\n5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning process."},{"id":"3.20.34","page":77,"heading":"Emergency Readiness, Verification and Improvement","text":"Hazardous material emergency arrangements shall consider, as applicable, material isolation, initial response, compatible response agents, containment, environmental protection and internal and external response interfaces."},{"id":"3.20.35","page":77,"heading":"Emergency Readiness, Verification and Improvement","text":"Hazardous material releases shall be promptly controlled and reported. Subsequent notification, investigation, remediation and management of resulting waste shall comply with the applicable MSHEM processes."},{"id":"3.20.36","page":77,"heading":"Emergency Readiness, Verification and Improvement","text":"Hazardous material information and controls shall be inspected or verified at frequencies based on risk and applicable obligations, with findings and operating experience evaluated, deficiencies controlled until resolved, and affected risk assessments, information and controls updated where required."}],"area":"Health","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Hazardous Material Management; hazmat, hazardous material, SDS, chemical compatibility; Hazardous Material Governance and Lifecycle Control; Identification, Classification and Hazard Information; Approval and Introduction of Hazardous Materials; Risk Assessment and Operational Control; Inventory, Safety Data Sheets and Hazard Communication; Storage, Containment and Transfer; Contractors, Transportation and Lifecycle Disposition; Asbestos Containing Materials; Emergency Readiness, Verification and Improvement","trigger":"Use when hazardous materials are procured, introduced, generated, stored, handled, used or transferred; use the specialist processes for explosives, radiation, hazardous waste and dangerous-goods transport.","refs":["2.6","3.6","3.12","3.43","5.1"]},{"id":"3.21","title":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment","page":77,"purpose":"Hazardous, non-routine and permit controlled work is planned, risk assessed, authorized, coordinated, performed, monitored, suspended when conditions change, and closed under controlled conditions that protect people, the environment, assets and operations.","scope":"This process applies to work performed by Maaden employees, contractors and subcontractors at locations, projects, facilities and activities under Maaden operational or project control where a Permit to Work or Task Risk Assessment is required.","clauses":[{"id":"3.21.1","page":77,"heading":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment System","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain a Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process for work within their operational or project control."},{"id":"3.21.2","page":77,"heading":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment System","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define competency, assessment, authorization and reauthorization requirements for personnel who prepare, issue, receive, authorize, suspend, revalidate, monitor and close permits. Authorized personnel shall perform only those Permit to Work roles, permit types, locations and activities included within their authorization."},{"id":"3.21.3","page":77,"heading":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment System","text":"A Permit Issuer and Permit Receiver shall be different authorized people. People performing Permit to Work responsibilities shall have the authority, operational knowledge and competency necessary for their assigned role."},{"id":"3.21.4","page":77,"heading":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment System","text":"Contractors and subcontractors assigned Permit to Work responsibilities shall meet the applicable Maaden competency and authorization requirements."},{"id":"3.21.5","page":78,"heading":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment System","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish arrangements to ensure that Permit to Work activities are adequately resourced and that the number and complexity of permits controlled by an individual Permit Issuer do not compromise worksite verification, coordination, monitoring or effective control of the work."},{"id":"3.21.6","page":78,"heading":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment System","text":"Where electronic Permit to Work systems are used, controls shall be established to prevent unauthorized issue or alteration, preserve required worksite verification and approvals, communicate changes to affected people, maintain permit availability at the work location, and provide appropriate contingency arrangements for system unavailability."},{"id":"3.21.7","page":78,"heading":"Permit to Work Applicability","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define the activities requiring a Permit to Work and implement a Permit to Work system that includes the following permit types where applicable:\na. General Work Permit;\nb. Hot Work Permit;\nc. Confined Space Entry Permit;\nd. Electrical Work Permit; and\ne. Line and Equipment Opening Permit."},{"id":"3.21.8","page":78,"heading":"Permit to Work Applicability","text":"The Permit to Work system shall, as a minimum, include the following supporting certificates where applicable to the work activity:\na. Excavation Certificate;\nb. LOTO Certificate;\nc. Lifting Certificate;\nd. Radiography Certificate;\ne. Confined Space Entry Log.\nf. Gas Test Record Log."},{"id":"3.21.9","page":78,"heading":"Permit to Work Applicability","text":"A Permit to Work shall not normally be required for a routine operational task performed within the approved scope, conditions and controls of a current and approved Standard Operating Procedure, or for a routine maintenance task performed within the approved scope, conditions and controls of a current and approved Standard Maintenance Procedure in a designated maintenance workshop."},{"id":"3.21.10","page":78,"heading":"Permit to Work Applicability","text":"An exemption under 3.21.9 shall not apply where the task:\na. falls outside the approved scope or conditions of the applicable procedure;\nb. involves permit controlled work, breaking containment, hazardous energy exposure or hazardous atmosphere;\nc. creates significant interfaces with other work or operations;\nd. is subject to a legal, regulatory or site requirement for a Permit to Work; Where the task cannot be performed within the approved procedure or any required control cannot be implemented or maintained, the exemption shall cease to apply and the task shall be reassessed before work starts or continues."},{"id":"3.21.11","page":78,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"Every Permit to Work shall be supported by an approved Task Risk Assessment completed before the permit is issued."},{"id":"3.21.12","page":79,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"Before issuing a permit, the Permit Issuer and Permit Receiver shall verify at the work location that the work scope is correctly defined, worksite conditions have been assessed and required controls have been implemented and are effective."},{"id":"3.21.13","page":79,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"The verification shall address, as applicable, hazardous energy isolation, atmospheric conditions, safe access and egress, working surfaces, area controls, interactions with operating equipment or other work, and emergency arrangements."},{"id":"3.21.14","page":79,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"Each permit shall clearly define the authorized work scope, location and equipment boundaries, date and authorized validity period, required controls, associated certificates, responsible people, validity conditions and conditions requiring suspension, revalidation or cancellation."},{"id":"3.21.15","page":79,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"Permit requirements shall not be bypassed through generic, blanket or area wide permits that do not adequately define and control the work."},{"id":"3.21.16","page":79,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"The Permit to Work process shall define arrangements for permit preparation, issue, acceptance, execution, monitoring, handover, suspension, revalidation, cancellation and closure."},{"id":"3.21.17","page":79,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"Before permitted work starts, the Permit Receiver shall ensure that affected members of the work party are briefed on and understand the authorized work scope, applicable hazards, required controls, permit limitations, simultaneous operations, emergency actions and conditions requiring work to stop. Changes affecting the work shall be communicated before work continues."},{"id":"3.21.18","page":79,"heading":"Permit Planning and Authorization","text":"Permitted work shall be subject to field verification during execution at a frequency proportionate to the hazards, complexity, duration, and changing worksite conditions, with identified deviations or ineffective controls corrected before work continues and the permit suspended where safe conditions cannot be maintained."},{"id":"3.21.19","page":79,"heading":"Interfaces and Simultaneous Operations","text":"The Permit to Work system shall define and control interfaces with applicable processes, including Hot Work and Ignition Source Control, Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy, Working at Height, Confined Space Entry, Lifting Operations, Excavation and Trenching, Radiation Management and Electrical Safety."},{"id":"3.21.20","page":79,"heading":"Interfaces and Simultaneous Operations","text":"Interactions between concurrent or simultaneous activities shall be identified, assessed and coordinated by the permit issuer where they could create additional hazards, impair required controls or increase risk."},{"id":"3.21.21","page":79,"heading":"Interfaces and Simultaneous Operations","text":"Where multiple permits or Permit to Work systems apply to the same work activity, a responsible person shall be designated to coordinate and control the interfaces between them."},{"id":"3.21.22","page":79,"heading":"Interfaces and Simultaneous Operations","text":"The Permit to Work system shall maintain a current record of active and suspended permits and their status, including associated isolations where applicable. Current permits and supporting Task Risk Assessments shall be readily accessible at the work location and to the responsible operating or coordinating authority."},{"id":"3.21.23","page":79,"heading":"Permit Handover, Suspension and Completion","text":"A formal handover and revalidation shall be completed where permitted work continues across a shift or where responsible Permit to Work personnel change. The incoming responsible people shall verify that the work scope, worksite conditions, isolations, Task Risk Assessment and required controls remain valid before accepting responsibility."},{"id":"3.21.24","page":79,"heading":"Permit Handover, Suspension and Completion","text":"Permitted work shall be stopped and the permit suspended where:\na. An incident, emergency, alarm or evacuation affects the work;\nb. the approved work scope, work method, work location, equipment, hazards, isolation boundaries or worksite conditions change;\nc. a required control becomes unavailable or ineffective;\nd. atmospheric conditions fall outside approved limits;\ne. an uncontrolled interaction with another activity arises; or\nf. any person identifies that the work cannot be performed safely."},{"id":"3.21.25","page":80,"heading":"Permit Handover, Suspension and Completion","text":"Suspended work shall not resume until the conditions have been reassessed, required controls restored or revised and the permit revalidated by an authorized person."},{"id":"3.21.26","page":80,"heading":"Permit Handover, Suspension and Completion","text":"Before permit closeout, the Permit Issuer and Permit Receiver shall verify that the work is complete or left in a defined safe condition; affected equipment and the work area are safe; personnel, temporary equipment and materials are accounted for; and associated permits, certificates and isolations have been reconciled."},{"id":"3.21.27","page":80,"heading":"Permit Handover, Suspension and Completion","text":"Equipment or systems shall not be reinstated, energized, pressurized, or returned to service until operational acceptance requirements are completed."},{"id":"3.21.28","page":80,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"Task Risk Assessment shall provide the overarching framework for identifying, assessing and controlling hazards associated with work tasks in accordance with Section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management."},{"id":"3.21.29","page":80,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish Task Risk Assessment methods and selection criteria proportionate to the hazards, complexity, credible consequences, worksite conditions and interfaces associated with the task."},{"id":"3.21.30","page":80,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"The framework shall include, as applicable:\na. A Last Minute Risk Assessment (LMRA) for simple, routine tasks where hazards and required controls are well understood and none of the JSA criteria in 3.21.31 apply; and\nb. A team based Job Safety Analysis (JSA) for tasks requiring step by step assessment, greater assessment rigor, specialized knowledge or multidisciplinary participation."},{"id":"3.21.31","page":80,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"A Job Safety Analysis (JSA) shall be completed by a team including competent personnel with knowledge of the tasks, hazards and required controls. A JSA shall be required where the task:\na. is high risk, complex or non-routine, including but not limited to hot work, confined space entry, working at height, lifting activities, work involving energy isolation, excavation, radiography, use of explosives and diving;\nb. requires detailed step by step assessment because of the nature of the hazards or controls;\nc. involves significant operational, contractor or simultaneous work interfaces;\nd. is new, unfamiliar, being performed for the first time, or involves new methods, equipment or technology;\ne. involves changing or uncertain conditions;\nf. requires specialized or multidisciplinary knowledge."},{"id":"3.21.32","page":80,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"Task Risk Assessments shall identify, as applicable:\na. the task and work conditions;\nb. hazards, exposures, interactions and credible consequences;\nc. people who may be affected;\nd. existing and required controls;\ne. applicable critical controls and verification requirements;\nf. responsibilities for implementing controls; and\ng. conditions requiring work to stop or the assessment to be reviewed."},{"id":"3.21.33","page":81,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"Risk identified through a Job Safety Analysis shall be rated using the Maaden EHSS Risk Matrix. Required risk review and approval shall be based on the residual risk level in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.21.34","page":81,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"Personnel performing the work shall be involved in the Task Risk Assessment where their knowledge or experience is necessary to identify task-specific hazards, work conditions and controls."},{"id":"3.21.35","page":81,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"Before work begins, the work party shall be briefed on and understand the planned work scope, applicable hazards, required controls, emergency arrangements and stop-work conditions. The briefing shall be repeated where significant changes occur, or new personnel join the work party."},{"id":"3.21.36","page":81,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"A previous Job Safety Analysis shall only be reused after competent review confirms its applicability to the current task, hazards, worksite conditions and interfaces. Generic or unreviewed assessments shall not be used to authorize work."},{"id":"3.21.37","page":81,"heading":"Task Risk Assessment","text":"Where required controls cannot reduce risk to an acceptable level in accordance with Section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management, the task shall be stopped and escalated through the EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.21.38","page":81,"heading":"Monitoring and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define and implement risk-based frequencies for verifying Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment arrangements, considering the hazards and complexity of work, credible consequences, permit volume, simultaneous operations, changing worksite conditions and previous verification results."},{"id":"3.21.39","page":81,"heading":"Monitoring and Assurance","text":"Findings shall be documented, assigned and tracked to closure. Relevant incidents, observations, findings and performance trends shall be used to improve the Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process."},{"id":"3.21.40","page":81,"heading":"Monitoring and Assurance","text":"Completed, cancelled and expired permits, associated certificates and task risk assessments shall be retained for at least three months."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment; PTW, permit to work, task risk assessment, TRA, JSA, JHA; Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment System; Permit to Work Applicability; Permit Planning and Authorization; Interfaces and Simultaneous Operations; Permit Handover, Suspension and Completion; Task Risk Assessment; Monitoring and Assurance","trigger":"if the work requires task risk assessment, authorization or a permit to work","refs":["2.5"]},{"id":"3.22","title":"Hot Work and Ignition Source Control","page":81,"purpose":"Prevent fires, explosions and other ignition related incidents by eliminating hot work wherever a cold work alternative exists and, where hot work or other ignition sources cannot be eliminated, ensuring they are identified, assessed, authorized, controlled and monitored wherever they could cause a fire or explosion.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden controlled work and activities that generate, introduce or create an ignition source where combustible materials, ignitable substances, flammable or explosive atmospheres are present or could credibly arise.","clauses":[{"id":"3.22.1","page":81,"heading":"Hot Work and Ignition Source Identification and Assessment","text":"Hot Work shall include welding, cutting, brazing, open flame soldering, gouging, grinding, heat treatment and other work that produces or uses flame, sparks or heat capable of igniting combustible materials or a flammable or explosive atmosphere. Hot Work shall be managed in accordance with applicable legal and regulatory requirements and NFPA 51B, Standard for Fire Prevention During Welding, Cutting, and other Hot Work."},{"id":"3.22.2","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work and Ignition Source Identification and Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify activities, equipment, materials, locations and conditions that could generate or introduce an ignition source and assess where interaction with combustible materials or an ignitable atmosphere could result in fire, explosion or escalation of another hazardous event."},{"id":"3.22.3","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work and Ignition Source Identification and Assessment","text":"Potential ignition sources shall include, as applicable, Hot Work, open flames and smoking, spark producing mechanical activities, internal combustion engines and vehicles, electrical or electronic equipment unsuitable for the hazardous area classification, electrical arcs, faults and electrostatic discharge, friction and mechanical heating, and self-heating, spontaneous combustion or exothermic reactions."},{"id":"3.22.4","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work and Ignition Source Identification and Assessment","text":"Ignition source assessments shall consider the materials and atmospheres present or capable of release, hazardous area classification, potential paths for sparks and heat, adjacent and lower levels, simultaneous activities, changing conditions and the potential for fire or explosion to spread or escalate."},{"id":"3.22.5","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work and Ignition Source Identification and Assessment","text":"Ignition sources shall be eliminated or substituted wherever reasonably practicable. Where this is not practicable, suitable engineering, isolation and administrative controls shall be implemented. Controls designated as critical controls shall be managed and verified in accordance with section 2.6 Critical Control Management process."},{"id":"3.22.6","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work Planning and Authorization","text":"Hot work shall be authorized only where the task cannot reasonably be completed using a cold work method or relocated to a designated hot work area."},{"id":"3.22.7","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work Planning and Authorization","text":"Before authorization, hot work and potentially affected areas shall be assessed for flammable or explosive atmospheres, combustible materials and dusts, process and equipment conditions, openings and potential releases, simultaneous operations, spark and heat travel, hazardous residues or previous contents, environmental conditions, fire protection availability and emergency arrangements, with identified fire and explosion hazards controlled before work begins."},{"id":"3.22.8","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work Planning and Authorization","text":"Where a flammable or explosive atmosphere is present or could credibly arise, atmospheric testing shall be completed before hot work and continued periodically or continuously as required, with flammable gas or vapor concentrations maintained below 10% of the Lower Explosive Limit and work stopped when this limit is reached or exceeded."},{"id":"3.22.9","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work Planning and Authorization","text":"Atmospheric testing shall be performed by competent personnel using suitable instruments calibrated in accordance with manufacturer requirements and function tested before use, at locations and frequencies based on the hazards and changing conditions but not exceeding two hours, including tests of affected equipment and adjacent or connected spaces that could contain or receive flammable gases or vapors."},{"id":"3.22.10","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work Planning and Authorization","text":"Hot work outside a designated hot work area shall require a Hot Work Permit in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process, except where work remains within the approved activities and conditions of a current designated area assessment, required controls remain effective and no additional hazard requires permit authorization."},{"id":"3.22.11","page":82,"heading":"Hot Work Planning and Authorization","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall designate hot work areas through documented assessment confirming that each area is constructed or protected with non-combustible or fire-resistant materials, separated from fire and explosion hazards, kept free from uncontrolled combustible or flammable materials, provided with suitable firefighting, housekeeping, inspection and supervision arrangements, and reviewed periodically and following relevant changes."},{"id":"3.22.12","page":83,"heading":"Hot Work Planning and Authorization","text":"Hot tapping and other intentional welding, cutting, drilling or process boundary penetration of in-service equipment or piping containing hazardous substances shall not be treated as conventional Hot Work. Such work shall only be performed under an approved Hot Tapping process or procedure supported by appropriate engineering and risk assessment, and in accordance with API RP 2201."},{"id":"3.22.13","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Ignition sources not arising from permitted Hot Work shall be controlled through applicable hazardous area classification, design, operation, inspection, maintenance, electrical safety, fire prevention, hazardous material and other relevant MSHEM requirements. Electrical and electronic equipment used in hazardous classified areas shall be suitable for the area classification and conditions of use."},{"id":"3.22.14","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Business Units shall identify and control ignition risks arising from electrostatic discharge, lightning and stray currents. Required bonding, grounding and other engineered controls shall be designed, installed, inspected, tested and maintained in accordance with the identified hazards and applicable technical standards."},{"id":"3.22.15","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Smoking, open flames and unauthorized ignition devices shall be prohibited where fire or explosion hazards exist. Internal combustion engines or other potential ignition producing equipment introduced into areas where an ignitable atmosphere is present or could credibly arise shall be assessed and controlled in accordance with the hazardous area classification and applicable Hot Work Permit requirements."},{"id":"3.22.16","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Coatings, residues and contaminants capable of producing toxic substances or creating an additional fire or explosion hazard shall be identified and removed or controlled before Hot Work. Pyrophoric, water-reactive, self- heating or unstable materials shall be assessed by a competent person and required treatment or controls established before Hot Work is authorized."},{"id":"3.22.17","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Hot work equipment shall be suitable for its intended use, inspected before use, maintained in safe operating condition, and removed from service when defective or unsuitable."},{"id":"3.22.18","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Combustible and flammable materials shall be removed from within at least 11 meters of Hot Work in all directions, including adjacent, upper and lower levels. Where removal is not practicable, they shall be protected using suitable fire-resistant covers, blankets, screens or barriers. The controlled distance shall be increased where sparks, slag, heat or other ignition sources could travel beyond 11 meters."},{"id":"3.22.19","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Openings, gaps, grating, floor and wall penetrations, drains, vents and process release points through which sparks, slag, heat or flammable material could pass to adjacent or lower areas shall be covered, sealed or otherwise effectively controlled before Hot Work begins."},{"id":"3.22.20","page":83,"heading":"Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls","text":"Firefighting equipment appropriate to the identified hazards shall be immediately available at the Hot Work location. A Hot Work Permit shall not be issued while fire detection, alarm, fire water or suppression systems serving the area are impaired unless compensatory controls have been assessed and formally approved in accordance with section\n5.3 Emergency Response Equipment Maintenance, Testing and Interruption."},{"id":"3.22.21","page":83,"heading":"Fire Watch and Post Work Monitoring","text":"A competent fire watch shall continuously monitor hot work outside a designated hot work area in accordance with NFPA 51B, remain free from conflicting duties, have suitable firefighting equipment and emergency communication, understand the applicable alarm procedures, and stop the work when unsafe conditions develop. Additional fire watches shall be assigned where all exposed areas cannot be directly observed."},{"id":"3.22.22","page":84,"heading":"Fire Watch and Post Work Monitoring","text":"Fire watch and post-work monitoring shall continue for the periods required by NFPA 51B, the fire hazard assessment and the Hot Work Permit, including at least 60 minutes of fire watch after hot work and up to three hours of additional monitoring where required."},{"id":"3.22.23","page":84,"heading":"Competency, Verification and Assurance","text":"People who authorize, supervise, perform, conduct atmospheric testing for, or act as fire watch for Hot Work shall be competent and authorized for their assigned responsibilities. Personnel involved in Hot Work shall understand the applicable hazards, controls, permit requirements, emergency actions and conditions requiring work to stop."},{"id":"3.22.24","page":84,"heading":"Competency, Verification and Assurance","text":"Required controls shall be re-verified whenever Hot Work resumes after an interruption and whenever the work scope, atmospheric conditions, process conditions, simultaneous activities or other relevant conditions change. Where a required control is missing, ineffective or invalidated, Hot Work shall stop and shall not resume until conditions have been reassessed, required controls restored or revised and the permit revalidated in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment."},{"id":"3.22.25","page":84,"heading":"Competency, Verification and Assurance","text":"The work area shall be inspected after completion of Hot Work to verify that no ignition source, smoldering material or unsafe condition remains."},{"id":"3.22.26","page":84,"heading":"Competency, Verification and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically verify the implementation and effectiveness of Hot Work and Ignition Source Control arrangements through risk-based field verification, review of Hot Work activities and designated areas, and applicable assurance activities."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Hot Work and Ignition Source Control; hot work, welding, cutting, grinding, ignition; Hot Work and Ignition Source Identification and Assessment; Hot Work Planning and Authorization; Ignition Source and Fire Prevention Controls; Fire Watch and Post-Work Monitoring; Competency, Verification and Assurance","trigger":"if welding, cutting, grinding, heating or another ignition source is introduced","refs":["2.6","3.21","5.3"]},{"id":"3.23","title":"Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy","page":84,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for identifying, isolating, controlling and verifying hazardous energy before work is performed to prevent unintended energization, start-up, movement, release of stored energy or exposure to hazardous substances.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden controlled activities, facilities, projects, personnel, contractors and subcontractors where hazardous energy or hazardous substances may expose personnel during installation, commissioning, operation, inspection, testing, maintenance, servicing, modification, decommissioning, demolition, breaking containment, confined space entry or similar work.","clauses":[{"id":"3.23.1","page":84,"heading":"Hazardous Energy Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented arrangements for identifying, isolating, controlling, verifying and restoring hazardous energy."},{"id":"3.23.2","page":84,"heading":"Hazardous Energy Management","text":"Hazardous energy controls shall be implemented in accordance with SAIS SAF-13 and recognized standards including OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147 and ANSI/ASSP Z244.1."},{"id":"3.23.3","page":84,"heading":"Hazardous Energy Management","text":"All hazardous energy sources, including stored, residual, accumulated or regenerated energy, shall be identified before work begins."},{"id":"3.23.4","page":85,"heading":"Hazardous Energy Management","text":"Required isolation and energy control measures shall be authorized in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process."},{"id":"3.23.5","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Equipment, systems and processes shall be isolated before personnel are exposed to hazardous energy unless an approved alternative method provides equivalent or greater protection and is permitted by applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.23.6","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Isolation methods shall prevent unintended energization, start-up, movement, material release or other exposure to hazardous energy."},{"id":"3.23.7","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Before personnel are exposed, stored, residual, accumulated or regenerated energy shall be dissipated, relieved, blocked, restrained or otherwise controlled to a safe state, with the effectiveness of the isolation verified at the point of work using an appropriate try/test or equivalent method and measures maintained to control any energy that may reaccumulate or regenerate."},{"id":"3.23.8","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"The isolation method shall be selected based on the energy source, task, system configuration, hazardous material characteristics, stored energy and credible consequences of isolation failure."},{"id":"3.23.9","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Positive isolation shall be prioritized where failure of a primary isolation could result in serious harm, significant environmental impact or a major process safety event."},{"id":"3.23.10","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Business Unit and Corporate Functions procedures shall define activities and conditions requiring positive isolation, including applicable breaking containment, confined space, hazardous material and process system activities."},{"id":"3.23.11","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Where positive isolation is required but cannot be achieved, the work shall not proceed unless an alternative arrangement is permitted by applicable requirements and supported by documented technical review, risk assessment and authorization."},{"id":"3.23.12","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Isolation points and devices shall be identified, secured and controlled using locks and tags where personnel protection depends on maintaining the isolation."},{"id":"3.23.13","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Where an energy isolating device can be locked, lockout shall be used unless an approved assessment demonstrates equivalent or greater protection from an alternative arrangement."},{"id":"3.23.14","page":85,"heading":"Isolation Selection and Control","text":"Isolation methods not addressed by approved procedures or technical standards shall require documented technical or engineering review, risk assessment and authorization before use."},{"id":"3.23.15","page":85,"heading":"Energy Isolation Planning","text":"Documented isolation plans shall be established for complex, critical, multipoint or other isolations requiring enhanced planning and verification."},{"id":"3.23.16","page":85,"heading":"Energy Isolation Planning","text":"Isolation plans and records shall identify applicable isolation points and devices and provide sufficient information to control installation, verification, transfer, restoration and removal."},{"id":"3.23.17","page":85,"heading":"Energy Isolation Planning","text":"Temporary blinds, disconnections and other positive isolation devices shall remain traceable throughout their lifecycle."},{"id":"3.23.18","page":85,"heading":"Energy Isolation Planning","text":"Extended isolations shall include defined responsibility, periodic verification, controlled handover and maintenance of isolation integrity."},{"id":"3.23.19","page":86,"heading":"Electrical Isolation","text":"Electrical equipment shall be de-energized before work where personnel could be exposed to electrical hazards unless energized work is specifically authorized under section 3.30 Electrical Safety and section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment processes."},{"id":"3.23.20","page":86,"heading":"Electrical Isolation","text":"Electrical isolation shall identify all applicable electrical energy sources, use approved energy isolating devices, secure isolation against unauthorized operation, and verify de-energization before personnel are exposed."},{"id":"3.23.21","page":86,"heading":"Electrical Isolation","text":"Control circuits, software controls, selector switches, push buttons or interlocks shall not be used as the only means of electrical isolation."},{"id":"3.23.22","page":86,"heading":"Electrical Isolation","text":"Electrical isolation and absence of voltage verification shall be performed by qualified people in accordance with the applicable Electrical Safety requirements, including NFPA 70E and OSHA 29 CFR 1910.333."},{"id":"3.23.23","page":86,"heading":"Personal and Group Protection","text":"Each person exposed to hazardous energy shall be protected through a personal lock or an approved group lockout arrangement providing equivalent individual control."},{"id":"3.23.24","page":86,"heading":"Personal and Group Protection","text":"Personal locks shall be uniquely identifiable, standardized, distinguishable from locks used for other purposes, and remain under the control of the individual to whom they are assigned."},{"id":"3.23.25","page":86,"heading":"Personal and Group Protection","text":"Group lockout arrangements shall provide effective individual protection and shall define responsibility for control, personnel accountability, shift or work group changes and transfer of protection."},{"id":"3.23.26","page":86,"heading":"Personal and Group Protection","text":"Personal locks or tags shall normally be removed only by the person who applied them. Emergency removal shall be performed only through an approved authorization process that protects the affected person and verifies that safe removal conditions exist as per OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147."},{"id":"3.23.27","page":86,"heading":"Personal and Group Protection","text":"A designated authorized person shall control each group lockout, as per OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147 and ANSI/ASSP Z244.1."},{"id":"3.23.28","page":86,"heading":"Personal and Group Protection","text":"Controls shall be established for personnel changes, shift changes, and work group transitions."},{"id":"3.23.29","page":86,"heading":"Personal and Group Protection","text":"The Permit To Work Issuer’s isolation control lock shall be the first control lock applied and the last removed. It shall not replace any personal lock."},{"id":"3.23.30","page":86,"heading":"Restoration and Re-Energization","text":"Equipment, systems and processes shall not be restored to service until the work is complete and restoration has been authorized in accordance with the LOTO certificate."},{"id":"3.23.31","page":86,"heading":"Restoration and Re-Energization","text":"Before isolation controls are removed, it shall be verified that:\na. affected work is complete;\nb. personnel are clear;\nc. tools, temporary equipment and materials are removed;\nd. guards, protective devices and safeguards are restored; and\ne. equipment is suitable for safe return to service."},{"id":"3.23.32","page":86,"heading":"Restoration and Re-Energization","text":"Before re-energization or return to service, personnel involved in or affected by the task shall be notified and the notification recorded on the LOTO Certificate."},{"id":"3.23.33","page":87,"heading":"Restoration and Re-Energization","text":"Temporary energization required for testing or positioning shall be specifically authorized and controlled, with personnel cleared before energization and required isolations reapplied and reverified before work resumes."},{"id":"3.23.34","page":87,"heading":"Competency and Assurance","text":"Personnel performing, authorizing or verifying hazardous energy isolation shall be trained, competent and authorized for their assigned responsibilities."},{"id":"3.23.35","page":87,"heading":"Competency and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify the implementation and effectiveness of hazardous energy control arrangements through documented program monitoring and procedure inspections conducted at least annually by an authorized person other than the person implementing the procedure being inspected, in accordance with OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147(c)(6) and ANSI/ASSP Z244.1."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy; LOTO, lockout, tagout, isolation, stored energy; Hazardous Energy Management; Isolation Selection and Control; Energy Isolation Planning; Electrical Isolation; Personal and Group Protection; Restoration and Re-Energization; Competency and Assurance","trigger":"if hazardous energy or stored energy must be isolated, controlled or verified","refs":["3.21","3.30"]},{"id":"3.24","title":"Working at Height","page":87,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for planning, authorizing and controlling work at height to prevent falls and falling objects and to ensure safe access, suitable equipment, competent personnel and effective rescue arrangements.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden controlled activities, facilities and projects where a person may be exposed to a risk of falling from one level to another during work or access, including elevated or temporary work areas, unprotected edges and gratings opening , fragile surfaces, ladders, scaffolds, mobile elevating work platforms and personal fall protection systems.","clauses":[{"id":"3.24.1","page":87,"heading":"Working at Height Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain arrangements for controlling work at height consistent with applicable legal requirements and ANSI/ASSP Z359.2 requirements."},{"id":"3.24.2","page":87,"heading":"Working at Height Management","text":"Roles, responsibilities, authorities and competency requirements for planning, authorizing, supervising, performing, inspecting and supporting work at height shall be defined."},{"id":"3.24.3","page":87,"heading":"Working at Height Management","text":"Personnel performing or supporting work at height shall be trained and, where required, formally authorized for their assigned responsibilities."},{"id":"3.24.4","page":87,"heading":"Planning and Risk Control","text":"Work at height shall, where reasonably practicable, be avoided or otherwise controlled in the following order:\na. fall prevention;\nb. collective fall protection;\nc. travel restraint;\nd. work positioning; and\ne. fall arrest."},{"id":"3.24.5","page":87,"heading":"Planning and Risk Control","text":"Fall hazards shall be identified and risk assessed before work begins, including hazards associated with access, working surfaces, edges and openings, fragile surfaces, equipment, falling objects, weather, electrical or process hazards, traffic and simultaneous operations."},{"id":"3.24.6","page":87,"heading":"Planning and Risk Control","text":"Work at height shall be planned to define, as applicable:\na. the safe work method and access;\nb. working surfaces and equipment;\nc. collective and personal fall protection measures;\nd. anchorage requirements;\ne. falling object and area controls;\nf. environmental operating limits;\ng. interfaces with other work and applicable permits; and\nh. rescue arrangements."},{"id":"3.24.7","page":88,"heading":"Planning and Risk Control","text":"Work at height shall be authorized in accordance with Section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment, where required, and stopped until reassessment and reauthorization are completed where conditions change or required controls become unavailable or ineffective."},{"id":"3.24.8","page":88,"heading":"Safe Access, Work Platforms and Openings","text":"Safe access, egress and movement shall be provided and maintained for work at height locations."},{"id":"3.24.9","page":88,"heading":"Safe Access, Work Platforms and Openings","text":"Areas where people walk or work shall be stable, capable of supporting the intended loads and maintained in a safe condition."},{"id":"3.24.10","page":88,"heading":"Safe Access, Work Platforms and Openings","text":"Open sides, edges, floor or wall openings and fragile surfaces shall be identified and protected against falls of people, tools, equipment and materials."},{"id":"3.24.11","page":88,"heading":"Safe Access, Work Platforms and Openings","text":"The creation of a temporary opening, including the removal of grating, floor plates, covers, hatches or sections of a platform, shall be planned, risk assessed and authorized before the opening is created."},{"id":"3.24.12","page":88,"heading":"Safe Access, Work Platforms and Openings","text":"Temporary openings shall be continuously protected by secured load bearing covers, guardrail systems or other physical barriers providing equivalent protection, including during the removal and reinstatement of the affected surface."},{"id":"3.24.13","page":88,"heading":"Safe Access, Work Platforms and Openings","text":"Removed grating, floor plates and covers shall be controlled against unintended movement and reinstated, secured and verified before the area is returned to normal use."},{"id":"3.24.14","page":88,"heading":"Safe Access, Work Platforms and Openings","text":"Where an opening cannot be fully protected by a cover or physical barrier, access shall be restricted and work controlled through an approved fall protection arrangement."},{"id":"3.24.15","page":88,"heading":"Ladders, Scaffolds and Elevated Platforms","text":"Ladders and fixed access systems shall meet applicable design, inspection, maintenance and use requirements, including OSHA 29 CFR 1910.23 where applicable. Ladders shall be used only where appropriate for the task and where a safer means of access or working platform is not reasonably practicable."},{"id":"3.24.16","page":88,"heading":"Ladders, Scaffolds and Elevated Platforms","text":"Scaffolds and temporary work platforms shall be selected, designed, erected, altered, inspected, used and dismantled by competent or qualified personnel, as applicable to their assigned responsibilities, considering the work, location, configuration, environmental conditions and anticipated loads, and in accordance with OSHA 1926.451 and ANSI/ASSP A10.8."},{"id":"3.24.17","page":88,"heading":"Ladders, Scaffolds and Elevated Platforms","text":"Scaffolds shall be provided with a clearly visible identification status at the access points following inspection by a competent person. The system shall identify whether the scaffold is approved for use, subject to restrictions, or prohibited for use in accordance with ANSI/ASPP A10.8."},{"id":"3.24.18","page":88,"heading":"Ladders, Scaffolds and Elevated Platforms","text":"Non-standard or complex scaffolds and temporary platforms shall be subject to documented design and approval by a qualified person before use."},{"id":"3.24.19","page":89,"heading":"Ladders, Scaffolds and Elevated Platforms","text":"Scaffolds and temporary work platforms shall provide stable foundations or supports, working platforms, safe access, edge protection and falling object controls, and be protected from use when incomplete, defective, overloaded or unauthorized."},{"id":"3.24.20","page":89,"heading":"Ladders, Scaffolds and Elevated Platforms","text":"Mobile elevating work platforms and personnel lifting equipment shall be fit for purpose, inspected, maintained, and operated by competent and authorized people."},{"id":"3.24.21","page":89,"heading":"Personal Fall Protection","text":"Fall hazards shall be controlled where a person could fall 1.8 m or more to a lower level, or at a lower height where the risk assessment identifies a potential for serious injury. Personal fall protection shall be provided where collective protection does not adequately control the fall exposure."},{"id":"3.24.22","page":89,"heading":"Personal Fall Protection","text":"Personal fall protection systems shall be selected and used according to the activity, user, exposure, environmental conditions and available fall clearance, in compliance with applicable OSHA 29 CFR 1910.140 and ANSI/ASSP Z359 requirements."},{"id":"3.24.23","page":89,"heading":"Personal Fall Protection","text":"Fall protection components and systems shall be compatible, fit for their intended purpose and connected to anchorages identified and verified before use, with engineered anchorages and fall protection systems designed and approved by qualified personnel."},{"id":"3.24.24","page":89,"heading":"Personal Fall Protection","text":"Fall protection equipment shall be removed from service where it is damaged, defective, altered without authorization, involved in a fall or impact loading, overdue for required inspection, or outside its approved service life."},{"id":"3.24.25","page":89,"heading":"Personal Fall Protection","text":"Falling object hazards associated with work at height shall be controlled by securing or containing tools, materials and equipment that could fall and protecting areas below or adjacent to overhead work through access control, barricading or other defined controls."},{"id":"3.24.26","page":89,"heading":"Rescue and Emergency Arrangements","text":"Before personnel are exposed to a fall hazard, rescue arrangements shall be established based on the location and fall protection system and address required rescue personnel, equipment, communication, access, medical support and protection of rescuers."},{"id":"3.24.27","page":89,"heading":"Rescue and Emergency Arrangements","text":"Required rescue capability shall remain available for the duration of the work and be periodically verified based on the risk and complexity of the task."},{"id":"3.24.28","page":89,"heading":"Inspection and Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain an inspection schedule identifying the inspection types, frequencies, competent roles, acceptance criteria and record requirements for applicable work at height equipment."},{"id":"3.24.29","page":89,"heading":"Inspection and Verification","text":"Personal fall protection equipment shall be inspected by the user before each use and undergo a documented inspection by a competent person at least annually, or more frequently where required by the applicable ANSI/ASSP Z359 equipment standard, manufacturer instructions, conditions and frequency of use, environmental exposure or inspection history."},{"id":"3.24.30","page":89,"heading":"Inspection and Verification","text":"Scaffolds shall be inspected for visible defects by a competent person before each work shift and shall undergo documented inspection by a competent scaffold inspector at intervals not exceeding two weeks and following erection, alteration, relocation, severe weather, impact, overloading, or any other occurrence that could affect structural integrity, before use or continued use."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Working at Height; working at height, scaffold, ladder, roof, fall protection; Working at Height Management; Planning and Risk Control; Safe Access and Work Platforms; Ladders, Scaffolds and Elevated Platforms; Personal Fall Protection; Falling Objects and Area Control; Rescue and Emergency Arrangements; Verification and Improvement","trigger":"if a person or object could fall from an elevated work position or opening","refs":["3.21"]},{"id":"3.25","title":"Confined Space Entry","page":90,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for identifying, assessing, authorizing, controlling, and monitoring confined space entry to protect personnel from hazardous atmospheres, energy releases, restricted access, and other safety and health hazards.","scope":"This process applies to Business Unit and Corporate Functions, employees, contractors, and subcontractors involved in or affected by entry into confined spaces during operations, maintenance, inspection, cleaning, construction, testing, and emergency activities at Maaden controlled sites.","clauses":[{"id":"3.25.1","page":90,"heading":"Confined Space Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented arrangements for managing confined space entry in accordance with SAIS SAF-13, OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146 and NFPA 350."},{"id":"3.25.2","page":90,"heading":"Confined Space Management","text":"Confined spaces shall be identified, assessed, recorded and clearly marked, with all entry controlled in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process, and unauthorized entry prevented in accordance with applicable requirements, including OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146."},{"id":"3.25.3","page":90,"heading":"Confined Space Management","text":"Roles, responsibilities, and authorities for confined space entry planning, authorization, supervision, atmospheric testing, attendance, entry, and rescue shall be defined."},{"id":"3.25.4","page":90,"heading":"Confined Space Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that personnel assigned to confined space responsibilities are trained, competent, qualified and authorized, as applicable to their roles, and that activities involving Maaden personnel, contractors, subcontractors and other affected parties are coordinated."},{"id":"3.25.5","page":90,"heading":"Confined Space Management","text":"Changes to a confined space, its use, configuration, hazards, or surrounding conditions shall trigger reassessment and, where necessary, reclassification and revision of entry controls."},{"id":"3.25.6","page":90,"heading":"Confined Space Management","text":"Facility and equipment design or modification shall consider eliminating confined spaces or reducing entry hazards in accordance with NFPA 350."},{"id":"3.25.7","page":90,"heading":"Hazard Assessment and Entry Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess confined space hazards and prepare a documented entry plan before entry is authorized."},{"id":"3.25.8","page":90,"heading":"Hazard Assessment and Entry Planning","text":"The hazard assessment shall consider inherent, introduced, adjacent, and interacting hazards that could affect entrants, attendants, rescuers, or other affected people, including hazardous atmospheres, engulfment, entrapment, restricted access or egress, hazardous energy, process materials, temperature extremes, biological agents, falls, falling objects, and surrounding operations, as applicable."},{"id":"3.25.9","page":90,"heading":"Hazard Assessment and Entry Planning","text":"The entry plan shall define the work scope, acceptable entry conditions, required personnel and competencies, isolation, atmospheric testing and controls, access and egress, communication, PPE, tools and equipment, monitoring, supervision, and emergency and rescue arrangements."},{"id":"3.25.10","page":91,"heading":"Hazard Assessment and Entry Planning","text":"Hazardous energy and material sources capable of entering or otherwise affecting the confined space shall be identified and controlled through the applicable requirements established in section 3.23 Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy process."},{"id":"3.25.11","page":91,"heading":"Hazard Assessment and Entry Planning","text":"Confined space entry shall be coordinated with applicable Permit to Work, energy isolation, hot work, line opening, electrical safety, working at height, respiratory protection, lifting, emergency preparedness, and simultaneous operations requirements."},{"id":"3.25.12","page":91,"heading":"Hazard Assessment and Entry Planning","text":"The hazard assessment and entry plan shall be reviewed and revised when the work scope, space conditions, hazards, personnel, equipment, or surrounding operations change."},{"id":"3.25.13","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall authorize confined space entry through an approved Confined Space Entry Permit, supported by an entry plan that includes ventilation and rescue arrangements, and a Job Safety Analysis along with any other permits or certificates required for the work."},{"id":"3.25.14","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"The confined space entry permit shall identify the space, work scope, hazards, controls, authorized personnel, validity period, acceptable atmospheric conditions, communication requirements, and rescue arrangements."},{"id":"3.25.15","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"Before authorizing entry, the Permit Issuer and Receiver shall verify that required isolations, atmospheric conditions and controls, access and egress, equipment, communication, PPE, attendant coverage, and rescue arrangements are in place."},{"id":"3.25.16","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"Energy, material and process sources capable of entering or otherwise affecting the confined space shall be isolated and the effectiveness of the isolation verified in accordance with the applicable MSHEM Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy requirements. Connected material and process sources shall be isolated by blinding or physical disconnection before entry; valve closure alone shall not satisfy this requirement. Any alternative shall only be used where permitted by applicable legal and regulatory requirements and supported by documented technical review, risk assessment and authorization."},{"id":"3.25.17","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"A trained, competent and authorized Standby Man shall remain outside at each designated entry point while personnel are inside the confined space, continuously monitor the entry, maintain communication with entrants and other Standby Men, and perform no duty that could interfere with Standby Man responsibilities. Each entrant shall sign in and out for every entry and exit using a controlled entry/exit log, and the Standby Man shall maintain an accurate account of personnel inside the confined space."},{"id":"3.25.18","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"Personnel performing entry, attendance, atmospheric testing, entry supervision, or rescue duties shall be competent and authorized for their assigned roles in accordance with ANSI/ASSP Z117.1."},{"id":"3.25.19","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"Related permits and simultaneous activities shall be coordinated throughout the entry to prevent interacting or introduced hazards."},{"id":"3.25.20","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"Entry shall be suspended and entrants evacuated when acceptable entry conditions are exceeded, a required control or communication system fails, an emergency alarm activates, or any other condition could endanger personnel."},{"id":"3.25.21","page":91,"heading":"Entry Authorization and Control","text":"Entry shall resume only after conditions have been reassessed, required controls restored, acceptable entry conditions reverified, and entry reauthorized."},{"id":"3.25.22","page":92,"heading":"Atmospheric Control and Safe Entry Operations","text":"The atmosphere of each confined space shall be tested before entry and monitored during entry at frequencies determined by the hazard assessment and entry plan, but not exceeding two hours. Testing shall address oxygen, flammable gases and vapors, toxic contaminants, and other identified atmospheric hazards, and shall be conducted for oxygen first, followed by flammable gases and vapors and then toxic contaminants, in accordance with OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146."},{"id":"3.25.23","page":92,"heading":"Atmospheric Control and Safe Entry Operations","text":"Acceptable entry conditions shall include an oxygen concentration from 19.5 to 22 percent, flammable gases and vapors below 10 percent of the lower flammable limit, and airborne contaminants within applicable occupational exposure limits in accordance with section 3.13 Airborne Contaminants and Chemical Exposure Control process."},{"id":"3.25.24","page":92,"heading":"Atmospheric Control and Safe Entry Operations","text":"Atmospheric testing and monitoring equipment shall be suitable for the identified hazards, maintained and calibrated in accordance with manufacturer requirements, and used by competent and authorized personnel."},{"id":"3.25.25","page":92,"heading":"Atmospheric Control and Safe Entry Operations","text":"Ventilation, purging, cleaning, or other controls shall be used where required to establish and maintain acceptable entry conditions without introducing additional hazards."},{"id":"3.25.26","page":92,"heading":"Atmospheric Control and Safe Entry Operations","text":"Inerted or oxygen deficient confined spaces shall be treated as immediately dangerous to life or health and entered only under approved respiratory protection, monitoring, communication and rescue controls."},{"id":"3.25.27","page":92,"heading":"Atmospheric Control and Safe Entry Operations","text":"Atmospheric testing and monitoring results shall be documented and communicated to the entry team."},{"id":"3.25.28","page":92,"heading":"Emergency, Rescue and Entry Completion","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish entry specific emergency and rescue arrangements (Rescue Plan) before entry begins, based on the confined space, access and egress, identified hazards, number of entrants, and foreseeable emergency conditions."},{"id":"3.25.29","page":92,"heading":"Emergency, Rescue and Entry Completion","text":"Confined space rescue services shall be competent, authorized, properly equipped, informed of the space hazards, provided access for planning and preparation, and capable of responding within the time required to protect entrants."},{"id":"3.25.30","page":92,"heading":"Emergency, Rescue and Entry Completion","text":"Non-entry rescue shall be used where practicable and where its use does not increase risk, in accordance with OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146."},{"id":"3.25.31","page":92,"heading":"Emergency, Rescue and Entry Completion","text":"Internal rescue teams shall practice confined space rescue at least once every 12 months using actual or representative confined spaces in accordance with OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146(k)."},{"id":"3.25.32","page":92,"heading":"Emergency, Rescue and Entry Completion","text":"Required rescue equipment, communication systems, first-aid capability, and medical support shall remain available throughout the entry."},{"id":"3.25.33","page":92,"heading":"Emergency, Rescue and Entry Completion","text":"On completion of the entry, all people shall be accounted for, temporary equipment and materials removed, and the space inspected. Isolations shall be restored under authorization before permit closure and return to service."},{"id":"3.25.34","page":92,"heading":"Entry Verification and Program Monitoring","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish risk-based verification arrangements to evaluate compliance with and effectiveness of confined space entry requirements."},{"id":"3.25.35","page":92,"heading":"Entry Verification and Program Monitoring","text":"Active confined space entries shall be inspected at defined intervals to verify compliance with the entry permit and required controls, including isolation, atmospheric conditions, entry team responsibilities, and rescue readiness."},{"id":"3.25.36","page":93,"heading":"Entry Verification and Program Monitoring","text":"Completed and suspended entries shall be reviewed where deviations, control failures, unexpected hazards, evacuations, incidents, near misses, or rescue activations occurred, and relevant findings communicated to affected parties."},{"id":"3.25.37","page":93,"heading":"Entry Verification and Program Monitoring","text":"Confined space events, including unauthorized entries, evacuations, atmospheric deviations, control failures, incidents, near misses, and rescue activations, shall be reported and managed through the section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship process."},{"id":"3.25.38","page":93,"heading":"Entry Verification and Program Monitoring","text":"Confined space performance shall be periodically evaluated using the results of entry inspections, permit reviews, atmospheric monitoring, incidents, control failures, rescue events, contractor performance and other relevant assurance activities, with identified deficiencies corrected and used to improve confined space arrangements."},{"id":"3.25.39","page":93,"heading":"Entry Verification and Program Monitoring","text":"Confined space permits, atmospheric monitoring results, inspection and review records, and records of significant events and corrective actions shall be maintained."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Confined Space Entry; confined space, tank entry, vessel entry, silo entry; Confined Space Management; Hazard Assessment and Entry Planning; Entry Authorization and Control; Atmospheric Control and Safe Entry Operations; Emergency, Rescue and Entry Completion; Entry Verification and Program Monitoring","trigger":"if entry into a tank, vessel, silo, pit, duct or other confined space is required","refs":["3.13","3.21","3.23","6.1"]},{"id":"3.26","title":"Lifting Operations","page":93,"purpose":"Lifting operations are planned, authorized, executed and verified so that loads remain controlled, lifting equipment remains within verified capacity and operating limits, and people, structures, plant and the environment are protected from dropped loads, unintended movement, collision, instability and equipment failure.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden owned, operated, managed or leased locations and to Maaden personnel, contractors, subcontractors and service providers involved in lifting operations. It covers lifting machines, lifting accessories, rigging and engineered lifting points used to raise, lower, suspend or support a load.","clauses":[{"id":"3.26.1","page":93,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain arrangements for managing lifting operations and lifting equipment."},{"id":"3.26.2","page":93,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Authority, responsibility and competency requirements shall be defined for personnel who plan, approve, supervise, operate, rig, signal, inspect, maintain or verify lifting operations and equipment."},{"id":"3.26.3","page":93,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Personnel shall only undertake a lifting role where they have been assessed as competent and, where applicable, formally authorized for the role and equipment concerned."},{"id":"3.26.4","page":93,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Contractor lifting personnel and equipment shall meet applicable Maaden requirements."},{"id":"3.26.5","page":93,"heading":"Lifting Equipment","text":"Lifting equipment, accessories and lifting points shall be suitable for their intended duty, load, configuration and operating environment and shall have identifiable rated capacities or working load limits."},{"id":"3.26.6","page":93,"heading":"Lifting Equipment","text":"New, hired, contractor provided, modified or relocated lifting equipment shall undergo an acceptance process before first use that verifies conformity with applicable Saudi legal and regulatory requirements, manufacturer requirements, and applicable international standards for the relevant type of lifting equipment including ASME B30 series."},{"id":"3.26.7","page":94,"heading":"Lifting Equipment","text":"Custom designed lifting equipment, attachments and lifting points shall be engineered, verified and marked with their rated capacity before use."},{"id":"3.26.8","page":94,"heading":"Lifting Equipment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain an inventory of lifting equipment and accessories requiring controlled inspection, testing, examination or maintenance."},{"id":"3.26.9","page":94,"heading":"Lifting Equipment","text":"Inspection, testing, examination and maintenance requirements and frequencies shall be established in accordance with applicable legal requirements, adopted technical standards, manufacturer requirements and equipment risk or service conditions."},{"id":"3.26.10","page":94,"heading":"Lifting Equipment","text":"Lifting equipment shall be inspected before use and removed from service if it is damaged or defective, has expired inspection or certification, has no identifiable capacity, or has any inoperable safety function."},{"id":"3.26.11","page":94,"heading":"Lifting Equipment","text":"Equipment involved in an overload, dropped load, collision, uncontrolled movement or other event that could affect its integrity shall be removed from service until its condition and suitability for return to service have been verified and authorized."},{"id":"3.26.12","page":94,"heading":"Lift Assessment and Classification","text":"Each lifting operation shall be assessed before execution to determine the required level of planning, supervision, technical review and authorization."},{"id":"3.26.13","page":94,"heading":"Lift Assessment and Classification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish documented criteria for classifying routine, non-routine, complex and critical lifts."},{"id":"3.26.14","page":94,"heading":"Lift Assessment and Classification","text":"As a minimum, the following shall be treated as critical lifts unless a more stringent Business Unit criterion applies:\na. lifts using more than one lifting machine;\nb. lifting of personnel;\nc. lifts over or adjacent to occupied areas, live plant, hazardous inventories or public areas;\nd. lifts within applicable approach distances of energized electrical conductors;\ne. lifts above 75% of available rated capacity at the planned configuration;\nf. lifts where load weight or center of gravity is not adequately verified;\ng. blind lifts or lifts with restricted operator visibility; and\nh. erection, dismantling or major reconfiguration of a lifting machine."},{"id":"3.26.15","page":94,"heading":"Lift Assessment and Classification","text":"A change in load, equipment, configuration, work area, personnel, environmental conditions or interacting activities shall trigger reassessment of the lifting classification and plan where the change may affect risk."},{"id":"3.26.16","page":94,"heading":"Lift Planning and Authorization","text":"A lifting plan shall be established before lifting begins. The extent and detail shall be proportionate to the lift classification, complexity and credible consequences."},{"id":"3.26.17","page":94,"heading":"Lift Planning and Authorization","text":"The lifting plan shall address, as applicable:\na. the load, weight, center of gravity, integrity and lifting points;\nb. lifting machine, configuration, capacity and operating radius;\nc. rigging arrangement;\nd. equipment positioning and supporting conditions;\ne. lift path and landing location;\nf. exclusion zones;\ng. communication and signaling;\nh. weather and environmental limits;\ni. electrical, process, traffic and adjacent work hazards;\nj. hold points and stop-work conditions;\nk. emergency or load recovery arrangements; and\nl. required supervision, authorization and interfaces with other work controls."},{"id":"3.26.18","page":95,"heading":"Lift Planning and Authorization","text":"Routine lifting shall be performed under an approved SOP/SMP or repeat lifting plan where the operation remains within its defined equipment, loads, configuration, location and operating limits."},{"id":"3.26.19","page":95,"heading":"Lift Planning and Authorization","text":"Non-routine, complex and critical lifting operations shall require an activity specific lifting plan reviewed and approved by personnel with appropriate authority and technical competence. As a minimum, lifting plan review and approval shall require Rigger Level 1 competency for Routing Lifts, Rigger Level 2 competency for Non-Routine Simple/Moderate Lifts, and Rigger Level 3 competency for Complex/critical Lifts."},{"id":"3.26.20","page":95,"heading":"Lift Planning and Authorization","text":"A lift shall not commence until responsible personnel have verified that the equipment, load, work area and required controls correspond with the approved plan."},{"id":"3.26.21","page":95,"heading":"Lift Planning and Authorization","text":"Where conditions deviate from the plan, the lift shall stop until the change is assessed and authorization reconfirmed."},{"id":"3.26.22","page":95,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Ground, foundations, supporting structures, underground conditions and adjacent slopes that could affect lifting equipment stability shall be assessed before setup."},{"id":"3.26.23","page":95,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Loads shall be rigged using equipment suitable for the load, lifting points, configuration and planned movement, with the weight, center of gravity, integrity and lifting points established before lifting."},{"id":"3.26.24","page":95,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Exclusion zones shall be established based on credible movement, swing, overturning, collision and dropped load hazards. Personnel shall not stand, pass or work beneath suspended loads."},{"id":"3.26.25","page":95,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Lifting operations shall use a defined communication method understood by the lifting team. Loss or misunderstanding of required communication shall result in the lift being stopped."},{"id":"3.26.26","page":95,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Erection, dismantling, major reconfiguration and relocation of lifting machines shall be performed as per the manufacturer’s requirements or an approved engineered method under the supervision of competent personnel."},{"id":"3.26.27","page":95,"heading":"Special and High Consequence Lifting","text":"Critical lifts shall be subject to enhanced technical, operational and authorization controls appropriate to the credible consequences."},{"id":"3.26.28","page":95,"heading":"Special and High Consequence Lifting","text":"Personnel shall not be lifted using lifting equipment where a safer means of access is reasonably practicable. Where personnel lifting is necessary, the equipment, carrier, fall protection, rescue arrangements and authorization shall meet comply with applicable legal and technical requirements, including OSHA 29 CFR 1926.1431 and ASME B30.23."},{"id":"3.26.29","page":95,"heading":"Special and High Consequence Lifting","text":"Lifting near electrical conductors shall comply with section 3.30 Electrical Safety requirements and applicable minimum approach distances. Electrical lines shall be treated as energized unless confirmed de-energized, isolated and earthed by the owning authority."},{"id":"3.26.30","page":96,"heading":"Special and High Consequence Lifting","text":"Lifting over or adjacent to operating plant, hazardous material inventories or critical equipment shall be eliminated where reasonably practicable. Where it cannot be eliminated, the lifting plan shall address the applicable loss of containment consequences, operational restrictions, isolation, process monitoring and emergency arrangements."},{"id":"3.26.31","page":96,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"Critical controls for lifting shall be identified, assigned performance and verification requirements and managed in accordance with Section 2.6 Critical Control Management."},{"id":"3.26.32","page":96,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"A lift shall not proceed where a required critical control is absent, ineffective or cannot be verified."},{"id":"3.26.33","page":96,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify implementation of lifting requirements through field observation, equipment inspections, lifting plan reviews and assurance activities."},{"id":"3.26.34","page":96,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"Lifting related defects, dropped loads, overloads, plan deviations, uncontrolled movements, near misses and incidents shall be reported."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Lifting Operations; lifting, crane, hoist, rigging, critical lift; Governance and Competency; Lifting Equipment; Lift Assessment and Classification; Lift Planning and Authorization; Operational Controls; Special and High-Consequence Lifting; Verification and Improvement","trigger":"if a crane, hoist, lifting appliance or rigging arrangement is used","refs":["2.6","3.30"]},{"id":"3.27","title":"Mobile Equipment Safety","page":96,"purpose":"Mobile equipment hazards are systematically identified and controlled so that mobile equipment is designed, selected, operated, maintained, inspected and managed to prevent fatalities, serious injuries, equipment related incidents and uncontrolled interactions with people, vehicles, infrastructure and operating areas.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden owned, operated, managed, leased or controlled locations and to Maaden personnel and contractors who operate, maintain, inspect or authorize mobile equipment. It covers heavy mobile equipment across its lifecycle, from selection to disposal.","clauses":[{"id":"3.27.1","page":96,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented arrangements for the selection, acceptance, operation, inspection, maintenance, modification, withdrawal from service and disposal of mobile equipment, proportionate to their equipment types, material risks and operating conditions."},{"id":"3.27.2","page":96,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Authority, responsibility and competency requirements shall be defined for personnel who operate, supervise, inspect, maintain, modify, authorize or verify mobile equipment."},{"id":"3.27.3","page":96,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Personnel shall only operate mobile equipment where they have been assessed as competent for the equipment category and operating environment concerned and formally authorized, subject to defined review and revalidation criteria in accordance with section 2.7 EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication."},{"id":"3.27.4","page":96,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Mobile Equipment Operators shall receive site specific induction and operating area orientation, and shall meet applicable fitness for work requirements."},{"id":"3.27.5","page":96,"heading":"Governance and Competency","text":"Contractor mobile equipment, operators and maintenance arrangements shall meet applicable Maaden requirements."},{"id":"3.27.6","page":96,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Mobile equipment shall be suitable for its intended duty, load, operating environment and terrain, and shall be operated and maintained in accordance with manufacturer requirements unless an approved engineering assessment establishes an equivalent or higher level of risk control. Powered industrial trucks shall comply with applicable requirements of OSHA 29 CFR 1910.178 and ANSI/ITSDF B56.1, or equivalent recognized standards."},{"id":"3.27.7","page":97,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Mobile equipment shall be fitted with the braking, restraint, warning, visibility, emergency control and unauthorized use prevention systems required to control its credible operating hazards, and with collision warning or proximity detection where required by risk assessment."},{"id":"3.27.8","page":97,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Where applicable, earth-moving equipment braking systems shall conform to ISO 3450 or equivalent; roll-over protective structures (ROPS) to ISO 3471 or equivalent; and falling object protective structures (FOPS) to ISO 3449, or ISO 10262 for hydraulic excavators, or equivalent. Safe access and egress shall be provided in accordance with ISO 2867 or equivalent."},{"id":"3.27.9","page":97,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Fire hazards associated with fuels, hydraulic systems, electrical systems, batteries, hot surfaces and combustible materials shall be assessed, and appropriate fire prevention, detection and suppression controls shall be provided based on equipment type and fire risk."},{"id":"3.27.10","page":97,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain a register of mobile equipment identifying equipment category and the safety critical systems and components subject to controlled inspection, testing or maintenance."},{"id":"3.27.11","page":97,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Inspection, testing and preventive maintenance programs shall be established, with frequencies for safety critical components determined based on manufacturer requirements, equipment duty, operating environment and risk, and records retained and readily accessible."},{"id":"3.27.12","page":97,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Mobile equipment shall be withdrawn from service and prevented from use where it is defective or damaged, has an expired inspection or certification, or has an inoperable safety critical system, with controls established for defect classification and return to service."},{"id":"3.27.13","page":97,"heading":"Mobile Equipment","text":"Changes to mobile equipment design, safety systems, operating envelope or operating conditions shall be assessed and managed in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change before implementation, verified before return to service and supported by manufacturer or competent engineering approval where equipment capacity, stability or safety critical systems are affected."},{"id":"3.27.14","page":97,"heading":"Assessment and Operating Limits","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess mobile equipment hazards and interactions with people, vehicles, equipment, infrastructure, process facilities, terrain and geotechnical features, and define operating controls addressing equipment fire, loss of load, towing or coupling failure, tyre or wheel failure, uncontrolled movement and other credible hazards."},{"id":"3.27.15","page":97,"heading":"Assessment and Operating Limits","text":"Documented safe operating limits and controls shall be established where rollover, collision, engulfment, ground failure, loss of control on grades, impact with structures or contact with energized services is credible."},{"id":"3.27.16","page":97,"heading":"Assessment and Operating Limits","text":"Mobile equipment activity falling outside established operating limits, procedures or designated operating areas shall be subject to task risk assessment and authorization in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment."},{"id":"3.27.17","page":97,"heading":"Interaction and Area Controls","text":"Interaction between mobile equipment and pedestrians or light vehicles shall be controlled through elimination, physical separation, engineered separation or equivalent verified controls."},{"id":"3.27.18","page":98,"heading":"Interaction and Area Controls","text":"Traffic management, route design, speed limits and right of way rules for areas in which mobile equipment operates shall be established in accordance with section 3.29 Traffic and Journey Management process."},{"id":"3.27.19","page":98,"heading":"Interaction and Area Controls","text":"Loading, dumping, reversing, parking, refueling and maintenance interface areas shall be designated and controlled."},{"id":"3.27.20","page":98,"heading":"Interaction and Area Controls","text":"Where flagman, spotters or ground personnel are used, their responsibilities, positioning, communication method and exclusion zones shall be defined."},{"id":"3.27.21","page":98,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Mobile equipment shall be inspected before use against defined criteria, with defects reported and any equipment that is unsafe removed from service before operation."},{"id":"3.27.22","page":98,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Mobile equipment shall be operated only by an authorized operator and only within its established operating limits, rated capacity and designated operating areas."},{"id":"3.27.23","page":98,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Seat restraints and required operator protection systems shall be used whenever the equipment is capable of movement."},{"id":"3.27.24","page":98,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Unattended mobile equipment shall be parked and secured against unintended movement using parking and immobilization controls."},{"id":"3.27.25","page":98,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Mobile equipment and raised implements shall maintain applicable minimum approach distances from energized electrical infrastructure in accordance with section 3.30 Electrical Safety."},{"id":"3.27.26","page":98,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Mobile equipment maintenance and repair shall be performed with the equipment isolated, stored energy controlled and raised components mechanically supported in accordance with sections 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment and 3.23 Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy."},{"id":"3.27.27","page":98,"heading":"Operational Controls","text":"Tire and wheel maintenance involving stored energy hazards, including multipiece or split-rim assemblies, shall be performed in accordance with section 3.37 Tire and Rim Management."},{"id":"3.27.28","page":98,"heading":"Underground Mobile Equipment","text":"Mobile equipment designed or intended for underground mining shall meet the applicable safety requirements of ISO 19296, Mining — Mobile Machines Working Underground — Machine Safety, or an equivalent recognized standard. Surface designed mobile equipment shall not be introduced underground unless its suitability for the underground environment has been assessed and any required modifications managed in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.27.29","page":98,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"Critical controls for mobile equipment shall be identified, assigned performance and verification requirements, and managed in accordance with section 2.6 Critical Control Management, with equipment prevented from operation where a required critical control is absent, ineffective or cannot be verified."},{"id":"3.27.30","page":98,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify implementation of this process through field observation, equipment inspection, authorization and competency checks and assurance activities."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Mobile Equipment Safety; mobile equipment, haul truck, loader, excavator, dozer; Governance and Competency; Mobile Equipment; Assessment and Operating Limits; Interaction and Area Controls; Operational Controls; Underground Mobile Equipment; Verification and Improvement","trigger":"if mobile mining, construction or industrial equipment is operated or interacted with","refs":["2.6","2.7","3.21","3.29","3.30","3.37","3.52"]},{"id":"3.28","title":"Light Vehicle Safety","page":99,"purpose":"Light vehicles used for Maaden work are fit for purpose, legally compliant and safely maintained, and that people who drive them are competent, fit and formally authorized, to prevent collisions, rollovers, loss of control, injury and property damage.","scope":"This process applies to road-going passengers and utility vehicles classified as light vehicles. It covers Maaden owned, leased, rented and contractor provided vehicles, and privately owned vehicles formally authorized for Maaden work, when operated on public roads, Maaden controlled premises or operational areas.","clauses":[{"id":"3.28.1","page":99,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Business Units and sites shall establish risk-based light vehicle safety arrangements that comply with applicable Saudi traffic law and vehicle registration, insurance, periodic technical inspection and SASO/GSO requirements."},{"id":"3.28.2","page":99,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Responsibilities shall be assigned for fleet safety, vehicle acceptance and authorization, driver authorization, inspection, maintenance and assurance."},{"id":"3.28.3","page":99,"heading":"Governance and Accountability","text":"Contractor arrangements shall be managed through section 4.1 Contractor EHSS Management and verified by the relevant Maaden contract owner."},{"id":"3.28.4","page":99,"heading":"Driver Authorization, Competence and Fitness","text":"A person shall not drive a light vehicle for Maaden work unless they hold a valid driving license legally recognized in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia for the relevant vehicle class and have been formally authorized by Maaden. Authorization shall verify:\na. Relevant driving experience and an acceptable driving record.\nb. Completion of Maaden and applicable site induction.\nc. Defensive driving training and practical competency assessment appropriate to the vehicle and operating environment.\nd. Awareness of applicable In-Vehicle Monitoring System (IVMS), traffic and journey management requirements.\ne. Additional competence for off-road, remote area or specialized driving.\nf. Current fitness to drive."},{"id":"3.28.5","page":99,"heading":"Driver Authorization, Competence and Fitness","text":"Training and competency assessments shall be delivered by competent people or approved providers. Renewal and reassessment intervals shall be established, documented and reviewed based on legal, regulatory and certification requirements, the level of risk, the complexity and frequency of the task."},{"id":"3.28.6","page":99,"heading":"Driver Authorization, Competence and Fitness","text":"Business Units and contractors shall maintain a current Authorized Driver Register showing permitted vehicle classes, authorization expiry and applicable route, time, operating or fitness related restrictions. Vehicle allocation shall provide traceability between the driver and vehicle used."},{"id":"3.28.7","page":99,"heading":"Driver Authorization, Competence and Fitness","text":"Drivers shall meet section 3.18 fitness for work requirements and shall not drive when affected by fatigue, alcohol, drugs, illness, injury, medication or another condition that could impair safe driving."},{"id":"3.28.8","page":99,"heading":"Driver Authorization, Competence and Fitness","text":"Driver authorization shall be reviewed, restricted, suspended or withdrawn following license expiry or suspension, a relevant fitness change, a serious or repeated traffic violation, a significant vehicle incident, demonstrated unsafe behavior or an extended absence from driving. Reinstatement shall require completion of specified corrective actions, reassessment where applicable and approval by the designated authority."},{"id":"3.28.9","page":100,"heading":"Vehicle Authorization and Safety Requirements","text":"Maaden owned, leased, rented and contractor light vehicles shall not be used for Maaden-controlled work until they have been formally accepted, authorized and recorded. Acceptance criteria shall address vehicle registration, insurance, statutory technical inspection, age, condition, maintenance status, and compliance with applicable Saudi requirements (SASO/GSO) and Maaden vehicle specifications. Authorization shall be reviewed at defined intervals and withdrawn when the acceptance criteria are no longer met"},{"id":"3.28.10","page":100,"heading":"Vehicle Authorization and Safety Requirements","text":"Light vehicles shall be selected and configured for the intended use, road and terrain conditions, environmental conditions, passenger capacity and load, and shall be operated within manufacturer limitations. Safety critical systems, including brakes, steering, tires, lights, mirrors, warning devices, occupant restraints and manufacturer- fitted protective systems, shall remain functional and shall not be removed, bypassed or defeated. Vehicles used off- road or in remote areas shall have the capability, tires, ground clearance and additional safety features identified through risk assessment."},{"id":"3.28.11","page":100,"heading":"Vehicle Authorization and Safety Requirements","text":"Modifications that may affect vehicle safety, structural integrity, handling, occupant protection or load capacity shall be approved by the vehicle manufacturer or assessed, engineered and certified by a competent engineering authority. Additional rollover protection shall only be installed where required by a Maaden specification or documented risk assessment and shall meet the same approval, engineering and certification requirements."},{"id":"3.28.12","page":100,"heading":"Vehicle Authorization and Safety Requirements","text":"Maaden owned, leased, rented and contractor light vehicles used for Maaden controlled work shall be fitted with an IVMS meeting Maaden specifications, unless an exception is supported by an approved risk assessment. The IVMS shall identify the driver, monitor the required driving and journey parameters, remain operational during vehicle use, and be protected against unauthorized interference or disabling. IVMS monitoring, alert escalation and response to identified violations shall be managed through section 3.29 Traffic and Journey Management process."},{"id":"3.28.13","page":100,"heading":"Inspection, Defect and Maintenance Control","text":"A documented pre-use inspection shall be completed before a vehicle is first used during each shift and following any event or change that may have affected its safe condition. Identified defects shall be reported and assessed before continued use. A vehicle with a safety critical defect shall be removed from service, secured against unauthorized use, and not returned to service until the defect has been corrected and the vehicle has been verified as safe by a competent person."},{"id":"3.28.14","page":100,"heading":"Inspection, Defect and Maintenance Control","text":"Vehicles shall be maintained under a preventive maintenance program based on manufacturer requirements, applicable legal requirements and operating conditions. Maintenance and repair work that may affect vehicle safety shall be performed by competent personnel using parts and methods that meet manufacturer specifications. Vehicles shall not be used when overdue for a legally required inspection or safety critical maintenance."},{"id":"3.28.15","page":100,"heading":"Safe Vehicle Operation","text":"Drivers shall comply with applicable traffic laws, approved routes, posted speed limits and site traffic rules, and shall adjust speed and vehicle operation to road, traffic, visibility, weather, vehicle and load conditions."},{"id":"3.28.16","page":100,"heading":"Safe Vehicle Operation","text":"Drivers and passengers shall occupy designated seats and wear functioning seat belts whenever the vehicle is moving. Seating and load limits shall not be exceeded, people shall not be carried in cargo areas, and cargo or loose items shall be secured so they do not obstruct safe vehicle operation or emergency egress."},{"id":"3.28.17","page":101,"heading":"Safe Vehicle Operation","text":"Drivers shall not use mobile phones or undertake other distracting activities while the vehicle is moving. Operational communication systems shall only be used where necessary for safe operations and without handling the device or compromising vehicle control. Other communication device use shall only occur when the vehicle is parked and secured."},{"id":"3.28.18","page":101,"heading":"Safe Vehicle Operation","text":"Vehicles shall be parked in authorized locations, secured against unintended movement and positioned without obstructing emergency routes, equipment or access. Before leaving a vehicle unattended, the engine shall be stopped, the parking brake applied and additional securing measures used where conditions require. Vehicles shall not be refueled while the engine is running or where ignition sources could cause a fire or explosion."},{"id":"3.28.19","page":101,"heading":"Safe Vehicle Operation","text":"Following a collision, breakdown or vehicle emergency, the driver shall stop safely, request assistance, take reasonable measures to prevent further harm and follow applicable emergency response and security arrangements. Incidents and near misses shall be reported and managed in accordance with section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship."},{"id":"3.28.20","page":101,"heading":"Safe Vehicle Operation","text":"Records necessary to demonstrate driver authorization, vehicle fitness and implementation of applicable light- vehicle controls shall be maintained and retained in accordance with applicable legal and regulatory requirements."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Light Vehicle Safety; light vehicle, pickup, car, bus, driver; Governance and Accountability; Driver Authorization, Competence and Fitness; Vehicle Authorization and Safety Requirements; Inspection, Defect and Maintenance Control; Safe Vehicle Operation; Records","trigger":"if light vehicles are driven, inspected, maintained or used for personnel travel","refs":["3.18","3.29","4.1","6.1"]},{"id":"3.29","title":"Traffic and Journey Management","page":101,"purpose":"Manage risks arising from the movement and interaction of vehicles, mobile equipment and pedestrians, and from work- related road journeys, through effective traffic planning, route controls, journey risk management, monitoring and emergency arrangements.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden Business Units, Corporate Functions and controlled workplaces where light vehicles, road- going trucks, buses, coaches or mobile equipment interact with one another, pedestrians or rail movements. It also applies to work-related road journeys undertaken under Maaden operational control on public roads, site roads, access roads and remote or off-road routes.","clauses":[{"id":"3.29.1","page":101,"heading":"Management Arrangements and Responsibilities","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions that manage workplace traffic, road journeys or land transport activities shall establish risk-based traffic and journey management arrangements applicable to their activities. The arrangements shall comply with applicable Saudi legal and road authority requirements. ISO 39001, IOGP Report 365 and HSE HSG136 shall be considered as technical references."},{"id":"3.29.2","page":101,"heading":"Management Arrangements and Responsibilities","text":"Plant roads and access routes shall comply with applicable SAIS SAF-06 requirements; mine and mineral processing facility roads with applicable SAIS SAF-18 requirements; and mining-site traffic controls with applicable provisions of MSHA 30 CFR Part 56, Subpart H for surface mines and Part 57, Subpart H for underground mines, as technical references."},{"id":"3.29.3","page":101,"heading":"Management Arrangements and Responsibilities","text":"Responsibilities shall be assigned to competent personnel for road and area ownership, Traffic Management Plan development, approval and implementation, road inspection and maintenance, journey screening, approval and monitoring, overdue journey escalation, corrective action, and traffic controller, spotter and flagger duties."},{"id":"3.29.4","page":102,"heading":"Management Arrangements and Responsibilities","text":"Fatal or material unwanted events involving vehicle interaction, loss of control, rollover or work-related journeys and their associated critical controls shall be managed and verified through the applicable MSHEM EHSS Risk Management and Critical Control Management processes."},{"id":"3.29.5","page":102,"heading":"Traffic Management Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish documented traffic controls proportionate to its traffic risks. A Traffic Management Plan shall be established where documented risk assessment identifies significant risks from interactions between pedestrians, vehicles, mobile equipment or rail; traffic density, reversing or restricted visibility or clearance; loading, unloading or logistics activities; or temporary or permanent changes to roads, routes or work areas."},{"id":"3.29.6","page":102,"heading":"Traffic Management Planning","text":"Traffic Management Plans shall be based on documented risk assessment and developed with input from competent people representing affected operations and relevant engineering, EHSS, security and emergency response functions. Plans shall be approved by the person accountable for the affected site or area, maintained current and communicated to affected employees, contractors and visitors before exposure to the traffic risks."},{"id":"3.29.7","page":102,"heading":"Traffic Management Planning","text":"Traffic Management Plans shall define, as applicable, traffic routes, classifications and access restrictions; direction, speed and right of way rules; controls for intersections, crossings, rail interfaces and restricted clearances; separation of pedestrians, light vehicles and mobile equipment; parking, loading, unloading and passenger transfer areas; reversing and communication controls; and emergency access and escape routes."},{"id":"3.29.8","page":102,"heading":"Traffic Management Planning","text":"Traffic controls shall prioritize elimination of unnecessary movement and physical separation of pedestrians, light vehicles and mobile equipment. Where physical separation cannot be achieved, the reasons shall be documented and access control, time separation, traffic sequencing or other risk reduction controls shall be implemented."},{"id":"3.29.9","page":102,"heading":"Road and Traffic Controls","text":"Roads and traffic routes shall be designed, constructed and maintained for the vehicles, loads and traffic volumes using them. Road design shall address geometry, surface and drainage, visibility, lighting, signs, intersections, clearances and emergency access. Effective edge protection or restraints shall be provided where drop-offs, dumping activities or overtravel could result in a vehicle overturning. Road and rail crossings shall be controlled to prevent collisions."},{"id":"3.29.10","page":102,"heading":"Road and Traffic Controls","text":"Roads and traffic routes shall be inspected at defined intervals and after events or changes that could affect their safety, with identified hazards corrected and affected routes restricted or closed until verified safe for use."},{"id":"3.29.11","page":102,"heading":"Road and Traffic Controls","text":"Site traffic rules and arrangements shall control speed, right-of-way, direction of travel, parking, overtaking, communication and reversing; provide appropriate traffic signs, signals, road markings and pedestrian crossings; minimize conflicting vehicle, equipment and pedestrian movements; and ensure temporary or materially changed routes are risk assessed, authorized, marked, communicated and verified before use."},{"id":"3.29.12","page":102,"heading":"Road and Traffic Controls","text":"Mine traffic arrangements shall establish controls for interactions among heavy equipment, light vehicles and pedestrians and for access to mining areas, haul and service routes, restricted clearance areas, parking and refuge areas, and maintenance or contractor activities. Pedestrians shall only enter mine traffic routes through designated walkways or specifically controlled work arrangements."},{"id":"3.29.13","page":103,"heading":"Journey Management","text":"Work-related journeys shall be evaluated to eliminate or reduce unnecessary travel and identify journeys requiring a documented Journey Management Plan. The criteria shall consider remoteness, route familiarity, journey duration, night or off-road travel, adverse conditions, communication and emergency support limitations, lone travel and security risk. Repeated journeys under stable conditions may be managed through a standing journey plan that defines its applicability, controls, review and change triggers."},{"id":"3.29.14","page":103,"heading":"Journey Management","text":"A Journey Management Plan shall define the journey, route, driver, vehicle, passengers and load; applicable hazards and controls; driving and rest arrangements; communication and monitoring requirements; and emergency, recovery and overdue journey arrangements. The journey shall be authorized only after confirming that the driver, vehicle, route, conditions and support arrangements meet applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.29.15","page":103,"heading":"Journey Management","text":"Drivers shall comply with the authorized route, driving, rest, communication and check-in arrangements. A journey shall not start or continue when conditions exceed established limits or the driver is fatigued or otherwise unable to drive safely. Significant changes or deviations shall be communicated and reassessed before the journey continues, except where immediate action is necessary to reach a safe location."},{"id":"3.29.16","page":103,"heading":"Journey Management","text":"Journeys requiring a Journey Management Plan shall be monitored to the level determined by risk, including required check-ins, significant deviations and confirmation of safe arrival. IVMS or another reliable tracking method shall be used where required by the journey risk assessment and shall remain functional and monitored for the duration of the journey. Missed check-ins, overdue arrival, loss of tracking or significant alerts shall trigger defined contact and escalation actions. Remote journeys shall have communication, emergency and recovery arrangements based on the route, environmental conditions and expected response time."},{"id":"3.29.17","page":103,"heading":"Emergency, Violation and Performance Management","text":"Traffic and journey emergency arrangements shall address foreseeable vehicle and journey emergencies and align with section 5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning process. Vehicle recovery shall be planned and controlled to protect people from vehicle movement, stored energy and recovery forces."},{"id":"3.29.18","page":103,"heading":"Emergency, Violation and Performance Management","text":"Business Units and sites shall manage traffic violations and IVMS events, including validation, communication and proportionate action. Serious or repeated violations shall trigger review and, where necessary, restriction, suspension or withdrawal of driving or site access authorization. The effectiveness of traffic and journey controls shall be evaluated using relevant inspection, verification, IVMS, journey, incident and violation information."},{"id":"3.29.19","page":103,"heading":"Emergency, Violation and Performance Management","text":"Records necessary to demonstrate implementation of traffic and journey controls shall be maintained and retained in accordance with applicable legal and regulatory requirements."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Traffic and Journey Management; traffic, journey, road, travel, pedestrian; Management Arrangements and Responsibilities; Traffic Management Planning; Road and Traffic Controls; Journey Management; Emergency, Violation and Performance Management","trigger":"if site traffic, public-road travel, journey planning or vehicle–pedestrian interaction is involved","refs":["5.1"]},{"id":"3.30","title":"Electrical Safety","page":103,"purpose":"Electrical hazards are identified, assessed, and controlled to prevent fatalities, injuries, occupational illnesses, fires, explosions, and asset damage arising from electrical systems, equipment, and work activities.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden owned, operated, managed, leased, or controlled facilities, projects, and activities where electrical systems, equipment, or electrical hazards are present. It applies to employees, contractors, and other people who perform electrical work or may be exposed to electrical hazards under Maaden's control.","clauses":[{"id":"3.30.1","page":104,"heading":"Electrical Safety Governance and Program","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement, maintain, and periodically review an Electrical Safety Program based on the nature and level of electrical risk. The program shall define roles, responsibilities, and authorization requirements, competency requirements, electrical risk controls and safe work requirements, inspection, testing, and maintenance arrangements, and arrangements for evaluating program performance and improving electrical safety."},{"id":"3.30.2","page":104,"heading":"Electrical Safety Governance and Program","text":"Electrical hazards shall be identified, assessed, and controlled in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.30.3","page":104,"heading":"Electrical Safety Governance and Program","text":"Electrical work shall only be performed by personnel who are competent and authorized for the type and scope of work."},{"id":"3.30.4","page":104,"heading":"Electrical Safety Governance and Program","text":"Changes to electrical systems, equipment, operating conditions, electrical area classifications, procedures, or work practices that may affect electrical risk shall be managed in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.30.5","page":104,"heading":"Electrical Safety Governance and Program","text":"Relevant lessons from electrical incidents, equipment failures, audits, inspections, assessments, and operating experience shall be communicated and incorporated into electrical risk controls and work practices."},{"id":"3.30.6","page":104,"heading":"Planning and Control of Electrical Work","text":"Electrical work shall be planned, risk assessed, authorized, coordinated, and supervised based on the nature and level of electrical risk and in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process."},{"id":"3.30.7","page":104,"heading":"Planning and Control of Electrical Work","text":"Electrical equipment and circuits shall be de-energized and placed in an electrically safe work condition before work begins, unless de-energization would introduce a greater hazard or the work cannot be performed in a de-energized state."},{"id":"3.30.8","page":104,"heading":"Planning and Control of Electrical Work","text":"Where energized electrical work is permitted, it shall require documented justification, a task specific risk assessment, authorization by designated personnel, and the permits required by section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process."},{"id":"3.30.9","page":104,"heading":"Planning and Control of Electrical Work","text":"Controls identified through the risk assessment shall be implemented to protect personnel from electric shock, arc flash, arc blast, inadvertent contact, unauthorized access, and other electrical hazards."},{"id":"3.30.10","page":104,"heading":"Electrical Isolation and Establishment of an Electrically Safe Work Condition","text":"Electrical work shall be performed with the equipment de-energized and placed in an electrically safe work condition, unless energized work is justified and controlled in accordance with the energized electrical work requirements of this process."},{"id":"3.30.11","page":104,"heading":"Electrical Isolation and Establishment of an Electrically Safe Work Condition","text":"Electrical energy isolation shall be performed in accordance with section 3.23 Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy process."},{"id":"3.30.12","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Isolation and Establishment of an Electrically Safe Work Condition","text":"Electrical equipment shall not be considered to be in an electrically safe work condition until all applicable electrical energy sources have been identified and isolated, stored electrical energy has been controlled, and absence of voltage has been verified. Generated, induced, and back-fed energy, including energy from batteries, uninterruptible power supplies, photovoltaic systems, and capacitors, shall be considered."},{"id":"3.30.13","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Isolation and Establishment of an Electrically Safe Work Condition","text":"Temporary protective grounding shall be applied where identified as necessary to control induced voltage, backfeed, residual electrical energy, or inadvertent energization."},{"id":"3.30.14","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Hazard Controls","text":"Electrical shock, arc flash, arc blast, fire, explosion, lightning, static electricity, stray current, and other applicable electrical hazards shall be identified and controlled. Electrical hazards shall be eliminated where practicable, with priority given to engineering controls where elimination is not practicable."},{"id":"3.30.15","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Hazard Controls","text":"Electrical studies shall be completed for systems where required to identify electrical hazards, evaluate system performance, and determine necessary protective measures. Study scope shall be based on the system characteristics, electrical hazards, operating conditions, and applicable legal, regulatory, and adopted technical requirements."},{"id":"3.30.16","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Hazard Controls","text":"Electrical studies shall be reviewed and updated where necessary following changes to system configuration, fault- current levels, protective devices or settings, operating conditions, significant equipment modifications, incidents, equipment failures, or other evidence that the study data or assumptions may no longer be valid."},{"id":"3.30.17","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Hazard Controls","text":"Shock protection boundaries and arc-flash boundaries shall be established, maintained, and applied in accordance with NFPA 70E Article 130, including Tables 130.4(E)(a) and 130.4(E)(b) and Article 130.5. Access to exposed energized electrical conductors or circuit parts shall be controlled in accordance with the applicable boundaries."},{"id":"3.30.18","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Hazard Controls","text":"Electrical equipment that may expose personnel to electrical hazards shall be identified and labelled or marked in accordance with NFPA 70E and other applicable legal and regulatory requirements."},{"id":"3.30.19","page":105,"heading":"Electrical Hazard Controls","text":"Grounding and bonding systems shall be installed, inspected, tested, and maintained. Their integrity and effectiveness shall be verified before initial service, following modifications or conditions that may affect their performance, and at defined intervals."},{"id":"3.30.20","page":105,"heading":"Hazardous Areas, Static Electricity, Lightning and Stray Currents","text":"Hazardous area classifications shall be determined, documented, maintained, and reviewed when changes or new information may affect the classification or ignition risk."},{"id":"3.30.21","page":105,"heading":"Hazardous Areas, Static Electricity, Lightning and Stray Currents","text":"Electrical and electronic equipment installed or used in hazardous classified areas shall comply with the applicable area classification and operating conditions."},{"id":"3.30.22","page":105,"heading":"Hazardous Areas, Static Electricity, Lightning and Stray Currents","text":"Activities involving flammable or combustible materials shall control ignition risks from static electricity and stray currents through bonding, grounding, equipment selection, and operating controls necessary to prevent hazardous electrical discharge."},{"id":"3.30.23","page":105,"heading":"Hazardous Areas, Static Electricity, Lightning and Stray Currents","text":"Lightning protection, grounding, and bonding systems shall be provided, inspected, tested, and maintained where required by the risk assessment or applicable legal, regulatory, permit, or Maaden adopted technical requirements."},{"id":"3.30.24","page":106,"heading":"Overhead and Underground Electrical Services","text":"Overhead power lines and underground electrical services shall be identified and controlled to prevent electrical contact, encroachment, mechanical damage, and unintended exposure or energization."},{"id":"3.30.25","page":106,"heading":"Overhead and Underground Electrical Services","text":"Work involving cranes, mobile equipment, lifting operations, elevated work platforms, scaffolding, temporary structures, or other activities near energized overhead power lines shall be planned and controlled so that no person, equipment, load, attachment, or material encroaches within the applicable minimum clearance distance. For cranes and derricks in construction, minimum clearance distances shall comply with OSHA 29 CFR 1926 Subpart CC and applicable utility owner requirements."},{"id":"3.30.26","page":106,"heading":"Overhead and Underground Electrical Services","text":"Where the applicable minimum clearance distance cannot be maintained, the overhead power line shall be de- energized, isolated, verified as de-energized, and grounded where required, or relocated or protected by an approved engineered means before work begins. These controls shall be established in coordination with the utility owner or person responsible for the electrical system."},{"id":"3.30.27","page":106,"heading":"Overhead and Underground Electrical Services","text":"Before excavation, trenching, drilling, or other ground disturbance begins, the presence, location, route, and where relevant, status of underground electrical services shall be verified and the services protected from contact or damage in accordance with section 3.34 Excavation and Trenching process."},{"id":"3.30.28","page":106,"heading":"Electrical Equipment Management","text":"Electrical equipment, grounding, bonding, lightning protection, and other electrical protective systems shall be inspected, tested, and maintained in accordance with section 3.50 Asset Integrity Management process."},{"id":"3.30.29","page":106,"heading":"Tools, Equipment and PPE","text":"Electrical PPE shall be selected for the identified electrical hazards and exposure levels and managed in accordance with section 3.31 Personal Protective Equipment process."},{"id":"3.30.30","page":106,"heading":"Tools, Equipment and PPE","text":"Electrical tools, test instruments, and equipment shall be rated for the electrical hazard, intended task, and operating conditions in which they are used."},{"id":"3.30.31","page":106,"heading":"Tools, Equipment and PPE","text":"Electrical tools, test instruments and safety equipment shall be inspected, tested, maintained and, where required, calibrated in accordance with manufacturer instructions and adopted electrical safety requirements, with defective or out of calibration items removed from service."},{"id":"3.30.32","page":106,"heading":"Competency and Authorization","text":"Personnel shall perform only electrical work for which they are competent, qualified where required, and currently authorized."},{"id":"3.30.33","page":106,"heading":"Competency and Authorization","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain a documented electrical authorization system defining work categories, competency and qualification requirements, authorization scope and limits, approval authority, validity, reassessment, suspension and withdrawal."},{"id":"3.30.34","page":106,"heading":"Competency and Authorization","text":"Current authorization for the type and scope of electrical work shall be verified before work begins and remain readily available to personnel assigning, supervising or authorizing the work."},{"id":"3.30.35","page":106,"heading":"Competency and Authorization","text":"Contractor personnel shall comply with the same competency, qualification and authorization requirements as Maaden personnel for equivalent electrical work."},{"id":"3.30.36","page":107,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness","text":"Credible electrical emergency scenarios, including electric shock, arc flash, arc blast, battery incidents and electrical fires, shall be identified and addressed in applicable emergency preparedness and response plans."},{"id":"3.30.37","page":107,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness","text":"Personnel who may encounter an electrical emergency shall receive relevant emergency awareness, with personnel assigned response responsibilities trained and competent for their roles."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Electrical Safety; electrical, switching, testing, arc flash, temporary power; Electrical Safety Governance and Program; Planning and Control of Electrical Work; Electrical Isolation and Establishment of an Electrically Safe Work Condition; Electrical Hazard Controls; Hazardous Areas, Static Electricity, Lightning and Stray Currents; Overhead and Underground Electrical Services; Electrical Equipment Management; Tools, Equipment and PPE; Competency and Authorization; Emergency Preparedness","trigger":"if electrical operation, switching, testing, maintenance or temporary power is involved","refs":["2.5","3.21","3.23","3.31","3.34","3.50","3.52"]},{"id":"3.31","title":"Personal Protective Equipment","page":107,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for the assessment, selection, provision, use, care, and control of personal protective equipment to protect people from hazards that cannot be eliminated or adequately controlled by other measures.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions, employees, contractors, subcontractors, visitors, and other personnel required to use personal protective equipment or protective clothing during Maaden controlled activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.31.1","page":107,"heading":"PPE Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, document, and maintain PPE arrangements that define responsibilities for the assessment, selection, approval, procurement, provision, use, inspection, maintenance, storage, replacement, and control of PPE."},{"id":"3.31.2","page":107,"heading":"PPE Management","text":"PPE shall be selected and used in accordance with the hierarchy of controls where hazards or exposures cannot be eliminated or sufficiently controlled through higher level controls."},{"id":"3.31.3","page":107,"heading":"PPE Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide required PPE to their employees without charge and shall ensure that contractors provide required PPE to their personnel without charge."},{"id":"3.31.4","page":107,"heading":"PPE Management","text":"Employees, contractors, subcontractors, visitors, and other affected personnel shall use, inspect, care for, and report defects in PPE in accordance with applicable requirements and provided instructions."},{"id":"3.31.5","page":107,"heading":"PPE Management","text":"PPE selection and use shall be coordinated with relevant hazard and exposure assessments, work control processes, contractor arrangements, emergency response requirements, and applicable specialist protection processes."},{"id":"3.31.6","page":107,"heading":"PPE Management","text":"PPE arrangements and requirements shall be reviewed and updated when changes, incidents, findings, performance results, or other information indicate that PPE controls may no longer be effective or compliant."},{"id":"3.31.7","page":107,"heading":"PPE Hazard Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess workplaces and activities to identify hazards requiring PPE, considering affected personnel, exposed body areas, exposure conditions, and routine, non-routine, emergency, and foreseeable abnormal activities."},{"id":"3.31.8","page":107,"heading":"PPE Hazard Assessment","text":"The assessment shall consider applicable physical, chemical, biological, electrical, thermal, radiation, noise, impact, fall, and airborne contaminant hazards."},{"id":"3.31.9","page":107,"heading":"PPE Hazard Assessment","text":"Combined hazards and interactions or incompatibilities among required PPE shall be assessed."},{"id":"3.31.10","page":108,"heading":"PPE Hazard Assessment","text":"PPE hazard assessments and resulting PPE requirements shall be documented, communicated to affected personnel and relevant functions, and reviewed when changes could affect their validity or effectiveness."},{"id":"3.31.11","page":108,"heading":"PPE Selection and Approval","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall select and approve PPE based on the documented hazard assessment, required protection level, and applicable legal, regulatory, and adopted technical requirements."},{"id":"3.31.12","page":108,"heading":"PPE Selection and Approval","text":"Selected PPE shall be appropriate for the task, user, hazards, exposure conditions, environment, and duration of use; properly fit the user; be compatible with other required equipment; and not reduce protection, introduce additional hazards, or unreasonably impair safe task performance."},{"id":"3.31.13","page":108,"heading":"PPE Selection and Approval","text":"Approved PPE requirements, protection levels, specifications, combinations, and permitted alternatives shall be documented and communicated to affected personnel and relevant functions."},{"id":"3.31.14","page":108,"heading":"PPE Selection and Approval","text":"Electrical and arc-rated PPE shall be selected according to the assessed shock and arc-flash hazards in accordance with NFPA 70E, Standard for Electrical Safety in the Workplace, and applicable adopted ASTM and IEC product standards."},{"id":"3.31.15","page":108,"heading":"PPE Selection and Approval","text":"Eye, face, head, hand, foot, and high-visibility PPE shall comply with Saudi legal and regulatory requirements, including applicable SASO technical regulations and standards, and OSHA 29 CFR part 1910 Subpart I."},{"id":"3.31.16","page":108,"heading":"PPE Selection and Approval","text":"Respiratory protection, hearing protection, fall protection PPE, chemical and biological protective PPE, and fire and emergency response PPE shall comply with applicable MSHEM processes and adopted technical standards. Any substitution or deviation from an approved PPE specification shall be assessed and approved before use."},{"id":"3.31.17","page":108,"heading":"PPE Conformity, Procurement and Acceptance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall procure PPE that meets approved specifications, the applicable SASO Technical Regulation for Personal Protective Equipment and Clothing, and applicable adopted product standards."},{"id":"3.31.18","page":108,"heading":"PPE Conformity, Procurement and Acceptance","text":"Before PPE is accepted, compliance with applicable conformity assessment requirements shall be verified through required certificates, declarations of conformity, technical documentation, product markings, labels, warnings, and manufacturer instructions."},{"id":"3.31.19","page":108,"heading":"PPE Conformity, Procurement and Acceptance","text":"PPE shall be obtained from approved sources and shall be identifiable and traceable to the manufacturer, supplier, product model, and applicable conformity documentation where required."},{"id":"3.31.20","page":108,"heading":"PPE Conformity, Procurement and Acceptance","text":"PPE shall be inspected upon receipt or before acceptance to confirm that it matches the approved specification, is complete and undamaged, is within its usable service life, and has the required documentation and markings."},{"id":"3.31.21","page":108,"heading":"PPE Conformity, Procurement and Acceptance","text":"Contractor provided and personally owned PPE shall meet the same applicable selection, conformity, acceptance, and product standard requirements as PPE provided by Maaden."},{"id":"3.31.22","page":108,"heading":"PPE Conformity, Procurement and Acceptance","text":"Counterfeit, recalled, expired, unauthorizedly modified, or otherwise nonconforming PPE shall not be accepted, issued, or used and shall be controlled to prevent unintended use."},{"id":"3.31.23","page":108,"heading":"PPE Provision, Use and Specialized Protection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that approved PPE of the required type, protection level, size, and configuration is available before exposure. Work shall not proceed or continue when required PPE is unavailable or unable to provide the required protection."},{"id":"3.31.24","page":109,"heading":"PPE Provision, Use and Specialized Protection","text":"PPE shall be individually assigned where hygiene, fit, traceability, or manufacturer requirements prohibit shared use. Where sharing is permitted, PPE shall be cleaned, disinfected, inspected, and controlled before reissue, as applicable."},{"id":"3.31.25","page":109,"heading":"PPE Provision, Use and Specialized Protection","text":"Affected personnel shall use, wear, and adjust designated PPE in accordance with established requirements, training, and manufacturer instructions. PPE shall not be shared where prohibited, used for unintended purposes, or modified without approval."},{"id":"3.31.26","page":109,"heading":"PPE Provision, Use and Specialized Protection","text":"People responsible for supervising or controlling an activity shall verify throughout the activity that required PPE is available, correctly used, functional, and capable of providing the required protection."},{"id":"3.31.27","page":109,"heading":"PPE Provision, Use and Specialized Protection","text":"Specialized PPE shall be provided and used in accordance with the applicable MSHEM processes and adopted technical standards for respiratory, hearing, electrical, arc-flash, fall, chemical, biological, thermal, fire, and emergency response protection."},{"id":"3.31.28","page":109,"heading":"PPE Provision, Use and Specialized Protection","text":"Breathing air supplied to respiratory protective equipment shall meet at least Grade D breathing-air quality in accordance with ANSI/CGA G-7.1 or an equivalent adopted standard."},{"id":"3.31.29","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that personnel are trained and competent before using required PPE. Training shall address when PPE is required, correct fitting and use, limitations, inspection, care, storage, replacement, and disposal, consistent with OSHA 29 CFR 1910.132, General Requirements, manufacturer instructions, and applicable adopted standards."},{"id":"3.31.30","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"Personnel competence shall be reassessed, and additional training provided where required, when hazards, tasks, PPE, or workplace conditions change, or when inadequate knowledge or incorrect use is identified."},{"id":"3.31.31","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"PPE shall be inspected before use and at defined intervals based on the PPE type, conditions of use, exposure, manufacturer instructions, and applicable adopted standards."},{"id":"3.31.32","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"PPE shall be cleaned, decontaminated, maintained, and stored to preserve its hygiene, integrity, and protective performance."},{"id":"3.31.33","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"PPE subject to identification, inspection status, service life, expiry, component replacement, or consumable requirements shall be controlled to ensure continued protective performance."},{"id":"3.31.34","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"PPE shall be repaired or modified only where permitted by the manufacturer, applicable product certification, and adopted standards, and through authorized arrangements."},{"id":"3.31.35","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"Defective, damaged, contaminated, expired, recalled, or otherwise ineffective PPE shall be removed from service, controlled against unintended use, and repaired, replaced, or disposed of as applicable."},{"id":"3.31.36","page":109,"heading":"PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control","text":"Training, competency, inspection, maintenance, repair, and replacement records shall be maintained where required by applicable requirements, manufacturer instructions, adopted standards, or the PPE arrangements."},{"id":"3.31.37","page":109,"heading":"PPE Verification and Performance Monitoring","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify before and during work that required PPE is available, suitable for the identified hazard, exposure, task, and user, properly fitted, serviceable, and correctly used."},{"id":"3.31.38","page":110,"heading":"PPE Verification and Performance Monitoring","text":"PPE availability, use, contractor compliance, storage, maintenance, inspection, and lifecycle controls shall be monitored at defined intervals based on risk, operating conditions, and performance, with records maintained where required."},{"id":"3.31.39","page":110,"heading":"PPE Verification and Performance Monitoring","text":"PPE incidents, exposure events, defects, failures, misuse, incompatibility, recalls and user concerns shall be reported and reviewed, with PPE requirements reassessed where protective performance may be affected and identified deficiencies corrected, tracked and verified for effective closure."},{"id":"3.31.40","page":110,"heading":"PPE Verification and Performance Monitoring","text":"PPE performance, monitoring results, recurring deficiencies and management arrangements shall be periodically reviewed to identify improvements and opportunities to eliminate hazards or reduce reliance on PPE through higher level controls."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Personal Protective Equipment; personal protective equipment; PPE Management; PPE Hazard Assessment; PPE Selection and Approval; PPE Conformity, Procurement and Acceptance; PPE Provision, Use and Specialized Protection; PPE Training, Inspection and Lifecycle Control; PPE Verification and Performance Monitoring","trigger":"if PPE is required as part of the selected control measures","refs":[]},{"id":"3.32","title":"Hand and Power Tool Safety","page":110,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for the selection, acquisition, inspection, maintenance, storage, transport and safe use of hand and power tools to prevent injuries, occupational illnesses, equipment damage and tool related incidents.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions where hand tools or portable power tools are selected, acquired, supplied, inspected, maintained, stored, transported or used. It covers electric, battery-powered, pneumatic and hydraulic tools and their accessories, attachments, guards, power supplies, cords, plugs, batteries, chargers, hoses, fittings and couplings. It applies to Maaden personnel and contractors who select, use, supervise, inspect, maintain or may be exposed to hazards from these tools.","clauses":[{"id":"3.32.1","page":110,"heading":"Hand and Power Tool Management","text":"Hand and power tools, including accessories and attachments, shall be selected and used only when they are compatible, rated for the intended task and operating environment, and used within the limits specified by the manufacturer."},{"id":"3.32.2","page":110,"heading":"Hand and Power Tool Management","text":"Tools shall be procured, hired or accepted for use only when applicable product conformity or certification, operating instructions and safety information are available and verifiable."},{"id":"3.32.3","page":110,"heading":"Hand and Power Tool Management","text":"Homemade tools and unauthorized modifications to tools, accessories, attachments, guards, controls or safety devices shall be prohibited."},{"id":"3.32.4","page":110,"heading":"Hand and Power Tool Management","text":"Before work commences, hazards associated with hand and power tool activities shall be assessed and controlled in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process. Tools used where electrical insulation, non-conductivity, non-sparking construction or hazardous area certification is required shall meet the applicable requirements for those conditions."},{"id":"3.32.5","page":110,"heading":"Inspection, Maintenance and Defect Control","text":"Hand and power tools, including accessories and attachments, shall be inspected before use and periodically inspected, tested and maintained at frequencies based on tool type, risk, service conditions, frequency of use, manufacturer requirements and operating experience."},{"id":"3.32.6","page":110,"heading":"Inspection, Maintenance and Defect Control","text":"Tools requiring certification, specialist inspection, testing or other safety critical periodic verification shall be uniquely identified and included in a controlled inventory showing their current status."},{"id":"3.32.7","page":111,"heading":"Inspection, Maintenance and Defect Control","text":"Inspections and tests shall verify the condition and operation of components and safety devices that could affect safe use."},{"id":"3.32.8","page":111,"heading":"Inspection, Maintenance and Defect Control","text":"Inspection, testing, maintenance and repair requiring specialist knowledge or authorization shall be performed by competent and, where required, authorized personnel."},{"id":"3.32.9","page":111,"heading":"Inspection, Maintenance and Defect Control","text":"Defective, damaged, deteriorated, unauthorized, modified or otherwise unsafe tools shall be removed from service and prevented from unintended use until repaired and confirmed safe through the required inspection, testing or recertification."},{"id":"3.32.10","page":111,"heading":"Guards, Controls and Accessories","text":"Guards, handles, controls, interlocks, retaining devices and other safety features shall be installed, maintained and used as designed, without being removed, bypassed, defeated or otherwise made ineffective."},{"id":"3.32.11","page":111,"heading":"Guards, Controls and Accessories","text":"Abrasive wheels, cutting discs, wire wheels and other rotating accessories shall be compatible with the tool, rated for the intended application, correctly mounted and guarded, and used within manufacturer defined operating limits."},{"id":"3.32.12","page":111,"heading":"Guards, Controls and Accessories","text":"Powered tools shall have controls that prevent unintended starting and enable the operator to maintain control during operation."},{"id":"3.32.13","page":111,"heading":"Safe Use of Hand and Power Tools","text":"Hand and power tools shall be used in a manner that maintains control of the tool, with workpieces secured where unintended movement, rotation or ejection could expose personnel to moving parts, reaction forces, ejected materials or other tool related hazards."},{"id":"3.32.14","page":111,"heading":"Safe Use of Hand and Power Tools","text":"Tools shall be disconnected from their energy source and, where hazardous energy could cause harm, isolated, depressurized or otherwise made safe before maintenance, adjustment, cleaning, repair, clearing blockages or replacing accessories."},{"id":"3.32.15","page":111,"heading":"Safe Use of Hand and Power Tools","text":"Tools shall not be operated until guards, controls, accessories and other safety features disturbed during the activity have been correctly restored."},{"id":"3.32.16","page":111,"heading":"Electrical, Pneumatic, Hydraulic and Battery-Powered Tools","text":"Electrical hazards associated with portable electric tools and their power supplies shall be controlled in accordance with section 3.30 Electrical Safety process."},{"id":"3.32.17","page":111,"heading":"Electrical, Pneumatic, Hydraulic and Battery-Powered Tools","text":"Pneumatic and hydraulic tool systems shall use hoses, fittings, couplings and restraints that are compatible with the system and rated for the maximum operating pressure."},{"id":"3.32.18","page":111,"heading":"Electrical, Pneumatic, Hydraulic and Battery-Powered Tools","text":"Pneumatic and hydraulic tool systems shall be configured and used to prevent attachment ejection, hose separation, hose whip and uncontrolled release of pressure."},{"id":"3.32.19","page":111,"heading":"Electrical, Pneumatic, Hydraulic and Battery-Powered Tools","text":"Pneumatic and hydraulic energy shall be isolated, and stored pressure shall be safely released, before pressure- containing components or tool attachments are disconnected or changed."},{"id":"3.32.20","page":111,"heading":"Electrical, Pneumatic, Hydraulic and Battery-Powered Tools","text":"Batteries and chargers shall be compatible with the tool and managed in accordance with manufacturer requirements to control electrical, thermal, fire, chemical and physical hazards. Damaged, leaking, swollen, overheated or otherwise defective batteries or chargers shall be removed from service and controlled against unintended use."},{"id":"3.32.21","page":112,"heading":"Storage, Transport and Personal Protective Equipment","text":"Tools, accessories and attachments shall be stored and transported in a manner that prevents damage, deterioration, unintended movement and unauthorized use."},{"id":"3.32.22","page":112,"heading":"Storage, Transport and Personal Protective Equipment","text":"Tools used or placed where they could fall and harm people, damage equipment or affect operating areas shall be secured or otherwise controlled against falling."},{"id":"3.32.23","page":112,"heading":"Storage, Transport and Personal Protective Equipment","text":"Personal protective equipment for hand and power tool activities shall be selected and used based on the tool, task, operating environment and assessed hazards, in accordance with section 3.31 Personal Protective Equipment process."},{"id":"3.32.24","page":112,"heading":"Competency Requirements and Verification Process","text":"Personnel who select, use, inspect, test, maintain or repair hand and power tools shall be competent for their activities and receive any specialized training or authorization required for the tool or activity."},{"id":"3.32.25","page":112,"heading":"Competency Requirements and Verification Process","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically verify compliance with this process and the effectiveness of hand and power tool controls through risk-based inspection, testing, monitoring and review."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Hand and Power Tool Safety; hand tool, power tool, grinder, drill, saw; Hand and Power Tool Management; Inspection, Maintenance and Defect Control; Guards, Controls and Accessories; Safe Use of Hand and Power Tools; Electrical, Pneumatic, Hydraulic and Battery-Powered Tools; Storage, Transport and Personal Protective Equipment; Competency Requirements and Verification Process","trigger":"if hand tools or portable powered tools are used","refs":["3.21","3.30","3.31"]},{"id":"3.33","title":"Machine Safety and Guarding","page":112,"purpose":"Machinery and powered equipment hazards are identified and controlled throughout the equipment lifecycle to prevent injury from hazardous movement, unexpected energy release, access to danger zones, ejected materials and loss of control.","scope":"This process applies to machinery and powered equipment that Maaden owns, controls, supplies, leases or uses, including contractor provided equipment. It applies to fixed, portable, temporary and integrated machinery systems throughout their lifecycle, from design and procurement to decommissioning.","clauses":[{"id":"3.33.1","page":112,"heading":"Governance and Machinery Risk Management","text":"Machinery safety activities requiring specialist knowledge shall be performed or reviewed by competent personnel."},{"id":"3.33.2","page":112,"heading":"Governance and Machinery Risk Management","text":"Machinery shall be uniquely identified, with current information maintained on its hazards, operating limits, safeguards, safety functions, and inspection, verification and maintenance requirements."},{"id":"3.33.3","page":112,"heading":"Governance and Machinery Risk Management","text":"Machinery safety requirements shall be incorporated into design, procurement, fabrication, installation, commissioning, operation, maintenance, modification, relocation and decommissioning decisions, including the selection and acceptance of contractor provided machinery."},{"id":"3.33.4","page":112,"heading":"Governance and Machinery Risk Management","text":"Before machinery is placed into service, a documented machinery safety risk assessment shall be completed, or an existing assessment shall be verified as applicable and current."},{"id":"3.33.5","page":112,"heading":"Governance and Machinery Risk Management","text":"The machinery safety risk assessment shall be reviewed when a repair, modification, relocation, change in use, incident, failure or operating experience could affect the machinery hazards, safeguards or safety functions."},{"id":"3.33.6","page":112,"heading":"Governance and Machinery Risk Management","text":"The machinery safety risk assessment shall consider the intended use and reasonably foreseeable misuse of the machinery; operation, cleaning, inspection, maintenance and other foreseeable interactions; people who may be exposed and their access to danger zones; mechanical and other relevant hazards, including hazardous energy, pressure and the ejection or release of materials; and foreseeable failure, defeat or bypass of safeguards and safety functions."},{"id":"3.33.7","page":113,"heading":"Governance and Machinery Risk Management","text":"The risk assessment shall define the required risk controls, safeguards and safety functions, including their performance and acceptance criteria, and the controls required for residual risk."},{"id":"3.33.8","page":113,"heading":"Risk Reduction and Safeguarding","text":"Machinery hazards shall be eliminated through inherently safer design where reasonably practicable, with residual risks reduced in accordance with the hierarchy of controls and engineered safeguards prioritized over administrative controls, warnings and personal protective equipment."},{"id":"3.33.9","page":113,"heading":"Risk Reduction and Safeguarding","text":"Machinery hazards that could cause injury shall be safeguarded by preventing access while a hazardous condition exists or stopping the hazardous condition before a person can reach the danger zone."},{"id":"3.33.10","page":113,"heading":"Risk Reduction and Safeguarding","text":"Hand tools, feeding devices and material removal devices shall only supplement and not replace required safeguards."},{"id":"3.33.11","page":113,"heading":"Risk Reduction and Safeguarding","text":"Guards shall be fixed where access is not required during normal operation and interlocked where access is required, with guard locking provided where the hazard cannot stop before a person can reach the danger zone."},{"id":"3.33.12","page":113,"heading":"Risk Reduction and Safeguarding","text":"Guards and protective structures shall be securely retained, withstand foreseeable loads, contain or deflect ejected materials where required, and not introduce additional hazards."},{"id":"3.33.13","page":113,"heading":"Risk Reduction and Safeguarding","text":"Guard openings, barriers and perimeter safeguarding shall maintain safety distances that prevent access to danger zones and, where whole-body access is possible, provide controlled entry and measures that prevent hazardous operation while any person remains within the safeguarded space."},{"id":"3.33.14","page":113,"heading":"Risk Reduction and Safeguarding","text":"Machinery shall be stable and secured against hazardous unintended movement."},{"id":"3.33.15","page":113,"heading":"Machine Controls and Safety Functions","text":"Machinery shall have clearly identifiable and accessible controls positioned to prevent unintended operation and hazardous reaching, including an accessible normal stop control that brings hazardous functions to a safe condition."},{"id":"3.33.16","page":113,"heading":"Machine Controls and Safety Functions","text":"Machinery shall require a deliberate start command to initiate or resume hazardous operation following energy restoration, control reset, guard closure or restoration of a protective device, unless automatic restart is necessary and demonstrated to be safe through documented risk assessment."},{"id":"3.33.17","page":113,"heading":"Machine Controls and Safety Functions","text":"Where required by the machinery risk assessment, emergency-stop devices shall be identifiable, readily accessible and capable of overriding conflicting operating commands. Emergency stopping shall remain effective until manually reset, and resetting shall not initiate machinery operation. Emergency stopping shall not replace safeguarding, normal stopping controls or required energy isolation."},{"id":"3.33.18","page":113,"heading":"Machine Controls and Safety Functions","text":"Interlocked guards shall stop the associated hazardous function or prevent its initiation when opened, removed or displaced."},{"id":"3.33.19","page":113,"heading":"Machine Controls and Safety Functions","text":"Safety related control systems shall perform reliably, respond safely to faults and prevent foreseeable bypass or manipulation, in accordance with the machinery risk assessment and applicable international standards."},{"id":"3.33.20","page":113,"heading":"Machine Controls and Safety Functions","text":"Reset controls shall allow verification that the danger zone is clear before hazardous operation resumes, using an engineered means where direct verification is not possible."},{"id":"3.33.21","page":114,"heading":"Machine Controls and Safety Functions","text":"Presence sensing, pressure sensitive and two-hand controls shall be used only where they prevent access to the danger zone or stop the hazardous condition before it can be reached."},{"id":"3.33.22","page":114,"heading":"Operation, Intervention and Hazardous Energy","text":"Machinery shall not be operated where any required guard, interlock, emergency stop device or protective device is missing, damaged, bypassed, defeated or ineffective."},{"id":"3.33.23","page":114,"heading":"Operation, Intervention and Hazardous Energy","text":"Before a guard is removed or any part of the body enters a danger zone or line of fire, hazardous motion and energy shall be isolated and stored energy controlled in accordance with section 3.23 Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy process."},{"id":"3.33.24","page":114,"heading":"Operation, Intervention and Hazardous Energy","text":"Where testing, fault finding or commissioning requires temporary energization, access, hazardous motion and machine operation shall be controlled through a documented task-specific risk assessment."},{"id":"3.33.25","page":114,"heading":"Conveyors","text":"Conveyors shall meet the applicable requirements of ASME B20.1, Safety Standard for Conveyors and Related Equipment."},{"id":"3.33.26","page":114,"heading":"Conveyors","text":"Conveyors shall be guarded at accessible nip points, pulleys, transfer points, counterweights, screw flights and other hazardous parts, with safe walkways, access routes and designated crossovers provided and riding prohibited unless the conveyor is specifically designed for personnel transportation."},{"id":"3.33.27","page":114,"heading":"Conveyors","text":"Conveyors shall incorporate controls that prevent hazardous exposure during start-up, provide an effective pre- start warning where the conveyor is not fully visible from the starting control, and provide accessible emergency stop devices or pull cords wherever people may approach an operating conveyor."},{"id":"3.33.28","page":114,"heading":"Conveyors","text":"Inclined conveyors shall have backstops, brakes or equivalent protection where unintended reverse movement could create a hazard. Interdependent conveyors shall be controlled to prevent hazardous material accumulation or movement when associated equipment stops."},{"id":"3.33.29","page":114,"heading":"Conveyors","text":"Underground belt conveyors shall use fire resistant belting and meet the applicable fire prevention and protection requirements of SAIS SAF-18 and NFPA 122."},{"id":"3.33.30","page":114,"heading":"High Pressure Water Jetting","text":"High-pressure water-jetting systems shall comply with AS 4233.1, ASTM E1575 or EN 1829; use compatible components rated for the maximum operating pressure; have hoses and connections protected or restrained against damage, separation and hose whip; and be inspected before use."},{"id":"3.33.31","page":114,"heading":"High Pressure Water Jetting","text":"Manual jetting systems shall require continuous deliberate activation, be protected against unintended operation, rapidly stop or release pressure when the operating control is released, and have an independent emergency shutdown operated by a competent person other than the jetting operator."},{"id":"3.33.32","page":114,"heading":"High Pressure Water Jetting","text":"Manual jetting shall be conducted within a controlled exclusion zone, with reaction force, operator stability, communication and line of fire hazards assessed and controlled, and mechanically supported or remotely operated equipment used where safe manual control cannot be maintained."},{"id":"3.33.33","page":114,"heading":"High Pressure Water Jetting","text":"High-pressure water-jetting work shall be performed with objects securely supported and not hand-held, and with flexible lances fitted with an engineered device that prevents uncontrolled withdrawal or ejection."},{"id":"3.33.34","page":115,"heading":"Abrasive Blasting","text":"Abrasive blasting equipment and operations shall comply with applicable legal requirements and ISO 23779 standards."},{"id":"3.33.35","page":115,"heading":"Abrasive Blasting","text":"Abrasive blasting equipment shall be compatible with the abrasive and operating conditions, rated for the maximum operating pressure and protected against unintended pressure release, hose whip, coupling separation, static discharge and unintended abrasive flow."},{"id":"3.33.36","page":115,"heading":"Abrasive Blasting","text":"Manual abrasive blasting shall use a hold to activate nozzle control that stops abrasive flow when released, is tested before use, and cannot be bypassed, defeated or secured in the operating position, with reliable communication and controls enabling both the nozzle operator and blasting machine attendant to stop abrasive flow when these roles are performed by different people."},{"id":"3.33.37","page":115,"heading":"Abrasive Blasting","text":"Abrasive blasting shall be contained and provided with dust extraction where reasonably practicable. Where full containment is not practicable, exclusion zones and access controls shall prevent exposure of unprotected people. Abrasive materials, coatings and substrates shall be assessed for hazardous contaminants, and lower hazard materials and methods shall be used where reasonably practicable."},{"id":"3.33.38","page":115,"heading":"Abrasive Blasting","text":"People shall be protected from the abrasive blast stream, airborne contaminants and hazardous movement of objects being blasted."},{"id":"3.33.39","page":115,"heading":"Fixed Workshop Machinery","text":"Fixed workshop machinery shall comply with applicable machine specific standards and manufacturer limitations. Rotating parts, tooling, workpieces, protruding stock and ejected materials shall be guarded, contained or segregated to prevent exposure."},{"id":"3.33.40","page":115,"heading":"Fixed Workshop Machinery","text":"Measurement, adjustment, cleaning, lubrication, swarf removal and other interventions shall not expose people to hazardous movement. Machinery shall be stopped and, where required, isolated before these activities. Purpose- designed devices shall be used to remove swarf or materials, and rotating components shall not be stopped by hand."},{"id":"3.33.41","page":115,"heading":"Fixed Workshop Machinery","text":"Stationary abrasive wheel machinery shall be fitted with guards capable of containing fragments from wheel failure and use correctly rated and inspected wheels, properly adjusted work rests and tongue guards, and spindle speeds within the wheel and manufacturer limits."},{"id":"3.33.42","page":115,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Modification","text":"Machinery and safeguarding shall comply with applicable Saudi legal and regulatory requirements, including SASO 33358 where applicable, approved specifications, manufacturer requirements and international standards."},{"id":"3.33.43","page":115,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Modification","text":"Suppliers shall provide the information necessary for safe installation, commissioning, operation, isolation, cleaning, maintenance, modification and decommissioning."},{"id":"3.33.44","page":115,"heading":"Design, Procurement and Modification","text":"Modifications to machinery, safeguards, safety related control systems or safety software shall be subject to machinery risk assessment and the applicable Management of Change requirements before implementation."},{"id":"3.33.45","page":115,"heading":"Verification, Inspection and Competence","text":"New, relocated or modified machinery shall be verified before operational use against approved specifications, identified safety requirements and defined acceptance criteria. Verification shall confirm the installation, safeguards, safety functions, equipment specific controls and communication of residual risks."},{"id":"3.33.46","page":116,"heading":"Verification, Inspection and Competence","text":"Safety functions dependent on stopping time, response time, safeguard position or control system reliability shall be validated by a competent person against defined acceptance criteria. Machinery subject to mandatory conformity assessment, certification or third-party validation shall not be placed into service until the required evidence has been accepted."},{"id":"3.33.47","page":116,"heading":"Verification, Inspection and Competence","text":"Guards, protective devices and safety related control functions shall be subject to documented inspection, functional testing and preventive maintenance at frequencies based on risk, manufacturer instructions, operating conditions, failure history and previous results. Defects presenting unacceptable risk shall be corrected before operation, and affected safety functions shall be retested after repair, replacement, adjustment or modification."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Machine Safety and Guarding; machine guarding, interlock, blockage, entanglement; Governance and Machinery Risk Management; Risk Reduction and Safeguarding; Machine Controls and Safety Functions; Operation, Intervention and Hazardous Energy; Conveyors; High-Pressure Water Jetting; Abrasive Blasting; Fixed Workshop Machinery; Design, Procurement and Modification; Verification, Inspection and Competence","trigger":"if fixed machinery, guarding, interlocks, cleaning, adjustment or blockage removal is involved","refs":["3.23"]},{"id":"3.34","title":"Excavation and Trenching","page":116,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for controlling excavation and trenching hazards to prevent injury, asset damage and environmental impact.","scope":"This process applies to excavation, trenching and ground disturbance activities controlled by Business Units and Corporate Functions, including manual and mechanical excavation, ground penetration, drilling, pits, trenches, foundations, shafts, grading, utility work and associated contractor activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.34.1","page":116,"heading":"Excavation and Trenching Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented arrangements for managing excavation and trenching activities."},{"id":"3.34.2","page":116,"heading":"Excavation and Trenching Management","text":"Roles, responsibilities and authorities for planning, designing, authorizing, performing, supervising, inspecting and closing excavation work shall be defined."},{"id":"3.34.3","page":116,"heading":"Excavation and Trenching Management","text":"Personnel shall be competent, qualified and authorized, as applicable to their assigned responsibilities, with an Excavation Competent Person designated where competent-person oversight is required."},{"id":"3.34.4","page":116,"heading":"Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"Excavation and trenching work shall be planned and risk assessed before work begins, considering, as applicable:\na. excavation scope, dimensions and method;\nb. ground and soil conditions;\nc. underground and overhead services;\nd. adjacent structures, foundations, roads and facilities;\ne. groundwater, water ingress and flooding;\nf. hazardous or contaminated atmospheres and ground;\ng. access and egress;\nh. equipment, vehicles and lifting activities;\ni. spoil and surface loads;\nj. fall and falling object hazards;\nk. electrical, process, fire, explosion and environmental hazards; and\nl. simultaneous operations and emergency conditions."},{"id":"3.34.5","page":117,"heading":"Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"An excavation plan shall be established with detail proportionate to the risk and define the excavation method, required protective systems, access and area controls, spoil management, inspections and emergency arrangements."},{"id":"3.34.6","page":117,"heading":"Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"Interfaces with Permit to Work, Energy Isolation, Confined Space Entry, Lifting Operations, Electrical Safety, Working at Height, traffic management, environmental controls and emergency response shall be coordinated where applicable."},{"id":"3.34.7","page":117,"heading":"Services, Ground and Structural Integrity","text":"Underground and overhead services shall be identified and located before excavation begins through review of available drawings and records, consultation with relevant service owners and appropriate field verification."},{"id":"3.34.8","page":117,"heading":"Services, Ground and Structural Integrity","text":"Services shall be located and verified using suitable detection and safe excavation methods before work could expose or damage them, with exposed services protected, supported, isolated or otherwise controlled as required."},{"id":"3.34.9","page":117,"heading":"Services, Ground and Structural Integrity","text":"Where an unidentified, incorrectly located or damaged service is encountered, work shall stop, the area shall be made safe and the applicable responsible parties shall be notified before work resumes."},{"id":"3.34.10","page":117,"heading":"Services, Ground and Structural Integrity","text":"Ground and soil conditions shall be assessed by a competent person before excavation and following changes that could affect stability."},{"id":"3.34.11","page":117,"heading":"Services, Ground and Structural Integrity","text":"Excavations shall be assessed for potential effects on adjacent structures, foundations, roads, equipment and facilities, with qualified engineer review and approval required where engineered support is necessary or structural stability could be affected."},{"id":"3.34.12","page":117,"heading":"Services, Ground and Structural Integrity","text":"Applicable structural requirements shall comply with SBC 303-2024 and the approved engineering basis."},{"id":"3.34.13","page":117,"heading":"Excavation Authorization","text":"Excavation work shall be authorized in accordance with section 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment only after confirming that the approved scope, risk assessment and required excavation controls are in place."},{"id":"3.34.14","page":117,"heading":"Excavation Authorization","text":"Required service controls, protective systems, access, area controls, equipment, competent personnel and emergency arrangements shall be verified before excavation begins."},{"id":"3.34.15","page":117,"heading":"Excavation Authorization","text":"Excavation work shall be stopped where changes to the scope, ground conditions, services, adjacent operations or required controls could affect safety, and resumed only after the risk assessment, excavation plan and authorization have been reviewed and updated."},{"id":"3.34.16","page":117,"heading":"Excavation Protective Systems","text":"Personnel exposed to cave-in or ground-collapse hazards shall be protected by an approved protective system suitable for the excavation and ground conditions."},{"id":"3.34.17","page":117,"heading":"Excavation Protective Systems","text":"Protective systems shall be selected, designed, installed, used, inspected and removed in accordance with applicable Saudi requirements, SBC 303-2024, OSHA 29 CFR 1926 Subpart P, approved engineering information and manufacturer requirements."},{"id":"3.34.18","page":117,"heading":"Excavation Protective Systems","text":"Protective systems requiring engineering design shall be designed or approved by a qualified engineer and fully installed, inspected and verified as safe before personnel enter the excavation."},{"id":"3.34.19","page":118,"heading":"Access, Excavation Edges and Material Control","text":"Safe access, egress and movement shall be provided and maintained for excavations where personnel entry is required."},{"id":"3.34.20","page":118,"heading":"Access, Excavation Edges and Material Control","text":"Excavation edges and surrounding areas shall be protected against falls and unauthorized access, with suitable crossings provided where personnel or equipment are required to cross the excavation."},{"id":"3.34.21","page":118,"heading":"Access, Excavation Edges and Material Control","text":"Spoil, materials, equipment and surface loads shall be positioned or controlled to prevent material falling into the excavation, overloading excavation sides or adversely affecting stability."},{"id":"3.34.22","page":118,"heading":"Access, Excavation Edges and Material Control","text":"A stairway, ladder, ramp, or other safe means of egress shall be provided in trenches 1.22 m or more in depth and positioned so that lateral travel does not exceed 7.62 m."},{"id":"3.34.23","page":118,"heading":"Access, Excavation Edges and Material Control","text":"Loose soil, rock, surface encumbrances, and other unstable materials shall be removed, supported, retained, or otherwise controlled."},{"id":"3.34.24","page":118,"heading":"Access, Excavation Edges and Material Control","text":"Open excavations shall remain secured and clearly identified during work interruptions, inactivity and darkness, with suitable lighting and warning devices provided where personnel or vehicles may be exposed."},{"id":"3.34.25","page":118,"heading":"Equipment, Traffic and Adjacent Operations","text":"Excavation equipment and vehicles shall be suitable for the work, inspected before use and operated by competent and authorized personnel."},{"id":"3.34.26","page":118,"heading":"Equipment, Traffic and Adjacent Operations","text":"Equipment movement, traffic and adjacent operations shall be controlled to prevent harm to personnel and adverse effects on excavation stability, services and structures."},{"id":"3.34.27","page":118,"heading":"Equipment, Traffic and Adjacent Operations","text":"Personnel shall be separated from operating excavation equipment, vehicle operating areas and suspended loads through exclusion zones or other effective controls."},{"id":"3.34.28","page":118,"heading":"Atmospheric and Emergency Controls","text":"Excavations shall be assessed before personnel enter for hazardous atmospheres, confined space conditions, water ingress, flooding, process releases and restrictions that could affect emergency access or escape."},{"id":"3.34.29","page":118,"heading":"Atmospheric and Emergency Controls","text":"Atmospheric testing and monitoring shall be performed where a hazardous atmosphere exists or could reasonably occur, at locations and frequencies determined by the risk assessment and changing conditions."},{"id":"3.34.30","page":118,"heading":"Atmospheric and Emergency Controls","text":"Excavations deeper than 1.2 m shall be managed in accordance to section 3.25 Confined Space Entry process."},{"id":"3.34.31","page":118,"heading":"Atmospheric and Emergency Controls","text":"Personnel shall not enter or remain in an excavation affected by uncontrolled water accumulation, a hazardous atmosphere or any other condition that could prevent safe escape."},{"id":"3.34.32","page":118,"heading":"Atmospheric and Emergency Controls","text":"Emergency and rescue arrangements shall be established before entry where excavation conditions could expose personnel to collapse, hazardous atmospheres, flooding, energy or material release, or restricted access or egress."},{"id":"3.34.33","page":118,"heading":"Worksite Verification and Inspection","text":"The Excavation Competent Person shall inspect applicable excavations, adjacent areas, protective systems, access arrangements, services, spoil placement and other required controls before each shift and following events or changes that could increase risk."},{"id":"3.34.34","page":118,"heading":"Worksite Verification and Inspection","text":"Work shall be stopped and exposed personnel removed where an unsafe condition is identified, with work resumed only after the condition is corrected and required controls are reverified."},{"id":"3.34.35","page":119,"heading":"Worksite Verification and Inspection","text":"Excavations shall be backfilled, covered, barricaded, supported or otherwise secured when work is completed or suspended."},{"id":"3.34.36","page":119,"heading":"Worksite Verification and Inspection","text":"Before final closure, affected services, structures, drainage and other systems shall be reinstated, tested or accepted as applicable, and the worksite shall be verified safe before the Permit to Work is closed."},{"id":"3.34.37","page":119,"heading":"Worksite Verification and Inspection","text":"Newly discovered, relocated or modified services shall be recorded for updating applicable drawings and asset records."},{"id":"3.34.38","page":119,"heading":"Worksite Verification and Inspection","text":"Cave-ins, ground movements, utility strikes, flooding, hazardous atmosphere events, protective system failures and relevant near misses shall be reported and addressed in accordance with section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Excavation and Trenching; excavation, trench, test pit, ground penetration; Excavation and Trenching Management; Planning and Risk Assessment; Services, Ground and Structural Integrity; Excavation Authorization; Excavation Protective Systems; Access, Excavation Edges and Material Control; Equipment, Traffic and Adjacent Operations; Atmospheric, Water and Emergency Controls; Worksite Verification and Inspection","trigger":"if excavation, trenching, test-pitting or ground penetration is undertaken","refs":["3.21","3.25","6.1"]},{"id":"3.35","title":"Geotechnical and Ground Control Management","page":119,"purpose":"Geotechnical and ground control hazards are identified, assessed and controlled throughout the mine lifecycle so that ground instability, falls of ground, slope failure, structural collapse, subsidence and other geotechnical events do not create unacceptable risk to people, the environment, assets or operations.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden owned, managed or controlled surface and underground mining activities and mine related projects throughout planning, design, construction, operation, modification, temporary closure and final closure where geotechnical or ground control hazards may exist. It applies to excavations, slopes, mine workings, ground support systems, shafts, waste dumps, stockpiles, engineered ground structures, natural or man-made voids and other areas where ground instability could create EHSS risk.","clauses":[{"id":"3.35.1","page":119,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Compliance","text":"Business Units shall define responsibilities, accountabilities and interfaces for geotechnical and ground control management across relevant technical, operational and EHSS functions."},{"id":"3.35.2","page":119,"heading":"Governance, Accountability and Compliance","text":"Geotechnical and ground control arrangements shall comply with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, including applicable requirements of SAF-18 Mines and Mineral Processing Plants. Relevant requirements of 30 CFR Part 56 – Safety and Health Standards for Surface Metal and Nonmetal Mines and 30 CFR Part 57 – Safety and Health Standards for Underground Metal and Nonmetal Mines shall be considered as recognized technical guidance for surface and underground mining, respectively, where relevant and consistent with applicable Saudi requirements."},{"id":"3.35.3","page":119,"heading":"Geological, Geotechnical and Hydrogeological Information","text":"Before applicable mine or ground control design is undertaken, sufficient geological, geotechnical, hydrogeological, structural and, where relevant, seismic information shall be collected, analyzed and interpreted by qualified personnel. Documented geological, geotechnical and hydrogeological models and databases shall be established and maintained throughout the mine lifecycle."},{"id":"3.35.4","page":119,"heading":"Geological, Geotechnical and Hydrogeological Information","text":"Geotechnical investigation, testing and modelling shall use recognized engineering practices and defined quality control arrangements. Data sources, assumptions and material limitations shall be documented and considered in design and risk decisions."},{"id":"3.35.5","page":120,"heading":"Geological, Geotechnical and Hydrogeological Information","text":"Where groundwater, surface water, pore pressure or seismicity could contribute to a credible ground control failure mechanism, assessment, modelling, monitoring and response arrangements proportionate to the identified risk shall be established and integrated into geotechnical design and ground control management."},{"id":"3.35.6","page":120,"heading":"Geotechnical Risk Assessment and Control","text":"Each applicable mine or operation shall conduct and maintain a documented geotechnical and ground control risk assessment in accordance with Section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management."},{"id":"3.35.7","page":120,"heading":"Geotechnical Risk Assessment and Control","text":"The assessment shall consider, as applicable:\na. current and proposed mine design and mining method;\nb. credible geotechnical and ground control failure mechanisms;\nc. geological, geotechnical, hydrogeological and seismic conditions;\nd. excavation geometry, mine sequencing and blasting;\ne. ground support systems;\nf. interaction with existing workings, excavations or voids;\ng. previous ground instability or geotechnical events; and\nh. exposure of people, equipment and infrastructure to potential failure. The assessment shall be conducted under the direction of a qualified person with involvement of the technical disciplines necessary to understand the credible failure mechanisms and controls."},{"id":"3.35.8","page":120,"heading":"Geotechnical Risk Assessment and Control","text":"Geotechnical critical controls shall be managed in accordance with section 2.6 Critical Control Management, with their performance and verification requirements incorporated into applicable inspection, monitoring and Trigger Action Response Plans."},{"id":"3.35.9","page":120,"heading":"Ground Control Management Plan","text":"Each mine shall establish, implement and maintain a Ground Control Management Plan (GCMP) proportionate to its geotechnical risk profile and covering applicable surface and underground ground control hazards throughout the mine lifecycle. The GCMP shall be technically reviewed by a qualified geotechnical professional and approved by the mine manager."},{"id":"3.35.10","page":120,"heading":"Ground Control Management Plan","text":"The GCMP shall integrate the applicable requirements of this process, including the technical basis for ground control decisions, geotechnical risks and controls, design requirements, inspection and monitoring arrangements, Trigger Action Response Plans, operational ground control requirements, competency and emergency response interfaces."},{"id":"3.35.11","page":120,"heading":"Ground Control Management Plan","text":"The GCMP and the effectiveness of its implementation shall be formally reviewed at least annually and following significant geotechnical incidents, changes that could affect geotechnical risk, design assumptions or control effectiveness, or identification of previously unrecognized failure mechanisms."},{"id":"3.35.12","page":120,"heading":"Geotechnical Design and Technical Governance","text":"Geotechnical designs shall be based on current and validated technical data, credible failure mechanisms and recognized engineering methods, with documented design acceptance criteria proportionate to the risk."},{"id":"3.35.13","page":120,"heading":"Geotechnical Design and Technical Governance","text":"Designs shall consider relevant lifecycle factors, including mining sequence, blasting, groundwater, seismic loading, adjacent workings and changing mine geometry. Where deformation, yielding or failure of an engineered geotechnical component is intentionally accepted as part of the design, this shall be justified and supported by defined acceptance criteria, monitoring and response requirements."},{"id":"3.35.14","page":121,"heading":"Geotechnical Design and Technical Governance","text":"High risk or complex geotechnical designs shall be subject to independent technical review by a person who was not responsible for the original design. The extent of review shall be proportionate to the risk and complexity of the design."},{"id":"3.35.15","page":121,"heading":"Geotechnical Design and Technical Governance","text":"Mining activities shall be conducted within the geotechnical operating limits, design assumptions and control measures defined in the approved geotechnical design, Ground Control Management Plan and associated Trigger Action Response Plans."},{"id":"3.35.16","page":121,"heading":"Surface Ground Control","text":"Surface mine excavations and ground structures, including pit walls, highwalls, benches, slopes, dumps, stockpiles and embankments, shall be designed, constructed, operated and monitored to maintain stability consistent with the approved geotechnical design and GCMP. Bench, berm, face and slope geometry shall be appropriate to the geotechnical conditions, mining method and equipment used."},{"id":"3.35.17","page":121,"heading":"Surface Ground Control","text":"Dumping locations and other areas where mobile equipment may be exposed to unstable ground shall be assessed before use and as conditions change. Where ground stability cannot be assured, appropriate setbacks, restraints, operating restrictions or other controls shall be established."},{"id":"3.35.18","page":121,"heading":"Surface Ground Control","text":"Where surface mining is conducted above, adjacent to or through underground workings or other significant voids, their location and condition shall be determined and appropriate controls established. Controls shall address, as applicable, investigation and survey requirements, safe ground thickness, personnel and equipment access, blasting, infill or backfill, barricading, monitoring and response to previously unidentified voids."},{"id":"3.35.19","page":121,"heading":"Underground Ground Control","text":"Underground excavations and ground support systems shall be designed, installed and maintained to control ground where personnel work or travel. Ground support design shall define, as applicable, support capacity and deformation characteristics, loading conditions, support type and density, installation sequence, allowable unsupported advance, requirements for intersections and large spans, and testing and quality control requirements."},{"id":"3.35.20","page":121,"heading":"Underground Ground Control","text":"Systematic ground support shall, wherever practicable, be designed and installed without requiring personnel to enter unsupported ground beyond the last effective line of support. Where this cannot be achieved, the work shall be supported by a documented risk assessment, an approved safe method of work and independent technical review proportionate to the risk."},{"id":"3.35.21","page":121,"heading":"Underground Ground Control","text":"Underground geotechnical design shall address, where applicable, pillar and crown pillar stability, seismicity and rock burst risk, surface subsidence and interaction with natural or man-made voids. Where seismicity or rock burst represents a ground control risk, appropriate design criteria, mining sequence, ground support, monitoring, exposure controls and response arrangements shall be established."},{"id":"3.35.22","page":121,"heading":"Geotechnical Inspection, Monitoring and Reconciliation","text":"Inspection and monitoring arrangements shall be established to detect material changes in ground conditions in time to implement corrective action, work restriction or evacuation before loss of control occurs. They shall define applicable methods, locations, frequencies, responsibilities, trigger criteria, communication and data quality requirements. Monitoring equipment relied upon for critical geotechnical decisions or TARP activation shall be inspected, maintained, calibrated and validated."},{"id":"3.35.23","page":122,"heading":"Geotechnical Inspection, Monitoring and Reconciliation","text":"Following geotechnical, hydrological, seismic or operational events that could affect ground stability, affected areas shall be reassessed and any required controls implemented before normal activities resume."},{"id":"3.35.24","page":122,"heading":"Geotechnical Inspection, Monitoring and Reconciliation","text":"Ground conditions shall be examined by designated competent personnel before work begins, after blasting, when conditions change or indicate increased risk, and at any additional frequencies defined in the GCMP based on risk and applicable regulatory requirements."},{"id":"3.35.25","page":122,"heading":"Geotechnical Inspection, Monitoring and Reconciliation","text":"Actual ground performance and monitoring results shall be periodically compared with design assumptions and predicted behavior, with material deviations triggering review and, where necessary, revision of the applicable risk assessment, design, critical controls, GCMP or TARP."},{"id":"3.35.26","page":122,"heading":"Trigger Action Response Plans","text":"Trigger Action Response Plans shall be established for geotechnical failure mechanisms where monitoring, inspection or observed ground behavior can indicate deteriorating conditions, and define:\na. engineering based trigger levels;\nb. required actions for each trigger level;\nc. stop-work, access restriction and evacuation criteria, where applicable;\nd. responsible people and escalation requirements;\ne. communication arrangements; and\nf. interfaces with applicable emergency-response arrangements."},{"id":"3.35.27","page":122,"heading":"Trigger Action Response Plans","text":"Monitoring information used to activate a TARP shall be sufficiently reliable and communicated in time to allow the required response."},{"id":"3.35.28","page":122,"heading":"Trigger Action Response Plans","text":"TARPs involving potential evacuation or other critical emergency response shall be tested through drills or simulations at least annually and after significant changes, and reviewed for effectiveness following drills, activation or significant geotechnical events."},{"id":"3.35.29","page":122,"heading":"Hazardous Ground, Scaling and Fall of Material","text":"Hazardous or potentially unstable ground shall be taken down, supported or otherwise made safe before work or travel is permitted, with affected areas restricted, warning signs posted or barricades installed until the hazard is controlled."},{"id":"3.35.30","page":122,"heading":"Hazardous Ground, Scaling and Fall of Material","text":"Damaged or ineffective ground support shall be repaired or replaced before work or travel resumes where its condition could create a hazard."},{"id":"3.35.31","page":122,"heading":"Hazardous Ground, Scaling and Fall of Material","text":"Scaling and removal of loose or unconsolidated ground shall be performed by competent personnel using suitable methods and tools from a safe position, with appropriate stand-off distances, exclusion zones and access controls established where people or equipment could be exposed to falling or sliding ground."},{"id":"3.35.32","page":122,"heading":"Hazardous Ground, Scaling and Fall of Material","text":"Changes that could affect the approved geotechnical design basis, ground control arrangements or credible failure mechanisms shall be managed in accordance with the applicable Maaden Management of Change requirements."},{"id":"3.35.33","page":123,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness and Mine Closure","text":"Ground control emergency scenarios identified through risk assessment or TARPs shall be incorporated into applicable emergency response arrangements, including mine collapse or cave-in where relevant."},{"id":"3.35.34","page":123,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness and Mine Closure","text":"Temporary closure, final closure and abandonment planning shall address geotechnical risks and ensure that remaining mine openings, excavations and other ground control hazards are secured, rehabilitated, monitored or otherwise controlled to prevent unacceptable residual risk."},{"id":"3.35.35","page":123,"heading":"Competency, Assurance and Records","text":"Personnel performing, supervising or technically approving geotechnical and ground control activities shall be competent for their assigned responsibilities in accordance with section 2.7 EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication."},{"id":"3.35.36","page":123,"heading":"Competency, Assurance and Records","text":"Personnel exposed to ground control hazards shall receive appropriate information and training on hazard recognition, applicable controls and TARPs."},{"id":"3.35.37","page":123,"heading":"Competency, Assurance and Records","text":"Business Units shall periodically verify the implementation and effectiveness of geotechnical and ground control arrangements using risk-based performance measures, inspections, monitoring and assurance activities."},{"id":"3.35.38","page":123,"heading":"Competency, Assurance and Records","text":"Geological and geotechnical data, models, risk assessments and material design information necessary to understand and safely manage the long term geotechnical and ground control basis shall be retained for the life of the facility."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Geotechnical and Ground Control Management; ground control, highwall, slope, fall of ground, TARP; Governance, Accountability and Compliance; Geological, Geotechnical and Hydrogeological Information; Geotechnical Risk Assessment and Control; Ground Control Management Plan; Geotechnical Design and Technical Governance; Surface Ground Control; Underground Ground Control; Geotechnical Inspection, Monitoring and Reconciliation; Trigger Action Response Plans; Hazardous Ground, Scaling and Fall of Material; Management of Change; Emergency Preparedness and Mine Closure; Competency, Assurance and Records","trigger":"if highwalls, benches, slopes, stopes, ground support or fall-of-ground hazards are present","refs":["2.5","2.6","2.7"]},{"id":"3.36","title":"Underground Mines Ventilation, Fire and Refuge Safety","page":123,"purpose":"Underground mine ventilation, fire and explosion prevention, emergency egress and refuge arrangements are established and maintained to provide a safe underground working environment, prevent and control fires and explosions, and enable safe evacuation or refuge during emergencies.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden owned, managed or controlled underground mining operations and associated projects throughout their lifecycle. It applies, as relevant, to underground workplaces, ventilation systems, fire and explosion hazards, mobile and fixed equipment, combustible and flammable materials, refuge chambers, escapeways, atmospheric monitoring and emergency arrangements.","clauses":[{"id":"3.36.1","page":123,"heading":"Governance, Risk and Planning","text":"Business Units shall define responsibilities and accountabilities for underground ventilation, fire and explosion controls, refuge arrangements and escapeways."},{"id":"3.36.2","page":123,"heading":"Governance, Risk and Planning","text":"Underground ventilation, fire protection, refuge and emergency-egress arrangements shall comply with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, including SAF-18 Mines and Mineral Processing Plants and applicable SAIS Safety and Fire Protection Directives, with relevant provisions of 30 CFR Part 57 used as technical guidance where consistent with Saudi requirements."},{"id":"3.36.3","page":123,"heading":"Governance, Risk and Planning","text":"Underground ventilation, fire, explosion, emergency egress and refuge hazards shall be assessed and managed in accordance with Section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management."},{"id":"3.36.4","page":124,"heading":"Governance, Risk and Planning","text":"Underground ventilation, fire, explosion, emergency egress and refuge hazards shall be assessed and managed in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management, considering credible atmospheric, heat, flammable gas, combustible dust, ignition, equipment fire, smoke propagation, power loss, evacuation and refuge scenarios."},{"id":"3.36.5","page":124,"heading":"Governance, Risk and Planning","text":"Ventilation, fire, explosion, emergency egress and refuge risks requiring critical controls shall be managed in accordance with section 2.6 Critical Control Management."},{"id":"3.36.6","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Each underground mine shall establish and maintain a Ventilation Plan approved by an appropriately qualified ventilation engineer that defines:\na. the ventilation design basis and required airflow distribution;\nb. primary and auxiliary ventilation arrangements;\nc. atmospheric monitoring and exposure controls;\nd. ventilation system critical controls and TARPs; and\ne. emergency ventilation arrangements."},{"id":"3.36.7","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Underground ventilation shall maintain a safe and respirable atmosphere, control heat and humidity, and dilute or remove hazardous gases, diesel emissions (DPM), dust, fumes and other airborne contaminants."},{"id":"3.36.8","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Ventilation circuits, fans, ducting and control devices shall be designed, installed, operated, inspected and maintained to provide capacity for foreseeable personnel, equipment, production and contaminant loads, and to:\na. provide controlled airflow to occupied areas;\nb. prevent unacceptable recirculation and contamination of fresh air circuits;\nc. maintain required circuit separation and system integrity; and\nd. protect escapeways and refuge locations from foreseeable smoke and hazardous-gas migration."},{"id":"3.36.9","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Critical ventilation systems shall incorporate monitoring, alarms, reliability and backup arrangements based on risk and applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.36.10","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Loss or failure of required ventilation shall result in equipment shutdown, work stoppage, access restriction or personnel withdrawal until safe conditions are restored and verified."},{"id":"3.36.11","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Atmospheric conditions shall be monitored at locations and frequencies defined by the Ventilation Plan, risk assessment and applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.36.12","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Monitoring instruments used for critical decisions or TARP activation shall be maintained, calibrated and verified."},{"id":"3.36.13","page":124,"heading":"Ventilation Management","text":"Personnel shall be withdrawn and access restricted where atmospheric conditions exceed established safe limits, except for controlled entry specifically authorized for emergency response or recovery."},{"id":"3.36.14","page":124,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Each underground mine shall establish and maintain a Fire and Explosion Management Plan proportionate to its risk profile that addresses credible fire and explosion scenarios, fuel and ignition controls, detection and suppression arrangements, powered equipment, fixed plant, combustible materials, TARPs and emergency response interfaces."},{"id":"3.36.15","page":124,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Where required by SAF-18, fixed fire protection and suppression requirements shall be determined through a Fire Hazard Analysis performed or approved by an appropriately qualified engineer experienced in mining fire hazards."},{"id":"3.36.16","page":125,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Underground fire prevention and protection arrangements shall comply with applicable requirements of SAF-04, SAF-18, NFPA 122 and NFPA 520 and, where applicable, NFPA 30."},{"id":"3.36.17","page":125,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Underground operations shall control ignition sources, combustible materials, flammable and combustible liquids, equipment leaks, combustible waste, hot work and other credible sources of fire or explosion."},{"id":"3.36.18","page":125,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Where flammable gas, combustible dust, frictional ignition or spontaneous combustion hazards are credible, detection, monitoring, prevention and response controls shall be established."},{"id":"3.36.19","page":125,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Electrical and electronic equipment used where flammable atmospheres may occur shall be suitable for the applicable hazardous area classification and comply with SAF-12, applicable NFPA 70 requirements and Section 3.30 Electrical Safety."},{"id":"3.36.20","page":125,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Powered mobile equipment, fixed plant equipment and conveyor systems shall be assessed for fire risk and provided with fire prevention, detection, isolation, suppression and firefighting controls based on the identified hazards and applicable requirements. Self-propelled equipment used underground shall be provided with a Compressed Air Foam (CAF) fire suppression system and at least one portable multipurpose dry chemical extinguisher, with the fixed suppression system arranged to shut down the protected equipment upon actuation."},{"id":"3.36.21","page":125,"heading":"Fire and Explosion Management","text":"Fire detection, alarm, warning, suppression and firefighting systems shall be provided, inspected, tested and maintained in accordance with the Fire Hazard Analysis, SAF-04, SAF-18, NFPA 122, NFPA 520 and approved design requirements."},{"id":"3.36.22","page":125,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Underground mines shall provide and maintain refuge chambers or refuge areas where required by SAF-18, NFPA 520, risk assessment and the approved mine emergency strategy. Refuge capacity shall be at least twice the maximum number of persons expected to rely on the refuge. Where travel distance to an exit exceeds 400 m, one or more refuge areas or a separate means of egress shall be provided based on risk assessment. Refuge location, capacity and required operating duration shall account for credible emergencies, workforce distribution, escapeways, travel time and self-rescuer capability."},{"id":"3.36.23","page":125,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Refuge chambers shall:\na. protect occupants from credible underground emergency conditions;\nb. provide the required life-support capacity and duration;\nc. remain accessible during foreseeable emergencies;\nd. have reliable emergency power, monitoring and two-way communication; and\ne. be commissioned, inspected, tested and maintained to demonstrate readiness."},{"id":"3.36.24","page":125,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Underground mines shall establish and maintain emergency escapeways in accordance with SAF-18, NFPA 520 and the approved mine emergency strategy."},{"id":"3.36.25","page":125,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Escapeways shall be protected, clearly identified, unobstructed and available under foreseeable emergency conditions."},{"id":"3.36.26","page":125,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Underground workplaces shall have independent means of evacuation where required by applicable legal, regulatory or adopted technical requirements."},{"id":"3.36.27","page":126,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Where independent evacuation routes cannot be provided, alternative egress or refuge arrangements shall be justified by risk assessment and approved through applicable mine design, emergency planning and Management of Change processes."},{"id":"3.36.28","page":126,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Refuge locations, capacities, escapeways, emergency routes and relevant ventilation circuits shall be shown on current mine emergency plans."},{"id":"3.36.29","page":126,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Travel time to safe egress or refuge shall be validated against workforce locations, credible emergency conditions and the capability and rated duration of the self-rescuers provided."},{"id":"3.36.30","page":126,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"All personnel required to go underground shall be provided with an approved self-rescue device having a minimum one hour rated duration and suitable for the identified underground emergency hazards."},{"id":"3.36.31","page":126,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Personnel required to go underground shall receive training in mine emergency actions and the location, limitations and correct use of applicable self-rescue devices before undertaking underground work and at least annually thereafter."},{"id":"3.36.32","page":126,"heading":"Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress","text":"Reliable emergency communications shall be available for underground personnel, refuge locations and other locations identified through risk assessment and emergency planning."},{"id":"3.36.33","page":126,"heading":"Emergency Conditions and Re-entry","text":"TARPs shall be established for critical ventilation, atmospheric, fire, explosion, refuge and escapeway conditions and define trigger levels, alarms, equipment shutdown, stop-work and withdrawal criteria, communication and escalation requirements, emergency activation, and conditions for re-entry or resumption of work."},{"id":"3.36.34","page":126,"heading":"Emergency Conditions and Re-entry","text":"Underground fire, ventilation failure, evacuation, refuge and communication arrangements shall be integrated with the applicable Emergency Response Plan and periodically tested through drills or simulations."},{"id":"3.36.35","page":126,"heading":"Emergency Conditions and Re-entry","text":"Re-entry to an underground area affected by fire, explosion, smoke, hazardous atmosphere, ventilation failure or another emergency shall only occur after competent personnel have:\na. assessed the affected area;\nb. verified that conditions are safe for the intended activity;\nc. confirmed the availability of required controls, monitoring, communications and emergency arrangements; and\nd. authorized entry in accordance with applicable TARPs and emergency-response arrangements."},{"id":"3.36.36","page":126,"heading":"Review and Verification","text":"Ventilation Plans, Fire and Explosion Management Plans, risk assessments, TARPs and emergency arrangements shall be formally reviewed at least annually and following relevant incidents, significant changes or evidence that required controls may be ineffective."},{"id":"3.36.37","page":126,"heading":"Review and Verification","text":"Changes affecting ventilation, fire and explosion controls, refuge arrangements or escapeways shall be managed in accordance with Section 3.52 Management of Change."},{"id":"3.36.38","page":126,"heading":"Review and Verification","text":"Business Units shall verify the implementation and effectiveness of critical underground ventilation, fire, refuge and escapeway controls."},{"id":"3.36.39","page":126,"heading":"Review and Verification","text":"Personnel performing underground ventilation, fire-protection, refuge or emergency-safety responsibilities shall be competent in accordance with Section 2.7 EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Underground Mines Ventilation, Fire and Refuge Safety; underground ventilation, refuge, mine fire, egress; Governance, Risk and Planning; Ventilation Management; Fire and Explosion Management; Refuge, Escapeways and Emergency Egress; Emergency Conditions and Re-entry; Review and Verification","trigger":"if underground ventilation, fire prevention, refuge, atmospheric monitoring or egress is relevant","refs":["2.5","2.6","2.7","3.30","3.52"]},{"id":"3.37","title":"Tire and Rim Management","page":127,"purpose":"Maaden manages tires, wheels, rims and associated assemblies throughout their lifecycle to prevent uncontrolled release of stored energy, tire fires, bursts and explosions, separation or projection of components, vehicle loss of control, and crushing or struck-by incidents.","scope":"This process applies to tires, wheels and rims fitted to mining and other heavy mobile equipment throughout their lifecycle, including selection, use, inspection, maintenance, repair and disposal.","clauses":[{"id":"3.37.1","page":127,"heading":"Tire and Rim Management Governance","text":"Business Units shall implement and maintain documented arrangements for managing tires, wheels, rims and associated assemblies throughout their lifecycle, from selection and procurement through disposal."},{"id":"3.37.2","page":127,"heading":"Tire and Rim Management Governance","text":"Roles, responsibilities, authorities and competency requirements shall be defined for people who perform, inspect, supervise or authorize tire and rim activities. Safety critical tire and rim work shall be performed only by people who have been assessed as competent and authorized for the assigned activities."},{"id":"3.37.3","page":127,"heading":"Tire and Rim Management Governance","text":"Contractor personnel and service providers shall comply with applicable Maaden tire and rim requirements."},{"id":"3.37.4","page":127,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Critical Controls","text":"Business Units shall identify and assess hazards and credible consequences associated with tires, wheels, rims and associated assemblies throughout their lifecycle in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.37.5","page":127,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Critical Controls","text":"Critical controls for applicable tire, wheel and rim fatal risk scenarios shall be identified and managed in accordance with section 2.6 Critical Control Management process."},{"id":"3.37.6","page":127,"heading":"Selection and Procurement","text":"Tires, wheels, rims and associated components shall be selected in accordance with equipment manufacturer requirements and for the intended application, including load, speed, duty cycle, inflation pressure, haul profile, terrain, environmental conditions, operating temperature, wheel-end heat exposure and foreseeable abnormal operating conditions. Earth-mover tires and rims shall conform, as applicable, to ISO 4250-1, ISO 4250-2 and ISO 4250-3. Tires and rims for mobile cranes and similar specialized machines shall conform, as applicable, to ISO\n10571."},{"id":"3.37.7","page":127,"heading":"Selection and Procurement","text":"Only compatible tires, wheels, rims and associated components identified through current OEM or manufacturer matching information shall be assembled and used together."},{"id":"3.37.8","page":127,"heading":"Selection and Procurement","text":"Current manufacturer specifications, compatibility information, operating limits, inspection and rejection criteria, tightening torques and maintenance requirements shall be available to people responsible for selection, procurement, acceptance or servicing."},{"id":"3.37.9","page":127,"heading":"Selection and Procurement","text":"Multipiece rim assemblies shall not be specified, installed or used on light vehicles. Light vehicles shall be fitted with single piece rims approved by the vehicle manufacturer or verified as compatible with the vehicle and tire application by a competent person using manufacturer information."},{"id":"3.37.10","page":128,"heading":"Selection and Procurement","text":"Tires, wheels, rims and associated components shall be inspected against defined acceptance criteria before initial use, with nonconforming items identified, segregated and prevented from use."},{"id":"3.37.11","page":128,"heading":"Receipt, Identification and Traceability","text":"Tires, wheels, rims and associated components shall be verified on receipt against specified acceptance criteria, including identity, specification, condition and compatibility."},{"id":"3.37.12","page":128,"heading":"Receipt, Identification and Traceability","text":"Tires, wheels, rims and associated components requiring traceability shall be uniquely identified, and records of their relevant inspection, testing, repair and service history and current status shall be maintained against that identification. Traceability requirements shall be determined by risk assessment, manufacturing requirements and applicable standards."},{"id":"3.37.13","page":128,"heading":"Receipt, Identification and Traceability","text":"Tires, wheels, rims and associated components that are damaged, defective, suspect or otherwise not approved for service shall be clearly identified, segregated where practicable and prevented from use until their serviceability is determined."},{"id":"3.37.14","page":128,"heading":"Storage and Handling","text":"Tires, wheels, rims and associated components shall be stored in designated areas that control rolling, toppling, fire, vehicle interaction, damage and deterioration risks. Rubber tires and related products shall be stored in accordance with applicable manufacturer requirements and ISO 2230."},{"id":"3.37.15","page":128,"heading":"Storage and Handling","text":"Tires, wheels, rims and assemblies shall be lifted, handled and transported using equipment designed or verified for the intended task and rated for the known load. Handling equipment shall be operated within its rated capacity and applicable manufacturer requirements, in accordance with section 3.26 Lifting Operations process."},{"id":"3.37.16","page":128,"heading":"Storage and Handling","text":"People shall not work, stand or pass beneath suspended or elevated tires, wheels, rims or assemblies."},{"id":"3.37.17","page":128,"heading":"Storage and Handling","text":"Tires and assemblies shall not be inflated or deflated while gripped or supported by tire-handling equipment unless the equipment manufacturer expressly permits that use and the equipment is designed and rated for the resulting loads."},{"id":"3.37.18","page":128,"heading":"Maintenance, Inspection and Repair","text":"Inspection, monitoring, testing and maintenance requirements for tires, wheels, rims and associated assemblies shall be established based on applicable manufacturer instructions, operating conditions, duty severity, service history and risk. These requirements shall include inspection of wheel and rim components when disassembled and at defined intervals for wear, corrosion, cracking, distortion, fatigue, damage and compatibility."},{"id":"3.37.19","page":128,"heading":"Maintenance, Inspection and Repair","text":"Tire repairs shall be performed within applicable tire manufacturer limits and shall be inspected and authorized before return to service. Earth mover tires shall be operated within the applicable load and inflation pressure limits specified by the equipment OEM and tire manufacturer and consistent with ISO 4250-2."},{"id":"3.37.20","page":128,"heading":"Maintenance, Inspection and Repair","text":"Safety related defects shall be assessed and corrected before they create a hazard. Equipment, tires, wheels, rims or associated components for which continued operation is hazardous shall be removed from service, identified against further use and verified as serviceable before return to service, consistent with MSHA 30 CFR 56.14100(b)– (c) and 57.14100(b)–(c)."},{"id":"3.37.21","page":128,"heading":"Maintenance, Inspection and Repair","text":"Pre-use inspections conducted in accordance with section 3.27 Mobile Equipment Safety process shall include externally visible tire, wheel and rim conditions, including damage, abnormal wear, signs of incorrect inflation and loose or missing fasteners, consistent with the pre-shift examination requirements of MSHA 30 CFR 56.14100(a) and\n57.14100(a)."},{"id":"3.37.22","page":129,"heading":"Maintenance, Inspection and Repair","text":"Tire service equipment and safety devices shall be designed or verified for their intended use, appropriately rated, inspected and maintained in accordance with applicable manufacturer requirements and relevant MSHEM processes. Measuring and torque equipment whose accuracy affects safety shall be calibrated or verified at defined intervals."},{"id":"3.37.23","page":129,"heading":"Maintenance, Inspection and Repair","text":"Changes that may affect tire, wheel or rim compatibility, operating limits, integrity, servicing or risk controls shall be managed in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.37.24","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"Tire and rim activities shall be performed under documented procedures that define authorized people, task sequence and critical controls. Servicing shall be conducted in a designated service area or controlled field work area with a load-bearing surface, controlled access, task lighting and applicable exclusion zones."},{"id":"3.37.25","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"Before wheel removal or servicing, equipment shall be isolated, immobilized and supported against rolling, articulation, falling or lowering. Controls shall comply with Section 3.23 Energy Isolation and Control of Stored Energy, and the applicable technical requirements of MSHA 30 CFR 56.14105, 57.14105, 56.14211 and 57.14211."},{"id":"3.37.26","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"Before demounting, repair or wheel removal, tires shall be completely deflated, the valve core removed and zero pressure verified from outside the potential trajectory. Tires that are damaged, suspected to be unsafe or significantly underinflated shall be completely deflated before wheel removal. Where either tire or wheel in a dual assembly is damaged or suspected to be unsafe, both tires shall be completely deflated. Controls shall meet the applicable technical requirements of OSHA 29 CFR 1910.177 and MSHA 30 CFR 56.14104 and 57.14104."},{"id":"3.37.27","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"Tires shall be inflated using a verified restraining device or engineered stand-off arrangement appropriate to the wheel type, size and inflation pressure. Inflation equipment shall permit pressure control and monitoring from outside the potential trajectory. Inflation shall be continuously attended and shall not exceed manufacturer or equipment OEM pressure limits. Controls shall meet the applicable technical requirements of OSHA 29 CFR 1910.177, including 1910.177(d)(4), and MSHA 30 CFR 56.14104 and 57.14104."},{"id":"3.37.28","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"Before inflation, components shall be inspected and verified as compatible, serviceable, correctly positioned, seated and locked using current OEM or manufacturer information. Pressurized components shall not be struck, adjusted or repositioned. Wheel fasteners shall be tightened and verified to OEM-specified torque, including re-torque where required."},{"id":"3.37.29","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"Heat shall not be applied to a tire, wheel, rim, associated component or fastener while a tire is mounted. Wheel and rim components shall not be welded, brazed, heated or reworked unless permitted by OSHA 29 CFR 1910.177(e), (f) and (g), authorized by the component manufacturer and completed under an approved repair specification. Components that cannot be safely repaired shall be removed from service."},{"id":"3.37.30","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"Equipment affected by tire overheating, fire, electrical contact, lightning exposure, suspected pyrolysis or another condition capable of delayed failure shall be stopped and isolated. Exclusion zones and restricted approach periods shall be established using documented OEM, tire manufacturer and emergency response criteria."},{"id":"3.37.31","page":129,"heading":"Tire and Rim Servicing","text":"The affected assembly shall not be approached, moved, deflated or serviced until the defined criteria have been met and an authorized competent person confirms that work may proceed. Emergency responders shall act under an approved emergency response plan in accordance with section 5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning."},{"id":"3.37.32","page":130,"heading":"End of Life and Disposal","text":"Tires, wheels and rims removed from service shall be identified and segregated to prevent unauthorized reuse pending repair, return to the supplier or disposal."},{"id":"3.37.33","page":130,"heading":"End of Life and Disposal","text":"Condemned tires, wheels and rim components shall be rendered unusable before disposal and managed in accordance with section 3.6 Waste Management, and applicable environmental requirements."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Tire and Rim Management; tire, tyre, rim, inflation; Governance and Tire and Rim Management Planning; Risk Assessment and Critical Controls; Selection and Procurement; Receipt, Identification and Traceability; Storage and Handling; Maintenance, Inspection and Repair; Tire and Rim Servicing; End of Life and Disposal","trigger":"if large tires or rims are inspected, inflated, deflated, removed, installed or repaired","refs":["2.5","2.6","3.6","3.23","3.26","3.27","3.52","5.1"]},{"id":"3.38","title":"Explosives and Blasting Management","page":130,"purpose":"Explosives and blasting activities are authorized, planned, conducted and verified so that explosive materials remain secure and accounted for, unintended initiation is prevented, blast effects are controlled, and people, assets, underground workings, communities and the environment are protected.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units, Corporate Functions and contractors that manufacture, store, transport, handle, use or dispose of explosive materials, conduct blasting, or may expose people, infrastructure, communities or the environment to blast effects.","clauses":[{"id":"3.38.1","page":130,"heading":"Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall comply with applicable explosives legislation and authority requirements and hold the required licenses, permits and approvals before explosive materials are manufactured, procured, stored, transported or used. SAIS SAF-14, including the Declaration of Compliance under Section 15, shall be met. NFPA 495 shall be the minimum technical basis, supported by NFPA 400 Chapter 11 and NFPA 498 where applicable."},{"id":"3.38.2","page":130,"heading":"Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall appoint an accountable person for explosives and blasting, a custodian for each storage location and an authorized blaster for each blast. Only competent, authorized and appropriately licensed personnel shall conduct explosives or blasting activities."},{"id":"3.38.3","page":130,"heading":"Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement an explosives and blasting management plan proportionate to their activities and risks. The plan shall define how applicable legal, authority, manufacturer, NFPA 495 and this process’s requirements are implemented, including controls for the explosives lifecycle, blasting, misfires and emergencies."},{"id":"3.38.4","page":130,"heading":"Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish acceptance criteria and monitoring methods for ground vibration, air overpressure, flyrock and blast fumes that meet applicable permit and authority requirements and NFPA 495."},{"id":"3.38.5","page":130,"heading":"Governance and Planning","text":"Explosives and blasting hazards shall be managed under section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process and, where a material unwanted event is identified under section 2.6 Critical Control Management process."},{"id":"3.38.6","page":130,"heading":"Governance and Planning","text":"Changes to explosive materials, initiation systems, blast designs, facilities or transport arrangements shall be managed under section 3.52 Management of Change."},{"id":"3.38.7","page":131,"heading":"Governance and Planning","text":"Contractors conducting explosives or blasting activities shall meet the requirements of section 4.1 Contractor EHSS Management and the applicable requirements of this section."},{"id":"3.38.8","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Only explosive materials and initiation systems authorized by the applicable authority and accepted by Maaden shall be used. Products shall be classified, compatible and used within their approved conditions in accordance with SAIS SAF-14 Section 6, NFPA 495 and manufacturer requirements. Detonators and initiating devices shall be segregated from other explosives until required for priming or loading unless an authority approved alternative complies with NFPA 495."},{"id":"3.38.9","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Explosive materials shall be stored only in licensed facilities authorized for their classification and quantity. Storage facilities shall be sited, separated, constructed, protected and maintained in accordance with SAIS SAF-14 Sections 8–10 and applicable NFPA 495 quantity-distance requirements."},{"id":"3.38.10","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Continued use of a facility that does not meet applicable quantity-distance requirements shall require a quantitative risk assessment, compensating controls and written approval from SAIS."},{"id":"3.38.11","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Permanent underground explosive storage shall be permitted only where expressly authorized by SAIS and where development, egress, siting, ventilation, fire risk, security and emergency arrangements have been assessed under section 3.36 Underground Mines Ventilation, Fire and Refuge Safety process."},{"id":"3.38.12","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain traceable explosives inventory, reconcile each issue and return, complete physical stock counts at least monthly and promptly return unused materials to authorized storage. Access, keys and initiation equipment shall be controlled under SAIS SAF-14 Section 12 and SAIS Security Directives. Losses, discrepancies or security breaches shall be reported under authority requirements."},{"id":"3.38.13","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Transportation of explosive materials shall comply with SAIS SAF-14 Section 14 and NFPA 495, including requirements for authorization, compatibility, custody, security, routes, stops and emergency arrangements; applicable NFPA 498 requirements for safe havens and interchange lots; and, for off-site transport, section 3.43 Transportation of Dangerous Goods and Materials."},{"id":"3.38.14","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Manufacture, sensitization or on-site production of explosives or blasting agents shall require specific government or authority approval under SAIS SAF-14 Section 5 and shall meet NFPA 495."},{"id":"3.38.15","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Ammonium nitrate shall be stored, handled and used in accordance with SAIS SAF-14 Section 7 and NFPA 400 Chapter 11."},{"id":"3.38.16","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Maintenance or modification involving potential ignition, impact or contamination shall not begin at an explosives facility or vehicle until explosive materials have been removed or protected under MSHEM sections 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment and 3.22 Hot Work and Ignition Source Control processes."},{"id":"3.38.17","page":131,"heading":"Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport","text":"Vehicles and equipment shall be confirmed free of explosive materials before maintenance."},{"id":"3.38.18","page":131,"heading":"Blast Planning and Execution","text":"Each blast shall have a documented design meeting NFPA 495 and applicable authority requirements and approved by a person authorized for the type and scale of blasting. Design assumptions, critical controls, field and hole conditions, exclusion requirements and monitoring needs shall be verified before loading or firing. Deviations beyond approved tolerances shall be assessed and authorized before work continues."},{"id":"3.38.19","page":132,"heading":"Blast Planning and Execution","text":"The blast site and risk-based exclusion zone shall remain controlled from the arrival of explosives until the authorized blaster issues the all-clear. Access shall be restricted and unrelated people, vehicles and activities excluded. Loaded blasts shall be fired without unnecessary delay; any delay shall be authorized and the blast secured, monitored, handed over and recorded."},{"id":"3.38.20","page":132,"heading":"Blast Planning and Execution","text":"Initiation systems shall be installed, connected and tested under the approved blast design, manufacturer instructions and NFPA 495, and shall remain under the authorized blaster’s control. Measures shall prevent unintended initiation from lightning, radio frequency energy, stray current, static electricity or other energy sources, with activities suspended and people withdrawn where required."},{"id":"3.38.21","page":132,"heading":"Blast Planning and Execution","text":"Before firing, the authorized blaster shall verify that warnings and notifications have been given, people and equipment are in safe locations, access points are effectively controlled, safe escape arrangements are available and affected facilities, underground workings and external parties have been notified where required."},{"id":"3.38.22","page":132,"heading":"Blast Planning and Execution","text":"Re-entry shall not occur until the applicable waiting period has elapsed, fumes and atmospheric conditions are acceptable, the area has been examined for misfires and other hazards, and the authorized blaster has issued the all clear. Waiting and re-entry criteria shall meet NFPA 495, manufacturer and applicable authority requirements."},{"id":"3.38.23","page":132,"heading":"Blast Planning and Execution","text":"Business Units and sites shall maintain a misfire procedure meeting NFPA 495 and applicable authority requirements. Any charge suspected of failing to initiate as designed shall be treated as a misfire; the area shall remain secured, access restricted and the required waiting period observed. Misfires shall be resolved under an authorized blaster, with drilling or disturbance prohibited until formal release, and shall be recorded and reported."},{"id":"3.38.24","page":132,"heading":"Blast Planning and Execution","text":"Recovered, damaged, deteriorated, contaminated or unidentifiable explosive materials shall be quarantined, secured and disposed of only by authorized specialists at an approved location in accordance with NFPA 495, manufacturer requirements, authority approvals and documented Business Unit or site procedures."},{"id":"3.38.25","page":132,"heading":"Special Activities and Emergency Controls","text":"Blasting in gassy, dusty, hot, oxidizing, reactive or sulfide bearing ground shall not proceed until the ground and material conditions have been tested and characterized and appropriate explosive selection, loading time limits, monitoring and withdrawal criteria have been approved. Testing shall occur before initial use, following relevant changes and at a periodic frequency established by the Business Unit or site based on the risk assessment, technical specialist advice and explosive manufacturer recommendations."},{"id":"3.38.26","page":132,"heading":"Special Activities and Emergency Controls","text":"Underwater, compressed air and other specialized blasting methods shall be supported by appropriate technical expertise, a documented risk assessment and an approved procedure meeting NFPA 495 and applicable authority requirements. The procedure shall address the method specific interaction with vessels, diving or swimming activities, communication signals, charge identification, misfire recovery, unintended electrical currents, pressure- lock controls, electrical bonding and grounding, personnel restrictions and breakthrough conditions, as applicable."},{"id":"3.38.27","page":132,"heading":"Special Activities and Emergency Controls","text":"Where a fire has reached, or cannot be prevented from reaching, explosive materials or ammonium nitrate, the area shall be evacuated immediately, and no attempt shall be made to fight the fire."},{"id":"3.38.28","page":132,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish inspection, testing and maintenance requirements for explosives facilities, vehicles, bulk loading, blasting and initiation equipment and monitoring instruments. Defects affecting security, explosive condition, transport, loading, initiation or monitoring shall be corrected before further use."},{"id":"3.38.29","page":133,"heading":"Verification and Improvement","text":"Each blast shall have a traceable record meeting NFPA 495 and applicable authority requirements. Blast performance and control effectiveness shall be evaluated using blast records, inventory reconciliation, monitoring results, flyrock, fumes, misfires, deviations and complaints. Abnormal outcomes and repeated control failures shall be reported and investigated under MSHEM section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship, with corrective actions tracked to closure."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Explosives and Blasting Management; explosives, blasting, detonator, charging, misfire; Governance and Planning; Explosive Materials, Storage and Transport; Blast Planning and Execution; Special Activities and Emergency Controls; Verification and Improvement","trigger":"if explosives are transported, stored, issued, charged, fired, inspected or disposed","refs":["2.5","2.6","3.36","3.43","3.52","4.1","6.1"]},{"id":"3.39","title":"Drilling Operations Safety","page":133,"purpose":"Establish and maintain safe systems of work for drilling operations, from planning and mobilization through drilling, maintenance, demobilization and rehabilitation, to prevent harm to people, the environment, assets and operations.","scope":"This process applies to all Business Units and Corporate Functions, employees, contractors, subcontractors, service providers and visitors involved in or exposed to drilling operations under Maaden’s operational control. Covers all drilling methods, including auger, RAB, air-core, reverse-circulation, diamond core, sonic and water-bore drilling, together with associated equipment, access roads and remote field support activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.39.1","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions planning or managing drilling operations shall establish a documented drilling plan addressing the work scope, drilling method, location, resources and operational interfaces."},{"id":"3.39.2","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"Applicable permits, licenses, approvals and authorizations shall be obtained before the relevant activities commence."},{"id":"3.39.3","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"The drilling location shall be assessed for environmental sensitivities, underground and overhead services, ground conditions, restricted areas and other site specific hazards."},{"id":"3.39.4","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"Drilling risks and critical controls shall be identified, communicated, implemented and verified before work commences."},{"id":"3.39.5","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"Personnel shall be competent, trained, inducted and authorized for their assigned duties before commencing work."},{"id":"3.39.6","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"Drilling and support equipment shall be available, inspected and confirmed fit for purpose before mobilization and use."},{"id":"3.39.7","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"Required materials, emergency response arrangements, communication systems and medical support shall be available before operations commence."},{"id":"3.39.8","page":133,"heading":"Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness","text":"Access routes and journey management arrangements shall be assessed before mobilization."},{"id":"3.39.9","page":133,"heading":"Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess road access, drill pad locations and associated earthworks before construction or drilling operations commence."},{"id":"3.39.10","page":133,"heading":"Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements","text":"Road access, drill pads and associated earthworks shall be designed or approved by a competent person in accordance with applicable regulatory requirements and engineering standards."},{"id":"3.39.11","page":133,"heading":"Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements","text":"Access roads shall be designed, constructed and maintained for the safe movement of personnel, drilling equipment, support vehicles and emergency response vehicles."},{"id":"3.39.12","page":134,"heading":"Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements","text":"Drill pads shall provide sufficient dimensions, stability, gradient and drainage for rig operations, rod handling, equipment storage, exclusion zones and emergency response."},{"id":"3.39.13","page":134,"heading":"Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements","text":"Geotechnical hazards, steep slopes, soft or unstable ground, flood-prone areas and other ground related hazards shall be identified and controlled before construction."},{"id":"3.39.14","page":134,"heading":"Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements","text":"Safe access, turning, parking, pedestrian movement, reversing controls and emergency escape routes shall be provided and maintained at drilling locations."},{"id":"3.39.15","page":134,"heading":"Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements","text":"Access roads and drill pads shall be inspected and verified as safe and fit for purpose before drilling equipment is mobilized or operations commence."},{"id":"3.39.16","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Drilling operations shall be planned and conducted in accordance with approved operating procedures, risk assessments and applicable MSHEM requirements."},{"id":"3.39.17","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Only competent and authorized personnel shall perform drilling activities or operate drilling equipment."},{"id":"3.39.18","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Drill rigs, compressors and associated support equipment shall be inspected and maintained, and their safety critical functions shall be verified before use."},{"id":"3.39.19","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Drill rigs, masts and supporting systems shall be positioned, erected, lowered, operated and moved on stable supporting conditions and within manufacturer or approved engineered limits. Personnel shall be protected from mast movement, instability, suspended components and other associated line of fire hazards."},{"id":"3.39.20","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Personnel shall remain clear of rotating augers, drill stems, rods, feed mechanisms and other hazardous moving components. Personnel shall not pass under or step over a moving auger or drill stem. Rod and drill steel handling shall use mechanized handling where reasonably practicable and shall control crushing, pinch-point, caught- between and line of fire exposure."},{"id":"3.39.21","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"High pressure air, water and fluid systems shall be rated for the maximum operating pressure and provided with suitable restraints, locking devices or automatic protection where hose or connection failure could create a hazard. Pressure shall be isolated and safely released before connections are opened, equipment is maintained or blockages are cleared."},{"id":"3.39.22","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Following loss of power or control energy, drilling controls shall be placed in a safe or neutral condition and equipment secured against unintended movement before power is restored. Drilling shall not resume until safe conditions and required controls have been verified."},{"id":"3.39.23","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"During movement or repositioning of drilling equipment, effective communication or continuous visual contact shall be maintained between the operator and personnel assisting the movement. Drill steel, tools and other loose equipment shall be secured and the mast placed in a safe position before movement."},{"id":"3.39.24","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Drilling specific critical controls, including guarding, emergency stops, line of fire controls and exclusion zones, shall be implemented and verified before drilling commences and when conditions change."},{"id":"3.39.25","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Pre-start inspections and work team briefings shall be completed before each shift, and affected personnel shall be informed of changes to hazards, conditions or controls before work continues."},{"id":"3.39.26","page":134,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Drilling operations involving lifting or hazardous energy shall comply with applicable MSHEM lifting and energy isolation requirements, with unintended movement and the release of stored energy effectively controlled."},{"id":"3.39.27","page":135,"heading":"Drilling Equipment and Operational Controls","text":"Changes to drilling equipment, methods or operating parameters shall be assessed before implementation and shall be managed through the Management of Change process where its applicability criteria are met."},{"id":"3.39.28","page":135,"heading":"Remote Operational Requirements","text":"Where drilling operations are conducted in remote areas, the applicable requirements of 3.40 Exploration and Remote Field Safety shall also apply."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Drilling Operations Safety; drilling, drill rig, borehole, core, RC, DTH; Pre-Operational Planning and Readiness; Road Access and Drill Pad Requirements; Operational Requirements; Remote Operational Requirements","trigger":"if exploration, production or geotechnical drilling equipment and boreholes are involved","refs":[]},{"id":"3.40","title":"Exploration and Remote Field Safety","page":135,"purpose":"Define the controls for managing health and safety risks during exploration and remote field activities, including isolation, travel, difficult access, limited communication, environmental conditions and delayed emergency response.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden employees, contractors, subcontractors, service providers and visitors involved in exploration and remote field activities, including geological mapping, surveying, sampling, geophysics, drilling support, remote inspections, field camps, vehicle travel, equipment recovery and other work away from established operating facilities, from planning and mobilization through field operations, demobilization and rehabilitation.","clauses":[{"id":"3.40.1","page":135,"heading":"Pre-Commencement Requirements","text":"Required land access approvals, environmental permits and restricted area authorizations shall be confirmed, and applicable stakeholder commitments shall be addressed before site entry or ground disturbance."},{"id":"3.40.2","page":135,"heading":"Pre-Commencement Requirements","text":"A joint pre-mobilization survey shall be completed and used to develop the field execution plan and site-specific risk assessment. The survey shall involve relevant Maaden, contractor and technical representatives where significant interfaces, unfamiliar locations or shared responsibilities require joint assessment."},{"id":"3.40.3","page":135,"heading":"Pre-Commencement Requirements","text":"Personnel shall be competent, inducted and authorized for their assigned field activities."},{"id":"3.40.4","page":135,"heading":"Pre-Commencement Requirements","text":"Vehicles, equipment, travel routes, accommodation, communication systems, medical support and emergency resources shall be verified against the approved field execution plan and risk assessment before mobilization."},{"id":"3.40.5","page":135,"heading":"Pre-Commencement Requirements","text":"Field work shall commence only after a documented readiness review confirms that safety critical requirements are complete."},{"id":"3.40.6","page":135,"heading":"Field Site and Camp Requirements","text":"Field sites, camps, shelters, accommodation and equipment shall be located on stable ground, away from flood paths, steep slopes, old workings and other uncontrolled hazards, and secured against foreseeable weather conditions."},{"id":"3.40.7","page":135,"heading":"Field Site and Camp Requirements","text":"Safe access, vehicle parking, pedestrian routes, muster points and emergency escape routes shall be established, identified and maintained."},{"id":"3.40.8","page":135,"heading":"Field Site and Camp Requirements","text":"Drinking water, protection from heat and weather, sanitation, lighting, first aid, fire protection, spill response and waste facilities shall be provided based on personnel numbers, work duration, environmental conditions and the site-specific risk assessment."},{"id":"3.40.9","page":135,"heading":"Field Site and Camp Requirements","text":"Open holes, excavations and fall hazards shall be protected against unintended access, clearly marked and secured before being left unattended."},{"id":"3.40.10","page":136,"heading":"Remote Operational Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and implement a Remote Site Management Plan before commencing remote field activities. The extent and detail of the plan shall be proportionate to the activity risk, remoteness, duration, workforce, accessibility and available emergency response capability."},{"id":"3.40.11","page":136,"heading":"Remote Operational Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain a personnel accountability system that covers buddy arrangements, personnel locations, planned movements, assigned vehicles and expected return times. The system shall define when buddy arrangements are mandatory, the required communication intervals, based on risk, and the escalation and emergency response actions for missed check-ins or overdue personnel."},{"id":"3.40.12","page":136,"heading":"Remote Operational Requirements","text":"Primary and backup communication systems shall be available, maintained and verified before personnel are deployed."},{"id":"3.40.13","page":136,"heading":"Remote Operational Requirements","text":"Remote field emergency response and medical evacuation arrangements shall be established, communicated and verified before deployment, in coordination with the applicable emergency preparedness and medical services processes."},{"id":"3.40.14","page":136,"heading":"Remote Operational Requirements","text":"Remote field activities shall not begin or continue when communication systems, emergency response capability or required critical controls are unavailable or ineffective."},{"id":"3.40.15","page":136,"heading":"Journey Management Requirements","text":"Remote journeys shall be supported by a documented route assessment and Journey Management Plan addressing route and terrain conditions, occupants, driver, vehicle, planned departure and return times, rest breaks, communications, check-ins and overdue escalation."},{"id":"3.40.16","page":136,"heading":"Journey Management Requirements","text":"Remote journeys shall be authorized before departure, with changes to the approved journey reassessed, communicated and reauthorized before continuing."},{"id":"3.40.17","page":136,"heading":"Journey Management Requirements","text":"Vehicles shall be legally compliant, inspected, maintained and suitable for the route, terrain and load."},{"id":"3.40.18","page":136,"heading":"Journey Management Requirements","text":"Drivers shall be competent, authorized and fit for work, and all occupants shall wear seat belts."},{"id":"3.40.19","page":136,"heading":"Journey Management Requirements","text":"Lone and night travel shall be avoided where practicable and shall be specifically risk assessed where required."},{"id":"3.40.20","page":136,"heading":"Journey Management Requirements","text":"Vehicle recovery shall be performed by competent personnel using approved methods, rated recovery points and suitable equipment."},{"id":"3.40.21","page":136,"heading":"Health, Weather and Environmental Requirements","text":"Health, environmental and weather related risks specific to remote field activities shall be assessed and controlled in accordance with the applicable MSHEM processes."},{"id":"3.40.22","page":136,"heading":"Health, Weather and Environmental Requirements","text":"Weather forecasts and warnings shall be monitored, and defined trigger actions shall be established for heat, flood, lightning, wind, dust and poor visibility."},{"id":"3.40.23","page":136,"heading":"Health, Weather and Environmental Requirements","text":"Personnel shall not enter or remain in wadis, drainage channels or flood prone areas when flash flooding is possible."},{"id":"3.40.24","page":136,"heading":"Health, Weather and Environmental Requirements","text":"Approved access tracks and work areas shall be used, and unnecessary vegetation clearing, off-road travel and land disturbance shall be avoided."},{"id":"3.40.25","page":137,"heading":"Health, Weather and Environmental Requirements","text":"Fuel, chemicals and waste shall be stored, handled, transported and disposed of using approved environmental and spill controls."},{"id":"3.40.26","page":137,"heading":"Health, Weather and Environmental Requirements","text":"Temporary facilities, waste and equipment shall be removed and disturbed areas rehabilitated following completion of work."},{"id":"3.40.27","page":137,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and approve a site- or project-specific Emergency Response Plan addressing credible emergencies before remote work commences, in accordance with section 5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning."},{"id":"3.40.28","page":137,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness","text":"Emergency roles, contacts, GPS coordinates, access directions, maps, assembly points, evacuation routes and transfer locations shall be current and available."},{"id":"3.40.29","page":137,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness","text":"Suitable first-aid, Emergency Communication Devices, fire equipment, spill kits and rescue equipment shall be available, inspected and ready for use."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Exploration and Remote Field Safety; exploration, remote field, remote camp, reconnaissance; Pre-Commencement Requirements; Field Site and Camp Requirements; Remote Operational Requirements; Journey Management Requirements; Health, Weather and Environmental Requirements; Emergency Preparedness","trigger":"if work is performed in exploration or a remote field location","refs":["5.1"]},{"id":"3.41","title":"Molten Metal Safety","page":137,"purpose":"Molten metal activities are designed, operated and maintained to prevent uncontrolled contact between molten metal and people, water, incompatible materials or uncontrolled energy, and to prevent or mitigate spills, run-outs, splashes, fires, explosions and harmful exposure.","scope":"This process applies to all Business Units that melt, charge, retain, treat, tap, sample, pour, cast, granulate, transfer, transport, or otherwise handle metal in a molten state throughout design, construction, commissioning, operation, inspection, maintenance, modification, shutdown, and decommissioning, including activities performed by contractors.","clauses":[{"id":"3.41.1","page":137,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"Business Units shall assess molten metal hazards and credible loss of control scenarios for normal operation, startup, shutdown, maintenance, abnormal conditions, and emergencies."},{"id":"3.41.2","page":137,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"The assessment shall address, where applicable, hazards, initiating events, failure modes, and potential consequences associated with:\na. contact with water, moisture, or wet materials, tools, or equipment;\nb. incompatible, contaminated, reactive, or enclosed charge materials;\nc. splashing, foaming, overflow, leakage, run-out, breakout, or loss of containment;\nd. failure of refractory, cooling, structural, lifting, transfer, or containment systems;\ne. loss or disturbance of power, cooling, fuel, air, hydraulic, or control systems;\nf. uncontrolled movement, collision, derailment, rollover, or dropped loads;\ng. exposure to heat, flame, fumes, gases, dust, noise, or projected material;\nh. electrical or electromagnetic hazards;\ni. restricted access, egress, or emergency escape; and\nj. resulting fire, explosion, injury, exposure, or asset damage."},{"id":"3.41.3","page":138,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"Controls shall prioritize elimination, separation, mechanization, automation, remote operation, engineered containment, and safeguarding before reliance on administrative controls or personal protective equipment."},{"id":"3.41.4","page":138,"heading":"Risk Assessment and Controls","text":"Controls required to prevent or mitigate credible molten metal events shall be managed in accordance with section\n2.6 Critical Control Management requirements, including defined performance requirements and verification arrangements."},{"id":"3.41.5","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"Molten metal facilities and equipment shall have a documented design basis that addresses the hazards and credible consequences identified through the applicable risk assessment process."},{"id":"3.41.6","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"HAZOP or another structured hazard assessment technique shall be used where warranted by facility complexity or credible consequences. Changes affecting molten metal facilities, equipment, materials, operating conditions, safe operating limits, or controls shall be assessed before implementation and managed in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.41.7","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"The design basis shall define:\na. the safe operating envelope and applicable material compatibility requirements;\nb. design criteria for containment, diversion, and release management;\nc. cooling and utility requirements, including the required response to interruption and restoration;\nd. alarms, trips, shutdown functions, and emergency controls, including the need for automatic, local, or remote operation as determined by the hazard assessment; and\ne. access control, shielding, emergency escape, and safe refuge requirements."},{"id":"3.41.8","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"Where foreseeable events could obstruct escape, emergency arrangements shall provide at least two independent escape routes that cannot be obstructed by the same event, or safe refuge where immediate evacuation cannot be achieved safely."},{"id":"3.41.9","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"Molten metal equipment and areas shall contain or safely divert credible spills, run-outs, breakouts, excessive flows, overflows, foaming, and splashes away from people, occupied areas, escape routes, and equipment required for safety."},{"id":"3.41.10","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"Water systems, drains, hoses, and locations where water could accumulate shall be separated from credible molten metal release paths or protected against molten metal contact."},{"id":"3.41.11","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"Electrical, hydraulic, pneumatic, fuel, oxygen, control, and compressed gas systems shall be separated or protected where exposure to molten metal could initiate or escalate a credible event."},{"id":"3.41.12","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"Equipment and systems required for safety shall achieve or maintain a defined safe state following the loss or disturbance of energy, control, cooling, or other required utilities."},{"id":"3.41.13","page":138,"heading":"Design and Engineered Safeguards","text":"Where continuous cooling is required to prevent a credible high consequence event, an assured cooling supply shall be provided, and the required response to cooling interruption and restoration shall be defined."},{"id":"3.41.14","page":138,"heading":"Materials, Tools and Moisture Control","text":"Charge materials shall meet defined acceptance criteria for dryness, compatibility, contamination, and the presence of closed, hollow, sealed, or enclosed items that could retain moisture, liquid, gas, or pressure before introduction into molten metal."},{"id":"3.41.15","page":139,"heading":"Materials, Tools and Moisture Control","text":"Where the source, type, or history of charge materials indicates potential radioactive contamination, the materials shall be assessed and controlled in accordance with section 3.16 Radiation Management before acceptance and introduction into molten metal."},{"id":"3.41.16","page":139,"heading":"Materials, Tools and Moisture Control","text":"Tools, molds, sampling devices, and other equipment or surfaces that may contact molten metal shall meet defined requirements for dryness, cleanliness, compatibility, condition, temperature, and preparation before use to prevent moisture related, chemical, or exothermic reactions."},{"id":"3.41.17","page":139,"heading":"Operating and Transfer Controls","text":"Molten metal processes shall be operated within defined safe operating limits. Safety significant parameters, indicators of deviation, alarms, and required responses shall be defined and understood by affected personnel."},{"id":"3.41.18","page":139,"heading":"Operating and Transfer Controls","text":"Personnel exposure during tapping, pouring, casting, granulation, and other activities with credible molten metal exposure shall be minimized through mechanization, automation, remote operation, or engineered separation, as determined by the risk assessment."},{"id":"3.41.19","page":139,"heading":"Operating and Transfer Controls","text":"Entry into and presence within molten metal exposure zones shall be restricted to authorized people whose presence is required."},{"id":"3.41.20","page":139,"heading":"Operating and Transfer Controls","text":"Molten metal transfer equipment, containers, and lifting attachments shall be designed, selected, operated, and maintained to prevent loss of containment, uncontrolled movement, dropped loads, overflow, and personnel exposure to heat or splash."},{"id":"3.41.21","page":139,"heading":"Operating and Transfer Controls","text":"Molten metal transfer routes shall be defined and controlled to address credible collision, derailment, rollover, route obstruction, loss of control, and spill consequences."},{"id":"3.41.22","page":139,"heading":"Integrity and Protective Systems","text":"Equipment and protective systems required to prevent, detect, control, or mitigate a credible molten metal event shall be identified and managed in accordance with section 3.50 Asset Integrity Management, with defined inspection, testing, maintenance, and acceptance criteria. This shall include, where applicable, containment, refractory and cooling systems, supporting structures, lifting and transfer equipment, alarms, trips, interlocks, shutdown functions, emergency controls, and backup systems or services required for safety."},{"id":"3.41.23","page":139,"heading":"Integrity and Protective Systems","text":"Safety critical functions shall be tested at defined intervals and following any modification, repair, maintenance, or other intervention that could affect the required safety function."},{"id":"3.41.24","page":139,"heading":"Integrity and Protective Systems","text":"Equipment or protective systems that do not meet defined safety or integrity criteria shall be removed from service or placed in a safe condition. Continued operation with an impaired safety function shall be risk assessed, authorized, controlled, time-bound, and managed in accordance with section 3.51 EHSS Critical Systems Bypass process."},{"id":"3.41.25","page":139,"heading":"Competency, Exposure Control and Emergency Readiness","text":"People who operate, maintain, inspect, or supervise molten metal activities shall be competent and authorized for their assigned duties, including applicable emergency duties. Other people entering molten metal exposure areas shall receive relevant hazard information and emergency instructions before entry."},{"id":"3.41.26","page":139,"heading":"Competency, Exposure Control and Emergency Readiness","text":"Exposure to molten metal, heat, flame, hot surfaces, airborne contaminants, and noise shall be assessed and controlled in accordance with sections 3.13 Airborne Contaminants and Chemical Exposure Control, 3.14 Noise and Hearing Conservation, 3.15 Thermal Stress and Physical Hazards, and 3.31 Personal Protective Equipment, as applicable."},{"id":"3.41.27","page":140,"heading":"Competency, Exposure Control and Emergency Readiness","text":"Protective clothing and PPE for molten metal exposure shall be selected in accordance with Process 3.31 Personal Protective Equipment, comply with ISO 11612, and, where applicable, have molten metal performance evaluated using ASTM F955."},{"id":"3.41.28","page":140,"heading":"Competency, Exposure Control and Emergency Readiness","text":"Emergency arrangements for credible molten metal scenarios shall address warning, safe shutdown and isolation, evacuation or refuge, exclusion zones, fire response, casualty rescue, spill or release control and process stabilization, as applicable, and be established and tested in accordance with section 5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Molten Metal Safety; molten metal, furnace, tapping, casting, dross, slag; Risk Assessment and Controls; Design and Engineered Safeguards; Materials, Tools and Moisture Control; Operating and Transfer Controls; Integrity and Protective Systems; Competency, Exposure Control and Emergency Readiness","trigger":"if molten metal, matte, slag, dross, furnaces, tapping, transfer or casting is involved","refs":["2.6","3.16","3.50","3.51","3.52","5.1"]},{"id":"3.42","title":"Laboratory Safety","page":140,"purpose":"Laboratory activities are planned, conducted and verified to prevent injury, harmful exposure, fire, explosion, uncontrolled reaction, loss of containment, contamination and environmental harm, while maintaining conditions necessary for safe laboratory work.","scope":"This process applies to laboratories and associated sample receipt, preparation, chemical storage, instrument, compressed gas and waste areas under Maaden control.","clauses":[{"id":"3.42.1","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Hazard and Risk Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify laboratories within their control and define their authorized activities, operating boundaries and access restrictions."},{"id":"3.42.2","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Hazard and Risk Management","text":"Laboratory hazards shall be assessed before work begins and when changes to materials, equipment, facilities, methods, personnel, operating conditions or activities could affect risk."},{"id":"3.42.3","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Hazard and Risk Management","text":"The assessment shall determine the required controls, operating limits, prohibited conditions, competency and authorization requirements, emergency arrangements, performance criteria and stop-work conditions."},{"id":"3.42.4","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Hazard and Risk Management","text":"Where the composition or hazard of a material or sample is uncertain, controls shall be based on credible worst- case properties until the hazards have been characterized."},{"id":"3.42.5","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Hazard and Risk Management","text":"Laboratory activities involving new materials, new methods, non-routine work or significantly changed conditions shall not proceed until associated hazards and controls have been evaluated."},{"id":"3.42.6","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Operations","text":"Laboratory activities shall be conducted by competent and authorized personnel in accordance with approved Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) that define operating limits, protective measures, abnormal condition response and safe shutdown requirements."},{"id":"3.42.7","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Operations","text":"Laboratory access shall be restricted to authorized people or people under appropriate supervision."},{"id":"3.42.8","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Operations","text":"Laboratory activities shall not begin or continue unless required controls are available, functional and meet their established performance criteria."},{"id":"3.42.9","page":140,"heading":"Laboratory Operations","text":"Unattended or after hours laboratory activities shall be assessed and authorized, with arrangements for monitoring, abnormal condition response and safe shutdown."},{"id":"3.42.10","page":141,"heading":"Materials and Samples","text":"Hazardous materials, samples and wastes shall be identified and controlled according to their hazards, compatibility, quantities, condition and intended use."},{"id":"3.42.11","page":141,"heading":"Materials and Samples","text":"Hazardous materials, samples, intermediates, wastes and secondary containers shall be identified to prevent misidentification, unintended use and incompatible interaction."},{"id":"3.42.12","page":141,"heading":"Materials and Samples","text":"Materials shall be stored, handled and segregated to prevent incompatible interaction, fire, explosion, uncontrolled reaction or hazardous release."},{"id":"3.42.13","page":141,"heading":"Materials and Samples","text":"Quantities and storage arrangements for flammable, combustible and other fire hazardous materials shall remain within the approved laboratory fire protection design and applicable fire protection requirements, including NFPA 45 requirements."},{"id":"3.42.14","page":141,"heading":"Materials and Samples","text":"Expired, deteriorated, unknown, leaking or otherwise unserviceable materials shall be isolated from use until their safety, fitness for use and disposition have been determined."},{"id":"3.42.15","page":141,"heading":"Materials and Samples","text":"Samples requiring legal, regulatory, contractual or quality traceability shall be controlled through documented receipt, custody, retention and disposition arrangements."},{"id":"3.42.16","page":141,"heading":"Engineering and Equipment Controls","text":"Ventilation, containment and other engineering controls shall be provided where hazardous exposure, airborne contamination or uncontrolled release could occur and shall be verified against documented performance criteria, including laboratory ventilation requirements based on ANSI/ASSP Z9.5."},{"id":"3.42.17","page":141,"heading":"Engineering and Equipment Controls","text":"Laboratory facilities, equipment, utilities, safeguards, alarms and protective systems shall be designed, selected and maintained to manage the applicable hazards and intended service conditions."},{"id":"3.42.18","page":141,"heading":"Engineering and Equipment Controls","text":"Equipment affecting laboratory safety or containment shall be removed from service or otherwise controlled when its condition could adversely affect safe operation."},{"id":"3.42.19","page":141,"heading":"Engineering and Equipment Controls","text":"Compressed gases, cryogenic substances, flammable liquids and reactive materials shall be controlled against release, incompatible interaction, fire, explosion, overpressure and hazardous exposure, with compressed-gas cylinders identified, secured against falling, protected from valve damage, stored in adequately ventilated locations, segregated according to compatibility, and clearly marked as full, in use or empty."},{"id":"3.42.20","page":141,"heading":"Engineering and Equipment Controls","text":"Environmental conditions and operating parameters that could affect laboratory safety, containment or the validity of laboratory results shall be controlled within defined limits."},{"id":"3.42.21","page":141,"heading":"Engineering and Equipment Controls","text":"Engineering controls, containment systems and safety critical laboratory equipment shall be inspected, tested, calibrated, maintained or otherwise verified at frequencies necessary to demonstrate their continued effectiveness."},{"id":"3.42.22","page":141,"heading":"Engineering and Equipment Controls","text":"A deficiency affecting a required laboratory control shall be corrected before the affected activity continues unless authorized interim controls reduce the risk to an acceptable level."},{"id":"3.42.23","page":141,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness","text":"Laboratory emergency arrangements and equipment shall address credible exposure, spill, fire, uncontrolled reaction, loss of containment and control failure scenarios."},{"id":"3.42.24","page":141,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness","text":"Emergency eyewash and shower equipment shall be provided where immediate flushing could be required and shall meet established location, accessibility and performance criteria, including ANSI/ISEA Z358.1 requirements."},{"id":"3.42.25","page":142,"heading":"Waste and Decontamination","text":"Laboratory waste, contaminated equipment and contamination hazards shall be identified, contained, decontaminated where required, and managed through applicable hazardous material, waste and environmental processes."},{"id":"3.42.26","page":142,"heading":"Waste and Decontamination","text":"Equipment, materials or containers leaving laboratory control for maintenance, transport, storage, disposal or reuse shall be assessed and decontaminated where required to prevent exposure, contamination or unintended release of hazardous substances."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Laboratory Safety; laboratory, lab, sample preparation, analytical; Laboratory Hazard and Risk Management; Laboratory Operations; Materials and Samples; Engineering and Equipment Controls; Emergency Preparedness; Waste and Decontamination","trigger":"if laboratory samples, chemicals, gases, heated equipment or analytical instruments are used","refs":[]},{"id":"3.43","title":"Transportation of Dangerous Goods and Materials","page":142,"purpose":"Goods and materials transported for or on behalf of Maaden are classified, prepared, transported and delivered through controlled arrangements that prevent harm to people, the environment, assets, communities and transport infrastructure.","scope":"This process applies to the transport of dangerous goods, hazardous materials, waste and other regulated materials by or on behalf of Maaden. Passenger transport, stationary storage, waste treatment and disposal, radiation protection programs, lifting operations and general vehicle operation shall be managed through the applicable interfacing MSHEM processes.","clauses":[{"id":"3.43.1","page":142,"heading":"Transport Governance and Authorization","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify the materials, transport activities, transport modes, jurisdictions and supply chain roles under their responsibility or control."},{"id":"3.43.2","page":142,"heading":"Transport Governance and Authorization","text":"Responsibilities for classification, preparation, loading, transport, unloading, emergency response and transfer of custody shall be defined before transport begins."},{"id":"3.43.3","page":142,"heading":"Transport Governance and Authorization","text":"Transport activities shall comply with applicable legal, regulatory, permit, authority, contractual and formally adopted Maaden requirements, and all required licenses, permits, registrations, approvals and certificates shall be valid before transport begins."},{"id":"3.43.4","page":142,"heading":"Classification and Risk Assessment","text":"Goods and materials shall be identified and classified by competent personnel before packaging, documentation, carrier selection or transport, with materials of unknown identity or uncertain classification prevented from transport."},{"id":"3.43.5","page":142,"heading":"Classification and Risk Assessment","text":"The transport determination shall identify applicable shipping descriptions, identifiers, hazard classifications, quantities, physical state, containment requirements, transport restrictions and emergency information requirements."},{"id":"3.43.6","page":142,"heading":"Classification and Risk Assessment","text":"Transport risks shall be assessed before initial transport operations and when changes to the material, quantity, containment, mode, route, destination or applicable requirements may affect risk."},{"id":"3.43.7","page":142,"heading":"Classification and Risk Assessment","text":"Risk assessments shall consider credible loss of containment, fire, explosion, exposure, incompatibility, cargo movement, collision, rollover, route conditions, weather, security threats, affected communities, environmentally sensitive areas and emergency response capability. Additional journey management, monitoring, tracking, escort, security or emergency controls shall be implemented where required by applicable obligations or risk assessments."},{"id":"3.43.8","page":143,"heading":"Carriers, Personnel and Transport Equipment","text":"Carriers and transport contractors shall be selected and monitored through a risk-based process that verifies, as applicable: legal authorization to operate; competent and authorized personnel; compatible and serviceable transport equipment; required insurance; emergency response capability; and control of subcontracted activities."},{"id":"3.43.9","page":143,"heading":"Carriers, Personnel and Transport Equipment","text":"All vehicles and containers used to transport hazardous materials shall be operated and maintained such that the release of liquids, litter, dust, solids or odor are prevented while in transit."},{"id":"3.43.10","page":143,"heading":"Carriers, Personnel and Transport Equipment","text":"Contracts shall define applicable EHSS, technical, competency, emergency response, reporting, verification and subcontracting requirements."},{"id":"3.43.11","page":143,"heading":"Carriers, Personnel and Transport Equipment","text":"Personnel performing regulated or safety critical transport activities shall be competent, authorized and, where required, licensed or certified."},{"id":"3.43.12","page":143,"heading":"Carriers, Personnel and Transport Equipment","text":"Vehicles, transport units and equipment shall be compatible with the cargo, maintained, inspected and operated within their design limits. Equipment with defects that could affect safe transport shall not be used."},{"id":"3.43.13","page":143,"heading":"Containment, Loading and Cargo Control","text":"Goods and materials shall be packaged or contained in approved, compatible and serviceable systems capable of maintaining integrity during foreseeable transport conditions. Damaged, leaking, contaminated, expired or otherwise nonconforming containment shall not be loaded or transported."},{"id":"3.43.14","page":143,"heading":"Containment, Loading and Cargo Control","text":"Cargo shall be segregated, distributed and secured to prevent incompatible interaction, movement, loss of stability or release of materials."},{"id":"3.43.15","page":143,"heading":"Containment, Loading and Cargo Control","text":"Loading, unloading, filling and transfer activities shall be performed by competent and authorized personnel using controls that prevent loss of containment, incompatible mixing, fire, explosion, exposure and environmental release."},{"id":"3.43.16","page":143,"heading":"Containment, Loading and Cargo Control","text":"Material identity, receiving containment, available capacity, connections, compatibility and applicable protective systems shall be verified before transfer operations commence. Applicable protective systems may include isolation, overfill prevention, emergency shutdown, ignition control, bonding, earthing and vapor control measures."},{"id":"3.43.17","page":143,"heading":"Containment, Loading and Cargo Control","text":"Containment systems, connections, closures, vehicles and transfer areas shall be inspected before release from loading or unloading locations."},{"id":"3.43.18","page":143,"heading":"Hazard Communication and Custody","text":"Packages, containers, vehicles and transport units shall display applicable hazard markings, labels, placards, identification numbers and warnings. Where applicable, facilities shall clearly placard the vehicle with the hazard classification of the material being transported in accordance with the United Nations chemical hazard classification system for the transport of dangerous goods."},{"id":"3.43.19","page":143,"heading":"Hazard Communication and Custody","text":"Regulated shipments shall be accompanied by complete, accurate and current transport documentation and emergency information."},{"id":"3.43.20","page":143,"heading":"Hazard Communication and Custody","text":"The receiving party shall verify shipment identity and condition, including any evidence of leakage, damage, tampering or discrepancy, before accepting custody."},{"id":"3.43.21","page":144,"heading":"Hazard Communication and Custody","text":"Rejected, returned or undeliverable shipments shall remain under controlled custody until managed through an authorized arrangement."},{"id":"3.43.22","page":144,"heading":"Transport Readiness and Incident Management","text":"Transport activities shall not commence until required transport controls have been verified."},{"id":"3.43.23","page":144,"heading":"Transport Readiness and Incident Management","text":"Arrangements shall be established to manage credible transport incidents and emergencies, including the reporting and management of spills, releases, theft, tampering and loss of custody in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory and Maaden requirements."},{"id":"3.43.24","page":144,"heading":"Special Transport Requirements","text":"Waste shall be classified before transport and transported only by authorized transporters to authorized receiving facilities using required approvals, compatible containment, placarding, tracking and transport documentation. Transfers of custody and final receipt shall be recorded and rejected, or undeliverable waste shall remain under controlled custody."},{"id":"3.43.25","page":144,"heading":"Special Transport Requirements","text":"Radioactive materials shall be transported only with required authorization, approved packages, competent and authorized personnel, transport documentation, radiation protection and security controls, continuity of custody, and any category specific tracking, communication, escort and incident reporting requirements."},{"id":"3.43.26","page":144,"heading":"Special Transport Requirements","text":"Explosives and blasting agents shall be transported under authorized arrangements that define controls for vehicles, routes, segregation, ignition sources, security, parking, communication and emergencies based on their classification and quantity."},{"id":"3.43.27","page":144,"heading":"Special Transport Requirements","text":"Cyanide shall be transported using verified competent carriers, compatible and secure containment, segregation from incompatible materials, route risk assessment, tracking, communication, emergency arrangements, security controls and delivery verification, with any claim of conformance with the International Cyanide Management Code supported by verification evidence."},{"id":"3.43.28","page":144,"heading":"Special Transport Requirements","text":"Flammable and combustible liquids, gases, cryogenic fluids, elevated temperature materials and other dangerous goods shall be transported in accordance with applicable classification, packaging, containment, segregation, transfer, competency, documentation, carrier acceptance, security and emergency requirements for the material and mode of transport."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Transportation of Dangerous Goods and Materials; dangerous goods, hazardous transport, tanker, loading; Transport Governance and Authorization; Classification and Risk Assessment; Carriers, Personnel and Transport Equipment; Containment, Loading and Cargo Control; Hazard Communication and Custody; Transport Readiness and Incident Management; Special Transport Requirements","trigger":"Use when dangerous or regulated goods are classified, prepared, contained, loaded, transported, delivered or transferred in custody.","refs":[]},{"id":"3.44","title":"Building and Facility Safety","page":144,"purpose":"Establish requirements for the lifecycle management of buildings and facilities to protect people, assets, the environment and business continuity through safe design, construction, occupancy, operation, maintenance, modification and decommissioning.","scope":"This process applies to permanent, temporary and portable buildings and occupied structures under Maaden operational control in process and non-process areas. It includes offices, control rooms, workshops, laboratories, utility and support buildings, warehouses, accommodation and welfare facilities, temporary cabins, occupied tents, contractor occupied facilities and buildings essential to operations even when not routinely occupied.","clauses":[{"id":"3.44.1","page":145,"heading":"Building and Facility Safety Governance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain risk-based building and facility safety arrangements based on documented consideration of building type, location, occupancy, use, condition, hazard exposure and criticality. When establishing and reviewing these arrangements, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall consider relevant facility lifecycle and asset management principles described in ISO 41001 and ISO\n55001."},{"id":"3.44.2","page":145,"heading":"Building and Facility Safety Governance","text":"Each building or facility shall have an identified owner or responsible function accountable for its approved use, occupancy, compliance, inspection, maintenance and management of identified deficiencies. The owner or responsible function shall ensure that current approvals, design and as-built information, occupancy limitations, safety critical systems, inspection records, modifications and condition status are maintained"},{"id":"3.44.3","page":145,"heading":"Building and Facility Safety Governance","text":"Buildings and facilities shall comply with applicable legal, regulatory, permit, engineering, accessibility, fire and life safety requirements, including, where applicable, Saudi Building Code SBC 201, SBC 301, SBC 302, SBC 401, SBC 501, SBC 701 and SBC 801; Saudi Civil Defense requirements; SAIS Safety and Fire Protection Directives SAF-03, SAF-04, SAF-06 and SAF-09; and applicable SASO technical regulations."},{"id":"3.44.4","page":145,"heading":"Building and Facility Safety Governance","text":"Building and facility risks shall be managed in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management."},{"id":"3.44.5","page":145,"heading":"Facility Planning, Siting and Design","text":"Buildings in process and non-process areas shall be sited considering intended use and occupancy, adjacent activities, process and non-process hazards, traffic, security, natural and environmental hazards, separation distances, evacuation and emergency access. Critical facilities shall meet applicable emergency response and business continuity objectives consistent with ISO 22301."},{"id":"3.44.6","page":145,"heading":"Facility Planning, Siting and Design","text":"Permanent buildings, portable buildings and occupied tents exposed to credible process fire, explosion or toxic release hazards shall be evaluated in accordance with section 3.47 Process Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment, SAIS SAF-03 and API RP 752, API RP 753 or API RP 756, as applicable, regardless of their designated area. Approved siting, occupancy limitations and required protective measures shall be implemented and maintained."},{"id":"3.44.7","page":145,"heading":"Facility Planning, Siting and Design","text":"New buildings and modifications to existing buildings shall be designed by appropriately qualified professionals using an approved design basis addressing intended use, occupancy, life safety, structural and environmental loads, foreseeable hazards and applicable codes. The design shall provide structural integrity, fire protection, fire resistance and compartmentation, safe access and egress, accessibility, electrical and mechanical safety, ventilation, drainage, emergency systems, applicable environmental protection measures and safe maintenance access."},{"id":"3.44.8","page":145,"heading":"Facility Planning, Siting and Design","text":"Floors, roofs, stairs, ramps, platforms, walkways, ladders, elevated edges and openings shall be designed, protected and maintained to support foreseeable loads and prevent slips, trips and falls, in accordance with applicable Saudi Building Code and SAIS SAF-06 requirements and, at mining operations, applicable provisions of MSHA 30 CFR Parts 56 and 57, Subparts J and P."},{"id":"3.44.9","page":145,"heading":"Facility Planning, Siting and Design","text":"Temporary and portable buildings, cabins and occupied tents shall not be occupied until their location, structural stability, anchoring, weather resistance, fire and electrical safety, ventilation, occupancy capacity, access and egress have been assessed and authorized in accordance with applicable codes, manufacturer requirements and site hazards."},{"id":"3.44.10","page":146,"heading":"Construction, Commissioning and Occupancy","text":"New buildings and modifications affecting building or facility safety shall be subject to documented design and construction compliance verification in accordance with applicable project and Management of Change requirements. Verification shall confirm conformance with the approved design, applicable safety requirements and regulatory obligations."},{"id":"3.44.11","page":146,"heading":"Construction, Commissioning and Occupancy","text":"Buildings shall not be occupied or placed into service until required commissioning, inspections, statutory approvals and readiness verification are complete. Safety critical systems shall be functionally tested, and current drawings, certificates, operating and maintenance information and occupancy limitations shall be accepted by the responsible owner before handover."},{"id":"3.44.12","page":146,"heading":"Fire and Life Safety","text":"Buildings shall have fire prevention, detection, alarm, suppression, portable firefighting, emergency lighting and emergency communication measures based on the approved occupancy classification and documented fire risk assessment. Design and installation shall comply with applicable SBC 801, Saudi Civil Defense, SAIS SAF-03 and SAF-04, and NFPA 1, NFPA 10, NFPA 13, NFPA 72 and NFPA 101 requirements."},{"id":"3.44.13","page":146,"heading":"Fire and Life Safety","text":"Means of egress, emergency exits and evacuation routes shall be sufficient for the approved occupancy, clearly identified, illuminated, unobstructed and maintained in serviceable condition in accordance with applicable codes and, where specified by Maaden for mining operations, MSHA 30 CFR 56.4530 and 57.4530."},{"id":"3.44.14","page":146,"heading":"Fire and Life Safety","text":"Fire protection and life safety systems shall be inspected, tested and maintained, and restored following impairment, in accordance with section 5.3 Emergency Response Equipment Maintenance, Testing and Interruption process."},{"id":"3.44.15","page":146,"heading":"Fire and Life Safety","text":"Emergency and standby power systems supporting fire and life safety shall comply with applicable NFPA 110 requirements. System impairments shall be subject to documented risk assessment, authorization and compensatory measures until restoration in accordance with section 5.3."},{"id":"3.44.16","page":146,"heading":"Fire and Life Safety","text":"Building occupancy, fire hazards, access, egress, fire protection systems and credible emergency scenarios shall be addressed in emergency response and pre-incident planning in accordance with section 5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning requirements."},{"id":"3.44.17","page":146,"heading":"Operation, Inspection and Building Integrity","text":"Buildings and facilities shall be operated, used and maintained within approved design, occupancy, contents, structural loading, building siting and risk assessment limits and assumptions."},{"id":"3.44.18","page":146,"heading":"Operation, Inspection and Building Integrity","text":"Building safety critical systems, services and utilities shall be identified, inspected, tested and maintained to control associated safety, health and environmental risks."},{"id":"3.44.19","page":146,"heading":"Operation, Inspection and Building Integrity","text":"Workplaces, access routes, passageways, storerooms, service rooms and welfare facilities shall be maintained clean, orderly, sanitary and free from conditions that could affect safe use. Potable water, sanitary facilities and housekeeping at mining operations shall meet applicable MSHA 30 CFR 56.20002, 56.20003, 56.20008, 57.20002,\n57.20003 and 57.20008 requirements."},{"id":"3.44.20","page":146,"heading":"Operation, Inspection and Building Integrity","text":"Buildings and structures shall undergo risk-based inspection and condition assessment by qualified personnel at defined intervals and following events, damage or deterioration that could affect structural integrity, in accordance with applicable Saudi Building Code requirements."},{"id":"3.44.21","page":147,"heading":"Operation, Inspection and Building Integrity","text":"Deficiencies affecting structural integrity, fire protection, emergency response capability, occupancy safety or environmental performance shall be risk assessed, controlled, tracked and corrected. Where structural integrity, fire safety or life safety may be compromised, the affected area or building shall be evacuated, isolated or occupancy restricted until confirmed safe by a qualified person authorized by the responsible owner."},{"id":"3.44.22","page":147,"heading":"Environmental and Sustainability Controls","text":"Buildings and facilities shall be designed, operated, maintained, modified and decommissioned to prevent pollution and minimize adverse environmental impacts. Applicable environmental controls arising from legal and regulatory requirements, permit conditions, approved environmental assessments and environmental risk assessments shall be incorporated, maintained and verified."},{"id":"3.44.23","page":147,"heading":"Change Management and Decommissioning","text":"Changes affecting a building or facility’s approved use, occupancy, design, structural loading, layout, fire protection, ventilation, utilities or emergency arrangements shall be assessed and authorized before implementation in accordance with Process 3.52 Management of Change and applicable code, permit and authority approval requirements."},{"id":"3.44.24","page":147,"heading":"Change Management and Decommissioning","text":"Buildings and facilities removed from service shall remain safe, isolated and secured against unauthorized access until their final disposition, in accordance with applicable regulatory and MSHEM decommissioning requirements."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Building and Facility Safety; building, facility, office, occupancy, fire protection; Building and Facility Safety Governance; Facility Planning, Siting and Design; Construction, Commissioning and Occupancy; Fire and Life Safety; Operation, Inspection and Building Integrity; Environmental and Sustainability Controls; Change Management and Decommissioning","trigger":"if buildings, occupied facilities, utilities, fire protection or public-use areas are involved","refs":["2.5","3.47","5.1","5.3"]},{"id":"3.45","title":"Warehouse Safety","page":147,"purpose":"Establish requirements for the safe receipt, handling, storage, picking, packing, staging, loading, unloading and dispatch of materials within warehouses and storage facilities to protect people, assets, the environment and business continuity through effective warehouse design, operation, maintenance, inspection and control of storage and material handling risks.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden owned, leased, operated or managed warehouses, stores, laydown areas and other temporary or permanent indoor or outdoor storage facilities, including contractor operated warehouse and storage facilities under Maaden control.","clauses":[{"id":"3.45.1","page":147,"heading":"Governance, Planning and Competency","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall manage warehouse related obligations, EHSS risks, material unwanted events and associated critical controls in accordance with sections 2.4 EHSS Legal and Regulatory Requirements,\n2.5 EHSS Risk Management and 2.6 Critical Control Management."},{"id":"3.45.2","page":147,"heading":"Governance, Planning and Competency","text":"New warehouse projects and modifications to existing warehouses that may affect EHSS risk shall be managed in accordance with Process 4.2 Project EHSS Management."},{"id":"3.45.3","page":147,"heading":"Governance, Planning and Competency","text":"Personnel performing warehouse activities shall be competent and, where required, authorized. Powered industrial truck operators and people conducting formal storage system inspections shall meet applicable role-specific competency and authorization requirements."},{"id":"3.45.4","page":147,"heading":"Governance, Planning and Competency","text":"Warehouse operations and maintenance activities requiring procedural control shall be conducted in accordance with Standard Operating Procedures or Standard Maintenance Procedures established under Processes 3.48 and\n3.49, as applicable. Non-routine work and work requiring a permit shall be controlled in accordance with Process\n3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment process."},{"id":"3.45.5","page":148,"heading":"Warehouse Design and Operating Limits","text":"Warehouse buildings, layouts, loading facilities, outdoor storage areas and associated infrastructure shall be designed, constructed and maintained for their intended use, approved occupancy where applicable, materials and quantities stored, structural loads, material handling methods, traffic arrangements, fire protection requirements and emergency access. Design and construction shall comply with applicable requirements, including SBC 201, SBC 801, SAIS SAF-03 Buildings for Industrial Facilities, SAIS SAF-04 Fire Protection Systems and Equipment and SAIS SAF-06 Plant Layout, Spacing and Access."},{"id":"3.45.6","page":148,"heading":"Warehouse Design and Operating Limits","text":"Warehouse operating limits shall be defined, documented, communicated and verified for compliance, and address, as applicable:\na. permitted materials, commodity classifications and maximum quantities;\nb. permitted storage heights and configurations;\nc. floor, mezzanine and storage-system capacities;\nd. fire-protection design assumptions and limitations;\ne. material-handling equipment and methods;\nf. traffic arrangements; and\ng. loading, unloading and adjacent operational interfaces."},{"id":"3.45.7","page":148,"heading":"Warehouse Design and Operating Limits","text":"Changes to stored materials, commodity classifications or quantities, storage heights or configurations, warehouse operating limits, storage systems, layouts, traffic routes, material handling equipment, ventilation, drainage, spill containment, fire protection systems or design assumptions, or emergency arrangements that may introduce or alter EHSS risk shall be managed in accordance with Process 3.52 Management of Change."},{"id":"3.45.8","page":148,"heading":"Storage Systems and Material Control","text":"Racking, shelving and other storage systems shall be designed, installed, commissioned, used, inspected, modified and maintained in accordance with applicable legal, manufacturer, engineering requirements, including applicable provisions of ANSI MH16.1 and BS EN 15635."},{"id":"3.45.9","page":148,"heading":"Storage Systems and Material Control","text":"Load capacities and configuration restrictions for floors, mezzanines and storage systems shall be displayed or readily available at the point of use and not exceeded."},{"id":"3.45.10","page":148,"heading":"Storage Systems and Material Control","text":"Storage systems exposed to foreseeable mobile-equipment impact shall have impact protection or equivalent controls, with damage or defects that could affect load stability or structural integrity assessed by a competent person, the affected area unloaded and restricted according to risk, and the system returned to service only after repair, replacement or confirmation that it is safe for its intended use."},{"id":"3.45.11","page":148,"heading":"Storage Systems and Material Control","text":"Pallets, containers and other unit load devices shall be selected and used based on the material, load, handling method, storage configuration and environmental conditions, with items that do not meet defined acceptance criteria identified, removed from use and repaired, replaced or confirmed safe before reuse."},{"id":"3.45.12","page":148,"heading":"Storage Systems and Material Control","text":"Materials and unit loads shall be identified, segregated and arranged according to their physical and chemical characteristics, compatibility requirements and identified risks. They shall be stacked, blocked, interlocked, restrained or limited in height, as necessary, to prevent collapse, falling, rolling, sliding, deterioration, contamination or loss of containment, in accordance with applicable provisions of 29 CFR 1910.176 and MSHA 30 CFR 56.16001 and\n57.16001 for mining operations."},{"id":"3.45.13","page":149,"heading":"Storage Systems and Material Control","text":"Materials and unit loads shall be inspected before acceptance or put away for damaged packaging, leakage, contamination, incorrect or missing identification, load instability and other conditions that could affect safe handling or storage. Unsafe materials or loads shall be segregated and controlled pending assessment and authorized disposition."},{"id":"3.45.14","page":149,"heading":"Material Handling and Traffic Control","text":"Material handling activities shall be planned and controlled to prevent injury, unstable or dropped loads, uncontrolled material movement and loss of load control. Manual handling and lifting operations shall comply with sections 3.17 Ergonomics and Work Design and 3.26 Lifting Operations, respectively."},{"id":"3.45.15","page":149,"heading":"Material Handling and Traffic Control","text":"Pedestrians shall be physically separated from powered industrial trucks and other mobile equipment where reasonably practicable. Warehouse traffic controls shall address routes, crossings, visibility restrictions, reversing, speed, parking, access and exclusion zones in accordance with sections 3.27 Mobile Equipment Safety and 3.29 Traffic and Journey Management. Where physical separation is not reasonably practicable, alternative controls shall be established based on the interaction risk."},{"id":"3.45.16","page":149,"heading":"Material Handling and Traffic Control","text":"Loading and unloading activities shall be controlled to prevent vehicle or trailer movement, premature departure, trailer instability, falls of people or materials and unauthorized access. Vehicle restraints, trailer supports and driver release controls shall be established based on risk. Dock levelers, ramps, dockboards and bridging devices shall be load rated, secured, inspected and maintained."},{"id":"3.45.17","page":149,"heading":"Material Handling and Traffic Control","text":"Conveyors, automated storage and retrieval systems and other warehouse machinery shall be safeguarded in accordance with section 3.33 Machine Safety and Guarding. Associated activities involving energy isolation, work at height or electrical hazards shall comply with sections 3.23, 3.24 and 3.30, respectively."},{"id":"3.45.18","page":149,"heading":"Material Handling and Traffic Control","text":"Battery charging and equipment fueling areas shall be designated and controlled for equipment movement, ventilation, ignition sources, electrical hazards, spills and emergency response in accordance with sections 3.20,\n3.22, 3.27 and 3.30, as applicable. Battery charging areas at mine facilities shall also comply with applicable provisions of MSHA 30 CFR 56.4502 and 57.4502."},{"id":"3.45.19","page":149,"heading":"Hazardous and Regulated Materials","text":"Hazardous materials stored or handled in warehouses shall be identified, controlled and managed according to their hazards, quantities, compatibility requirements, storage conditions and applicable obligations in accordance with section 3.20 Hazardous Material Management. Controls shall address containment, segregation, ignition sources, fire protection, spills, emergency arrangements and access, as applicable."},{"id":"3.45.20","page":149,"heading":"Hazardous and Regulated Materials","text":"Radioactive materials, explosives and dangerous goods shall be managed in accordance with sections 3.16 Radiation Management, 3.38 Explosives and Blasting Management and 3.43 Transportation of Dangerous Goods and Materials, as applicable."},{"id":"3.45.21","page":149,"heading":"Housekeeping, Fire, Emergency and Security Controls","text":"Warehouses and storage areas shall be maintained to prevent stored materials, spills, waste, damaged packaging or other obstructions from affecting safe access, material containment, equipment operation, means of egress, fire protection or emergency response. Waste and hazardous materials shall be managed in accordance with sections\n3.6 Waste Management and 3.20 Hazardous Material Management, as applicable."},{"id":"3.45.22","page":150,"heading":"Housekeeping, Fire, Emergency and Security Controls","text":"Stored materials, quantities, heights and configurations shall remain within the approved warehouse fire protection design and operating limits."},{"id":"3.45.23","page":150,"heading":"Housekeeping, Fire, Emergency and Security Controls","text":"Warehouse fire protection systems and emergency equipment shall be inspected, tested and maintained in accordance with section 5.3 Emergency Response Equipment Maintenance, Testing and Interruption."},{"id":"3.45.24","page":150,"heading":"Housekeeping, Fire, Emergency and Security Controls","text":"Warehouse emergency response, means of egress, emergency access, ignition source control and security arrangements shall be maintained based on the materials stored, warehouse activities, credible emergency scenarios and applicable obligations, in accordance with applicable MSHEM processes."},{"id":"3.45.25","page":150,"heading":"Inspection and Improvement","text":"Warehouses and storage systems shall undergo routine checks and periodic formal inspections by competent personnel at frequencies based on risk, condition, operating experience, manufacturer and engineering requirements, and applicable obligations."},{"id":"3.45.26","page":150,"heading":"Inspection and Improvement","text":"Inspections shall verify the condition and safe operation of warehouse storage systems, materials, load supporting equipment, traffic arrangements, loading facilities, hazardous-material controls, machinery, housekeeping, fire protection and emergency arrangements."},{"id":"3.45.27","page":150,"heading":"Inspection and Improvement","text":"Warehouse deficiencies, damage and deviations from established operating limits shall be recorded, risk assessed and controlled until corrective action is completed and verified."}],"area":"Occupational Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Warehouse Safety; warehouse, racking, receiving, picking, dispatch, forklift; Governance, Planning and Competency; Warehouse Design and Operating Limits; Storage Systems and Material Control; Material Handling and Traffic Control; Hazardous and Regulated Materials; Housekeeping, Fire, Emergency and Security Controls; Inspection and Improvement","trigger":"if warehouse receiving, storage, racking, picking, forklifts or dispatch is involved","refs":["3.20","3.33","5.3"]},{"id":"3.46","title":"Process Safety Information Management","page":150,"purpose":"Define the requirements for maintaining Process Safety Information concerning process hazards, process technology, process equipment, buildings and structures associated with applicable Maaden facilities and projects.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions responsible for developing, receiving, maintaining or using Process Safety Information for processes and facilities with credible potential for a major process safety event. It also applies to contractor personnel performing these activities on behalf of Maaden.","clauses":[{"id":"3.46.1","page":150,"heading":"Governance and Information Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain a documented process to identify and maintain an inventory of required Process Safety Information, assign accountable ownership, create or obtain, review, approve and update the information, make current information accessible to authorized users, protect it from unauthorized alteration, damage or loss, maintain revision history, and prevent the unintended use of superseded information."},{"id":"3.46.2","page":150,"heading":"Governance and Information Management","text":"Process Safety Information shall be retained for the operating life of the facility and for any longer period required by applicable legal, regulatory, permit requirements."},{"id":"3.46.3","page":151,"heading":"Process Hazard Information","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that applicable facilities maintain information sufficient to identify and understand the physical, chemical, toxicological, reactive, corrosive, thermal stability and incompatibility hazards of substances and materials associated with the process, including hazardous interactions arising from mixing, contamination, loss of segregation or abnormal process conditions. The information shall include current Safety Data Sheets where required and other technical information where Safety Data Sheets do not provide sufficient information."},{"id":"3.46.4","page":151,"heading":"Process Hazard Information","text":"Facilities handling or storing explosives shall maintain information on explosive properties, storage requirements, separation distances, compatibility and applicable legal, regulatory and permit requirements."},{"id":"3.46.5","page":151,"heading":"Process Technology Information","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that applicable facilities maintain process technology information sufficient to understand the process and its hazards, including, as applicable, process descriptions, process flow diagrams, process chemistry, material and energy balances, design capacities, maximum intended inventories, safe operating limits, credible abnormal conditions and the consequences of deviation from those limits or conditions."},{"id":"3.46.6","page":151,"heading":"Process Technology Information","text":"Where required Process Safety Information is unavailable or incomplete, the applicable facility or project shall ensure that sufficient information is obtained, developed or reconstructed and technically validated by competent people before being used to support design, operation or other process safety activities."},{"id":"3.46.7","page":151,"heading":"Facility and Equipment Information","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that applicable facilities maintain information necessary to demonstrate that process equipment is designed and constructed in accordance with applicable Saudi regulations and the engineering codes and standards specified in the approved design basis. This information shall include, as applicable, design specifications, materials of construction, piping and instrumentation diagrams, process control system architecture, instrumented protection functions, relief and vent system design bases, electrical area classification, ventilation system design, and applicable design codes and standards."},{"id":"3.46.8","page":151,"heading":"Facility and Equipment Information","text":"Process Safety Information shall identify equipment and controls classified as EHSS critical under the section 3.50 Asset Integrity Management process."},{"id":"3.46.9","page":151,"heading":"Process Safety Critical Limits and Design Basis","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented safe operating limits for process safety critical parameters, including consequences of exceeding established safe operating limits."},{"id":"3.46.10","page":151,"heading":"Process Safety Critical Limits and Design Basis","text":"Each safety critical safeguard, including applicable relief systems, shutdown systems, alarms and interlocks, shall have a documented design basis defining its required function and performance criteria."},{"id":"3.46.11","page":151,"heading":"Information Accuracy, Updating and Change Control","text":"Changes affecting Process Safety Information shall be managed through the Management of Change process, including updating and verifying safety critical information before startup or restart."},{"id":"3.46.12","page":152,"heading":"Information Accessibility and Use","text":"Current Process Safety Information shall be accessible in a usable form to authorized personnel who require it for their assigned responsibilities."},{"id":"3.46.13","page":152,"heading":"Information Accessibility and Use","text":"Applicable Process Safety Information shall be used as an input to Process Hazard Analysis, Management of Change, Standard Operating Procedures, Standard Maintenance Procedures, Asset Integrity Management, Pre- Startup Safety Review, Emergency Response, Incident Management and Stewardship, and Training and Competency processes."},{"id":"3.46.14","page":152,"heading":"Verification and Validation","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify at a risk-based frequency that required Process Safety Information is complete, technically accurate and consistent with actual facility conditions."},{"id":"3.46.15","page":152,"heading":"Verification and Validation","text":"Process Safety Information supporting hazard studies, major modifications or startup activities shall be validated by personnel competent in the applicable process, engineering discipline or operational area."},{"id":"3.46.16","page":152,"heading":"Verification and Validation","text":"Process Safety Information deficiencies shall be documented, prioritized and tracked to closure."},{"id":"3.46.17","page":152,"heading":"Monitoring and Improvement","text":"Facilities shall monitor the implementation and effectiveness of process safety information management and evaluate recurring deficiencies, discrepancies, and information quality issues."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Process Safety Information Management; PSI, process safety information, process knowledge; Governance and Information Management; Process Hazard Information; Process Technology Information; Facility and Equipment Information; Process Safety Critical Limits and Design Basis; Information Accuracy, Updating and Change Control; Information Accessibility and Use; Verification and Validation; Monitoring and Improvement","trigger":"if process design, chemistry, equipment, hazards or safe operating limits must be documented","refs":["3.50"]},{"id":"3.47","title":"Process Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment","page":152,"purpose":"Establish effective systems to systematically identify, analyze and evaluate risks using appropriate methodology throughout the lifecycle of facilities, operations, projects, modifications, and decommissioning activities to ensure risks are reduced to ALARP and that critical controls remain effective.","scope":"This process applies to all the Business Units and Corporate Functions, including Process Plants, Non-Chemical Operations and Mining Risk Assessment (RA) studies related to new, existing and modified facilities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.47.1","page":152,"heading":"Governance and Applicability","text":"Business Units shall establish, implement and maintain a Process Hazard Analysis program that defines the facilities, operations, processes and activities requiring PHA and provides for the identification and evaluation of process hazards and associated risk controls."},{"id":"3.47.2","page":152,"heading":"Governance and Applicability","text":"The PHA method and level of detail shall be commensurate with process complexity, hazard significance, credible consequences, lifecycle stage, proposed or implemented changes, and applicable legal, regulatory and permit requirements."},{"id":"3.47.3","page":152,"heading":"Governance and Applicability","text":"PHA studies shall evaluate credible environmental, health, safety and security consequences using the applicable criteria and methodology established by the EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.47.4","page":153,"heading":"Hazard Identification and Method Selection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall select risk assessment methodologies based on process complexity, available information, lifecycle stage and risk significance."},{"id":"3.47.5","page":153,"heading":"Hazard Identification and Method Selection","text":"One or more of the following methodologies shall be applied where relevant Hazard Identification (HAZID), What-If, Structured What-If (SWIFT), Checklist, Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP), Failure Modes and Effects Analysis / Failure Modes, Effects and Criticality Analysis (FMEA / FMECA), Fault Tree Analysis (FTA), Event Tree Analysis (ETA), Layer of Protection Analysis (LOPA), Bowtie Analysis, Quantitative Risk Assessment (QRA) and Dust Hazard Analysis (DHA)."},{"id":"3.47.6","page":153,"heading":"Hazard Identification and Method Selection","text":"Methods selected shall be capable of identifying credible initiating events, hazard scenarios, escalation factors, consequences, prevention barriers, mitigation barriers and critical controls management.\n[Table table-153-image.png]\n\nTable 3.47-1. Applicable PHA methodologies"},{"id":"3.47.7","page":153,"heading":"Process Safety Information","text":"PHA studies shall not commence until required Process Safety Information has been verified as current, complete and available to the assessment team in accordance with section 3.46 Process Safety Information Management."},{"id":"3.47.8","page":153,"heading":"Process Hazard Analysis Execution","text":"PHAs shall evaluate all lifecycle modes including, normal operation, start-up, shutdown, emergency operation, emergency shutdown, maintenance, temporary operations, commissioning and decommissioning."},{"id":"3.47.9","page":153,"heading":"Process Hazard Analysis Execution","text":"The analysis shall identify hazards, initiating events and credible failure scenarios, including equipment, utility and control system failures and human error; evaluate their consequences, safeguards and escalation factors; and define interim actions, mitigation measures, critical controls and additional risk reduction requirements."},{"id":"3.47.10","page":153,"heading":"Process Hazard Analysis Execution","text":"Maaden EHSS Risk Matrix shall be used for conducting the appropriate process hazard analysis methodology in accordance with the EHSS risk management process."},{"id":"3.47.11","page":153,"heading":"Hazard Identification Study (HAZID)","text":"A Hazard Identification Study (HAZID) shall be conducted during the early stages of projects, new facilities, major modifications, or where limited process design information is available."},{"id":"3.47.12","page":154,"heading":"Hazard Identification Study (HAZID)","text":"HAZID shall evaluate the potential consequences of identified hazards and determine the need for additional risk assessment studies, including HAZOP, LOPA, QRA, DHA, FMEA, FTA, ETA, or Bowtie analyses."},{"id":"3.47.13","page":154,"heading":"Hazard Identification Study (HAZID)","text":"HAZID shall identify existing and proposed safeguards, critical controls, and actions required to reduce risk to ALARP."},{"id":"3.47.14","page":154,"heading":"What-If / Structured What-If (SWIFT)","text":"A What-If or Structured What-If (SWIFT) study shall be used where broad hazard identification is required for processes, operations, maintenance activities, utilities, infrastructure or support systems."},{"id":"3.47.15","page":154,"heading":"What-If / Structured What-If (SWIFT)","text":"The study shall use structured questioning across relevant operating modes and activities within the defined scope to identify credible hazard scenarios, equipment failures, human errors, external events and deviations from intended operation, and evaluate their potential consequences, existing safeguards, critical controls and required additional risk reduction measures."},{"id":"3.47.16","page":154,"heading":"Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP)","text":"A Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP) shall be conducted for new or modified process facilities and existing facilities where process hazards require detailed and systematic analysis."},{"id":"3.47.17","page":154,"heading":"Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP)","text":"HAZOP shall systematically evaluate deviations from design intent across applicable operating modes, including start-up, normal operation, shutdown, emergency operation, maintenance and abnormal conditions, to identify credible causes, consequences, safeguards, operability issues and environment, health, safety and process safety risks."},{"id":"3.47.18","page":154,"heading":"Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP)","text":"Major or severe consequence scenarios identified by HAZOP shall be subject to further analysis using LOPA, QRA or another suitable method, as appropriate, to evaluate risk and the adequacy of protection layers."},{"id":"3.47.19","page":154,"heading":"Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP)","text":"HAZOP recommendations shall define the actions required to eliminate hazards, improve operability, strengthen safeguards or reduce risk to ALARP."},{"id":"3.47.20","page":154,"heading":"Layer of Protection Analysis (LOPA)","text":"Layer of Protection Analysis (LOPA) shall be completed when major or severe consequences are identified, Safety Instrumented Functions are proposed, and PHA teams identify uncertainty regarding risk reduction adequacy in accordance with the applicable standards IEC61508 and IEC61511 or other relevant standards."},{"id":"3.47.21","page":154,"heading":"Layer of Protection Analysis (LOPA)","text":"LOPA studies shall define initiating events, quantify initiating event frequencies, identify independent protection layers, validate independence of IPLs, determine residual risk and determine required risk reduction."},{"id":"3.47.22","page":154,"heading":"Layer of Protection Analysis (LOPA)","text":"Safety Instrumented Systems identified as required risk reduction measures shall comply with IEC 61511 lifecycle requirements or other applicable standards."},{"id":"3.47.23","page":154,"heading":"Layer of Protection Analysis (LOPA)","text":"Protection layers claimed within LOPA shall be auditable and periodically verified."},{"id":"3.47.24","page":154,"heading":"Quantitative Risk Assessment and Facility Siting","text":"Facility Siting Assessments shall be completed for process facilities, occupied buildings, control rooms, laboratories, workshops, warehouses and other occupied structures exposed to process hazards."},{"id":"3.47.25","page":154,"heading":"Quantitative Risk Assessment and Facility Siting","text":"Industrial Building QRA shall be conducted when buildings are located within identified hazard impact zones, new occupied buildings are planned within process areas, significant facility modifications alter hazard exposure and facility siting studies identify potentially intolerable risk."},{"id":"3.47.26","page":155,"heading":"Quantitative Risk Assessment and Facility Siting","text":"QRA shall evaluate the following minimum requirements toxic impacts, fire radiation, explosion overpressure occupancy and vulnerability, adjacent facility impacts, domino effects and offsite consequences to the public, environment, and external infrastructure."},{"id":"3.47.27","page":155,"heading":"Quantitative Risk Assessment and Facility Siting","text":"Facility siting studies shall include but are not limited to the following administrative buildings, control rooms, maintenance facilities, emergency response facilities, warehouse, public exposure and nearby facilities in accordance with the SAIS SAF-03 Buildings for Industrial Facilities."},{"id":"3.47.28","page":155,"heading":"Dust Hazard Analysis (DHA)","text":"Facilities that manufacture, process, handle, convey, recycle or store combustible particulate solids shall conduct and document a Dust Hazard Analysis in accordance with applicable NFPA 660 requirements."},{"id":"3.47.29","page":155,"heading":"Dust Hazard Analysis (DHA)","text":"The DHA shall evaluate applicable buildings, process equipment, material handling systems, conveyors, bucket elevators, silos, dust collectors, workshops and storage facilities to identify combustible dust, fire, deflagration and explosion hazards; dust accumulation and migration pathways; potential ignition sources; and the adequacy of housekeeping, grounding and bonding, explosion prevention, isolation, venting and suppression controls."},{"id":"3.47.30","page":155,"heading":"Dust Hazard Analysis (DHA)","text":"The DHA shall be reviewed and, where necessary, updated following significant changes to materials, processes, equipment, facilities or combustible dust controls."},{"id":"3.47.31","page":155,"heading":"Human Factors Assessment","text":"Human factors shall be evaluated as part of all PHA studies and shall address staffing levels, workload, fatigue, ergonomics, procedures, human-machine interfaces, alarm management, Accessibility, maintenance error potential, training requirements and communication effectiveness."},{"id":"3.47.32","page":155,"heading":"Critical Controls Management","text":"Critical Control Management shall follow the requirements established in section 2.6."},{"id":"3.47.33","page":155,"heading":"Recommendations and Action Management","text":"PHA recommendations shall clearly identify the issue and required outcome, be assigned to a responsible owner with a target completion date and be tracked to verified closure through an approved action tracking process."},{"id":"3.47.34","page":155,"heading":"Recommendations and Action Management","text":"Residual risk following closure shall be documented and accepted by designated accountable management."},{"id":"3.47.35","page":155,"heading":"Revalidation PHA studies","text":"Each covered process shall undergo PHA revalidation at least every five years, unless a shorter interval is required by MOC's, RCA's, Audit Findings or applicable regulatory requirements."},{"id":"3.47.36","page":155,"heading":"Revalidation PHA studies","text":"Revalidation shall include review of management of change (MOCs), incidents, near misses, previous recommendations, updated risk information and updated facility information."},{"id":"3.47.37","page":155,"heading":"Revalidation PHA studies","text":"A new or updated PHA shall be completed when changes introduce new hazards or significantly modify existing risk."},{"id":"3.47.38","page":155,"heading":"Competency","text":"PHA teams shall be multidisciplinary and include personnel with knowledge of operations, engineering, maintenance, process safety, instrumentation and controls, safety and emergency response."},{"id":"3.47.39","page":156,"heading":"Competency","text":"Personnel leading or facilitating PHA studies including What-If, HAZOP, LOPA, QRA and DHA shall be competent in the selected methodology based on relevant training and experience, in accordance with section 2.7 EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication."},{"id":"3.47.40","page":156,"heading":"Documentation and Records","text":"PHA records shall include the study scope, methodology, team members and roles, worksheets, findings, recommendations, action tracking and closure evidence, and applicable revalidation records, and be retained for the life of the facility."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Process Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment; PHA, HAZOP, LOPA, process hazard analysis; Governance and Applicability; Hazard Identification and Method Selection; Process Safety Information; Process Hazard Analysis Execution; Hazard Identification Study (HAZID); What-If / Structured What-If (SWIFT); Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP); Layer of Protection Analysis (LOPA); Quantitative Risk Assessment and Facility Siting; Dust Hazard Analysis (DHA); Human Factors Assessment; Critical Controls Management; Recommendations and Action Management; Revalidation PHA studies; Competency; Documentation and Records","trigger":"if a new, existing or modified process requires process-hazard analysis","refs":["2.7","3.46"]},{"id":"3.48","title":"Standard Operating Procedures","page":156,"purpose":"Establish the minimum requirements for developing, maintaining, and communicating accurate and clear written Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) to manage EHSS risks and support the safe and consistent execution of operations.","scope":"This process applies to mining, processing, manufacturing, utilities, logistics, storage, material handling and associated support operations where documented operating procedures are required to control EHSS risks, including new, modified and existing facilities, equipment and processes, as well as temporary operating conditions and temporary process modifications requiring temporary operating procedures.","clauses":[{"id":"3.48.1","page":156,"heading":"SOP Identification and Coverage","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions responsible for operational activities shall identify operational activities that require documented standard operating procedures based on operational complexity, environment, health, safety, security and process safety risk, regulatory obligations and critical operating controls. Standard operating procedures shall be established where procedural control is necessary to achieve safe and consistent operation."},{"id":"3.48.2","page":156,"heading":"SOP Identification and Coverage","text":"The need for new or revised standard operating procedures shall be evaluated during Management of Change, project handover and Pre-Startup Safety Reviews, where applicable. Required standard operating procedures shall be developed, reviewed, approved, communicated and available before the applicable activity is performed or the facility, equipment or operational change is placed into operation."},{"id":"3.48.3","page":156,"heading":"SOP Development and Approval","text":"Standard operating procedures shall be developed by personnel who are competent in the activity and knowledgeable of the associated hazards, controls and operating requirements."},{"id":"3.48.4","page":156,"heading":"SOP Development and Approval","text":"Standard operating procedures shall be technically reviewed before approval and implementation to verify technical accuracy, consistency with current equipment and operating conditions, applicable regulatory requirements, identified hazards and controls and training implications."},{"id":"3.48.5","page":156,"heading":"SOP Development and Approval","text":"Responsibilities for development, review, approval and revision of standard operating procedures shall be defined and approved by the designated operational authority prior to use."},{"id":"3.48.6","page":157,"heading":"Operating Procedure Content","text":"Standard operating procedures shall define applicable operating modes, including start-up, normal operation, abnormal or upset operation, temporary operation where applicable, normal shutdown, emergency operation, emergency shutdown, and restart following shutdown, trip, maintenance or emergency conditions, as applicable."},{"id":"3.48.7","page":157,"heading":"Operating Procedure Content","text":"Standard operating procedures shall identify critical safeguards, interlocks, protective systems and required operating precautions."},{"id":"3.48.8","page":157,"heading":"Operating Procedure Content","text":"Standard operating procedures shall, where applicable, define operating limits, consequences of deviations from operating limits, actions required to prevent or correct deviations and conditions requiring escalation or shutdown."},{"id":"3.48.9","page":157,"heading":"Operating Procedure Content","text":"Standard operating procedures shall incorporate applicable process hazard assessment outcomes, risk assessment outcomes, lessons learned from incidents and investigations, relevant operational experience and regulatory requirements."},{"id":"3.48.10","page":157,"heading":"Operating Procedure Content","text":"Standard Operating Procedures applicable to start-up, shutdown and EHSS Critical SOPs shall incorporate documented checklists specifying the required sequence of actions for the safe and controlled operation of equipment or units."},{"id":"3.48.11","page":157,"heading":"Operating Procedure Content","text":"Standard operating procedures shall identify required actions when critical safeguards are bypassed, disabled or unavailable, where permitted by applicable bypass and management of change processes."},{"id":"3.48.12","page":157,"heading":"EHSS Critical Standard Operating Procedures","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and designate as EHSS Critical Operating Procedures those procedures required to prevent Major or Severe consequence scenarios identified through PHA studies and verify their implementation and use through defined assurance activities."},{"id":"3.48.13","page":157,"heading":"EHSS Critical Standard Operating Procedures","text":"EHSS Critical Operating Procedures shall clearly identify critical actions with cautionary notes, holding points and stop conditions necessary to prevent unwanted events."},{"id":"3.48.14","page":157,"heading":"EHSS Critical Standard Operating Procedures","text":"Deviations from EHSS Critical Operating Procedures shall be assessed and authorized through applicable risk management and management of change processes."},{"id":"3.48.15","page":157,"heading":"Temporary Operating Procedures","text":"Temporary operating procedures shall be developed where temporary operational conditions introduce changes to normal operating practices."},{"id":"3.48.16","page":157,"heading":"Temporary Operating Procedures","text":"Temporary operating procedures shall define the temporary condition, identify associated risks and controls, specify validity periods, identify approval requirements and define conditions for return to normal operation."},{"id":"3.48.17","page":157,"heading":"Temporary Operating Procedures","text":"The validity period for any temporary procedure shall not exceed one year, after which the procedure shall be removed from use upon expiry."},{"id":"3.48.18","page":157,"heading":"Communication, Competency and Use","text":"Relevant personnel shall have access to current operating procedures applicable to their work."},{"id":"3.48.19","page":157,"heading":"Communication, Competency and Use","text":"Personnel shall be informed of new, revised and temporary operating procedures before use."},{"id":"3.48.20","page":157,"heading":"Communication, Competency and Use","text":"Personnel performing activities governed by standard operating procedures shall be trained and assessed as competent before performing the activity independently."},{"id":"3.48.21","page":158,"heading":"Review and Continuous Improvement","text":"Standard Operating Procedures shall be periodically reviewed to confirm that they remain current, accurate and effective. EHSS Critical Operating Procedures shall be reviewed at least every three years and other Standard Operating Procedures at least every five years, or earlier when triggered by operational changes, incidents or lessons learned, audit or assurance findings, risk assessment outcomes, regulatory changes or other conditions affecting the procedure."},{"id":"3.48.22","page":158,"heading":"Review and Continuous Improvement","text":"Obsolete, superseded or withdrawn operating procedures shall be removed from operational use and prevented from unintended use."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Standard Operating Procedures; SOP, operating procedure, operating limit, startup, shutdown; SOP Identification and Coverage; SOP Development and Approval; Operating Procedure Content; EHSS Critical Operating Procedures; Temporary Operating Procedures; Communication, Competency and Use; Review and Continuous Improvement","trigger":"if startup, normal, abnormal, temporary, shutdown or emergency operating instructions apply","refs":[]},{"id":"3.49","title":"Standard Maintenance Procedures","page":158,"purpose":"Establish the minimum requirements for developing, maintaining, implementing and reviewing Standard Maintenance Procedures (SMPs) to support safe, reliable and consistent maintenance activities, preserve asset integrity, manage EHSS risks and maintain the effectiveness of critical controls and critical assets.","scope":"This process applies to maintenance of Maaden assets where documented maintenance procedures are required to manage EHSS risks or applicable legal, regulatory or permit requirements. It covers preventive, predictive, condition based and corrective maintenance, including inspection, testing, calibration, planned shutdown, integrity related and temporary maintenance activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.49.1","page":158,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Identification and Coverage","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions responsible for maintenance activities shall identify maintenance activities that require documented Standard Maintenance Procedures based on EHSS risk, process safety risk, asset integrity, equipment complexity, regulatory obligations and manufacturer requirements."},{"id":"3.49.2","page":158,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Identification and Coverage","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be established where procedural control is required to ensure safe, reliable and consistent execution of maintenance activities."},{"id":"3.49.3","page":158,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Identification and Coverage","text":"The need for new or revised Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be evaluated during management of change, project, commissioning, pre-startup safety reviews, incident investigations and asset integrity reviews where applicable."},{"id":"3.49.4","page":158,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Identification and Coverage","text":"Required Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be approved by the designated authority and available to affected personnel before the applicable maintenance activity is performed or the asset is placed into service."},{"id":"3.49.5","page":158,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Development and Approval","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be developed by personnel who are competent in the maintenance activity and knowledgeable of the associated hazards, controls, equipment, integrity requirements and regulatory obligations."},{"id":"3.49.6","page":158,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Development and Approval","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be technically reviewed before approval and implementation to verify technical accuracy, consistency with current equipment and operating conditions, asset integrity requirements, identified hazards, controls, applicable regulatory requirements and competency and training implications."},{"id":"3.49.7","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Development and Approval","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define accountability, responsibilities and authorities for the development, technical review, approval and implementation of Standard Maintenance Procedures."},{"id":"3.49.8","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Development and Approval","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures affecting safety critical functions, critical controls or safety instrumented functions shall include appropriate technical review by relevant engineering disciplines."},{"id":"3.49.9","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Development and Approval","text":"New or significantly revised Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be validated and approved to confirm that the procedure can be executed as intended and that identified controls are effective before implementation."},{"id":"3.49.10","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Content","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall define the minimum requirements, controls and acceptance criteria necessary to safely execute the maintenance activity."},{"id":"3.49.11","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Content","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall identify applicable equipment or asset types, maintenance boundaries and prerequisites, required integrity, reliability or safety objectives, applicable hazards and critical controls, conditions requiring escalation, specialist review or work stoppage and requirements for equipment restoration and return to service."},{"id":"3.49.12","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Content","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall incorporate applicable outcomes from process hazard analysis, risk assessments, asset integrity reviews, incident investigations, lessons learned and regulatory obligations."},{"id":"3.49.13","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Content","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall define activities requiring verification, inspection, testing, calibration, proof testing or validation necessary to maintain asset integrity or critical control effectiveness."},{"id":"3.49.14","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Content","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall define requirements for verification and return to service where maintenance activities can affect asset integrity, safety functions, critical controls, process safety or operational readiness."},{"id":"3.49.15","page":159,"heading":"Maintenance Procedure Content","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall reference applicable requirements of 3.51 EHSS Critical Systems Bypass,\n3.52 Management of Change, 3.21 Permit to Work and Task Risk Assessment and 3.31 Personal Protective Equipment and other MSHEM processes required to execute the task safely."},{"id":"3.49.16","page":159,"heading":"EHSS Critical Maintenance Procedures","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify all EHSS critical SMPs addressing Major or Severe consequence scenarios to maintain safe maintenance as identified through applicable Hazard Analysis."},{"id":"3.49.17","page":159,"heading":"EHSS Critical Maintenance Procedures","text":"Deviations from EHSS Critical Maintenance Procedures shall be assessed and authorized through applicable risk management and management of change processes."},{"id":"3.49.18","page":159,"heading":"EHSS Critical Maintenance Procedures","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish assurance activities to verify the implementation and effectiveness of EHSS Critical Maintenance Procedures."},{"id":"3.49.19","page":159,"heading":"Temporary Maintenance Procedures","text":"Temporary Maintenance Procedures shall be developed where temporary maintenance strategies, temporary equipment arrangements or temporary repairs introduce changes to established maintenance requirements."},{"id":"3.49.20","page":159,"heading":"Temporary Maintenance Procedures","text":"Temporary Maintenance Procedures shall define the temporary condition, identify associated risks and controls, identify affected assets and controls, define approval requirements, specify validity periods and define requirements for return to the original condition."},{"id":"3.49.21","page":160,"heading":"Temporary Maintenance Procedures","text":"The validity period for any temporary procedure shall not exceed one year, after which the procedure shall be removed from use upon expiry."},{"id":"3.49.22","page":160,"heading":"Temporary Maintenance Procedures","text":"Temporary repairs that affect asset integrity, process safety, critical controls or regulatory compliance shall be managed through applicable 3.52 Management of Change and 3.50 Asset Integrity processes."},{"id":"3.49.23","page":160,"heading":"Communication, Competency and Use","text":"Current applicable Standard Maintenance Procedures, including new, revised and temporary procedures, shall be made available and communicated to affected personnel before use."},{"id":"3.49.24","page":160,"heading":"Communication, Competency and Use","text":"Personnel, including contractors, shall be trained and assessed as competent and, where required, authorized before independently performing maintenance activities governed by Standard Maintenance Procedures."},{"id":"3.49.25","page":160,"heading":"Communication, Competency and Use","text":"Maintenance activities shall be performed in accordance with the applicable approved Standard Maintenance Procedure and stopped for review where the procedure cannot be followed as written or does not address the conditions encountered."},{"id":"3.49.26","page":160,"heading":"Review and Continual Improvement","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be reviewed and updated as necessary to remain current, accurate, effective and consistent with applicable maintenance, asset integrity, EHSS, legal, regulatory and manufacturer requirements. EHSS Critical Maintenance Procedures shall be reviewed at least every three years, and all other Standard Maintenance Procedures at least every five years."},{"id":"3.49.27","page":160,"heading":"Review and Continual Improvement","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall also be reviewed following changes, incidents or equipment failures, audit or assurance findings, critical control or asset integrity findings, changes to applicable legal or regulatory requirements, changes to manufacturer recommendations, or other relevant lessons learned that could affect the procedure."},{"id":"3.49.28","page":160,"heading":"Review and Continual Improvement","text":"Standard Maintenance Procedures shall be controlled so that only current approved versions are available for operational use and obsolete, superseded or withdrawn versions cannot be unintentionally used."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Standard Maintenance Procedures; maintenance procedure, work instruction, calibration, repair; Maintenance Procedure Identification and Coverage; Maintenance Procedure Development and Approval; Maintenance Procedure Content; EHSS Critical Maintenance Procedures; Temporary Maintenance Procedures; Communication, Competency and Use; Review and Continual Improvement","trigger":"if maintenance, inspection, repair, testing, calibration, reassembly or return to service is performed","refs":[]},{"id":"3.50","title":"Asset Integrity Management","page":160,"purpose":"Establish requirements for managing the integrity of assets across all Business Units and Corporate Functions to prevent Environment, Health, Safety, and Security (EHSS) consequences and support safe, reliable, and sustainable operations.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions that own, operate, maintain, inspect, test, monitor, modify or decommission assets requiring integrity management. These assets include, but are not limited to, pressure relief devices and relieving systems; EHSS critical instruments, alarms and protective functions; stationary and rotating equipment; electrical equipment and protection systems; civil and structural assets; cathodic and anodic protection systems; and utility and chemical hoses and flexible connections.","clauses":[{"id":"3.50.1","page":160,"heading":"Asset Integrity Governance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain an Asset Integrity Management system proportionate to their asset inventory, operating risks and applicable obligations."},{"id":"3.50.2","page":161,"heading":"Asset Integrity Governance","text":"Business Unit and Corporate Functions management shall be accountable for implementation and effectiveness of Asset Integrity Management and shall define applicable responsibilities and technical authorities."},{"id":"3.50.3","page":161,"heading":"Asset Integrity Governance","text":"Asset Integrity Management shall address applicable lifecycle stages from design and procurement through fabrication, installation, commissioning, operation, inspection, testing, maintenance, modification, preservation and decommissioning."},{"id":"3.50.4","page":161,"heading":"Asset Integrity Governance","text":"Asset Integrity Management shall be integrated with applicable operational, maintenance, reliability, EHSS Risk Management, Process Safety Information and Management of Change processes."},{"id":"3.50.5","page":161,"heading":"Asset Register and Criticality","text":"Assets within the scope of Asset Integrity Management shall be identified and maintained within controlled asset management or integrity registers."},{"id":"3.50.6","page":161,"heading":"Asset Register and Criticality","text":"The information maintained shall be sufficient to identify the asset, applicable service, criticality, technical basis and inspection, testing or maintenance strategy."},{"id":"3.50.7","page":161,"heading":"Asset Register and Criticality","text":"Business Units shall establish, implement and maintain documented criteria and a structured process for identifying and classifying EHSS Critical Assets."},{"id":"3.50.8","page":161,"heading":"Asset Register and Criticality","text":"An asset shall be designated as an EHSS Critical Asset where:\na. Asset Criticality Assessments determine that failure or unavailability of the assets could potentially result in a Major or Severe EHSS consequences.\nb. Applicable legal, regulatory, permit, approved engineering design or formally adopted technical standard requirements require the asset to perform a critical protection, monitoring, containment, prevention, mitigation or shutdown function. Examples include Pressure Safety Valves (PSVs), Continuous Emission Monitoring Systems (CEMS), Safety Instrumented Functions (SIFs), Emergency Shutdown Systems (ESDs), and other assets with mandated critical functions.\nc. A structured risk assessment determines that probability of failure or unavailability of the asset results in the associated scenario being assessed within the intolerable Risk Zone."},{"id":"3.50.9","page":161,"heading":"Asset Register and Criticality","text":"EHSS Critical Asset lists shall be controlled and maintained current. Changes to asset criticality or required protective function shall be technically justified and managed through the applicable Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.50.10","page":161,"heading":"Asset Integrity Strategy","text":"Business Units shall establish an Asset Integrity Strategy defining how applicable degradation, failure and integrity risks are identified, assessed, controlled, monitored and reviewed throughout the asset lifecycle."},{"id":"3.50.11","page":161,"heading":"Asset Integrity Strategy","text":"Inspection, testing, monitoring and maintenance strategies shall be based on an approved technical basis appropriate to the asset and service."},{"id":"3.50.12","page":161,"heading":"Asset Integrity Strategy","text":"Applicable methodologies shall include:\na. Risk Based Inspection; for stationary equipment in accordance with API 580 and 581.\nb. Reliability Centered Maintenance; in accordance with SAE JA1011, SAE JA1012, IEC 60300-3-11, or other adopted standards, based on asset criticality and failure modes.\nc. Reliability Instrumented System (RIS), for Safety Instrumented Systems (SIS) and critical process instruments in accordance with IEC 61508 and IEC 61511 standards.\nd. Inspection and maintenance intervals established through applicable regulatory requirements, recognized codes and standards including API 580, API 581, API 510, API 570, API 653, ASME PCC-3 and API 579-1/ASME FFS-1 where relevant to the asset or manufacturer requirements."},{"id":"3.50.13","page":162,"heading":"Asset Integrity Strategy","text":"Asset Integrity strategies shall define applicable damage or failure mechanisms, required inspection or maintenance activities, methodologies, frequencies, acceptance criteria and actions where acceptance criteria are not achieved."},{"id":"3.50.14","page":162,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Monitoring","text":"Inspection, testing, calibration, condition monitoring and other integrity verification activities shall be established for applicable assets and performed by competent personnel using approved standard operating and maintenance procedures and suitable equipment."},{"id":"3.50.15","page":162,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Monitoring","text":"Inspection, testing and maintenance shall be completed within the frequency established by the approved Asset Integrity Strategy and applicable legal, technical or manufacturer requirements."},{"id":"3.50.16","page":162,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Monitoring","text":"Results shall be documented and retained in a form that supports traceability, assessment of equipment condition, trend analysis and verification of continued suitability for service."},{"id":"3.50.17","page":162,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Monitoring","text":"New, modified, repaired or replaced assets shall be subject to applicable inspection, testing, commissioning and acceptance requirements before being placed or returned into service."},{"id":"3.50.18","page":162,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"Pressure relief devices and relieving systems shall be designed, installed, inspected, tested, maintained and repaired in accordance with the applicable construction and in-service inspection codes and recognized standards, including API 520, API 521, API 576 and API 2000, where applicable."},{"id":"3.50.19","page":162,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define applicable pressure relief device service classifications, inspection and testing frequencies, acceptance criteria, and rules for extending or reducing inspection intervals based on service conditions, operating experience and applicable technical requirements."},{"id":"3.50.20","page":162,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"Pressure relief valves, devices, appliances and vacuum breakers shall be classified as follows:\na. Class A: Pressure relief valves, devices and vacuum breakers installed in highly corrosive service, services susceptible to plugging or fouling, new or unknown service conditions, or fired boilers.\nb. Class B: Pressure relief valves, devices and vacuum breakers not classified as Class A or Class C, including those installed in clean process service, non-corrosive service, and steam service excluding fired boilers.\nc. Class C: Pressure relief valves, devices and vacuum breakers installed on non-process utility systems where the primary function is thermal expansion protection or utility system protection rather than process overpressure protection, including utility water, cooling water, dry compressed air and nitrogen systems."},{"id":"3.50.21","page":162,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"Unless shorter interval is required by an applicable obligation, adopted standard, manufacturer requirement, service condition or operating experience, the initial maximum scheduled inspection/test interval shall be 2 years for Class A, 3 years for Class B and 5 years for Class C."},{"id":"3.50.22","page":163,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"The inspection interval for Class A pressure relief valves and devices can be extended by up to 50% of the current interval if two consecutive inspections confirm satisfactory performance. However, the total interval shall not exceed a maximum of five (5) years."},{"id":"3.50.23","page":163,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"The inspection interval for Class B and Class C pressure relief valves and devices can be extended by up to 50% of the current interval if two consecutive inspections confirm satisfactory performance. However, the total interval shall not exceed a maximum of five (5) years."},{"id":"3.50.24","page":163,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"The inspection interval of Class A, Class B and Class C pressure relief valves and devices shall be reduced where the as-received opening pressure exceeds the applicable CDTP by more than 10%, or where inspection indicates that the pressure relief valve or device is heavily fouled or stuck. In such cases, the inspection interval shall be reduced to the previous successful interval or, as a minimum, 50% of the current interval."},{"id":"3.50.25","page":163,"heading":"Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices","text":"All pressure relief valves and or devices and rupture disk shall be periodically inspected to ensure that existing block valves are in fully open position and seals are intact. The inspection frequency shall be conducted as a minimum, on quarterly basis."},{"id":"3.50.26","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain a controlled register of EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices, Alarms and associated protective functions identifying the protected hazard or scenario, required function, applicable performance requirement, test method, approved test frequency and operating status."},{"id":"3.50.27","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Safety Instrumented Functions shall be designed, verified, operated, proof tested, maintained and modified in accordance with IEC 61511, with IEC 61508 applied where required by the approved technical basis."},{"id":"3.50.28","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Inspection, proof testing and functional testing shall demonstrate that the complete protective function, including applicable sensors, logic solvers, alarms, diagnostics and final elements, can achieve its required performance and design intent."},{"id":"3.50.29","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"EHSS Critical Alarms and non-SIS protective instruments shall be maintained, calibrated and functionally tested to demonstrate their required function. Alarm management arrangements shall follow the approved alarm philosophy and IEC 62682 or ISA 18.2."},{"id":"3.50.30","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Any addition, removal, or modification of EHSS critical alarms shall be approved through the management of change process and protected from unauthorized suppression, shelving, or modification."},{"id":"3.50.31","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Failed, bypassed, inhibited, overridden or otherwise degraded EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices, Alarms and associated protective functions shall be controlled through section 3.51 EHSS Critical Systems Bypass process."},{"id":"3.50.32","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"A failure that prevents the required critical function from operating on demand shall be reported in accordance with section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship criteria."},{"id":"3.50.33","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Standard Maintenance and Operating Procedures shall define the requirements for proof testing and verification of Safety Instrumented Functions."},{"id":"3.50.34","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Full functional testing of the EHSS Critical Instruments and Alarms loop (from initial to final element) shall be carried out if any change is made to the application program of logic solvers for that loop to ensure that Integrity level is not compromised."},{"id":"3.50.35","page":163,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"EHSS Critical Emergency Shutdown valves shall be leak tested to ensure that they meet the leakage requirement of the application of the valve based on applicable codes, standards and or manufacturer’s recommendations."},{"id":"3.50.36","page":164,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Instrument equipment and system (complete loop or any individual component) or alarms shall not be taken out of service or tested during operation of the plant without prior approval, in accordance with section 3.51 EHSS Critical Systems Bypass process."},{"id":"3.50.37","page":164,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Initial interval for testing of each component of EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms including loop shall be one year. Testing intervals of more than a year are acceptable for any or all the components or complete loop of EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms, if the documented result of quantitative analysis ensures integrity of the component and loop to perform as per design intent, and justifies testing interval of more than one year."},{"id":"3.50.38","page":164,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"The testing interval of component or complete loop EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms shall be reduced to ½ of the current interval, if they fail to function within the acceptable limits. However, the testing interval shall not be changed if the EHSS Critical Instrument, Device and Alarm failed to function as per the design intent and the RCA indicated that it is other than time based, if the root cause has been determined and rectified."},{"id":"3.50.39","page":164,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices, and Alarms shall be tested following any failure, suspected failure, demand event, relevant incident, or repair, replacement, or modification that could affect their required function, to verify that the required performance, reliability, and protective function have been restored."},{"id":"3.50.40","page":164,"heading":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms","text":"Any deficiency found during the inspection and test that would have prevented the critical function to perform on demand, will result in inspection and test 'not satisfactory’ shall be reported in accordance with section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship process."},{"id":"3.50.41","page":164,"heading":"Stationary Equipment","text":"Pressure vessels, process piping, atmospheric and low pressure storage tanks, boilers, fired heaters and other stationary pressure containing equipment shall be designed, inspected, assessed, maintained, repaired and modified in accordance with the approved design basis, applicable legal requirements and recognized construction and in- service inspection codes. Applicable standards include API 510, API 570, API 653, API 573 and National Board Inspection Code (NBIC), as relevant to the equipment and service."},{"id":"3.50.42","page":164,"heading":"Stationary Equipment","text":"Inspection and integrity strategies for stationary equipment shall be based on credible damage and degradation mechanisms, equipment criticality, service conditions, operating history and applicable technical standards. Applicable methodologies and standards include API 571 for damage mechanism identification, API 970 for Damage Mechanism Reviews, API 580/581 where Risk-Based Inspection is used, API 579-1/ASME FFS-1 for fitness for service assessment, and API 584 for Integrity Operating Windows, where applicable."},{"id":"3.50.43","page":164,"heading":"Stationary Equipment","text":"Cryogenic piping, storage tanks, insulation systems and other cryogenic stationary equipment shall be included in the applicable Asset Integrity inspection and monitoring program, with inspection methods, frequencies and acceptance criteria established based on the approved technical basis, service conditions, manufacturer requirements, degradation mechanisms and operating experience. Evidence of leakage, moisture ingress, deterioration or loss of integrity shall trigger appropriate assessment and corrective action."},{"id":"3.50.44","page":164,"heading":"Electrical Equipment and Protection Systems","text":"Electrical equipment and systems shall be designed, installed, inspected, tested and maintained in accordance with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, the approved design basis, manufacturer requirements and recognized technical standards. EHSS Critical Electrical Equipment and protective systems shall be identified and maintained in the EHSS Critical Asset List. Applicable standards include NFPA 70B, applicable IEC, IEEE and ANSI/NETA standards, and IEC 60079 for electrical equipment in hazardous areas, including IEC 60079-17 where applicable."},{"id":"3.50.45","page":165,"heading":"Electrical Equipment and Protection Systems","text":"Inspection, preventive maintenance and functional testing strategies for EHSS Critical Electrical Equipment and protective systems shall be based on equipment criticality, credible failure modes, required protective function, manufacturer recommendations, operating experience and the applicable technical basis. Protection relays, circuit- breaker trip functions, grounding and bonding systems, emergency generators, uninterruptible power supplies, batteries and other electrical systems credited as EHSS safeguards shall be tested or verified at frequencies sufficient to demonstrate their required availability and protective function."},{"id":"3.50.46","page":165,"heading":"Electrical Equipment and Protection Systems","text":"Failures, degraded performance or test results outside approved acceptance criteria for EHSS Critical Electrical Equipment or protective functions shall be evaluated and corrected in accordance with the applicable Asset Integrity findings and EHSS Risk Management requirements. Required changes to inspection or testing frequencies shall be determined based on the failure assessment, applicable NFPA 70B, ANSI/NETA or manufacturer requirements, and the approved technical basis."},{"id":"3.50.47","page":165,"heading":"Civil and Structural Assets","text":"Civil and structural assets shall be managed in accordance with applicable legal, design, inspection and maintenance requirements. Structures whose failure could cause or contribute to a critical EHSS event, loss of containment, failure of EHSS Critical control, obstruction of safe access or escape, or loss of emergency response shall be designated and managed as EHSS Critical Assets."},{"id":"3.50.48","page":165,"heading":"Civil and Structural Assets","text":"Applicable civil and structural assets shall comply with the Saudi Building Code, ISO 2394 General principles on reliability for structures, and ISO 13822 Bases for design of structures or other applicable design and assessment standards. Integrity strategies shall include baseline, recurring and event driven inspection or engineering assessment at frequencies based on criticality, material, environment, deterioration rate, design life and previous findings."},{"id":"3.50.49","page":165,"heading":"Civil and Structural Assets","text":"Where structural integrity or stability is uncertain, appropriate controls shall be implemented until fitness for service is confirmed by a competent structural engineer. Changes affecting structural loading, configuration, supports, foundations, or use shall be subject to engineering review and Management of Change, where applicable."},{"id":"3.50.50","page":165,"heading":"Rotating Equipment and Mechanical Overspeed Protection","text":"Rotating equipment shall be identified, operated, inspected and maintained in accordance with the approved design basis, manufacturer requirements and applicable recognized technical standards. EHSS Critical Rotating Equipment and associated protective functions, including Mechanical Overspeed Protection where applicable, shall be identified and maintained in the EHSS Critical Asset List. Applicable standards include API 610, API 611, API 612, API 616, API 617, API 618, API 670, API 686 and ISO 20816, where relevant to the equipment and approved technical basis."},{"id":"3.50.51","page":165,"heading":"Rotating Equipment and Mechanical Overspeed Protection","text":"Inspection, preventive and predictive maintenance, condition monitoring and functional testing strategies for rotating equipment shall be based on credible failure modes, equipment criticality, approved operating limits, manufacturer recommendations, applicable technical standards and operating and maintenance history. Standby equipment credited as an EHSS safeguard shall be periodically tested or operated to demonstrate its required availability."},{"id":"3.50.52","page":165,"heading":"Rotating Equipment and Mechanical Overspeed Protection","text":"Mechanical Overspeed Protection and other EHSS Critical protective functions shall be inspected, functionally tested and verified at frequencies established by the approved technical basis and applicable manufacturer or technical standard requirements, and following maintenance, modification or other activities that could affect their required function. Testing and return to service acceptance criteria shall be defined in accordance with API 611/ 612 or other applicable technical procedure or standard."},{"id":"3.50.53","page":166,"heading":"Rotating Equipment and Mechanical Overspeed Protection","text":"Abnormal vibration, temperature, lubrication condition, seal performance, alignment, overspeed protection performance or other condition indicators outside approved limits shall be evaluated before continued operation where they could indicate an integrity threatening condition. Continued operation shall be managed in accordance with the applicable Asset Integrity findings and EHSS Risk Management requirements."},{"id":"3.50.54","page":166,"heading":"Cathodic and Anodic Protection Systems","text":"Cathodic and Anodic Protection systems required to maintain the integrity of assets within the scope of this process shall be identified and managed with documented system boundaries, protected assets, design criteria, required operating parameters, acceptance criteria and inspection/test frequencies. A system protecting an EHSS Critical Asset shall be designated EHSS Critical."},{"id":"3.50.55","page":166,"heading":"Cathodic and Anodic Protection Systems","text":"Buried or submerged piping systems shall apply AMPP SP0169 or another approved system specific standard, and cathodic protection for applicable aboveground storage tank bottoms shall comply with API 651 and applicable AMPP requirements. Initial and recurring testing shall demonstrate that the approved protection criteria are achieved. Electrical measurement techniques shall use AMPP/NACE TM0497 or another approved method where applicable."},{"id":"3.50.56","page":166,"heading":"Cathodic and Anodic Protection Systems","text":"Anodic Protection systems shall receive functional inspection and verification at intervals not exceeding 12 months. The inspection shall include, as applicable, the protection monitoring panel, programmable or controlled DC power supply, test and bonding stations, reference and cathode electrodes, protection potential, grounding, meter accuracy, circuit integrity and other components required to demonstrate the approved protective function."},{"id":"3.50.57","page":166,"heading":"Cathodic and Anodic Protection Systems","text":"Loss of power, physical damage, excavation, suspected interference, failed reference electrodes or unexplained changes in protection level shall trigger evaluation, classification of any finding and corrective action."},{"id":"3.50.58","page":166,"heading":"Utility and Chemical Hoses","text":"Utility and chemical hoses, flexible connections and associated fittings whose failure could release hazardous material, pressure or stored energy, or impair an EHSS Critical function, shall be selected, installed, identified, inspected, tested, maintained and replaced in accordance with the approved service specific technical basis, manufacturer requirements and applicable recognized standards. Applicable standards include ISO 7751, ISO 8031, ISO 6134, ISO 2398, ISO 8331, ISO 1402, EN 1765, EN 12115 and SAE J517, as relevant to the hose type and service. Fire hoses shall be managed under the applicable Emergency Response Equipment requirements."},{"id":"3.50.59","page":166,"heading":"Utility and Chemical Hoses","text":"Business Units shall establish technical requirements for hose and flexible connection systems that address, as applicable, material and service compatibility, pressure rating, proof testing, electrical continuity or antistatic requirements, coupling and connection integrity, prevention of backflow and cross-connection, restraint against uncontrolled movement, safe depressurization, inspection and rejection criteria, and defined service life or replacement requirements."},{"id":"3.50.60","page":166,"heading":"Utility and Chemical Hoses","text":"Hoses and flexible connections shall be verified as fit for service before use and removed from service where inspection, testing, condition or service life criteria are not met. Temporary hose connections to live process or operational equipment shall be subject to the Management of Change process unless specifically covered by an approved standard operating or maintenance procedure."},{"id":"3.50.61","page":167,"heading":"Findings, Defects and Risk Management","text":"Business Units shall establish arrangements for personnel and contractors to report observed or suspected Asset Integrity deficiencies. Inspection, testing, monitoring or integrity assessment results that do not meet approved acceptance criteria shall be recorded and managed as Asset Integrity findings."},{"id":"3.50.62","page":167,"heading":"Findings, Defects and Risk Management","text":"Asset Integrity findings that may affect safe or reliable operation shall be evaluated before continued operation. The evaluation shall consider, as applicable, the asset condition, degradation or failure mechanism, credible consequences, effect on the required function, associated EHSS risk, operating restrictions, compensating controls, monitoring requirements and required corrective actions. Continued operation where the finding affects EHSS risk shall require documented technical assessment and authorization in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management."},{"id":"3.50.63","page":167,"heading":"Findings, Defects and Risk Management","text":"Asset Integrity findings meeting applicable incident classification criteria shall be reported and managed in accordance with section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship. Corrective actions arising from findings, inspections, testing, failures or engineering assessments shall be prioritized, assigned, tracked and verified to closure."},{"id":"3.50.64","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Repairs and modifications affecting asset integrity shall comply with the applicable design, construction and in- service inspection codes and shall use approved procedures, qualified personnel and suitable quality assurance and quality control."},{"id":"3.50.65","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Preventive, predictive, corrective, and condition based maintenance activities affecting asset integrity shall be planned and managed through approved maintenance systems."},{"id":"3.50.66","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Asset integrity programs shall incorporate reliability information, failure history, degradation mechanisms, and operating experience."},{"id":"3.50.67","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Maintenance activities for preventive maintenance and corrective maintenance for test and inspection of assets shall be developed in accordance with Standard Operating and Maintenance procedure process. The procedure shall include the hazards during test/inspection and mitigation."},{"id":"3.50.68","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Changes affecting the approved design basis or integrity strategy shall be managed through Management of Change."},{"id":"3.50.69","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Temporary repairs shall be supported by an approved engineering assessment that defines applicable operating limits, monitoring, compensating controls and the timeframe for permanent resolution."},{"id":"3.50.70","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Materials and spare parts for EHSS Critical Assets shall be subject to controls necessary to verify conformity with approved specifications before installation."},{"id":"3.50.71","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"Third party inspection, testing and maintenance activities shall be performed by suitably qualified and approved organizations and subject to appropriate Maaden verification and acceptances."},{"id":"3.50.72","page":167,"heading":"Maintenance Repair and Modification","text":"New or modified assets shall complete the applicable requirements of Pre-Startup Safety Review process before being placed into service."},{"id":"3.50.73","page":168,"heading":"Asset Maintenance Deferment","text":"A scheduled inspection, testing, calibration, monitoring, maintenance shall be completed by its approved due date unless an Asset Maintenance Deferment is approved before the due date."},{"id":"3.50.74","page":168,"heading":"Asset Maintenance Deferment","text":"An Asset Maintenance Deferment request shall identify the affected asset and function, scheduled activity, original due date, applicable technical standard and maximum allowable interval, reason for deferment, current condition and previous results, credible failure modes and consequences, proposed deferred date or expiry date, compensating controls, additional monitoring, operating restrictions and required technical and risk approvals."},{"id":"3.50.75","page":168,"heading":"Asset Maintenance Deferment","text":"Asset Maintenance Deferments shall be supported by a documented technical assessment and EHSS Risk Assessment for all EHSS critical assets demonstrating the basis for continued operation in accordance with section\n2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.50.76","page":168,"heading":"Asset Maintenance Deferment","text":"The outcome of technical assessments for all deferred non-EHSS critical assets that highlight EHSS consequences shall trigger a risk assessment in accordance with section 2.5 EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.50.77","page":168,"heading":"Asset Maintenance Deferment","text":"The deferment period shall not exceed the applicable preventive maintenance frequency interval, and approved deferments shall be recorded in the Computerized Maintenance Management System (CMMS) or equivalent system, communicated and monitored to closure."},{"id":"3.50.78","page":168,"heading":"Asset Maintenance Deferment","text":"Repeated deferments shall be analyzed for recurring causes and cumulative risk. An expired deferment shall be treated as an overdue Asset Integrity activity and escalated through the applicable Maaden risk management process. Postponement or extension of a turnaround or planned unit shutdown shall include assessment of the resulting impact on scheduled Asset Integrity activities, EHSS Critical Assets and associated risks."},{"id":"3.50.79","page":168,"heading":"Asset Maintenance Deferment","text":"A third consecutive deferment of the same scheduled Asset Integrity activity shall require a documented reliability study or equivalent technical study to determine whether the inspection, testing or maintenance strategy remains appropriate and to identify any required strategy changes."},{"id":"3.50.80","page":168,"heading":"Critical Asset Status and Service Restoration","text":"Removal, bypass, override, isolation or impairment of an EHSS Critical Asset or protective function shall be controlled under section 3.51 EHSS Critical Systems Bypass process. The approved bypass or impairment status shall be identifiable to Operations and recorded in the applicable operating or asset management system."},{"id":"3.50.81","page":168,"heading":"Critical Asset Status and Service Restoration","text":"Return to service verification following inspection, testing, maintenance, repair or modification shall confirm, as applicable, that the work is complete; Satisfactory/Acceptable Limits are met; protective devices are installed and active; temporary isolations and bypasses are removed or controlled; setpoints and configurations are correct; required functional testing is complete; and applicable Oracle/CMMS, asset and inspection records are updated."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Asset Integrity Management; asset integrity, inspection, testing, pressure vessel, piping; Asset Integrity Governance; Asset Register and Criticality; Asset Integrity Strategy; Inspection, Testing and Monitoring; Pressure Relief Valves and or Devices; EHSS Critical Instruments, Devices and Alarms; Stationary Equipment; Electrical Equipment and Protection Systems; Civil and Structural Assets; Rotating Equipment and Mechanical Overspeed Protection; Cathodic and Anodic Protection Systems; Utility and Chemical Hoses; Findings, Defects and Risk Management; Maintenance Repair and Modification; Asset Maintenance Deferment; Critical Asset Status and Service Restoration","trigger":"if safety-critical equipment, structures, piping, vessels, controls or protective systems are involved","refs":["2.5","3.51","6.1"]},{"id":"3.51","title":"EHSS Critical Systems Bypass","page":168,"purpose":"Define the minimum requirements and controls for the safe and controlled bypassing of EHSS Critical Systems across all Maaden operations, to prevent EHSS incidents and ensure operational integrity.","scope":"This process applies to the bypassing of EHSS Critical systems within Maaden Business Units, facilities, and plants. It does not apply to Emergency Response equipment, which is governed by the Emergency Response Equipment Maintenance, Testing and Interruption process. This process also does not apply to devices installed on equipment that remain isolated or out of service.","clauses":[{"id":"3.51.1","page":169,"heading":"Identification and Control of EHSS Critical Systems","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine the applicability of this process to their activities, facilities and operations and establish and maintain a process for controlling EHSS Critical System bypasses."},{"id":"3.51.2","page":169,"heading":"Identification and Control of EHSS Critical Systems","text":"Applicable facilities shall identify and maintain a register of EHSS Critical Systems. The register shall include, as applicable, the system and component identification, critical control or protective function, associated hazard or scenario, accountable function, and required performance criteria."},{"id":"3.51.3","page":169,"heading":"Identification and Control of EHSS Critical Systems","text":"The register shall be reviewed following relevant management of change activities and major hazard studies."},{"id":"3.51.4","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"EHSS Critical Systems shall not be bypassed during all operation modes unless:\na. A formally approved bypass authorization form has been completed prior to initiating the bypass; and\nb. Alternative risk controls have been incorporated into an approved Standard Operating Procedure (SOP); or\nc. Alternative risk controls have been identified, assessed, and documented through a formal process safety risk assessment."},{"id":"3.51.5","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"Where no approved Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) exists for the anticipated operating condition, the proposed bypass shall be evaluated and authorized through the Management of Change process before the bypass is implemented."},{"id":"3.51.6","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"Risk assessments conducted to support EHSS Critical System bypasses shall use a quantitative or semi-quantitative process safety risk assessment methodology that considers the loss of the bypassed protection and any proposed alternative controls."},{"id":"3.51.7","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"Alternative controls shall be implemented and verified before the bypass is initiated."},{"id":"3.51.8","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"Where pressure relief protection is impaired, the technical adequacy of alternative overpressure protection arrangements and associated risk controls shall be verified before the bypass is initiated."},{"id":"3.51.9","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Planning and Risk Assessment","text":"Bypass risk and alternative controls shall be reassessed when operating, equipment, environmental or other conditions materially differ from those considered in the approved bypass risk assessment."},{"id":"3.51.10","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Authorization","text":"EHSS Critical Systems bypass shall not commence without SOP or risk assessment and documented bypass authorization form in accordance with the EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.51.11","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Authorization","text":"Bypass authorization shall specify affected system, justification, duration, alternative controls, approval requirements, communications and restoration details."},{"id":"3.51.12","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Authorization","text":"Shift Supervisor shall verify that the bypass authorization record and risk assessment is completed before authorizing implementation of the bypass."},{"id":"3.51.13","page":169,"heading":"Bypass Authorization","text":"If the Bypass Risk Level is higher than the Initial Risk Level (Design Risk), the bypass form shall be approved in accordance with the EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.51.14","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Authorization","text":"If the Bypass Risk Level is equal to or lower than the initial Risk Level (Design Risk), then the bypass risk form shall be approved according to the following conditions:\na. Shift Supervisor for bypass less than 8 hours.\nb. Operation Manager for bypass more than 8 hours but less than one week.\nc. Operations Director for bypass more than one week."},{"id":"3.51.15","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Authorization","text":"EHSS Critical Instruments, Alarms, Electrical Systems, and Pressure Relief Devices that remain bypassed for more than one week shall be evaluated through the Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.51.16","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Authorization","text":"Bypasses associated with shutdowns and turnarounds shall be formally tracked until closure."},{"id":"3.51.17","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Implementation and Monitoring","text":"Personnel initiating a bypass shall verify that all required approvals and alternative controls are in place before implementation."},{"id":"3.51.18","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Implementation and Monitoring","text":"Active bypasses shall be clearly identifiable and communicated to affected personnel and included in shift handovers."},{"id":"3.51.19","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Implementation and Monitoring","text":"Active bypasses shall be periodically reviewed to confirm continued necessity, effectiveness of alternative controls, and compliance."},{"id":"3.51.20","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Implementation and Monitoring","text":"Duration of the EHSS Critical Instruments, Alarms, Electrical Systems, and Pressure Relief Devices bypass shall not exceed eight hours unless formally approved in accordance with the EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.51.21","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Implementation and Monitoring","text":"Work on bypassed EHSS Critical Instruments, Alarms, Electrical Systems, and Pressure Relief Devices shall be completed continuously and in the shortest practicable time to minimize risk exposure."},{"id":"3.51.22","page":170,"heading":"Bypass Implementation and Monitoring","text":"Unexpected process conditions occurring during the bypass period shall be evaluated and escalated in accordance with the applicable EHSS risk management requirements."},{"id":"3.51.23","page":170,"heading":"Restoration of EHSS Critical Systems","text":"Bypassed EHSS Critical System shall be restored to service as soon as the purpose of the bypass has been completed."},{"id":"3.51.24","page":170,"heading":"Restoration of EHSS Critical Systems","text":"Prior to restoration, the person responsible shall verify that the system can perform its intended function."},{"id":"3.51.25","page":170,"heading":"Restoration of EHSS Critical Systems","text":"EHSS critical back-in service shall be documented including date and time of reinstatement, name and signature of the person performing the reinstatement, confirmation that the bypass has been removed and the device is operational."},{"id":"3.51.26","page":170,"heading":"Assurance and Performance Monitoring","text":"Business Units and applicable Corporate Functions shall, at planned intervals, verify compliance with this process and review active and completed bypasses, bypass duration and status, recurring causes, incidents, and assurance findings to identify adverse trends and required corrective actions."},{"id":"3.51.27","page":170,"heading":"Assurance and Performance Monitoring","text":"Business Units shall monitor bypass performance using key performance indicators that include, as a minimum, the number of active bypasses, bypass duration and aging, overdue bypasses, bypass-related incidents, and bypass- related assurance findings."},{"id":"3.51.28","page":171,"heading":"Competency and Records","text":"Personnel involved in bypass risk assessment, authorization, implementation, monitoring and restoration shall be competent for their assigned responsibilities."},{"id":"3.51.29","page":171,"heading":"Competency and Records","text":"Records of bypasses, approvals, risk assessments and restoration activities shall be maintained."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"EHSS Critical Systems Bypass; bypass, override, interlock, alarm, SIS, impairment; Identification and Control of EHSS Critical Systems; Bypass Planning and Risk Assessment; Bypass Authorization; Bypass Implementation and Monitoring; Restoration of EHSS Critical Systems; Assurance and Performance Monitoring; Competency and Records","trigger":"Use when an EHSS critical system will be bypassed, overridden, inhibited, impaired or temporarily unavailable.","refs":[]},{"id":"3.52","title":"Management of Change","page":171,"purpose":"Changes throughout the facility and asset lifecycle are systematically identified, evaluated, authorized, implemented, verified, documented and closed to prevent the introduction of new hazards, escalation of existing risks, noncompliance, and unintended impacts on people, the environment, security, process safety, asset integrity or operational performance.","scope":"This process applies to all Business Units, Corporate Functions, Contractors, and Third-Party assets on Maaden premises. It covers permanent, temporary, and emergency changes affecting design, equipment, materials, technology, operations, products, EHSS critical services, procedures, organization, temporary repairs, and regulatory compliance. Changes in kind that do not alter design, function, or risk are excluded and managed through engineering, procurement, and quality assurance systems.","clauses":[{"id":"3.52.1","page":171,"heading":"Management of Change Governance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement and maintain a management of change process for the identification, assessment, authorization, implementation, verification and closure of applicable changes."},{"id":"3.52.2","page":171,"heading":"Management of Change Governance","text":"The management of change process shall define accountabilities, responsibilities, authorities and competency requirements for personnel involved in managing changes."},{"id":"3.52.3","page":171,"heading":"Management of Change Governance","text":"Proposed changes shall be screened against defined criteria to determine whether the management of change process applies. The criteria shall consider potential impacts on EHSS risks, asset integrity, operational performance, emergency management and response, competency requirements, and legal and regulatory compliance."},{"id":"3.52.4","page":171,"heading":"Assessment and Authorization","text":"Changes shall be assessed through appropriate technical and risk assessments before authorization. The assessment shall identify the purpose of the change, affected activities, assets or functions, hazards and risks, impacts on existing controls and barriers, compliance obligations, and required risk reduction and mitigation measures."},{"id":"3.52.5","page":171,"heading":"Assessment and Authorization","text":"MOC process requires that any permanent, temporary or emergency changes shall only be implemented once all authorizations have been obtained."},{"id":"3.52.6","page":171,"heading":"Assessment and Authorization","text":"Higher risk changes shall be approved in accordance with the EHSS Risk Management process."},{"id":"3.52.7","page":171,"heading":"Implementation and Control","text":"Approved changes shall be implemented in accordance with the defined approved requirements."},{"id":"3.52.8","page":171,"heading":"Implementation and Control","text":"Before implementation, affected personnel shall be informed of relevant impacts of the change."},{"id":"3.52.9","page":172,"heading":"Implementation and Control","text":"MOC process shall require that all documents, procedures and processes affected by the change are updated, communicated, and appropriate training provided prior to implementation of the change."},{"id":"3.52.10","page":172,"heading":"Verification and Readiness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that requirements associated with the approved change have been completed before startup, operation and handover."},{"id":"3.52.11","page":172,"heading":"Verification and Readiness","text":"Verification activities shall confirm as applicable design requirements have been achieved, required actions have been completed, affected documents have been updated, required training has been completed and required testing and inspections have been completed."},{"id":"3.52.12","page":172,"heading":"Verification and Readiness","text":"Where applicable Pre-Start up Safety Review shall be completed before operation of a new, modified or reinstated facility, system or process."},{"id":"3.52.13","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"Temporary changes shall have a clearly defined scope and duration, not exceeding 6 months from the date of implementation. The duration shall be determined based on risk assessment, operational impact and regulatory requirements."},{"id":"3.52.14","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"Temporary changes shall be reviewed every 30 days and shall not be extended beyond their approved scope or duration without reassessment and authorization."},{"id":"3.52.15","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"Extension approval of temporary MOC from 6 months up to 1 year shall be risk-based in accordance with the EHSS risk management process."},{"id":"3.52.16","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"Temporary changes shall not exceed one year from the implementation date. However, changes that are expected to be in place for longer than one year and cannot be converted into permanent change can be extended provided that:\na. The extension does not exceed the next scheduled turnaround or shutdown cycle.\nb. Approved by Business Units or Corporate Functions department Vice President or N-3, ensuring that risk assessment and mitigation measures are systematically reviewed and documented.\nc. Risk mitigation measures remain effective and additional safeguards are implemented."},{"id":"3.52.17","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"If temporary MOC remain open for more than 2 years without execution, then MOC cannot be executed unless revised with a new risk assessment confirming conditions are unchanged."},{"id":"3.52.18","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"Temporary changes shall be returned to their original condition or converted into permanent changes through the MOC process before closure."},{"id":"3.52.19","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"PSSR shall be conducted before normal operations are resumed following a temporary MOC."},{"id":"3.52.20","page":172,"heading":"Temporary Changes","text":"MOC process shall include a methodology to monitor the remaining duration of temporary MOCs and notify change owners for escalation as required."},{"id":"3.52.21","page":172,"heading":"Emergency Changes","text":"MOC process shall include a documented provision for managing emergency changes."},{"id":"3.52.22","page":172,"heading":"Emergency Changes","text":"The use of the emergency change process shall be strictly limited to situations where failure to make an immediate change could result in injury, asset damage, property damage, loss of containment, fire, explosion, legal non- compliance, environmental impact, major production loss, or major off-spec production."},{"id":"3.52.23","page":173,"heading":"Emergency Changes","text":"Any emergency MOC shall be converted into a permanent or temporary MOC as soon as it is safe to do so, and in all cases within 48 hours of implementation, ensuring that risk mitigation measures are in place and compliance is maintained."},{"id":"3.52.24","page":173,"heading":"Emergency Changes","text":"MOC process shall require an investigation to be carried out into the causes, requiring the emergency MOC to avoid the recurrence of the event."},{"id":"3.52.25","page":173,"heading":"Emergency Changes","text":"The approval levels for emergency changes shall be documented and business units shall ensure the correct competencies and training are implemented for designated approvers in emergency situations."},{"id":"3.52.26","page":173,"heading":"Emergency Changes","text":"Affected personnel shall be informed prior implementation of the emergency change and change shall be recorded in the shift logbook."},{"id":"3.52.27","page":173,"heading":"Organizational and Personnel Changes","text":"Organizational and personnel changes that may affect EHSS risk management, asset integrity, process safety or legal and regulatory compliance shall be evaluated through the management of change process, including changes to critical roles, responsibilities, organizational interfaces and competency requirements."},{"id":"3.52.28","page":173,"heading":"Organizational and Personnel Changes","text":"Competency gaps resulting from organizational or personnel changes shall be addressed before affected personnel undertake duties independently."},{"id":"3.52.29","page":173,"heading":"MOC Closure","text":"The management of change process shall require all MOC requests, including cancelled and rejected requests, to be recorded, documented and closed out within defined timeframes in a format accessible to affected stakeholders."},{"id":"3.52.30","page":173,"heading":"MOC Closure","text":"Changes shall not be closed until required actions have been completed or formally transferred to an approved action management process with assigned accountability and target completion dates."},{"id":"3.52.31","page":173,"heading":"MOC Closure","text":"Implemented changes shall be subject to post-implementation verification, proportionate to risk, to confirm that intended outcomes have been achieved and affected risk controls remain effective."},{"id":"3.52.32","page":173,"heading":"Documentation, Performance and Learning","text":"Management of change records shall be maintained from initiation through closure and retained for the life of the applicable facility or plant to support future process hazard analysis revalidation and assurance activities."},{"id":"3.52.33","page":173,"heading":"Documentation, Performance and Learning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically evaluate management of change performance, including relevant findings and trends from completed changes, incidents, audits and assessments."},{"id":"3.52.34","page":173,"heading":"Documentation, Performance and Learning","text":"Relevant lessons learned shall be communicated to affected Business Units, Corporate Functions and other stakeholders."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Management of Change; MOC, management of change, temporary change; Management of Change Governance; Assessment and Authorization; Implementation and Control; Verification and Readiness; Temporary Changes; Emergency Changes; Organizational and Personnel Changes; MOC Closure; Documentation, Performance and Learning","trigger":"if a permanent or temporary technical, procedural, material or organizational change is proposed","refs":[]},{"id":"3.53","title":"Pre-Startup Safety Review","page":173,"purpose":"Establish the minimum requirements for conducting a Pre-Start Up Safety Review (PSSR) to verify the readiness of new or modified processes, equipment, and facilities before startup. The PSSR ensures that all safety systems, engineering controls, operational safeguards are implemented and effective, deficiencies are resolved, and EHSS risks are prevented.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions, including their facilities, plants, sites, mines and buildings, as well as all Maaden and contractor employees involved in operation, maintenance and projects.","clauses":[{"id":"3.53.1","page":174,"heading":"PSSR Governance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define accountabilities, responsibilities, authorities and competency requirements for the initiation, execution, review and approval of PSSR activities."},{"id":"3.53.2","page":174,"heading":"PSSR Governance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall conduct a PSSR before:\na. initial startup or commissioning of new facilities and major capital projects;\nb. startup or restart following modifications subject to the Management of Change process; and\nc. restart following major shutdowns or turnarounds where process conditions, equipment configuration or safeguards may have changed."},{"id":"3.53.3","page":174,"heading":"PSSR Governance","text":"PSSRs shall be performed by a competent multidisciplinary team and shall verify completion of applicable Management of Change and EHSS Risk Management requirements."},{"id":"3.53.4","page":174,"heading":"PSSR Governance","text":"Startup, restart, energization, pressurization, commissioning or introduction of hazardous materials shall not proceed until the PSSR has been completed and formally authorized by designated personnel."},{"id":"3.53.5","page":174,"heading":"PSSR Planning and Preparation","text":"The scope and level of detail of the PSSR shall be based on the nature, complexity and risk of the facility, process, equipment or modification."},{"id":"3.53.6","page":174,"heading":"PSSR Planning and Preparation","text":"The PSSR shall verify that applicable design, construction, installation, commissioning, testing, regulatory and inspection requirements have been completed and accepted before startup."},{"id":"3.53.7","page":174,"heading":"Technical and Operational Readiness Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, maintain and implement documented procedures and checklists that comply with the requirements of this process to ensure a safe start-up of new projects, existing facilities, buildings, plants, equipment, or business units following modifications, major shutdowns, turnarounds or system idle more than 6 months."},{"id":"3.53.8","page":174,"heading":"Technical and Operational Readiness Verification","text":"The PSSR shall verify that facilities, equipment and systems conform to the approved design basis and are capable of operating within approved operating limits."},{"id":"3.53.9","page":174,"heading":"Technical and Operational Readiness Verification","text":"The PSSR shall verify before startup that applicable commissioning, functional testing, integrity verification and acceptance activities are completed and documented, and that equipment, systems, safeguards and EHSS critical controls are physically installed, available and functioning as intended."},{"id":"3.53.10","page":174,"heading":"Technical and Operational Readiness Verification","text":"The PSSR shall verify before startup that current approved operating, maintenance and emergency procedures, and other documentation required for safe operation and emergency response, are available to affected personnel."},{"id":"3.53.11","page":174,"heading":"Risk and Change Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that applicable Management of Change requirements, approvals and actions required before startup have been completed."},{"id":"3.53.12","page":175,"heading":"Risk and Change Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that changes made during construction, commissioning or modification have been incorporated into current documentation in accordance with section 3.46 Process Safety Information Management process."},{"id":"3.53.13","page":175,"heading":"Risk and Change Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that actions arising from applicable EHSS risk assessments that are required before startup have been completed and that identified critical controls are available and effective."},{"id":"3.53.14","page":175,"heading":"Competence and Emergency Readiness","text":"PSSR shall be led by a qualified PSSR Team Leader appointed by the Area Manager or designated authority. The PSSR Team Leader shall possess relevant operational experience and technical competence to effectively manage the review and ensure findings are addressed prior to startup."},{"id":"3.53.15","page":175,"heading":"Competence and Emergency Readiness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that personnel responsible for operation, maintenance and emergency response have completed applicable training and demonstrated the required competence before startup."},{"id":"3.53.16","page":175,"heading":"Competence and Emergency Readiness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify that applicable emergency response and security arrangements are implemented and operationally ready before startup."},{"id":"3.53.17","page":175,"heading":"Startup Authorization and Deficiency Management","text":"The PSSR Team shall document completion of PSSR Checklist review, applicable supporting documents and approved PSSR Authorization form by Area Manager and Director prior to startup."},{"id":"3.53.18","page":175,"heading":"Startup Authorization and Deficiency Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish criteria defining deficiencies that shall be resolved before startup and the PSSR outcome shall be documented."},{"id":"3.53.19","page":175,"heading":"Startup Authorization and Deficiency Management","text":"Any deficiencies identified during the PSSR shall be documented, assessed, classified, and tracked until resolved, either prior to start-up or through defined post start-up action plans in accordance with the EHSS risk management process."},{"id":"3.53.20","page":175,"heading":"Records and Retention","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall retain PSSR records demonstrating completion, review and authorization of PSSR activities and make them available to support future risk assessments, incident investigations, audits and Management of Change activities."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Pre-Startup Safety Review; PSSR, pre-startup safety review, commissioning, restart; PSSR Governance; PSSR Planning and Preparation; Technical and Operational Readiness Verification; Risk and Change Verification; Competence and Emergency Readiness; Startup Authorization and Deficiency Management; Records and Retention","trigger":"Use before initial startup or commissioning; startup/restart after an MOC modification; restart after a major shutdown or turnaround; and for the documented extended-idle case (>6 months).","refs":["3.46"]},{"id":"3.54","title":"Blinds and Carseal Management","page":175,"purpose":"Define the minimum requirements for managing blinds and carseals installed on valves to prevent inadvertent or unauthorized opening that could result in EHSS incidents across all Maaden operations.","scope":"This process applies to the management of all blinds and carseals installed on valves intended to remain car seals open, locked open, car seals closed or locked closed for EHSS considerations in process areas and facilities across all Maaden. Blinds and car seals installed for emergency response equipment are not covered under the scope of this process and are managed according to the requirements of emergency management and response process.","clauses":[{"id":"3.54.1","page":176,"heading":"Identification and Register","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify permanent and temporary blinds, and valves required to be car sealed or locked in a defined position, based on their role in managing EHSS risks."},{"id":"3.54.2","page":176,"heading":"Identification and Register","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain current registers of identified blinds and carseal or locked valves, including the required valve position and the basis for inclusion."},{"id":"3.54.3","page":176,"heading":"Identification and Register","text":"Registered blinds and carseal or locked valves that their deviation can result in Major or Severe EHSS consequences shall be defined as EHSS critical blinds and carseal registered list."},{"id":"3.54.4","page":176,"heading":"Blinds Management","text":"Blinds list shall be developed, approved and maintained for normal operation, shutdown and startup based on review of as-built P&IDs and physical field verification. All installed blinds shall be clearly indicated on the as-built P&IDs and maintained throughout the asset lifecycle."},{"id":"3.54.5","page":176,"heading":"Blinds Management","text":"Documented procedure, including necessary forms, approval authority, and precautions, shall be developed and implemented for the installation or removal of blinds in accordance with the standard operating or maintenance procedures."},{"id":"3.54.6","page":176,"heading":"Blinds Management","text":"All blinds shall be selected, rated and installed for the design conditions of the service."},{"id":"3.54.7","page":176,"heading":"Blinds Management","text":"Documented verification shall be conducted prior to equipment or plant startup to confirm that blinds are in the approved operating configuration, any discrepancies shall be documented and resolved before startup proceeds."},{"id":"3.54.8","page":176,"heading":"Blinds Management","text":"Temporary blinds installed for maintenance, shutdown, startup, commissioning or similar activities shall be controlled throughout their installation and removal."},{"id":"3.54.9","page":176,"heading":"Blinds Management","text":"Addition or deletion of any blinds from the blinds list for normal operation, shutdown or startup shall be done in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.54.10","page":176,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall develop, approve and maintain a master list of carseals that are kept Car Seals Open, Locked Open, Car Seals Closed or locked Closed for EHSS consideration during normal operation, shutdown and startup in each operating plants."},{"id":"3.54.11","page":176,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain an up to date list of car seals valves. The list shall include the serial number of the car seals installed and the date of the installation."},{"id":"3.54.12","page":176,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Documented procedure, including necessary forms, approval authority, and precautions, shall be developed and implemented for the installation or removal of car seals to operate valves, in accordance with the standard operating or maintenance procedures."},{"id":"3.54.13","page":176,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Car Seals lists shall include all valves sizes on relief valves, rupture disc, flare lines, battery limit, bypass valves on EHSS critical control valves and any other valves which if closed or opened during the normal operations, shutdown and startup can result in Major or Severe consequences."},{"id":"3.54.14","page":176,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Risk assessments, Process Hazard Analysis studies, engineering review, P&ID review and physical verification in the field shall be carried out in developing and reviewing the list of car seals valves."},{"id":"3.54.15","page":176,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Carseals valves shall be maintained in their designated position, and their position shall be indicated on the as-built P&IDs."},{"id":"3.54.16","page":177,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Carseals shall be weatherproof, robust and strong enough to prevent inadvertent or accidental removal. They shall be attachable by hand or self-locking and non-releasable, with a minimum unlocking strength of no less than 50 pounds."},{"id":"3.54.17","page":177,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Addition or deletion of any car seals from the car seals list for normal operation, shutdown or startup shall be done in accordance with section 3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"3.54.18","page":177,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"All removed carseals during shutdown and startup shall be tagged and stored securely, with records maintained for traceability and future audits."},{"id":"3.54.19","page":177,"heading":"Carseal Management","text":"Pre-startup checklist shall include verification of all carseals positions as per the master list to ensure compliance with EHSS requirements before initiating any startup activities."},{"id":"3.54.20","page":177,"heading":"Verification and Monitoring","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall quarterly verify the status of the carseals valves and blinds list in the plant, with results documented and discrepancies addressed promptly."},{"id":"3.54.21","page":177,"heading":"Verification and Monitoring","text":"Verification shall include reconciliation against engineering documentation, blind and carseal registers, and physical field conditions, with damaged, missing or incorrectly installed carseals immediately replaced and all findings documented."},{"id":"3.54.22","page":177,"heading":"Verification and Monitoring","text":"Unauthorized removal, installation or alteration of blinds or car seals valves shall be reported and investigated in accordance with the Incident Management and Stewardship process."},{"id":"3.54.23","page":177,"heading":"Verification and Monitoring","text":"Corrective actions arising from inspections, audits, incidents or reviews shall be tracked to closure."},{"id":"3.54.24","page":177,"heading":"Competency","text":"Personnel responsible for identifying, installing, removing or verifying blinds or carseal valves shall be competent for their assigned responsibilities, including applicable process hazards, isolation and overpressure risks, configuration control, and management of change requirements."},{"id":"3.54.25","page":177,"heading":"Documentation and Records","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall retain records of inspections, surveys, startup verifications and corrective actions."},{"id":"3.54.26","page":177,"heading":"Documentation and Records","text":"Changes involving blinds or carseal valves shall be incorporated into affected engineering documents in accordance with the Management of Change and Process Safety Information Management processes."}],"area":"Process Safety","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Blinds and Carseal Management; blind, spade, spectacle blind, carseal, positive isolation, line break; Identification and Register; Blinds Management; Carseal Management; Verification and Monitoring; Competency; Documentation and Records","trigger":"if positive isolation, line breaking, blinds, spades, spectacles or carseals are used","refs":["3.52"]},{"id":"3.55","title":"Physical Security","page":177,"purpose":"Facilities establish and maintain risk-based physical security measures that protect people, assets, critical infrastructure, information and operations from unauthorized access, intrusion, theft, sabotage and other credible security threats.","scope":"This process applies to facilities and locations owned, operated, leased or controlled by Maaden where physical security measures are required based on the facility security classification, security risk assessment or applicable authority requirements.","clauses":[{"id":"3.55.1","page":178,"heading":"Physical Security Governance and Risk Basis","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall conduct a Business Criteria Analysis (BCA) as specified in SEC-01 and assign a Facility Security Classification (FSC) based on the BCA."},{"id":"3.55.2","page":178,"heading":"Physical Security Governance and Risk Basis","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine and implement the physical security requirements applicable to facilities and locations within their control based on the facility security classification and security risk assessment recommendations."},{"id":"3.55.3","page":178,"heading":"Physical Security Governance and Risk Basis","text":"The Security Risk Assessment (SRA) shall be conducted by a qualified SAIS-approved security consultant, and the resulting physical security measures defined from the approved SRA recommendations implemented by a SAIS- approved and qualified security contractor."},{"id":"3.55.4","page":178,"heading":"Physical Security Governance and Risk Basis","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and implement a tracking system to monitor and manage Security Risk Assessment (SRA) recommendations and corrective actions until completion."},{"id":"3.55.5","page":178,"heading":"Perimeter Protection","text":"Facility perimeter protection shall be designed, implemented, and maintained based on the facility security classification and security risk assessment recommendations, and in accordance with SEC-01 and SEC-02 Security Fencing Systems."},{"id":"3.55.6","page":178,"heading":"Perimeter Protection","text":"Security fencing systems shall be selected based on the facility security classification and shall comprise, as applicable, an external interface area, anti-personnel fence area, and internal interface area. The system shall include, as applicable, clear zones, patrol roads, gates, sensors, surveillance, and lighting in accordance with SAIS directives."},{"id":"3.55.7","page":178,"heading":"Perimeter Protection","text":"Where a facility contains explosives magazines, the perimeter fencing system shall comply with the requirements of a Category 1 fencing system."},{"id":"3.55.8","page":178,"heading":"Security Gates","text":"Security gates shall be designed and implemented based on the facility security classification, security risk assessment recommendations and in accordance with SEC-03 Security Gates."},{"id":"3.55.9","page":178,"heading":"Security Gates","text":"Gate and design shall integrate the required access control system, inspection, surveillance, lighting, communication, alarm and anti-vehicle protection measures."},{"id":"3.55.10","page":178,"heading":"Security Gates","text":"Power to the gatehouse and all security devices shall comply with the requirements of SEC-07 Power Supply."},{"id":"3.55.11","page":178,"heading":"Security Gates","text":"Security gates, emergency gates, heavy-equipment gates and other controlled access points shall maintain perimeter integrity during normal, degraded and emergency conditions."},{"id":"3.55.12","page":178,"heading":"Security Lighting","text":"Security lighting shall be designed and implemented based on the security risk assessment recommendations and in accordance with SEC-04 Security Lighting."},{"id":"3.55.13","page":179,"heading":"Security Lighting","text":"Security lighting shall be integrated with surveillance and intrusion-detection systems to prevent lighting conditions from impairing their required performance."},{"id":"3.55.14","page":179,"heading":"Access Control and ID Management System","text":"Facilities shall establish access control arrangements that verify identity and authorization and restrict access to authorized people."},{"id":"3.55.15","page":179,"heading":"Access Control and ID Management System","text":"Electronic access-control systems shall be designed, implemented and integrated in accordance with SEC-05 Security Systems at Industrial Facilities and applicable authority requirements."},{"id":"3.55.16","page":179,"heading":"Access Control and ID Management System","text":"Personal Identification Verification cards and other regulated security credentials shall be issued, protected, suspended, revoked and recovered in accordance with SEC-11 Personal Identification Verification Cards."},{"id":"3.55.17","page":179,"heading":"Security Systems, Surveillance and Intrusion Detection","text":"Electronic physical security systems shall be designed, installed, integrated and modified based on the facility security classification and security risk assessment and in accordance with SEC-05 Security Systems at Industrial Facilities."},{"id":"3.55.18","page":179,"heading":"Security Systems, Surveillance and Intrusion Detection","text":"Physical security systems shall incorporate the availability, resilience, backup, tamper protection and failure protection required to maintain their intended security functions."},{"id":"3.55.19","page":179,"heading":"Security Systems, Surveillance and Intrusion Detection","text":"Intrusion detection and surveillance systems shall detect and localize security events and provide information sufficient for alarm assessment and security response within designated areas."},{"id":"3.55.20","page":179,"heading":"Security Systems, Surveillance and Intrusion Detection","text":"Surveillance and intrusion detection coverage shall address designated perimeters, gates, access points, critical assets and other areas identified through the security risk assessment."},{"id":"3.55.21","page":179,"heading":"Inspection, Screening and Security Devices","text":"Inspection, screening, personnel protection, anti-vehicle and other security devices shall be selected and implemented based on the facility security classification and security risk assessment and in accordance with SEC- 06 Security Devices."},{"id":"3.55.22","page":179,"heading":"Inspection, Screening and Security Devices","text":"Security devices requiring certification, testing or specialist safety controls shall be appropriately certified and operated by competent and authorized personnel."},{"id":"3.55.23","page":179,"heading":"Security Control Centers and Protected Security Structures","text":"Where required by the facility security classification and security risk assessment recommendations, Security Control Centers shall provide the facilities and systems required for integrated security monitoring, alarm management, surveillance, communications and coordination of security response."},{"id":"3.55.24","page":179,"heading":"Security Control Centers and Protected Security Structures","text":"Security Control Centers and other structures, rooms and enclosures housing security systems or equipment shall be designed and protected in accordance with SEC-09 Structures Housing Security Equipment."},{"id":"3.55.25","page":179,"heading":"Security Control Centers and Protected Security Structures","text":"Structures housing security systems and equipment shall provide controlled access, environmental protection and resistance to unauthorized entry, interference and tampering."},{"id":"3.55.26","page":179,"heading":"Security Control Centers and Protected Security Structures","text":"Keys and other physical access devices for security facilities, rooms, and equipment shall be securely managed and controlled to prevent unauthorized access or misuse."},{"id":"3.55.27","page":180,"heading":"Power, Communication and System Integration","text":"Physical security systems shall be provided with power continuity and backup arrangements required to maintain their intended security functions in accordance with SEC-07 Power Supply."},{"id":"3.55.28","page":180,"heading":"Power, Communication and System Integration","text":"Physical security systems requiring communication or network connectivity shall be integrated in accordance with SEC-08 Security Communications and Networks and Process 3.60 Security Communication and Networks."},{"id":"3.55.29","page":180,"heading":"Physical Security Integrity and Impairment Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and implement a Maintenance and Support Program (MSP) for physical security infrastructure, systems, and equipment to ensure they are installed, inspected, tested, maintained, repaired, and commissioned by competent personnel in a manner that preserves their intended protective function."},{"id":"3.55.30","page":180,"heading":"Physical Security Integrity and Impairment Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess failures, degradation and deviations affecting physical security measures and implement corrective actions or compensating controls until the required protection is restored."},{"id":"3.55.31","page":180,"heading":"Management of Change","text":"Business Units shall manage changes that may affect the facility security classification, security risk assessment, approved physical security basis or physical security measures in accordance with applicable SAIS approval requirements and Process 3.52 Management of Change."}],"area":"Security","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Physical Security; physical security, CCTV, access control, perimeter; Physical Security Governance and Risk Basis; Perimeter Protection; Security Gates and Access Points; Security Lighting; Access Control and Identification; Security Systems, Surveillance and Intrusion Detection; Inspection, Screening and Security Devices; Security Control Centers and Protected Security Structures; Power, Communication and System Integration; Physical Security Integrity and Impairment Management; Management of Change","trigger":"if physical security, access, surveillance, barriers or asset protection is required","refs":[]},{"id":"3.56","title":"Facilities with Marine Interface","page":180,"purpose":"Industrial facilities with marine interfaces are assessed, designed, operated and maintained in accordance with applicable marine security requirements to protect people, assets, operations and business continuity.","scope":"This process applies to Maaden owned, operated, leased or controlled industrial facilities that have a marine interface, including coastal industrial facilities with access to coastal waters, seawater intakes, marine approaches, discharge channels or other marine side facility boundaries.","clauses":[{"id":"3.56.1","page":180,"heading":"Marine Security Governance and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units shall identify facilities with marine interfaces and determine the applicability of SEC-13 and other applicable SAIS SEC requirements."},{"id":"3.56.2","page":180,"heading":"Marine Security Governance and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units shall assess marine security risks through the facility security risk assessment process, considering marine side threats, vulnerabilities and access routes affecting critical assets, infrastructure, seawater intakes, discharge channels, marine approaches and other areas requiring protection."},{"id":"3.56.3","page":180,"heading":"Marine Security Governance and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units shall establish marine-security requirements based on the security risk assessment, facility security classification and applicable authority requirements."},{"id":"3.56.4","page":180,"heading":"Marine Security Design and Implementation","text":"Business Units shall implement marine security measures to protect vulnerable marine areas, critical assets and critical infrastructure identified through the security risk assessment process."},{"id":"3.56.5","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Design and Implementation","text":"Business Units shall design, implement and modify marine security infrastructure, systems and protective measures in accordance with SEC-13."},{"id":"3.56.6","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Design and Implementation","text":"Marine security arrangements shall address, where required marine surveillance and intrusion detection; exclusion zones and restricted marine areas; marine barriers and protective measures; security monitoring and alarm integration; marine side access control; and marine security communications."},{"id":"3.56.7","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Design and Implementation","text":"New facilities and modifications affecting marine security arrangements shall be submitted to SAIS for the required review or approval and comply with any conditions imposed."},{"id":"3.56.8","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Operations and Coordination","text":"Business Units shall establish and maintain marine security arrangements that are consistent with the facility security classification, security risk assessment and applicable security requirements."},{"id":"3.56.9","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Operations and Coordination","text":"Access to marine areas shall be restricted to authorized personnel and authorized activities."},{"id":"3.56.10","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Operations and Coordination","text":"Business Units shall establish and maintain response arrangements for marine security events."},{"id":"3.56.11","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Operations and Coordination","text":"Business Units shall establish coordination arrangements with relevant government agencies where required by applicable authority requirements or security risk assessment outcomes."},{"id":"3.56.12","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Compliance","text":"Business Units shall maintain marine security infrastructure, systems and protective measures in a condition capable of achieving their intended protective function."},{"id":"3.56.13","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Compliance","text":"Business Units shall assess failures, impairments and deviations affecting marine security measures and implement corrective actions or compensating controls where required."},{"id":"3.56.14","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Compliance","text":"Business Units shall maintain documented evidence demonstrating compliance with SEC-13 requirements."},{"id":"3.56.15","page":181,"heading":"Marine Security Compliance","text":"Business Units shall submit required regulatory compliance documentation where required by SAIS."}],"area":"Security","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Facilities with Marine Interface; marine, port, jetty, ship, vessel; Marine Security Governance and Risk Assessment; Marine Security Design and Implementation; Marine Security Operations and Coordination; Marine Security Compliance","trigger":"if the facility or activity has a port, jetty, vessel or other marine interface","refs":[]},{"id":"3.57","title":"Residential Compounds for Industrial Facilities","page":181,"purpose":"Residential compounds associated with Maaden industrial facilities are located, designed, operated and maintained in accordance with applicable security requirements to protect residents, personnel and assets.","scope":"This process applies to residential compounds owned, operated, leased or controlled by Maaden that are associated with an industrial facility.","clauses":[{"id":"3.57.1","page":181,"heading":"Design Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify residential compounds within their control and determine the applicability of SEC-19 Residential Compounds for Industrial Facilities."},{"id":"3.57.2","page":181,"heading":"Design Requirements","text":"Residential compounds associated with industrial facilities shall be located outside the industrial facility operational area and security perimeter, with separation distances established in accordance with SEC-19 and its referenced requirements."},{"id":"3.57.3","page":182,"heading":"Design Requirements","text":"New residential compounds and modifications to existing compounds shall be submitted to SAIS where required and shall address any additional security conditions imposed by the authority."},{"id":"3.57.4","page":182,"heading":"Design Requirements","text":"Residential compounds shall be designed and modified in accordance with SEC-19, including its requirements for perimeter protection and clear zones, vehicle and pedestrian gates, gatehouses and access control facilities, anti- vehicle protection, traffic, inspection and rejection arrangements, security lighting, video surveillance and analytics, security communications, and fire protection."},{"id":"3.57.5","page":182,"heading":"Security Operations","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish security arrangements for controlling access by residents, employees, visitors, contractors, service providers, vehicles and deliveries in accordance with SEC-19."},{"id":"3.57.6","page":182,"heading":"Security Operations","text":"Residential compounds shall maintain documented security procedures, post orders, patrol arrangements, alarm response arrangements and emergency security scenarios in accordance with SEC-19."},{"id":"3.57.7","page":182,"heading":"Security Operations","text":"Security communications for residential compounds shall be integrated with relevant Maaden security assets and emergency response arrangements in accordance with SEC-19."},{"id":"3.57.8","page":182,"heading":"Inspection, Verification and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that required security infrastructure, systems and arrangements are inspected, tested and maintained to demonstrate continued conformity with SEC-19."},{"id":"3.57.9","page":182,"heading":"Inspection, Verification and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall manage security system failures, impaired controls and deviations from SEC-19 until compliance is restored."},{"id":"3.57.10","page":182,"heading":"Inspection, Verification and Compliance","text":"New or modified residential compounds shall not be occupied until required security controls have been verified against the approved design, SEC-19 and SAIS conditions."},{"id":"3.57.11","page":182,"heading":"Inspection, Verification and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain documented evidence of compliance with SEC-19 and shall submit the required Proof of Compliance through the prescribed regulatory workflow where required by SAIS."}],"area":"Security","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Residential Compounds for Industrial Facilities; residential compound, accommodation, camp; Design Requirements; Security Operations; Inspection, Verification and Compliance","trigger":"Use for residential compounds associated with an industrial facility, according to applicable design, operating and security requirements.","refs":[]},{"id":"3.58","title":"Passive Defense Measures at Industrial Facilities","page":182,"purpose":"Industrial facilities under Maaden control identify, assess and protect critical assets and security critical infrastructure against credible drone and unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) threats through risk-based passive defense measures.","scope":"This process applies to industrial facilities owned, operated, leased or controlled by Maaden where passive defense measures are required to protect critical assets, security critical infrastructure or single points of failure from credible drone or unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) threats.","clauses":[{"id":"3.58.1","page":182,"heading":"Passive Defense Governance and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine the applicability of SEC-21 requirements based on facility security classification, security risk assessment outcomes and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.58.2","page":182,"heading":"Passive Defense Governance and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess drone and UAV threats through the facility security risk assessment process."},{"id":"3.58.3","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Governance and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that security risk assessments identify critical assets, security critical infrastructure and single points of failure that can require protection from drone or UAV threats."},{"id":"3.58.4","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Governance and Risk Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and document passive defense requirements based on the security risk assessment, facility security classification, credible threat scenarios and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.58.5","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Design and Implementation","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement passive defense measures where required to protect critical assets, security critical infrastructure and single points of failure from identified drone or UAV threats."},{"id":"3.58.6","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Design and Implementation","text":"Passive defense measures shall be designed and implemented in accordance with SEC-21 and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.58.7","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Design and Implementation","text":"New facilities and modifications affecting passive defense measures shall be submitted to the SAIS where required and shall address any additional conditions imposed by the authority."},{"id":"3.58.8","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Design and Implementation","text":"Where security risk assessments recommend the use of electronic anti-drone measures, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate and implement such measures in accordance with SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.58.9","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain passive defense measures in a condition capable of achieving their intended protective function."},{"id":"3.58.10","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess failures, impairments and deviations affecting passive defense measures and implement corrective actions or compensating controls where required to maintain their intended protective function."},{"id":"3.58.11","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain documented evidence demonstrating compliance with SEC- 21 requirements."},{"id":"3.58.12","page":183,"heading":"Passive Defense Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall submit required regulatory approvals, compliance documentation and completion records where required by SAIS."}],"area":"Security","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Passive Defense Measures at Industrial Facilities; passive defense, hardening, standoff, protective design; Passive Defense Governance and Risk Assessment; Passive Defense Design and Implementation; Passive Defense Operations and Compliance","trigger":"if passive-defense, standoff, hardening, zoning or protective-design measures are required","refs":[]},{"id":"3.59","title":"Security Operations at Industrial Facilities","page":183,"purpose":"Industrial facilities establish and maintain risk-based security operations that protect people, assets, information and operations from credible security threats and enable effective response to security events.","scope":"This process applies to industrial facilities owned, operated, leased or controlled by Maaden.","clauses":[{"id":"3.59.1","page":183,"heading":"Security Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine the applicability of SEC-15 Security Management at Industrial Facilities and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.59.2","page":183,"heading":"Security Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain a facility security organization with the accountability, authority, capability, staffing and resources required by the facility security classification and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.59.3","page":184,"heading":"Security Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall conduct and maintain a facility specific security risk assessment in accordance with applicable security directives and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.59.4","page":184,"heading":"Security Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review and approve security risk assessment recommendations and track their implementation."},{"id":"3.59.5","page":184,"heading":"Security Governance and Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, document and maintain a Facility Security Plan based on the security risk assessment, facility security classification and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.59.6","page":184,"heading":"Security Governance and Planning","text":"The Facility Security Plan shall define the security organization, physical and procedural controls, operating arrangements, required capabilities, and support and maintenance requirements."},{"id":"3.59.7","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented security procedures and post orders for security activities performed at the facility."},{"id":"3.59.8","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Security operating arrangements shall address, as applicable personnel, visitor, contractor and vehicle access; identification, credential, key and material control; gate, patrol and Security Control Center operations; security screening and prohibited items; security alarm assessment and response; security threat and incident response; coordination with emergency response activities; protection of security sensitive information; and temporary or degraded security arrangements."},{"id":"3.59.9","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish security arrangements for controlling contractors’ presence and activities within industrial facilities."},{"id":"3.59.10","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that security personnel are competent, authorized and fit for their assigned general, post specific and equipment specific duties."},{"id":"3.59.11","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall communicate applicable security threats, rules of conduct, reporting expectations and security awareness requirements to employees, contractors and visitors."},{"id":"3.59.12","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall control the possession, storage, issue and use of firearms and ammunition in accordance with applicable legal and regulatory requirements."},{"id":"3.59.13","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide security personnel with the vehicles, communication capability, protective equipment and other resources required to perform their assigned security duties."},{"id":"3.59.14","page":184,"heading":"Security Operating Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall integrate applicable EHSS requirements and safe work practices into security operations, procedures and post orders."},{"id":"3.59.15","page":184,"heading":"Construction, Commissioning and Operational Readiness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define and control security responsibilities and interfaces during construction, commissioning, startup, operation, modification and decommissioning."},{"id":"3.59.16","page":184,"heading":"Construction, Commissioning and Operational Readiness","text":"Construction activities affecting an operating facility shall be controlled to prevent unauthorized access and adverse impacts on the facility security posture."},{"id":"3.59.17","page":184,"heading":"Construction, Commissioning and Operational Readiness","text":"Security responsibility for facilities or areas entering commissioning shall be formally transferred to the operating security organization."},{"id":"3.59.18","page":185,"heading":"Construction, Commissioning and Operational Readiness","text":"New or modified industrial facilities shall not begin operation until required security infrastructure, systems, personnel, equipment, procedures, post orders and Security Control Center capabilities are operational or approved compensating controls have been implemented."},{"id":"3.59.19","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain security infrastructure, systems and equipment, including required technical support arrangements, in a condition capable of achieving their intended protective functions."},{"id":"3.59.20","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess security system failures, impaired controls and deviations from applicable security requirements and implement corrective actions or compensating controls where required."},{"id":"3.59.21","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall manage changes that can affect the facility security posture, security risk assessment or Facility Security Plan in accordance with Process 3.52 Management of Change."},{"id":"3.59.22","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review and update the Facility Security Plan and security operating arrangements following changes to the security risk assessment, facility changes, security incidents or changes in applicable requirements."},{"id":"3.59.23","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall conduct an annual assessment of compliance with applicable security directives and implement corrective actions for identified deficiencies."},{"id":"3.59.24","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall ensure that security incidents can be reported without retaliation and are investigated and addressed in accordance with Process 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.59.25","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall integrate credible security scenarios, responsibilities and response arrangements into facility emergency plans and shall test them through training, drills or exercises."},{"id":"3.59.26","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain documented evidence demonstrating implementation of SEC-15 and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.59.27","page":185,"heading":"Operational Control and Assurance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall submit required security risk assessments, Facility Security Plans, compliance documentation and completion records where required by SAIS."}],"area":"Security","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Security Operations at Industrial Facilities; guarding, patrol, security control room, dispatch; Security Governance and Planning; Security Operating Arrangements; Construction, Commissioning and Operational Readiness; Operational Control and Assurance","trigger":"Use for risk-based security operations at an industrial facility, including guarding, access control, patrol, dispatch, readiness and security response.","refs":[]},{"id":"3.60","title":"Security Communication and Networks","page":185,"purpose":"Security communication systems and networks provide secure, reliable and resilient voice, data and video communications for security, firefighting and emergency response activities.","scope":"This process applies to facilities owned, operated, leased or controlled by Maaden where wired or wireless communication systems or data networks are used to support security, firefighting or emergency response activities.","clauses":[{"id":"3.60.1","page":185,"heading":"Security Communication and Network Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify security communication systems and networks within their control and determine the applicability of SEC-08 Security Communications and Networks and other SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.60.2","page":186,"heading":"Security Communication and Network Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and document security communication and network requirements based on the facility security classification, security risk assessment recommendations, emergency response requirements and applicable authority requirements."},{"id":"3.60.3","page":186,"heading":"Security Communication and Network Requirements","text":"Security communication systems and networks shall be designed, implemented and modified in accordance with SEC-08, and applicable National Cybersecurity Authority (NCA) requirements."},{"id":"3.60.4","page":186,"heading":"Communication Capability and Coverage","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide wired and wireless voice, data and video communication capabilities required for security, firefighting and emergency response activities."},{"id":"3.60.5","page":186,"heading":"Communication Capability and Coverage","text":"Security communication systems shall provide the coverage and capacity required across locations and operating conditions identified through the facility security risk assessment and emergency response planning processes."},{"id":"3.60.6","page":186,"heading":"Communication Capability and Coverage","text":"Security communication systems shall support communication and coordination between security facilities, emergency responders, relevant command and control locations and external response agencies where required."},{"id":"3.60.7","page":186,"heading":"Communication Capability and Coverage","text":"Wireless communication equipment used in hazardous areas shall be selected, approved and used in accordance with SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.60.8","page":186,"heading":"Communication Capability and Coverage","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall obtain required regulatory approvals for radio frequencies, wireless communication equipment and related installations before use."},{"id":"3.60.9","page":186,"heading":"Network Security and Resilience","text":"Security systems requiring network connectivity shall use security networks segregated from other networks and protected from unauthorized users in accordance with SEC-08 and applicable National Cybersecurity Authority (NCA) requirements."},{"id":"3.60.10","page":186,"heading":"Network Security and Resilience","text":"Security voice, data and video communications shall be protected against unauthorized access, interception, alteration and disruption, including through encryption where transmitted over wireless, shared, backbone, wide area or public networks."},{"id":"3.60.11","page":186,"heading":"Network Security and Resilience","text":"Security communication systems and networks shall incorporate redundancy, power continuity and alternative communication capability based on the facility security classification, credible failure scenarios and emergency response requirements."},{"id":"3.60.12","page":186,"heading":"Network Security and Resilience","text":"Security communication systems and networks shall be monitored for failures, loss of connectivity, unauthorized access and attempts to compromise communication services."},{"id":"3.60.13","page":186,"heading":"Operation and Support","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall control the authorized use, administration and access to security systems, networks, equipment, configurations and encryption credentials."},{"id":"3.60.14","page":186,"heading":"Operation and Support","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain security communication systems, network infrastructure and supporting power supplies in a condition capable of achieving their intended communication and protective functions."},{"id":"3.60.15","page":186,"heading":"Operation and Support","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess failures, impairments and deviations affecting security communication systems or networks and implement corrective actions or compensating controls where required to maintain essential communication capability."},{"id":"3.60.16","page":187,"heading":"Verification and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify the coverage, capacity, connectivity, integration, cybersecurity and resilience of security communication systems before operational use and following material modification."},{"id":"3.60.17","page":187,"heading":"Verification and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain documented evidence demonstrating compliance with SEC- 08 and applicable authority requirements."},{"id":"3.60.18","page":187,"heading":"Verification and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall submit required regulatory approval, compliance and completion documentation where required by the applicable authority."}],"area":"Security","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Security Communication and Networks; security communications, network, protected information; Security Communication and Network Requirements; Communication Capability and Coverage; Network Security and Resilience; Operation and Support; Verification and Compliance","trigger":"Use when wired or wireless voice, data or video communication systems or networks support security, firefighting or emergency response.","refs":[]},{"id":"3.61","title":"Security for Non-Industrial Facilities","page":187,"purpose":"Non-industrial facilities under Maaden control are classified, assessed, designed, operated and maintained in accordance with applicable security requirements to protect people, assets, operations and business continuity.","scope":"This process applies to non-industrial facilities owned, operated, leased or controlled by Maaden.","clauses":[{"id":"3.61.1","page":187,"heading":"Security Governance and Classification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify non-industrial facilities within their control and determine the applicability of SEC-100, associated non-industrial security directives and other applicable SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.61.2","page":187,"heading":"Security Governance and Classification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, document and maintain the facility security classification and supporting basis for applicable non-industrial facilities in accordance with SEC-100 and applicable SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.61.3","page":187,"heading":"Security Governance and Classification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish security requirements for non-industrial facilities based on the facility security classification, security risk assessment, applicable security directives and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.61.4","page":187,"heading":"Security Risk Assessment and Design Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall conduct and maintain security risk assessments for applicable non- industrial facilities in accordance with SEC-100 and applicable SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.61.5","page":187,"heading":"Security Risk Assessment and Design Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review and update security risk assessments following material facility changes, operational changes, significant security incidents, significant changes in threat conditions or other circumstances that can affect the facility security posture."},{"id":"3.61.6","page":187,"heading":"Security Risk Assessment and Design Requirements","text":"New non-industrial facilities and modifications to existing facilities shall be submitted to the SAIS where required and shall address any additional security conditions imposed by the authority."},{"id":"3.61.7","page":187,"heading":"Security Risk Assessment and Design Requirements","text":"Security infrastructure and security systems for non-industrial facilities shall be designed, implemented and modified in accordance with SEC-100, applicable security directives and other applicable SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.61.8","page":187,"heading":"Security Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain security arrangements that are consistent with the facility security classification and security risk assessment recommendations."},{"id":"3.61.9","page":188,"heading":"Security Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall implement and maintain security operations to control access, respond to security events and monitor the effectiveness of security controls at non-industrial facilities."},{"id":"3.61.10","page":188,"heading":"Security Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall assess security system failures, impaired controls and deviations from applicable security requirements and implement corrective actions or compensating controls where required."},{"id":"3.61.11","page":188,"heading":"Security Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall maintain documented evidence of compliance with applicable security directives and SAIS requirements."},{"id":"3.61.12","page":188,"heading":"Security Operations and Compliance","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall submit required regulatory compliance documentation where required by SAIS."}],"area":"Security","part":"EHSS Processes","keywords":"Security for Non-Industrial Facilities; non-industrial facility, corporate office; Security Governance and Classification; Security Risk Assessment and Design Requirements; Security Operations and Compliance","trigger":"if a corporate office or other non-industrial facility is involved","refs":[]},{"id":"4.1","title":"Contractor EHSS Management","page":188,"purpose":"The purpose of this process is to establish Maaden wide requirements for managing contractor and subcontractor EHSS risks throughout the contracted work lifecycle.","scope":"This process applies to contracted work and services requiring contractor specific controls to manage EHSS risks or comply with applicable Maaden EHSS obligations, with those controls proportionate to the nature, complexity and risk of the contracted work.","clauses":[{"id":"4.1.1","page":188,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Governance and Coordination","text":"Accountability for integrating and verifying applicable EHSS requirements throughout the contracted work lifecycle shall be assigned through the applicable contractor management process and approved authority arrangements."},{"id":"4.1.2","page":188,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Governance and Coordination","text":"Business Units/Corporate Functions and the contractor shall define and communicate their EHSS responsibilities, authorities and interfaces before affected work begins."},{"id":"4.1.3","page":188,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Governance and Coordination","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish coordination arrangements where contractors, subcontractors, Maaden personnel or other employers perform interacting work or share a workplace."},{"id":"4.1.4","page":188,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Governance and Coordination","text":"The contractor accountable to Maaden shall manage its subcontractors and shall verify implementation of the applicable contract and EHSS requirements."},{"id":"4.1.5","page":188,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Governance and Coordination","text":"Business Units/Corporate Functions and contractor personnel shall have authority to stop affected work when a mandatory EHSS requirement or required control is absent, ineffective or not implemented."},{"id":"4.1.6","page":188,"heading":"Contracted Work EHSS Planning and Risk Profiling","text":"Before contractor selection or engagement, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define the contracted scope and assess its applicable EHSS risks and obligations, including the work locations, interfaces, resources and equipment; EHSS risks and credible emergencies; critical controls; subcontracting; and applicable legal, regulatory, permit and Maaden requirements."},{"id":"4.1.7","page":189,"heading":"Contracted Work EHSS Planning and Risk Profiling","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish a contracted work EHSS risk profile that determines the required capability assessment, contract controls, readiness verification, monitoring, assurance and closeout arrangements in accordance with the Contractor Management Manual."},{"id":"4.1.8","page":189,"heading":"Contracted Work EHSS Planning and Risk Profiling","text":"Where the contracted work EHSS risk profile identifies that the minimum Contractor Management Manual arrangement are insufficient, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish additional or enhanced controls proportionate to the risk."},{"id":"4.1.9","page":189,"heading":"Contracted Work EHSS Planning and Risk Profiling","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review the risk profile when a change may affect the risks, obligations, interfaces, critical controls or management arrangements."},{"id":"4.1.10","page":189,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Capability and Selection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and apply contractor EHSS pre-qualification, capability and selection criteria in accordance with the contractor management manual."},{"id":"4.1.11","page":189,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Capability and Selection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify the capability of the contractor that will perform the work to a level proportionate to the EHSS risks of the contracted activities."},{"id":"4.1.12","page":189,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Capability and Selection","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall not select or award work to a contractor that has not demonstrated compliance with applicable mandatory EHSS qualification and selection criteria established through the contractor management manual."},{"id":"4.1.13","page":189,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Capability and Selection","text":"Contractor EHSS capability shall be reassessed where an incident, nonconformity, ownership or organizational change, scope change or other material event may affect the contractor's capability. Findings that could affect contractor qualification or supplier status shall be managed through the applicable Procurement or contractor management process."},{"id":"4.1.14","page":189,"heading":"Contract EHSS Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall include applicable EHSS requirements in procurement and contract documents before contract award."},{"id":"4.1.15","page":189,"heading":"Contract EHSS Requirements","text":"Contract documents shall define the applicable MSHEM and other EHSS requirements, obligations, controls, restrictions and performance expectations and shall identify required EHSS deliverables, responsible parties, acceptance criteria and required submission or completion points."},{"id":"4.1.16","page":189,"heading":"Contract EHSS Requirements","text":"The contractor shall communicate applicable EHSS requirements to its personnel and subcontractors in a form they can understand and shall verify implementation."},{"id":"4.1.17","page":189,"heading":"Contract Transition, Mobilization and Readiness","text":"Contract transition, handover, mobilization, onboarding and readiness verification shall be managed through the applicable Contractor Management Manual arrangements, with additional EHSS controls established where required by the contracted work EHSS risk profile."},{"id":"4.1.18","page":189,"heading":"Contract Transition, Mobilization and Readiness","text":"Contractors shall not mobilize or begin affected work until the applicable contractor management readiness process has verified that required EHSS conditions are satisfied, including competent and authorized personnel, required inductions, equipment readiness, applicable work controls and authorizations, and emergency arrangements."},{"id":"4.1.19","page":190,"heading":"Work Execution, Coordination and Change Control","text":"Contractors and subcontractors shall implement applicable contract requirements, MSHEM requirements and scope specific EHSS controls throughout work execution."},{"id":"4.1.20","page":190,"heading":"Work Execution, Coordination and Change Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall provide contractors with information under its control that is necessary to understand relevant hazards, interfaces, emergency arrangements and critical controls."},{"id":"4.1.21","page":190,"heading":"Work Execution, Coordination and Change Control","text":"Changes affecting the contracted work EHSS risk, scope, personnel, competence, equipment, subcontracting, controls or management arrangements shall be assessed and controlled through the applicable Contractor Management Manual contract change arrangements and applicable MSHEM Management of Change process."},{"id":"4.1.22","page":190,"heading":"Performance Monitoring, Assurance and Escalation","text":"Contractor EHSS performance shall be monitored and assured through the Contractor Management Manual and the applicable requirements of sections 6.2 and 6.3, with reporting, corrective action and escalation proportionate to the EHSS risks, exposure and actual performance of the contracted work."},{"id":"4.1.23","page":190,"heading":"Performance Monitoring, Assurance and Escalation","text":"Monitoring and assurance shall be increased where contractor performance deteriorates, the risk profile increases or an EHSS nonconformity is identified. Material matters requiring contractual, qualification, supplier status or other commercial action shall be referred through the applicable Contractor Management Manual, Procurement or Legal process."},{"id":"4.1.24","page":190,"heading":"Worker Engagement, Welfare and Raising Concerns","text":"The contractor shall provide its personnel and subcontractors with the EHSS information necessary for their work and shall enable their participation in risk assessment and improvement of controls relevant to their activities."},{"id":"4.1.25","page":190,"heading":"Worker Engagement, Welfare and Raising Concerns","text":"Contractor personnel shall be able to report hazards, unsafe conditions, welfare concerns and EHSS nonconformities through HEMAYA without retaliation. Business Units and Corporate Functions shall communicate applicable reporting and escalation arrangements and ensure that material concerns are assessed and tracked to verified closure."},{"id":"4.1.26","page":190,"heading":"Worker Engagement, Welfare and Raising Concerns","text":"Where the contract includes accommodation, catering, sanitation, transport or other welfare services, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define the applicable requirements in the contract and shall verify readiness before the service begins."},{"id":"4.1.27","page":190,"heading":"Work Restriction, Suspension, Termination and Continuity of Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall restrict, suspend or stop contracted work where a mandatory EHSS requirement or required control is absent or ineffective, or where the work presents uncontrolled EHSS risk."},{"id":"4.1.28","page":190,"heading":"Work Restriction, Suspension, Termination and Continuity of Control","text":"Suspended work shall not resume until the cause is assessed, required corrective actions and controls are implemented, affected personnel are informed, and applicable authorizations and critical controls are reverified."},{"id":"4.1.29","page":190,"heading":"Work Restriction, Suspension, Termination and Continuity of Control","text":"During suspension, termination, transfer, demobilization or closeout, Business Units/Corporate Functions and the contractor shall maintain applicable critical controls, emergency arrangements, site security, environmental protection, permit conditions and required records until the work is made safe and responsibility is formally transferred or closed. Suspension, termination or commercial closeout shall not remove outstanding incident, corrective action, remediation, restoration or EHSS record handover obligations."},{"id":"4.1.30","page":191,"heading":"Land Use Permit (LUP)","text":"Where contracted work may occupy, use or change the use of Maaden controlled land, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine during scope definition whether a Land Use Permit or other land, environmental, security, building, excavation, access or work approval is required."},{"id":"4.1.31","page":191,"heading":"Land Use Permit (LUP)","text":"The contractor shall not establish or occupy a camp, temporary facility, laydown area, workshop, store, access route, utility corridor or other land use area until required approvals are granted and pre-use conditions are verified."},{"id":"4.1.32","page":191,"heading":"Land Use Permit (LUP)","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall incorporate applicable approval boundaries, duration, permitted use, responsibilities, controls, change conditions, restoration obligations and closeout criteria into the contract and readiness arrangements."},{"id":"4.1.33","page":191,"heading":"Land Use Permit (LUP)","text":"The contractor shall not transfer, share, expand or materially change an approved land use without prior authorization."},{"id":"4.1.34","page":191,"heading":"Land Use Permit (LUP)","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall review and, where required, amend, renew or reissue the approval before a change to the approved use, boundary, size, structure, route, duration, responsible party or approval basis."},{"id":"4.1.35","page":191,"heading":"Land Use Permit (LUP)","text":"Before approval expiry, handover or transfer, the contractor shall remove temporary facilities, materials, chemicals, equipment and waste, resolve outstanding obligations, and restore or remediate the affected land to the defined acceptance criteria."},{"id":"4.1.36","page":191,"heading":"Land Use Permit (LUP)","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify compliance during readiness, execution and closeout and shall retain the approval, amendments, material findings, restoration evidence and final acceptance record."},{"id":"4.1.37","page":191,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Records and Information Handover","text":"The contract shall define the EHSS records and information that the contractor is required to create, maintain, protect, submit and hand over. Such records shall meet applicable legal, permit, contract records management requirements."},{"id":"4.1.38","page":191,"heading":"Contractor EHSS Records and Information Handover","text":"Before closeout, Business Units and Corporate Functions shall verify through the applicable Contractor Management Manual arrangements that required EHSS records and information have been received and accepted."},{"id":"4.1.39","page":191,"heading":"Demobilization, EHSS Closeout and Learning","text":"Demobilization and closeout shall be managed through the applicable Contractor Management Manual arrangements and shall include verification of applicable EHSS closeout conditions and required EHSS clearance before the contracted scope is closed."},{"id":"4.1.40","page":191,"heading":"Demobilization, EHSS Closeout and Learning","text":"Incomplete EHSS actions remaining at demobilization or closeout shall have an assigned accountable party, due date, interim controls where required and a defined verification requirement."},{"id":"4.1.41","page":191,"heading":"Demobilization, EHSS Closeout and Learning","text":"Contractor and subcontractor EHSS performance and relevant lessons learned shall be captured through the applicable contractor management closeout arrangements and used to inform future EHSS capability assessments, contractor selection and contracted work planning."},{"id":"4.1.42","page":191,"heading":"Demobilization, EHSS Closeout and Learning","text":"Contractor EHSS incidents shall be reported, investigated, communicated and closed in accordance with section 6.1 Incident Management and Stewardship."}],"area":"Contractor and Project","part":"Contractor and Project EHSS Management","keywords":"Contractor EHSS Management; contractor, subcontractor, contract owner, mobilization; Contractor EHSS Governance and Coordination; Contracted-Work EHSS Planning and Risk Profiling; Contractor EHSS Capability and Selection; Contract EHSS Requirements; Contract Transition, Mobilization and Readiness; Work Execution, Coordination and Change Control; Performance Monitoring, Assurance and Escalation; Worker Engagement, Welfare and Raising Concerns; Work Restriction, Suspension, Termination and Continuity of Control; Land Use Permit (LUP); Contractor EHSS Records and Information Handover; Demobilization, EHSS Closeout and Learning","trigger":"if contractors or subcontractors perform, supervise or support the work","refs":["6.1"]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Project EHSS Management","page":192,"purpose":"Projects integrate and assure applicable EHSS requirements throughout the project lifecycle so that EHSS risks are managed, requirements are incorporated into project design and execution, and assets are safely transitioned to operations.","scope":"This process applies to greenfield and brownfield projects owned, managed or controlled by Maaden, including capital, enhancement, exploration and development projects, expansions, modifications and upgrades, and mine closure from early project definition through design, procurement, construction, commissioning, operational readiness, handover and closeout.","clauses":[{"id":"4.2.1","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Governance","text":"Projects shall define and communicate EHSS accountabilities and interfaces among the project team, relevant Corporate and Business Unit functions, contractors and the receiving operating organization."},{"id":"4.2.2","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Governance","text":"The Project Manager shall be accountable for integrating applicable EHSS requirements, resources, deliverables and assurance activities into project planning, decisions and stage gate arrangements."},{"id":"4.2.3","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Governance","text":"Projects shall integrate EHSS input from the earliest applicable project stage and before decisions are made that could affect site selection, technology selection, design or execution strategy."},{"id":"4.2.4","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Governance","text":"Projects shall establish EHSS governance and assurance arrangements appropriate to the applicable project delivery and governance model."},{"id":"4.2.5","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management","text":"Each project shall determine its EHSS risk profile based on the project scope, location, lifecycle stage, activities, technology, interfaces, credible consequences and applicable obligations."},{"id":"4.2.6","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management","text":"Project EHSS requirements and assurance activities shall be proportionate to the resulting EHSS risk profile."},{"id":"4.2.7","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management","text":"Projects shall establish and maintain an EHSS Management Plan or equivalent controlled arrangements defining applicable EHSS requirements, responsibilities, deliverables, assurance activities and stage gate criteria."},{"id":"4.2.8","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management","text":"Projects shall identify and integrate applicable EHSS legal, regulatory, permit and approval requirements throughout the project lifecycle in accordance with Process 2.4 EHSS Legal and Regulatory Requirements."},{"id":"4.2.9","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management","text":"Projects shall apply Process 2.5 EHSS Risk Management throughout the project lifecycle and maintain the risk and hazard information necessary to support project decisions and transfer to operations."},{"id":"4.2.10","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management","text":"Projects shall determine and complete the EHSS studies, assessments and reviews required by the project risk profile, applicable requirements and relevant MSHEM processes."},{"id":"4.2.11","page":192,"heading":"Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management","text":"Projects shall document EHSS decisions, assumptions, findings and recommendations and track resulting actions to verified closure or controlled transfer."},{"id":"4.2.12","page":192,"heading":"EHSS in Project Design","text":"Project design shall incorporate applicable MSHEM, legal, regulatory and approved Maaden technical requirements and apply the hierarchy of controls. All capital projects shall incorporate environmental and sustainability requirements addressed in MCIS_TK_0387."},{"id":"4.2.13","page":193,"heading":"EHSS in Project Design","text":"Projects shall identify EHSS critical assets, critical controls and required performance criteria during design and incorporate them into procurement, construction, commissioning and handover."},{"id":"4.2.14","page":193,"heading":"EHSS in Project Design","text":"Project design shall consider safe construction, commissioning, operation, inspection, maintenance, emergency response, decommissioning, closure and rehabilitation throughout the asset lifecycle."},{"id":"4.2.15","page":193,"heading":"EHSS in Project Design","text":"Facility siting and the location and design of permanent, temporary and occupied buildings shall be assessed, where applicable, for credible fire, explosion, toxic release and other major hazards that could affect occupants or emergency functions."},{"id":"4.2.16","page":193,"heading":"EHSS in Project Design","text":"Cybersecurity risks associated with Operational Technology systems shall be identified and addressed during project design and applicable hazard and risk assessments in accordance with Maaden cybersecurity requirements."},{"id":"4.2.17","page":193,"heading":"EHSS in Project Design","text":"Projects shall control changes that could affect the approved EHSS basis through project change control and section\n3.52 Management of Change process."},{"id":"4.2.18","page":193,"heading":"Environmental and Health Assurance","text":"Projects shall complete and integrate environmental and social baseline studies, impact assessments studies, project categorization and permitting deliverables and mitigation requirements in accordance with applicable legal requirements and relevant MSHEM environmental processes."},{"id":"4.2.19","page":193,"heading":"Environmental and Health Assurance","text":"Projects shall identify and control occupational health and industrial hygiene risks arising during project execution and future operation in accordance with applicable MSHEM health processes."},{"id":"4.2.20","page":193,"heading":"Exploration Projects","text":"Exploration projects shall establish environmental management arrangements before exploration activities commence, addressing applicable risks associated with locations, access routes, camps and temporary facilities, land disturbance, drilling, trenching and sampling, soil, water, waste, air quality, biodiversity, fuels and hazardous materials."},{"id":"4.2.21","page":193,"heading":"Exploration Projects","text":"Exploration areas and temporary facilities shall be progressively rehabilitated, where practicable, and when no longer required shall be appropriately decommissioned, remediated or restored in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory, permit obligations, and MSHEM requirements."},{"id":"4.2.22","page":193,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Projects shall identify applicable physical and industrial security requirements during early project development and integrate them into the project scope, design basis, schedule, procurement, construction, commissioning and operational-readiness arrangements."},{"id":"4.2.23","page":193,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Where applicable under Supreme Authority for Industrial Security (SAIS) requirements, the Facility Operator shall complete the required Business Criteria Analysis to determine the facility security classification and applicable security requirements."},{"id":"4.2.24","page":193,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Projects subject to SAIS requirements shall complete the applicable security approval stages and obtain required approvals before dependent design, procurement, construction, commissioning or operation proceeds."},{"id":"4.2.25","page":193,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Security Risk Assessments, security design and other activities requiring SAIS approval shall be performed by appropriately qualified and SAIS approved consultants or contractors, as applicable."},{"id":"4.2.26","page":194,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Corporate Security shall provide the applicable governance review and act as Maaden's interface with SAIS for required submissions and regulatory correspondence."},{"id":"4.2.27","page":194,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Security requirements shall be coordinated with civil, electrical, communications, information technology, infrastructure and other project packages necessary for effective implementation and commissioning of security systems."},{"id":"4.2.28","page":194,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Projects shall classify, control access to and protect security sensitive project information in accordance with applicable information protection and authority requirements."},{"id":"4.2.29","page":194,"heading":"Project Security Assurance","text":"Projects shall obtain required authority and Maaden approvals for proposed deviations from applicable security requirements before implementation."},{"id":"4.2.30","page":194,"heading":"Contractor and Construction EHSS Management","text":"Applicable EHSS requirements shall be incorporated into project procurement, contractor evaluation, contract documents and contractor management arrangements in accordance with Section 4.1 Contractor EHSS Management."},{"id":"4.2.31","page":194,"heading":"Contractor and Construction EHSS Management","text":"Before construction begins, the project shall verify that required construction EHSS plans, competent resources, risk assessments, emergency arrangements, work controls, temporary facilities and other applicable readiness conditions are established."},{"id":"4.2.32","page":194,"heading":"Contractor and Construction EHSS Management","text":"Projects shall manage construction EHSS risks, changing conditions and interfaces throughout execution."},{"id":"4.2.33","page":194,"heading":"Contractor and Construction EHSS Management","text":"Projects shall apply quality assurance, inspection, testing, preservation and verification controls to EHSS critical assets, systems and controls during procurement, construction and commissioning."},{"id":"4.2.34","page":194,"heading":"Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover","text":"Projects shall initiate operational readiness planning in time for the receiving organization to influence design and establish the people, competency, procedures, maintenance and emergency arrangements required for operation."},{"id":"4.2.35","page":194,"heading":"Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover","text":"Projects shall verify commissioning EHSS readiness before commissioning or the introduction of hazardous materials or energy."},{"id":"4.2.36","page":194,"heading":"Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover","text":"A Pre-Startup Safety Review shall be completed before startup or introduction of hazardous materials or energy in accordance with section 3.53 Pre-Startup Safety Review process."},{"id":"4.2.37","page":194,"heading":"Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover","text":"Projects shall verify that required EHSS critical assets and operational arrangements meet approved design, performance, regulatory and readiness requirements before operation."},{"id":"4.2.38","page":194,"heading":"Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover","text":"Before operational handover, the project and receiving organization shall verify that applicable EHSS requirements, critical controls, safety critical systems, approvals, procedures, competencies and outstanding actions are complete or subject to an approved controlled transfer arrangement."},{"id":"4.2.39","page":194,"heading":"Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover","text":"Residual EHSS risks and outstanding actions shall be understood and formally accepted by the receiving organization through applicable governance arrangements."},{"id":"4.2.40","page":194,"heading":"Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover","text":"Project handover shall provide the receiving organization with the controlled EHSS information required to operate, maintain, modify, decommission and close the asset."},{"id":"4.2.41","page":195,"heading":"Project EHSS Assurance, Closeout and Records","text":"Projects shall conduct risk-based EHSS assurance at defined lifecycle stages to verify conformance with applicable project, design, MSHEM, legal, regulatory and stage gate requirements."},{"id":"4.2.42","page":195,"heading":"Project EHSS Assurance, Closeout and Records","text":"Project closeout shall verify completion or controlled transfer of outstanding EHSS obligations, actions, commitments and required records."},{"id":"4.2.43","page":195,"heading":"Project EHSS Assurance, Closeout and Records","text":"Projects shall capture and communicate relevant EHSS lessons from design, execution, commissioning and assurance activities."},{"id":"4.2.44","page":195,"heading":"Project EHSS Assurance, Closeout and Records","text":"Projects shall control, retain and transfer EHSS records in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory and project requirements."}],"area":"Contractor and Project","part":"Contractor and Project EHSS Management","keywords":"Project EHSS Management; project, stage gate, construction, commissioning, handover; Project EHSS Governance; Project EHSS Planning and Risk Management; EHSS in Project Design; Environmental and Health Assurance; Exploration Projects; Project Security Assurance; Contractor and Construction EHSS Management; Commissioning, Operational Readiness and Handover; Project EHSS Assurance, Closeout and Records","trigger":"Use across applicable greenfield and brownfield project lifecycles, from early definition and design through construction, commissioning, readiness, handover and closeout.","refs":["3.52","3.53","4.1"]},{"id":"5.1","title":"Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning","page":195,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for identifying credible emergency scenarios and maintaining effective plans, capabilities, resources, and coordination arrangements to protect people, the environment, assets, operations, and affected communities.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions, employees, contractors, and relevant stakeholders involved in emergency preparedness and planning for Maaden controlled operational, administrative, project, mining, processing, and remote locations.","clauses":[{"id":"5.1.1","page":195,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement, and maintain documented emergency preparedness and response arrangements based on their activities, facilities, hazards, credible emergency scenarios, and potential consequences."},{"id":"5.1.2","page":195,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness Management","text":"Emergency preparedness and response arrangements shall comply with applicable legal, regulatory, permit, and authority requirements. Facilities comply with SAIS SAF-20, Pre-Incident Planning and Management of Emergencies, including applicable notification and coordination requirements."},{"id":"5.1.3","page":195,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness Management","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define accountability, roles, responsibilities, authorities, and interfaces for implementing and maintaining emergency preparedness and response arrangements."},{"id":"5.1.4","page":195,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness Management","text":"Emergency preparedness and response arrangements shall address people who may be affected and coordination with applicable internal functions, neighboring operations, emergency services, authorities, mutual aid organizations, and other response partners."},{"id":"5.1.5","page":195,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness Management","text":"Emergency preparedness requirements shall be integrated into the design, construction, modification, operation, and decommissioning of facilities and activities."},{"id":"5.1.6","page":195,"heading":"Emergency Preparedness Management","text":"Changes that can affect emergency scenarios or response capability shall be assessed, and affected emergency plans, Pre-Incident Plans, resources, training, and coordination arrangements shall be updated."},{"id":"5.1.7","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall identify and assess credible emergency scenarios arising from their activities, facilities, hazards, infrastructure, and surrounding conditions."},{"id":"5.1.8","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment","text":"Scenario assessments shall be conducted by competent personnel with collective knowledge of the applicable activities, hazards, potential consequences, and emergency response requirements."},{"id":"5.1.9","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment","text":"Scenario identification shall consider internal and external events and be informed by applicable risk assessments, Process Hazard Analyses, incidents, emergency responses, exercises, and relevant lessons learned."},{"id":"5.1.10","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment","text":"Credible scenarios shall include, where relevant, fire, explosion, loss of containment, hazardous material or radiological release, structural failure, rescue and medical emergencies, natural hazards, utility failure, transportation and security events, mine emergencies, and other events with potential major consequences."},{"id":"5.1.11","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment","text":"Assessments shall consider exposed people, hazardous material inventories and release paths, facility layout and access, geographic and climatic conditions, neighboring activities, environmental receptors, and potential off-site consequences."},{"id":"5.1.12","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment","text":"Credible scenarios shall be prioritized using the Maaden EHSS Risk Matrix and considering potential consequences, escalation, response complexity, required resources, and available response capabilities."},{"id":"5.1.13","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment","text":"Assessment results shall be documented and used to define emergency planning, capability, training, and exercise requirements."},{"id":"5.1.14","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement, and maintain an Emergency Response Plan based on identified credible scenarios and assessed consequences, including applicable environmental emergencies and spills."},{"id":"5.1.15","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Emergency response strategies shall prioritize the protection and rescue of people, including responders and affected communities; stabilization of the incident and prevention of escalation; protection of the environment and property; and transition to recovery."},{"id":"5.1.16","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Pre-Incident Plans shall be developed and maintained for prioritized credible scenarios requiring scenario specific tactics, resources, coordination, or protective actions."},{"id":"5.1.17","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Emergency plans shall define arrangements for emergency notification and activation; incident command, coordination, and escalation; protection and accountability of affected people; operational and protective response actions; control of the incident area; and transition to re-entry and recovery."},{"id":"5.1.18","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Emergency plans shall establish a scalable Incident Command System, including command authority, transfer of command, coordination interfaces, and escalation arrangements."},{"id":"5.1.19","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Required emergency response personnel, competencies, equipment, systems, facilities, and external support shall be determined from the credible scenarios and response strategies."},{"id":"5.1.20","page":196,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"An Emergency Operations Center or equivalent coordination facility shall be established and maintained where required by assessed consequences, response complexity, or applicable legal and regulatory requirements."},{"id":"5.1.21","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Emergency plans shall define coordination and communication interfaces with applicable internal functions, contractors, neighboring facilities, authorities, emergency services, mutual-aid organizations, and other response partners."},{"id":"5.1.22","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Where credible scenarios can result in off-site consequences, emergency plans shall define arrangements for warning, protective action communication, and coordination with affected facilities, communities, authorities, and authorized public-communication functions."},{"id":"5.1.23","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define the hierarchy and approval authority for Emergency Response Plans and Pre-Incident Plans and integrate them with applicable crisis management and business continuity arrangements."},{"id":"5.1.24","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning","text":"Current information required to support emergency command and response, including location, access, hazard, utility, isolation, resource, and contact information, shall be readily accessible to authorized internal and external responders."},{"id":"5.1.25","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Capabilities, Resources and Coordination","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall determine the emergency capabilities and resources required for identified credible scenarios, considering their nature, scale, location, potential consequences, escalation potential, and response complexity."},{"id":"5.1.26","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Capabilities, Resources and Coordination","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented readiness requirements for emergency personnel, equipment, facilities, communication systems, medical support, and internal and external response resources based on identified credible scenarios."},{"id":"5.1.27","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Capabilities, Resources and Coordination","text":"Emergency personnel and response coverage shall be sufficient for identified credible scenarios across operating shifts, remote locations, periods of reduced staffing, contractor activities, and foreseeable resource unavailability."},{"id":"5.1.28","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Capabilities, Resources and Coordination","text":"Responsibilities, communication arrangements, resource commitments, and response interfaces with contractors, authorities, external emergency services, neighboring facilities, and mutual aid organizations shall be documented."},{"id":"5.1.29","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Capabilities, Resources and Coordination","text":"The availability and mobilization of external and mutual aid resources, including communication interoperability and equipment compatibility, shall be verified through applicable drills or exercises."},{"id":"5.1.30","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Capabilities, Resources and Coordination","text":"Emergency capability gaps shall be assessed, and interim controls shall be implemented until the required capability is restored or an alternative providing equivalent risk control is established."},{"id":"5.1.31","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Competency, Training and Awareness","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define role specific competency and training requirements for personnel with emergency responsibilities, based on assigned roles, credible scenarios, hazards, response strategies, and equipment."},{"id":"5.1.32","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Competency, Training and Awareness","text":"Personnel shall demonstrate the required competency before assignment to an emergency role and shall be formally appointed or authorized where required by the role or applicable legal and regulatory requirements."},{"id":"5.1.33","page":197,"heading":"Emergency Competency, Training and Awareness","text":"Employees, contractor personnel, visitors, and other people at Maaden controlled workplaces shall receive information and instructions relevant to their expected actions during an emergency, including alarm recognition, emergency reporting, evacuation, assembly, and shelter arrangements."},{"id":"5.1.34","page":198,"heading":"Emergency Competency, Training and Awareness","text":"Competency for personnel with emergency response duties shall be assessed against defined role specific criteria at established intervals and following circumstances that can affect their ability to perform the role. Assessments shall include practical demonstration where the role requires applied response skills."},{"id":"5.1.35","page":198,"heading":"Emergency Competency, Training and Awareness","text":"Emergency competency, training, and awareness requirements shall be reviewed and updated following changes affecting emergency arrangements, identified competency deficiencies, or relevant findings from drills, exercises, incidents, and assurance activities."},{"id":"5.1.36","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, implement, and monitor an annual risk-based drill and exercise program covering prioritized credible emergency scenarios. The program shall address applicable legal and regulatory requirements, emergency risks, operational changes, previous exercise and emergency performance, and identified competency needs."},{"id":"5.1.37","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"Each drill or exercise shall have documented objectives, scope, participants, performance criteria, and evaluation arrangements established before it is conducted."},{"id":"5.1.38","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"Drills and exercises shall reflect the applicable scenario, response arrangements, location, operating conditions, and external interfaces. Each drill or exercise shall be subject to a documented safety review, and hazardous response actions shall be simulated where their physical execution would introduce unacceptable risk."},{"id":"5.1.39","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"The exercise program shall verify applicable emergency command, communication, notification, personnel accountability, protective actions, operational response, response capability, resource availability, external coordination, and initial recovery arrangements."},{"id":"5.1.40","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"Employees, contractors, emergency personnel, support functions, and external response parties shall participate in drills and exercises relevant to their assigned roles."},{"id":"5.1.41","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"Mining operations shall include mine specific emergency arrangements within the exercise program, where applicable. Each mine shall conduct at least two evacuation drills annually, covering representative work areas and operating conditions."},{"id":"5.1.42","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"As a minimum, the drill and exercise program shall:\na. include muster, evacuation, tabletop, and emergency team response exercises at least quarterly, conducted individually or in combination;\nb. include periodic area-, unit-, and shift-specific drills addressing the credible scenarios applicable to them;\nc. cover the full range of prioritized credible scenarios within a cycle not exceeding two years; and\nd. include at least one large-scale drill annually involving the full emergency organization and applicable mutual aid parties, with the required advance notification provided to SAIS."},{"id":"5.1.43","page":198,"heading":"Exercises and Preparedness Verification","text":"Exercise performance and findings shall be evaluated and documented, and resulting corrective actions and lessons learned shall be communicated, tracked to closure, and used to improve emergency preparedness and response arrangements."},{"id":"5.1.44","page":198,"heading":"Preparedness Monitoring and Plan Review","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate the effectiveness of applicable emergency preparedness and response arrangements at defined intervals using results from exercises, actual emergencies, audits, and other relevant performance information."},{"id":"5.1.45","page":199,"heading":"Preparedness Monitoring and Plan Review","text":"Emergency Response Plans and Pre-Incident Plans shall be formally reviewed, updated as necessary, and reapproved at least once every three years."},{"id":"5.1.46","page":199,"heading":"Preparedness Monitoring and Plan Review","text":"Emergency plans and arrangements shall be reviewed and updated as necessary following exercises, actual emergencies, relevant changes, audits, or findings that may affect their adequacy or effectiveness."},{"id":"5.1.47","page":199,"heading":"Preparedness Monitoring and Plan Review","text":"Lessons and deficiencies identified through exercises, emergencies, audits, and other evaluations shall be used to improve applicable emergency arrangements, with required actions managed through the applicable corrective- action process."},{"id":"5.1.48","page":199,"heading":"Preparedness Monitoring and Plan Review","text":"Documented evidence of emergency plan reviews, exercises, preparedness evaluations, lessons learned, and resulting actions shall be retained in accordance with applicable record-control requirements."}],"area":"Emergency Management","part":"Emergency Management and Response","keywords":"Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning; emergency plan, incident command, crisis, evacuation, rescue; Emergency Preparedness Management; Emergency Scenario Identification and Assessment; Emergency Strategies, Plans and Pre-Incident Planning; Emergency Capabilities, Resources and Coordination; Emergency Competency, Training and Awareness; Exercises and Preparedness Verification; Preparedness Monitoring and Plan Review","trigger":"if credible emergency scenarios require planning, response, escalation, crisis or recovery arrangements","refs":[]},{"id":"5.2","title":"Emergency Response Team Selection and Training","page":199,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for determining Emergency Response Team needs and for selecting, training, qualifying, equipping, authorizing and maintaining competent personnel capable of performing assigned emergency response duties safely and effectively.","scope":"This process applies to Business Units and Corporate Functions that establish, provide, manage, or rely upon internal, contractor, mutual aid, or external Emergency Response Teams for firefighting, rescue, medical, hazardous material, mine emergency or other specialized emergency response functions.","clauses":[{"id":"5.2.1","page":199,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented arrangements for managing Emergency Response Teams in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory, and adopted technical requirements."},{"id":"5.2.2","page":199,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"Required ERT functions and capabilities shall be determined through a documented and approved capability assessment considering credible emergency scenarios, response plans, risk assessments, operating conditions, and the capability requirements established under section 5.1 Emergency Response Preparedness and Planning."},{"id":"5.2.3","page":199,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"ERT capabilities shall address firefighting, rescue, medical, hazardous-material, mine-emergency, and other specialized response functions identified through the emergency capability assessment."},{"id":"5.2.4","page":199,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall define the ERT organization, operational capability level, authority, responsibilities, command arrangements, and response limitations in accordance with NFPA 600 and the applicable provisions of NFPA 1550."},{"id":"5.2.5","page":199,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish role specific criteria for selecting ERT members, addressing the knowledge, skills, experience, qualifications, availability, and medical and physical suitability required for their assigned emergency duties."},{"id":"5.2.6","page":199,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"ERT candidates and members shall be medically evaluated and cleared by a competent occupational health physician before assignment, at least annually thereafter, and before returning to emergency duties following an illness, injury, condition, or absence that could affect their capability. Medical information shall remain confidential, with only fitness status and necessary work restrictions communicated to management, in accordance with Process 3.19 and NFPA 1580, as applicable."},{"id":"5.2.7","page":200,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"ERT members shall only be assigned or authorized for functions for which they are medically and physically fit, trained, assessed as competent, qualified, equipped, and capable of performing the assigned duties safely."},{"id":"5.2.8","page":200,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"Minimum ERT staffing, availability, backup coverage, and personnel distribution shall be defined and maintained for the required response capabilities, operating shifts, locations, and foreseeable emergency conditions."},{"id":"5.2.9","page":200,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"Internal ERT arrangements shall be coordinated with relevant contractors, neighboring facilities, mutual-aid organizations, external emergency services, and authorities. Their roles, interfaces, resource commitments, communication arrangements, and limitations shall be defined."},{"id":"5.2.10","page":200,"heading":"ERT Management and Capability Requirements","text":"Activities or facilities shall not commence or continue operation unless the required ERT capability is available or approved interim controls and alternative response arrangements have been established. Changes affecting ERT capabilities or arrangements shall be assessed and managed through Process 3.52 where the Management of Change applicability criteria are met."},{"id":"5.2.11","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain an ERT training and qualification program based on credible emergency scenarios, required response capabilities, assigned roles, and applicable legal and adopted technical requirements."},{"id":"5.2.12","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"ERT members shall complete approved initial training and demonstrate the required competency before authorization to perform emergency response duties."},{"id":"5.2.13","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"ERT members shall demonstrate the safe use of assigned apparatus, equipment, communication systems, respiratory protection, and PPE before independent assignment."},{"id":"5.2.14","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"Training and qualification requirements shall be defined for each assigned operational level and function, including ERT leader, incident commander, incident safety officer, instructor, assessor, and other specialist roles, where applicable. An annual training, certification, and competency assessment plan shall be maintained. Minimum role specific qualification, initial training, refresher training, certification renewal, and competency reassessment requirements shall be defined in an approved ERT Training and Competency Matrix."},{"id":"5.2.15","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"ERT training shall address, as applicable to assigned roles, facility hazards, credible emergency scenarios, emergency-response plans, Pre-Incident Plans, fixed fire-protection and detection systems, assigned response procedures, command arrangements, communications, personnel accountability, responder safety, and the inspection, use, care, and limitations of assigned equipment and PPE."},{"id":"5.2.16","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"Facility fire-brigade members, firefighters, and fire-apparatus driver/operators shall be trained and qualified for their assigned operating levels and duties in accordance with NFPA 600, NFPA 1081, and NFPA 1010, as applicable. Live fire training shall be planned, delivered, and controlled in accordance with NFPA 1400."},{"id":"5.2.17","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"Personnel assigned technical-rescue duties shall be trained and qualified for the applicable rescue discipline and operational level in accordance with NFPA 1006."},{"id":"5.2.18","page":200,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"Personnel assigned hazardous-material response duties shall be trained and qualified for their assigned awareness, operations, technician, or incident-command level in accordance with NFPA 470. ERT members shall maintain valid CPR/AED certification and any first aid, bloodborne pathogen, or other medical response certification required by their assigned roles."},{"id":"5.2.19","page":201,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"ERT instructors and assessors shall be competent and authorized for the subjects, functions, and qualification levels they deliver or assess. Formal certifications identified in the approved ERT Training and Competency Matrix shall be issued through Pro Board, IFSAC, or another accreditation or certification system formally approved by Maaden."},{"id":"5.2.20","page":201,"heading":"ERT Training and Qualification","text":"ERT competency shall be maintained through refresher training, certification renewal, and competency reassessment. Each ERT member shall complete a documented practical competency evaluation at least annually. Additional training or reassessment shall be completed following relevant changes, extended absence, incidents, exercises, unsatisfactory assessment results, or identified competency deficiencies."},{"id":"5.2.21","page":201,"heading":"Operational Readiness and Competency","text":"Where an underground mine ERT capability is required, assigned members shall complete at least 80 hours of mine- emergency training annually, covering breathing-apparatus operations, mine fires and explosions, ventilation and controls, mine gases and atmosphere evaluation, exploration, search and rescue, emergency planning, and command integration. Practical underground training shall be conducted at least once every six months and shall include search, navigation, and rescue under smoke or controlled smoke-equivalent conditions."},{"id":"5.2.22","page":201,"heading":"Operational Readiness and Competency","text":"ERT members shall maintain current operational familiarity with facility-specific access and egress routes, response-equipment locations, communication systems, staging and command locations, and external response interfaces applicable to their assigned functions."},{"id":"5.2.23","page":201,"heading":"Operational Readiness and Competency","text":"ERT members shall have access to suitable and serviceable PPE, respiratory protection, apparatus, tools, communication systems, and other equipment required for their assigned functions and credible response conditions."},{"id":"5.2.24","page":201,"heading":"Operational Readiness and Competency","text":"Each ERT member shall participate in at least four scenario-based ERT drills annually, covering scenarios applicable to their assigned functions and identified through the emergency capability assessment. Drill planning and evaluation shall be coordinated through Process 5.1. Participation in an actual emergency response shall not substitute for required training unless an approved equivalency has been established and the member’s performance has been documented and assessed against the applicable competency requirements."},{"id":"5.2.25","page":201,"heading":"Operational Readiness and Competency","text":"Where SCBA or supplied-air respiratory protection is used, incident-scene controls shall provide for wearer identification, location and task tracking, entry and air-duration monitoring, emergency withdrawal, and PASS or equivalent devices. Incident-scene arrangements shall include an Incident Safety Officer and controls for responder rescue, rehabilitation, and infection prevention, based on the incident complexity and risk."},{"id":"5.2.26","page":201,"heading":"Operational Readiness and Competency","text":"Authorization to perform emergency-response duties shall be suspended when an ERT member no longer maintains the required medical or physical fitness, competency, qualification, certification, participation, or operational readiness and reinstated only after the applicable requirements are satisfied, supporting evidence is documented, and formal clearance is obtained."},{"id":"5.2.27","page":201,"heading":"ERT Performance Monitoring and Improvement","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and periodically review performance measures for ERT staffing and coverage, training, competency, qualification, authorization, medical and physical fitness, participation, equipment readiness, and internal and external response arrangements, using relevant results from training assessments, drills, exercises, actual responses, inspections, and compliance reviews."},{"id":"5.2.28","page":202,"heading":"ERT Performance Monitoring and Improvement","text":"ERT training and qualification requirements and programs shall be periodically reviewed for continued alignment with assigned duties, credible emergency scenarios, applicable legal requirements, adopted standards, certification conditions, and the approved ERT Training and Competency Matrix."},{"id":"5.2.29","page":202,"heading":"ERT Performance Monitoring and Improvement","text":"The availability and capability of internal and external response resources and arrangements shall be periodically verified, including the continued validity of applicable mutual-aid and external-service commitments."},{"id":"5.2.30","page":202,"heading":"ERT Performance Monitoring and Improvement","text":"Deficiencies affecting ERT capability shall be controlled and managed through the applicable corrective action process until the required capability is restored or an approved alternative response arrangement is established. Identified actions shall be assigned, tracked, and verified for effective closure, and findings and lessons learned shall be used to improve ERT management, selection, training, qualification, resources, readiness, and response arrangements."},{"id":"5.2.31","page":202,"heading":"ERT Performance Monitoring and Improvement","text":"Records demonstrating ERT capability, staffing, selection, medical and physical fitness, assignment, authorization, qualification, certification, training, competency reassessment, participation, equipment readiness, external- response arrangements, performance monitoring, and corrective action closure shall be maintained and retained in accordance with applicable legal and record control requirements in accordance with Process 3.19."}],"area":"Emergency Management","part":"Emergency Management and Response","keywords":"Emergency Response Team Selection and Training; ERT, emergency response team, rescue team; ERT Management and Capability Requirements; ERT Training and Qualification; Operational Readiness and Competency; ERT Performance Monitoring and Improvement","trigger":"if emergency-response personnel must be selected, medically cleared, trained or assessed","refs":["5.1"]},{"id":"5.3","title":"Emergency Response Equipment Maintenance, Testing and Interruption","page":202,"purpose":"Establish minimum requirements for maintaining emergency response equipment and systems throughout their lifecycle in a reliable and ready condition, including the control of inspection, testing, maintenance, interruption, restoration, decommissioning, disposal and readiness monitoring.","scope":"This process applies to emergency response equipment, materials, vehicles, fire protection systems, detection and alarm systems, communication systems, medical and rescue equipment, environmental response equipment, and other emergency resources provided or controlled by Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions.","clauses":[{"id":"5.3.1","page":202,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain documented arrangements for managing emergency equipment and systems under their control throughout their lifecycle, including acquisition, acceptance, operation, maintenance, modification, decommissioning and disposal."},{"id":"5.3.2","page":202,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Emergency equipment and systems shall be identified from credible emergency scenarios, response plans, Pre- Incident Plans, legal requirements, and required response capabilities."},{"id":"5.3.3","page":202,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"All emergency response equipment and systems shall be classified as EHSS critical assets, designated as AC0. This categorization ensures that such equipment receives the highest priority in terms of preventive maintenance, testing, and overall management."},{"id":"5.3.4","page":202,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Roles and authorities for equipment specification, approval, procurement, acceptance, custody, inspection, testing, maintenance, impairment control, and disposal shall be defined."},{"id":"5.3.5","page":202,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Emergency equipment and systems shall comply with applicable legal, regulatory, permit and authority requirements, including applicable SAIS Safety and Fire Protection Directives."},{"id":"5.3.6","page":203,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Emergency equipment shall be suitable for its intended function, operating environment, users, and identified emergency scenarios. Fire protection equipment and systems shall be approved, listed or certified, as applicable, by SASO or another competent Saudi authority and, where required by approved specifications, UL, FM or equivalent."},{"id":"5.3.7","page":203,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Emergency equipment and systems shall be recorded in an approved emergency-equipment register or maintenance-management system and managed against approved equipment-specific inspection, testing and maintenance schedules. Records shall include, as applicable, asset identity, location, ownership, criticality, technical requirements, service status, service life and applicable certification. Equipment shall be inspected and accepted before initial service, following replacement or modification, and after significant maintenance."},{"id":"5.3.8","page":203,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Contractor-provided, leased, mobile, shared and externally maintained emergency equipment shall meet the same applicable approval, readiness and asset-control requirements. Contractors performing inspection, testing, maintenance or certification shall meet applicable Saudi accreditation or authorization requirements."},{"id":"5.3.9","page":203,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Unauthorized modification, substitution, relocation, removal, or non-emergency use of emergency equipment shall be prevented. Changes affecting equipment type, quantity, location, capacity, performance, availability or supporting infrastructure shall be assessed and authorized before implementation and managed through Management of Change where they could affect required emergency capability."},{"id":"5.3.10","page":203,"heading":"Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control","text":"Obsolete, expired, recalled, damaged, nonconforming or decommissioned emergency equipment shall be identified, controlled, removed from service and disposed of through approved arrangements that prevent unauthorized reuse. Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically verify that equipment inventories and asset information remain complete, accurate and aligned with required emergency capabilities. Required specifications, approvals, certificates, asset changes and disposal records shall be maintained in accordance with applicable record control requirements."},{"id":"5.3.11","page":203,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish and maintain an approved inspection, testing and preventive maintenance program for emergency equipment and systems."},{"id":"5.3.12","page":203,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Inspection, testing and maintenance requirements shall be based on equipment criticality, credible emergency scenarios, applicable legal and regulatory requirements, manufacturer instructions and adopted technical standards, including NFPA 10, NFPA 25, NFPA 72, NFPA 110 and other applicable equipment specific standards."},{"id":"5.3.13","page":203,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Emergency equipment and systems shall be inspected, tested and maintained at defined intervals to confirm their availability, integrity and required performance against equipment-specific acceptance criteria."},{"id":"5.3.14","page":203,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Inspection, testing and maintenance activities shall be performed by competent and authorized personnel using approved methods and, where required, calibrated or functionally verified measuring and test equipment, with independent testing or certification conducted by an appropriately accredited or Saudi authorized organization."},{"id":"5.3.15","page":203,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Maintenance, replacement parts, consumables and modifications shall comply with approved specifications and manufacturer requirements."},{"id":"5.3.16","page":203,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Overdue or missed inspection, testing or maintenance activities shall be managed through the applicable Asset Maintenance Deferment requirements. The deferment shall identify the affected equipment and system components, assess the resulting risk, establish compensating controls and escalation requirements, assign an accountable owner and completion date, and require completion at the earliest practicable opportunity."},{"id":"5.3.17","page":204,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Inspection and testing activities shall not reduce emergency capability unless an approved impairment arrangement and suitable compensating controls are established."},{"id":"5.3.18","page":204,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Defective, damaged, expired, contaminated or noncompliant equipment shall be identified and removed from service until repaired or replaced and successfully inspected and tested for return to service. Equipment with expired mandatory certification or unresolved legal noncompliance shall not be approved for continued use."},{"id":"5.3.19","page":204,"heading":"Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements","text":"Inspection, testing, maintenance, defects, repairs, replacements and return-to-service results shall be recorded in the approved maintenance management system. Business Units and Corporate Functions shall periodically verify compliance with the approved program and completion of required corrective actions."},{"id":"5.3.20","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish documented arrangements to assess, authorize and control planned and unplanned interruptions of emergency equipment and systems. An interruption shall not be initiated without prior assessment and authorization, except where immediate action is necessary to protect personnel or prevent escalation of an emergency or equipment damage."},{"id":"5.3.21","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"The interruption shall be classified and assessed based on equipment criticality, the affected emergency capability, credible emergency scenarios, operating conditions, expected duration and potential consequences, and the assessment shall define the compensating controls and operating conditions required during the interruption."},{"id":"5.3.22","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"Compensating controls shall be implemented before a planned interruption and as soon as practicable following an unplanned interruption. The controls shall be maintained and periodically verified for effectiveness during the authorized interruption period and reassessed when the interruption duration, operating conditions or emergency risks change."},{"id":"5.3.23","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"Relevant affected parties shall be notified of the interruption, compensating controls and restoration of the equipment or system. External responders and authorities shall be notified where their response arrangements or applicable notification requirements are affected."},{"id":"5.3.24","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"Activities or facilities shall be restricted, suspended or shut down where the interruption assessment determines that the required emergency capability cannot be maintained."},{"id":"5.3.25","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"Interrupted equipment and systems shall be restored according to priorities and target completion requirements established based on equipment criticality, the affected emergency capability and assessed risk. Return to service shall be verified and authorized before the applicable compensating controls are removed."},{"id":"5.3.26","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"Interruption records shall include assessment, authorization, compensating controls, required notifications, interruption duration, restoration verification and return to service authorization. Significant or recurring interruptions shall be investigated to identify underlying causes and required improvements, and resulting actions shall be managed through the applicable corrective action process."},{"id":"5.3.27","page":204,"heading":"Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls","text":"Non-emergency use of firewater, hydrants or fire hoses shall be avoided where practicable and shall only occur following documented justification, risk assessment and authorization, with the required quantity and duration recorded. Such use shall not reduce the firewater storage tank level below the approved minimum fire service operating level necessary to maintain the required fire protection design demand, duration and system performance. Where the required fire protection capability cannot be maintained, the condition shall be managed as an interruption in accordance with this process."},{"id":"5.3.28","page":205,"heading":"Restoration and Return to Service","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish documented arrangements for the controlled restoration and return to service of emergency equipment and systems."},{"id":"5.3.29","page":205,"heading":"Restoration and Return to Service","text":"Required maintenance, repair, replacement or corrective work shall be completed before the equipment or system is inspected and functionally tested to verify its condition, configuration, functionality and required performance. The verification shall include settings, connections, power supplies, controls, alarms and supporting systems, as applicable."},{"id":"5.3.30","page":205,"heading":"Restoration and Return to Service","text":"Restoration inspection and testing shall be performed by competent and authorized personnel against approved equipment specific acceptance criteria, and return to service shall be authorized by the designated approval authority."},{"id":"5.3.31","page":205,"heading":"Restoration and Return to Service","text":"Emergency equipment and systems shall not be returned to service, and associated compensating controls or operating restrictions shall not be removed, until the acceptance criteria have been met and return to service has been verified and authorized."},{"id":"5.3.32","page":205,"heading":"Restoration and Return to Service","text":"Equipment or systems that fail restoration testing or do not meet the applicable acceptance criteria shall remain out of service, and the applicable compensating controls and operating restrictions shall remain in place. Repeated restoration test failures shall be investigated and managed through the applicable corrective action process."},{"id":"5.3.33","page":205,"heading":"Restoration and Return to Service","text":"Relevant affected parties shall be notified when emergency capability has been restored and associated compensating controls or operating restrictions have been removed. Asset status, applicable equipment inventories, maintenance records, inspection and test results, return-to-service authorization and affected emergency plans shall be updated and retained in the applicable approved systems."},{"id":"5.3.34","page":205,"heading":"Readiness Monitoring and Performance Review","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall monitor and review defined indicators of emergency-equipment readiness and maintenance performance. Monitoring shall address equipment availability, completion of required inspection, testing and maintenance, overdue critical maintenance, interruption closure, restoration-testing results, repeated defects, corrective-action completion and relevant findings from drills, exercises and actual emergency responses. Applicable indicator ownership, reporting frequency, targets and escalation thresholds shall be defined."},{"id":"5.3.35","page":205,"heading":"Readiness Monitoring and Performance Review","text":"The continuing suitability, sufficiency, location and availability of emergency equipment and systems shall be verified at planned intervals and following material changes to credible emergency scenarios, facilities, operations or response arrangements. The review shall include equipment and services provided, leased, shared or maintained by external parties where they contribute to the required emergency capability. Risk-based independent assurance or competent third-party assessment shall be conducted where required by regulatory obligations, adopted technical standards, equipment criticality or identified performance concerns."},{"id":"5.3.36","page":205,"heading":"Readiness Monitoring and Performance Review","text":"Monitoring results, trends, findings and lessons learned shall be used to improve equipment specifications, maintenance strategies, resource and spare-part arrangements, and emergency-response capability. Identified deficiencies and improvement actions shall be risk-prioritized and managed through the applicable corrective-action process."}],"area":"Emergency Management","part":"Emergency Management and Response","keywords":"Emergency Response Equipment Maintenance, Testing and Interruption; fire equipment, alarm, emergency equipment, impairment; Emergency Equipment Management and Asset Control; Inspection, Testing and Maintenance Requirements; Equipment Interruption and Compensating Controls; Restoration and Return to Service; Readiness Monitoring and Performance Review","trigger":"if emergency equipment is inspected, tested, maintained, impaired or restored","refs":[]},{"id":"6.1","title":"Incident Management and Stewardship","page":206,"purpose":"EHSS incidents, Near Misses, High Potential Incidents (HPIs) and EHSS Observations are consistently reported, classified, investigated, analyzed, communicated, recorded and used to drive effective risk reduction and continual improvement across Maaden.","scope":"This process applies to work-related EHSS incidents, occupational injuries and illnesses, near misses, High Potential Incidents, Life Saving Rule violations and EHSS observations involving Maaden employees, contractors or visitors arising from activities under Maaden operational control, including Maaden-managed transportation and work performed on behalf of Maaden outside Maaden-managed facilities. For joint ventures and other activities not under Maaden operational control, the applicable Business Unit shall establish documented arrangements for incident reporting, investigation information sharing, recordkeeping and performance reporting.","clauses":[{"id":"6.1.1","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"Employees, contractors and visitors shall report EHSS events that they are involved in or witness by recording them in the Maaden incident reporting system (HEMAYA). Where the event involves an occupational injury or illness, the incident shall also be reported to the designated medical and EHSS function."},{"id":"6.1.2","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"Business Units shall establish and maintain arrangements that encourage EHSS event reporting without fear of retaliation and communicate reporting expectations through EHSS inductions and awareness activities."},{"id":"6.1.3","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"EHSS events shall be reported and recorded within the Business Unit, department, project or activity responsible for the affected operation or work activity. Event reports shall contain the information to support notification, classification, investigation, recordkeeping and performance reporting."},{"id":"6.1.4","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"EHSS event reporting accountability shall be assigned to the Business Unit, project or activity responsible for the work activity, including transportation activities and project to operation transitions."},{"id":"6.1.5","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"The Business Unit EHSS function shall notify Corporate EHSS of Class 1, Class 2, Class 3 incidents and High Potential Incidents (HPIs). Class 1 incidents shall be immediately escalated to the highest Business Unit EHSS authority and to Corporate EHSS."},{"id":"6.1.6","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"EHSS incidents shall be reported to applicable government and regulatory authorities in accordance with applicable legal, regulatory and permit requirements."},{"id":"6.1.7","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"Facilities under SAIS supervision shall immediately notify SAIS of incidents occurring within the facility boundary through approved SAIS emergency communication channels. Where full incident information is not available at the time of notification, available information shall be reported and updated when additional information is available."},{"id":"6.1.8","page":206,"heading":"EHSS Event Reporting and Notification","text":"Facilities under SAIS supervision shall submit incident reports and investigation reports to SAIS in accordance with applicable SAF-25 requirements."},{"id":"6.1.9","page":207,"heading":"Incident Response and Evidence Preservation","text":"The responsible supervisor, line manager or designated incident lead shall ensure immediate actions are taken to protect people, the environment, assets and information following an incident."},{"id":"6.1.10","page":207,"heading":"Incident Response and Evidence Preservation","text":"Incidents requiring emergency response or site control shall be managed in accordance with applicable emergency management arrangements."},{"id":"6.1.11","page":207,"heading":"Incident Response and Evidence Preservation","text":"Following stabilization of the incident, relevant evidence, records and information shall be identified, collected, preserved and protected from unauthorized alteration or removal."},{"id":"6.1.12","page":207,"heading":"Incident Response and Evidence Preservation","text":"Where a fatality involving an employee or contractor is confirmed, the Business Unit shall secure and preserve the incident scene, including restricting access to authorized personnel, maintaining an entry and exit log, suspending related operations as required, and immediately notifying applicable government authorities, until the scene is released by the relevant authority."},{"id":"6.1.13","page":207,"heading":"Incident Response and Evidence Preservation","text":"Business Units shall provide cooperation and access to government authorities when required for incident response, investigation or regulatory review."},{"id":"6.1.14","page":207,"heading":"Incident Response and Evidence Preservation","text":"Where a contractor employee is involved in a reportable occupational injury or illness, the responsible Business Unit shall notify the contractor organization and obtain documented confirmation that applicable legal and regulatory reporting obligations have been addressed by the contractor employer."},{"id":"6.1.15","page":207,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"The EHSS events subject to reporting, classification and management under this process shall include incidents, near misses, Life Saving Rule (LSR) violations and EHSS observations (unsafe acts and unsafe conditions)."},{"id":"6.1.16","page":207,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"EHSS incidents shall be classified and categorized in accordance with Table 6.1-1:\n[Table table-207-1.png]\n\n[Table table-208-1.png]\n\nTable 6.1-1. EHSS Incident Classification Categories."},{"id":"6.1.17","page":208,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"Occupational injuries and illnesses shall be further classified using Table 6.1-2:\n[Table table-208-2.png]\n\nTable 6.1-2. Occupational Injury and Illness Classification."},{"id":"6.1.18","page":208,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"EHSS incidents shall be classified based on the initiating event that resulted in the incident, rather than solely on the resulting consequence(s)."},{"id":"6.1.19","page":208,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"EHSS incidents shall be assigned a severity classification based on the most severe consequence resulting from the incident and classified as Class 1, Class 2, Class 3 or Class 4 in accordance with the Maaden EHSS Incident Severity Classification Matrix."},{"id":"6.1.20","page":208,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"Process Safety Events shall be classified as Tier 1, Tier 2 or Tier 3. Tier 1 and Tier 2 Process Safety Events shall be classified in accordance with API Recommended Practice 754, Process Safety Performance Indicators for the Refining and Petrochemical Industries. A Process Safety Event that does not meet Tier 1 or Tier 2 criteria shall be classified as a Tier 3 Process Safety Event where one or more of the criteria in Table 6.1-3 apply.\n[Table table-208-3.png]\n\nTable 6.1-3. Process Safety Event Tier 3 Classification Criteria.\nNote: LOPCs not meeting Tier 1, Tier 2 or Tier 3 criteria shall be classified as Process Safety Near Miss."},{"id":"6.1.21","page":208,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"Occupational injury and illness recordability, work-relatedness and classification shall be determined in accordance with OSHA 29 CFR 1904 recordkeeping requirements, considering applicable medical and work activities assessments."},{"id":"6.1.22","page":208,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"Business Units shall maintain records supporting injury and illness classification decisions, including medical assessments, work-relatedness determinations and recordability evaluations."},{"id":"6.1.23","page":208,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"For Fire, Explosion and Property Damage incidents occurring within Maaden facilities, direct damage costs shall be considered when determining incident severity, regardless of liability or ownership of the damaged asset. The only exception shall be incidents involving personal vehicles (vehicle damage) in parking areas."},{"id":"6.1.24","page":209,"heading":"EHSS Event Classification","text":"Near misses shall be classified as Safety, Health, Environment, Security or Process Safety events based on the most credible potential consequence that could reasonably have occurred."},{"id":"6.1.25","page":209,"heading":"Reclassification of Incidents","text":"Incident classifications shall be reviewed and revised when new information, medical confirmation or investigation findings justify a change in classification or severity."},{"id":"6.1.26","page":209,"heading":"Reclassification of Incidents","text":"Reclassification of occupational injuries and illnesses from a higher classification to a lower classification shall be supported by a medical assessment and approved by the designated corporate doctor."},{"id":"6.1.27","page":209,"heading":"Reclassification of Incidents","text":"All other incident reclassifications shall be reviewed and approved by the designated appointing authority."},{"id":"6.1.28","page":209,"heading":"Reclassification of Incidents","text":"Approved incident reclassifications shall be documented, supported by appropriate evidence, and updated in the Maaden incident reporting system (HEMAYA), with corresponding updates reflected in applicable EHSS performance records and statistics."},{"id":"6.1.29","page":209,"heading":"High Potential Incidents (HPIs)","text":"Business Units shall establish and maintain a process to assess the credible worst-case consequences of EHSS incidents and near misses to identify High Potential Incidents (HPIs)."},{"id":"6.1.30","page":209,"heading":"High Potential Incidents (HPIs)","text":"As a minimum, the scenarios listed in Table 6.1-4 shall be considered during HPI assessment.\n[Table table-209-1.png]\n\nTable 6.1-4. High Potential Incident Assessment Scenarios."},{"id":"6.1.31","page":209,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"EHSS incidents and near misses shall be investigated. The level of investigation shall be commensurate with the classification, complexity and credible potential consequences of the event."},{"id":"6.1.32","page":209,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"The Business Unit, site, department or project responsible for the affected operation or activity shall be responsible for investigating the incident."},{"id":"6.1.33","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Incident investigations shall be initiated as soon as practicable and no later than 48 hours after the incident has been stabilized or the emergency has been declared over."},{"id":"6.1.34","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigation requirements shall be determined based on the highest credible potential consequence of the incident. Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 incidents, and High Potential Incidents (HPIs), shall be investigated by a formally appointed investigation team."},{"id":"6.1.35","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"The appointing authority shall appoint the investigation team and define the investigation scope, objectives, deliverables and completion requirements."},{"id":"6.1.36","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"The appointing authority and investigation team leader shall be, as a minimum, the levels specified in Table 6.1-5.\n[Table table-210-1.png]\n\nTable 6.1-5. Team Investigation Leadership Levels."},{"id":"6.1.37","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Where an incident is reclassified during the investigation, the appointing authority responsible for the revised classification shall review the investigation team composition and make any changes necessary to ensure alignment with the incident classification and investigation requirements."},{"id":"6.1.38","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigation team leaders shall be competent in incident investigation and causal analysis methodologies in accordance with section 2.7 EHSS Competency, Awareness and Communication requirements, and shall not have a conflict of interest that could compromise the independence, objectivity, or credibility of the investigation findings."},{"id":"6.1.39","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigation teams shall include personnel with knowledge relevant to the incident, including management or supervision, employees, subject matter experts and, where applicable, contractor representatives. Corporate EHSS representation shall be included in the investigation team for all Class 1 incidents."},{"id":"6.1.40","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Class 1, Class 2, Class 3 incidents and High Potential Incidents (HPIs) shall be investigated using Fault Tree Analysis. Class 4 incidents investigated by an individual shall use the 5 Whys methodology."},{"id":"6.1.41","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"For incidents requiring a team investigation, the Investigation Team Leader shall issue a preliminary investigation report to the appointing authority within 48 hours of the investigation team being formed. The preliminary report shall summarize the incident, investigation status, information collected and immediate actions required to control risk prior to resumption of normal operations."},{"id":"6.1.42","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigations shall identify causes, contributing factors, control failures and actions necessary to prevent recurrence, taking account of relevant previous incidents and lessons learned."},{"id":"6.1.43","page":210,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigation findings shall be documented in a written investigation report, reviewed and approved by the designated appointing authority, and shall include, as a minimum:\na. Executive Summary.\nb. Description of the incident.\nc. Observations and Evidence (Brief Analysis).\nd. Direct Causes, Root Causes, Contributing Factors and Latent Failures.\ne. Conclusions.\nf. Required corrective and preventative actions to address the findings, conclusions and recommendations.\ng. Lessons Learned.\nh. Report Appendices"},{"id":"6.1.44","page":211,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigation reports shall be completed within 30 calendar days of the incident date for incidents requiring a team investigation, unless an extension is approved by the appointing authority, and within 10 working days of the incident date for incidents not requiring a team investigation."},{"id":"6.1.45","page":211,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigation reports for incidents subject to regulatory reporting shall satisfy applicable regulatory reporting requirements."},{"id":"6.1.46","page":211,"heading":"Incident Investigation","text":"Investigation reports shall be managed in accordance with applicable privacy, confidentiality, and legal requirements, including the protection of personal and medical information and the review and approval of any external release."},{"id":"6.1.47","page":211,"heading":"Investigation Actions and Follow-Up","text":"Business Units shall assign, track, implement and verify the implementation and effectiveness of corrective and preventive actions arising from incident investigations, and shall close such actions only where documented evidence demonstrates that the actions have been implemented, the identified causes and control deficiencies have been addressed, and action effectiveness has been verified."},{"id":"6.1.48","page":211,"heading":"Investigation Actions and Follow-Up","text":"Each corrective and preventive action shall be assigned to a single accountable owner and target completion date by the appointing authority."},{"id":"6.1.49","page":211,"heading":"Investigation Actions and Follow-Up","text":"Changes to the recommendation, accountable owner, target completion date or cancellation of the recommendation shall be justified, documented and approved by the appointing authority before implementation of the change."},{"id":"6.1.50","page":211,"heading":"Investigation Actions and Follow-Up","text":"Management shall review the status of corrective and preventive actions arising from incidents at least quarterly to verify timely and effective closure."},{"id":"6.1.51","page":211,"heading":"EHSS Alerts and Lessons Learned","text":"Corporate EHSS shall establish and maintain arrangements to communicate significant external and internal lessons learned from EHSS incidents and High Potential Incidents (HPIs) across Maaden."},{"id":"6.1.52","page":211,"heading":"EHSS Alerts and Lessons Learned","text":"Lessons learned shall be developed for Class 1, Class 2 and Class 3 incidents and High Potential Incidents (HPIs), and shall identify key findings, causes, failed or missing controls, and actions necessary to prevent recurrence."},{"id":"6.1.53","page":211,"heading":"EHSS Alerts and Lessons Learned","text":"Business Units shall establish and maintain an incident learning process to identify, evaluate, communicate and apply lessons learned from internal and external incidents, near misses and High Potential Incidents (HPIs), including the communication of applicable EHSS Alerts and lessons learned to affected personnel and relevant stakeholders."},{"id":"6.1.54","page":211,"heading":"EHSS Alerts and Lessons Learned","text":"Lessons learned shall be considered in the review and improvement of procedures, training, risk assessments, operational controls and emergency preparedness arrangements."},{"id":"6.1.55","page":211,"heading":"EHSS Alerts and Lessons Learned","text":"Facilities under SAIS supervision shall submit lessons learned from incidents, near misses and High Potential Incidents (HPIs) to SAIS in accordance with applicable SAF-25 requirements."},{"id":"6.1.56","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"EHSS incident records, including occupational injury and illness records for employees , contractors and visitors, shall be maintained."},{"id":"6.1.57","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"Business Units shall maintain records of worked man-hours for employees and contractors for EHSS performance measurement, incident statistics and reporting purposes."},{"id":"6.1.58","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"Actual worked man-hours obtained from electronic timekeeping systems shall be used as the preferred basis for EHSS performance reporting. Where electronic timekeeping systems are not available, worked man-hours shall be calculated in accordance with Table 6.1-6.\n[Table table-212-1.png]\n\nTable 6.1-6. Worked man-hours calculation methods."},{"id":"6.1.59","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"Occupational injuries, illnesses and worked man-hours for contractor personnel performing work activities on Maaden premises shall be recorded and included in Maaden contractor injury and illness statistics and incidence rate calculations, including project personnel, transportation personnel, drivers and personnel involved in loading and unloading activities."},{"id":"6.1.60","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"Occupational injuries and illnesses involving students participating in internship programs on Maaden premises shall be recorded and included in contractor injury and illness statistics and incidence rate calculations."},{"id":"6.1.61","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"Visitor incidents shall be recorded and maintained as a separate category from employee and contractor incidents. Injuries, worked man-hours and incidence rates for the following categories of personnel shall not be included in Maaden EHSS performance statistics:\na. Mail and courier personnel.\nb. Office equipment service personnel.\nc. Incidental delivery service personnel (e.g., convenience store deliveries, vending machine deliveries, Incoming material deliveries).\nd. Casual visitors (e.g., Customers, Regulatory Authorities, Raw material/Utilities companies’ officials, port official, suppliers, Government /public officials, tours).\ne. Government Security staff assigned at the BUs."},{"id":"6.1.62","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"Where work is performed under a Permit to Work system, the issuing Maaden entity shall be responsible for recording and reporting the incident."},{"id":"6.1.63","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping","text":"Expansion projects and construction activities shall maintain EHSS performance statistics separate from operational statistics where required by approved Maaden reporting arrangements."},{"id":"6.1.64","page":212,"heading":"EHSS Incident Statistics and Reporting","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall designate responsible people for maintaining worked man-hours, EHSS incident statistics, performance data, and performance reporting records for employees and contractors."},{"id":"6.1.65","page":213,"heading":"EHSS Incident Statistics and Reporting","text":"EHSS incident statistics shall be maintained and reported on a monthly and annual basis."},{"id":"6.1.66","page":213,"heading":"EHSS Incident Statistics and Reporting","text":"EHSS performance reporting shall include the applicable incident performance indicators required by SAF-25."},{"id":"6.1.67","page":213,"heading":"EHSS Incident Statistics and Reporting","text":"Corporate EHSS shall consolidate EHSS performance information and periodically report EHSS performance to Senior Management."},{"id":"6.1.68","page":213,"heading":"EHSS Incident Statistics and Reporting","text":"Business Units shall periodically analyze EHSS incidents, trends and performance data to identify recurring causes, emerging risks, opportunities for improvement and actions necessary to reduce risk and improve performance."},{"id":"6.1.69","page":213,"heading":"EHSS Incident Statistics and Reporting","text":"Corporate EHSS shall periodically review Business Unit EHSS incident and performance analyses to identify Maaden- wide trends, systemic issues and opportunities for continual improvement."}],"area":"Performance and Assurance","part":"EHSS Performance, Assurance and Improvement","keywords":"Incident Management and Stewardship; incident, near miss, investigation, reporting; EHSS Event Reporting and Notification; Incident Response and Evidence Preservation; EHSS Event Classification; Reclassification of Incidents; High Potential Incidents (HPIs); Incident Investigation; Investigation Actions and Follow-Up; EHSS Alerts and Lessons Learned; EHSS Incident Data and Recordkeeping; EHSS Incident Statistics and Reporting","trigger":"if an incident, near miss, occupational illness, security event or environmental event occurs","refs":["2.7"]},{"id":"6.2","title":"EHSS Performance Monitoring, Measurement and Evaluation","page":213,"purpose":"Maaden uses reliable EHSS performance information to evaluate achievement of intended outcomes, fulfilment of applicable performance criteria and effectiveness of controls, and to identify matters requiring operational response or consideration through Management Review and Continual Improvement.","scope":"This process applies to EHSS performance monitoring, measurement, analysis, evaluation and reporting across Business Units and Corporate Functions, including contractor activities within Maaden’s management control.","clauses":[{"id":"6.2.1","page":213,"heading":"Accountability and Monitoring Framework","text":"Business Units, sites and Corporate functions shall establish responsibilities and competent resources for the collection, verification, analysis, evaluation, authorization, communication and retention of EHSS performance information within their assigned scope."},{"id":"6.2.2","page":213,"heading":"Accountability and Monitoring Framework","text":"Monitoring, measurement and evaluation arrangements shall define, as applicable, what is monitored or measured, the purpose and evaluation criteria, methods, frequency or triggers, responsibilities, information ownership, and verification, reporting and escalation requirements."},{"id":"6.2.3","page":213,"heading":"Accountability and Monitoring Framework","text":"The nature, timing and frequency of monitoring, measurement and evaluation shall be proportionate to applicable obligations, EHSS risks, controls, operational variability, performance history, information reliability and changes."},{"id":"6.2.4","page":213,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"Maaden shall establish common Corporate EHSS performance indicators required for governance, aggregation and Corporate EHSS performance reporting."},{"id":"6.2.5","page":213,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish additional EHSS performance indicators where required by their risks, obligations, objectives, activities or operating conditions."},{"id":"6.2.6","page":213,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"Performance indicators shall be relevant to defined EHSS outcomes, risks, controls, objectives or obligations; measurable or capable of objective evaluation; supported by reliable and verifiable information; and suitable for evaluating performance and trends."},{"id":"6.2.7","page":213,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"The EHSS performance indicator framework shall include leading and lagging indicators and measures of control implementation and effectiveness."},{"id":"6.2.8","page":214,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"Corporate EHSS performance indicators shall be managed in accordance with the approved Maaden EHSS KPI Bluebook, including applicable definitions, calculation or assessment methods, evidence requirements, evaluation criteria and scoring methods."},{"id":"6.2.9","page":214,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"Common definitions, reporting boundaries and calculation methods shall be used where EHSS performance information is aggregated or compared across Maaden."},{"id":"6.2.10","page":214,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"Differences in indicator definitions, scope, calculation methods, risk exposure, operating context or information quality that could affect interpretation or comparability shall be identified when information is aggregated, compared, benchmarked or communicated."},{"id":"6.2.11","page":214,"heading":"Performance Indicators and Information Sources","text":"Performance evaluation shall integrate relevant information from operational monitoring, critical control verification, incidents and investigations, occupational health, environmental monitoring, process safety, security, audits and assessments, contractor oversight and other applicable EHSS processes."},{"id":"6.2.12","page":214,"heading":"Data Integrity and Measurement Assurance","text":"EHSS performance information shall be collected and maintained in accordance with applicable indicator definitions, Corporate EHSS reporting requirements and the approved Maaden EHSS KPI Bluebook where applicable. The information shall be complete, accurate, traceable, consistent and available for the applicable reporting period."},{"id":"6.2.13","page":214,"heading":"Data Integrity and Measurement Assurance","text":"Where Maaden designates a Corporate system for EHSS performance information, that system shall be used as the authoritative source. Information obtained from other sources shall be supported by traceable evidence."},{"id":"6.2.14","page":214,"heading":"Data Integrity and Measurement Assurance","text":"EHSS performance information shall be verified before use or submission. The extent of verification shall reflect its reporting purpose and potential effect on compliance, performance scoring or decision making. Verification shall confirm, as applicable, the source evidence, reporting scope and period, calculation or assessment method, and evaluation or scoring criteria."},{"id":"6.2.15","page":214,"heading":"Data Integrity and Measurement Assurance","text":"Monitoring and measuring equipment whose accuracy can affect the validity of an EHSS result shall be maintained and verified or calibrated in accordance with defined requirements, with traceability to recognized measurement standards where available and applicable."},{"id":"6.2.16","page":214,"heading":"Data Integrity and Measurement Assurance","text":"Invalid, incomplete, estimated or uncertain performance information shall be corrected where practicable or clearly identified in the applicable evaluation and communication, including the nature and effect of the limitation."},{"id":"6.2.17","page":214,"heading":"Data Integrity and Measurement Assurance","text":"EHSS performance information, including personal, medical, security sensitive or other restricted information, shall be protected in accordance with applicable legal and Maaden information protection requirements."},{"id":"6.2.18","page":214,"heading":"Analysis and Evaluation","text":"Business Units and Corporate functions shall analyze EHSS performance information within their assigned scope to identify performance trends and deviations, recurring or systemic control weaknesses, emerging risks and matters requiring response or escalation."},{"id":"6.2.19","page":214,"heading":"Analysis and Evaluation","text":"EHSS performance shall be evaluated against applicable legal, regulatory and permit criteria; MSHEM requirements, objectives and plans; and defined risk, control and process performance criteria."},{"id":"6.2.20","page":214,"heading":"Analysis and Evaluation","text":"Performance evaluation shall determine whether intended outcomes and applicable criteria are being achieved, required controls are implemented and effective, performance is improving, stable or deteriorating, and further monitoring, investigation, escalation or action is required."},{"id":"6.2.21","page":215,"heading":"Analysis and Evaluation","text":"Business Units and Corporate functions shall identify performance indicators or evaluation methods requiring revision where they no longer provide reliable or meaningful information for evaluating the intended outcome."},{"id":"6.2.22","page":215,"heading":"Analysis and Evaluation","text":"Evaluation of significant incidents and control failures shall consider both actual outcomes and, where relevant, credible potential consequences."},{"id":"6.2.23","page":215,"heading":"Analysis and Evaluation","text":"Matters requiring immediate operational control, investigation, notification or regulatory response shall be escalated through the applicable MSHEM process without waiting for periodic performance reporting or Management Review."},{"id":"6.2.24","page":215,"heading":"Analysis and Evaluation","text":"Significant performance conclusions, adverse trends, systemic weaknesses and matters requiring management decision shall be provided as inputs to Section 6.4 Management Review and Continual Improvement."},{"id":"6.2.25","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"EHSS performance information required for Corporate reporting shall be verified and reviewed and approved by the Business Unit or Corporate Function EHSS VP before final submission."},{"id":"6.2.26","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"Corporate EHSS shall consolidate and validate submitted EHSS performance information against approved Maaden reporting requirements and the EHSS KPI Bluebook. Applicable technical functions shall support validation where technical interpretation or scoring is required. Performance information shall not be treated as final until issues are resolved and the required supporting evidence is available."},{"id":"6.2.27","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"Reporting errors shall be corrected through a controlled and traceable process that documents the reason for correction, supporting evidence, applicable authorization and the corrected result or score."},{"id":"6.2.28","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"Maaden shall prepare periodic and annual consolidated EHSS performance information for applicable governance, communication and consideration under Section 6.4 Management Review and Continual Improvement."},{"id":"6.2.29","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"Performance information that is preliminary, estimated, incomplete, corrected or subject to significant limitations or conditions shall be clearly identified where its status could affect interpretation or decision making."},{"id":"6.2.30","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"Corporate EHSS performance indicators, applicable Bluebook criteria and related evaluation arrangements shall be reviewed at intervals defined through approved EHSS KPI Bluebook governance and following change to confirm that they remain relevant to their intended outcomes; use reliable information sources and valid criteria; support consistent aggregation, evaluation and decision making; and do not create unintended consequences affecting reporting behavior or information reliability."},{"id":"6.2.31","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"Changes to Corporate performance indicator definitions, calculation or assessment methods, evidence requirements, reporting boundaries or scoring criteria shall be controlled through approved EHSS KPI Bluebook governance arrangements."},{"id":"6.2.32","page":215,"heading":"Validation, Reporting and Records","text":"Documented information supporting EHSS performance monitoring, verification, evaluation, authorization, reporting and correction shall be controlled, retained, retrievable and protected in accordance with applicable Maaden, legal and regulatory requirements."}],"area":"Performance and Assurance","part":"EHSS Performance, Assurance and Improvement","keywords":"EHSS Performance Monitoring, Measurement and Evaluation; KPI, monitoring, performance, measurement, reporting; Accountability and Monitoring Framework; Performance Indicators and Information Sources; Data Integrity and Measurement Assurance; Analysis and Evaluation; Validation, Reporting and Records","trigger":"if performance, compliance, exposure, control or monitoring data must be measured or evaluated","refs":["6.4"]},{"id":"6.3","title":"EHSS Audit and Assurance","page":215,"purpose":"Maaden obtains risk-based, objective and sufficiently independent assurance that applicable EHSS requirements and critical controls are implemented and effective, and that identified deficiencies are corrected, escalated and used to support continual improvement.","scope":"This process applies to all Maaden Business Units and Corporate Functions within the scope of MSHEM.","clauses":[{"id":"6.3.1","page":216,"heading":"Assurance Framework and Governance","text":"Maaden shall establish and maintain three coordinated layers of EHSS audit and assurance:\na. Self-Assessment, conducted by the applicable Business Unit or site to verify conformity with MSHEM requirements and the implementation and effectiveness of applicable controls;\nb. Internal Audit, conducted or commissioned by the applicable Business Unit with independence from the activities and processes being audited; and\nc. Corporate Audit, conducted or commissioned through Corporate EHSS to provide independent corporate-level assurance of conformity with MSHEM requirements and the implementation and effectiveness of applicable controls."},{"id":"6.3.2","page":216,"heading":"Assurance Framework and Governance","text":"Regulatory, certification, independent third-party, special and unscheduled assurance shall be integrated into the Audit and Assurance Program where required by an applicable obligation or defined assurance need."},{"id":"6.3.3","page":216,"heading":"Assurance Framework and Governance","text":"Maaden shall define accountability, authority, independence and resource requirements for the Audit and Assurance Program. Audit and assurance activities shall be conducted objectively, impartially and with due professional care using risk-based methods and verifiable evidence. Audit arrangements shall identify and control conflicts of interest and protect audit evidence, findings, conclusions and reporting from interference."},{"id":"6.3.4","page":216,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall establish, approve, implement and maintain a risk-based five-year EHSS Audit and Assurance Program supported by annual plans that define assurance coverage, timing, priorities, responsibilities, independence and resource requirements considering applicable MSHEM processes, material risks, critical controls, obligations, incidents, findings, performance trends and significant change."},{"id":"6.3.5","page":216,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"Changes, cancellations or deferrals affecting planned assurance coverage shall be risk assessed, authorized and recorded."},{"id":"6.3.6","page":216,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"Each applicable Business Unit shall complete the following minimum assurance sequence over each recurring three- year cycle:\na. a Self-Assessment in the first year;\nb. an Internal Audit in the second year; and\nc. a Corporate Audit or qualifying independent third-party assessment in the third year."},{"id":"6.3.7","page":216,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"The five-year EHSS Audit and Assurance Program shall identify the applicable year of the three-year assurance cycle for each Business Unit and shall maintain continuity of the cycle across successive program periods."},{"id":"6.3.8","page":216,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"The three-year assurance sequence establishes the minimum assurance frequency and level and shall not prevent additional or more frequent assurance where required by risk, performance, change or an applicable obligation."},{"id":"6.3.9","page":216,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"Self-Assessments shall verify the documented arrangements established to implement MSHEM and the implementation and effectiveness of applicable requirements and controls."},{"id":"6.3.10","page":216,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"Internal Audits shall evaluate conformity with applicable MSHEM, legal, regulatory and permit requirements and shall be conducted by competent personnel independent of the activities being audited."},{"id":"6.3.11","page":217,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"A Corporate Audit or qualifying independent third-party assessment may satisfy a required assurance activity where equivalent or greater assurance is achieved, no applicable obligation is displaced, and the substitution is approved and recorded in the Audit and Assurance Program."},{"id":"6.3.12","page":217,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"Unscheduled or additional assurance shall be completed where required by significant risk, incidents, noncompliance, control failures, adverse performance or material change."},{"id":"6.3.13","page":217,"heading":"Audit and Assurance Program","text":"Regulatory, certification, permit and other obligation-driven assurance shall be completed at the frequency required by the applicable obligation and shall not be displaced by the three-year assurance cycle."},{"id":"6.3.14","page":217,"heading":"Audit Planning and Execution","text":"Each audit shall have defined objectives, scope, criteria and methods appropriate to the audit purpose, risks and applicable Audit and Assurance Program."},{"id":"6.3.15","page":217,"heading":"Audit Planning and Execution","text":"Personnel managing, leading, conducting or supporting audits shall be competent for their assigned responsibilities, with audit teams collectively possessing the audit, EHSS, legal, operational and technical competence required for the audit scope and objectives."},{"id":"6.3.16","page":217,"heading":"Audit Planning and Execution","text":"The accountable auditee shall provide authorized access to personnel, locations, activities, systems, documented information and other evidence necessary to achieve the audit objectives."},{"id":"6.3.17","page":217,"heading":"Audit Planning and Execution","text":"Audit findings and conclusions shall be based on relevant and verifiable evidence evaluated against the approved audit criteria. Where sampling is used, limitations affecting confidence in the audit conclusions shall be recognized."},{"id":"6.3.18","page":217,"heading":"Audit Planning and Execution","text":"Material EHSS risks, legal or regulatory non-compliance and significant critical control failures identified during an audit shall be promptly escalated and controlled without waiting for completion of the final audit report."},{"id":"6.3.19","page":217,"heading":"Findings, Reporting and Corrective Action","text":"Audit results shall be supported by objective evidence and referenced to the applicable audit criterion. Audit results shall distinguish non-conformities and non-compliances from observations, improvement opportunities and recognized good practices and shall be evaluated using the Compliance-Based and Risk-Based classification criteria, as applicable.\na. Compliance-Based Classification:\n• Fully Implemented: the applicable requirement is implemented and effective.\n• Acceptable / Implemented with Areas for Improvement: the requirement is substantially implemented and effective, with areas for improvement identified.\n• Minor Improvements Required / Partially Implemented with Minor Gaps: the requirement is generally implemented, but minor gaps or weaknesses exist.\n• Major Improvements Required / Not Well Implemented with Major Gaps: material gaps or weaknesses exist in implementation or effectiveness.\n• Unacceptable Nonconformance / Not Implemented: the requirement is not implemented, or implementation is materially inadequate or ineffective.\nb. Risk-Based Classification: Where a non-conformity, non-compliance or other material deficiency is identified, its significance shall be classified as: Critical, High, Significant, Medium and Low. Risk-based classification shall consider, as applicable, actual or potential EHSS consequence, systemic significance, legal or regulatory significance, critical-control impact and recurrence."},{"id":"6.3.20","page":218,"heading":"Findings, Reporting and Corrective Action","text":"Findings shall be reviewed with the auditee for factual accuracy without compromising auditor independence. An authorized audit report shall document or reference the audit objectives, scope, criteria, findings and classifications, conclusions, significant limitations, and required corrective or follow-up actions."},{"id":"6.3.21","page":218,"heading":"Findings, Reporting and Corrective Action","text":"Findings meeting the defined escalation criteria, including significant, systemic, recurring, critical control or legal and regulatory findings, shall be promptly escalated."},{"id":"6.3.22","page":218,"heading":"Findings, Reporting and Corrective Action","text":"The accountable auditee shall implement correction where required, identify causes proportionate to the significance of the finding, assign corrective actions with accountable owners and completion dates, provide evidence of implementation, and ensure overdue, deferred or changed actions are appropriately escalated and authorized."},{"id":"6.3.23","page":218,"heading":"Findings, Reporting and Corrective Action","text":"Findings shall be closed only after corrective actions have been implemented and verified. Corrective action effectiveness and follow-up assurance shall be proportionate to the significance and recurrence of the finding, and ineffective actions shall remain open or be reopened ."},{"id":"6.3.24","page":218,"heading":"External Assurance, Program Monitoring and Records","text":"Findings and commitments arising from regulatory inspections, certification audits and external assurance shall be assigned, tracked, implemented and closed within the applicable response period."},{"id":"6.3.25","page":218,"heading":"External Assurance, Program Monitoring and Records","text":"Implementation of the Audit and Assurance Program shall be monitored to confirm completion of planned assurance activities, coverage of applicable MSHEM processes and obligations, significant findings and trends, and corrective action completion and effectiveness."},{"id":"6.3.26","page":218,"heading":"External Assurance, Program Monitoring and Records","text":"A missed minimum assurance frequency shall be escalated and shall not be considered fulfilled solely by revising or rescheduling the program."},{"id":"6.3.27","page":218,"heading":"External Assurance, Program Monitoring and Records","text":"Material assurance results, systemic or recurring findings, significant trends and unresolved corrective action risks shall be provided as inputs to Section 6.4 Management Review and Continual Improvement."},{"id":"6.3.28","page":218,"heading":"External Assurance, Program Monitoring and Records","text":"Audit and assurance records shall be controlled, protected and retained in accordance with applicable Maaden, legal, regulatory, permit and contractual requirements."}],"area":"Performance and Assurance","part":"EHSS Performance, Assurance and Improvement","keywords":"EHSS Audit and Assurance; audit, assurance, inspection, verification; Assurance Framework and Governance; Audit and Assurance Program; Audit Planning and Execution; Findings, Reporting and Corrective Action; External Assurance, Program Monitoring and Records","trigger":"if inspection, audit, assurance or critical-control verification is required","refs":["6.4"]},{"id":"6.4","title":"Management Review and Continual Improvement","page":218,"purpose":"Maaden management reviews the continuing suitability, adequacy and effectiveness of MSHEM and implements prioritized improvements to EHSS performance and the management system.","scope":"This process applies to all Business Units and Corporate Functions for the management review and continual improvement of MSHEM implementation, effectiveness and EHSS performance within their areas of responsibility.","clauses":[{"id":"6.4.1","page":218,"heading":"Management Review Arrangements","text":"All Business Units and Corporate Functions shall conduct management reviews at planned intervals through the forums specified in Table 6.4-1 to evaluate EHSS performance and the continuing suitability, adequacy and effectiveness of MSHEM."},{"id":"6.4.2","page":219,"heading":"Management Review Arrangements","text":"Each management review forum shall be chaired by the accountable management role specified in Table 6.4-1."},{"id":"6.4.3","page":219,"heading":"Management Review Arrangements","text":"Each management review shall include personnel with the authority and knowledge required to evaluate performance, make decisions and escalate matters where necessary."},{"id":"6.4.4","page":219,"heading":"Management Review Arrangements","text":"Business Units and Corporate Functions shall evaluate whether an EHSS issue requires management action before the next scheduled review and determine the appropriate management review, escalation or decision making arrangement.\n[Table table-219-1.png]\n\nTable 6.4-1. Required EHSS Review and Engagement Forums."},{"id":"6.4.5","page":219,"heading":"Management Review Inputs","text":"Corporate, Business Units and site management reviews shall consider, as applicable the status and effectiveness of actions from previous reviews; changes in activities, organizational context and interested-party expectations; changes in legal, regulatory, permit and other requirements; achievement of EHSS policy commitments, objectives, targets and plans; EHSS performance and significant trends; incidents, high-potential events and relevant learning; EHSS risks and critical control performance; compliance evaluation, audit and assurance results; contractor EHSS performance; workforce concerns, participation and relevant communications; the adequacy of resources; and opportunities for continual improvement."},{"id":"6.4.6","page":219,"heading":"Management Review Inputs","text":"Lower level meetings shall provide relevant information, concerns and unresolved matters to the next applicable level of management review."},{"id":"6.4.7","page":219,"heading":"Management Review Inputs","text":"Information used for management review shall be sufficiently complete and reliable to support the decisions being made."},{"id":"6.4.8","page":219,"heading":"Management Review Evaluation and Outputs","text":"Corporate Functions, Business Units and Sites management reviews shall evaluate:\na. whether MSHEM remains suitable for the activities, risks, obligations and operating context under review;\nb. whether MSHEM requirements, governance, resources and controls are sufficient; and\nc. whether MSHEM is effectively implemented and achieving the intended EHSS outcomes."},{"id":"6.4.9","page":220,"heading":"Management Review Evaluation and Outputs","text":"Management review outputs shall include decisions on required corrective and improvement actions; improvement priorities; changes to MSHEM or its implementation; resources required; changes to EHSS objectives, targets or plans; opportunities to improve integration with business processes; and matters requiring escalation."},{"id":"6.4.10","page":220,"heading":"Management Review Evaluation and Outputs","text":"Matters exceeding the authority of the reviewing forum shall be escalated to the management level authorized to decide them."},{"id":"6.4.11","page":220,"heading":"Management Review Evaluation and Outputs","text":"Relevant decisions and directions shall be communicated to affected functions, workers and contractors."},{"id":"6.4.12","page":220,"heading":"Continual Improvement","text":"Business Units, Corporate Functions and Sites shall identify and implement opportunities to improve EHSS performance and the suitability, adequacy and effectiveness of MSHEM."},{"id":"6.4.13","page":220,"heading":"Continual Improvement","text":"Improvement priorities shall consider applicable obligations; EHSS risks and credible high consequence events; critical control weaknesses; incidents and recurring findings; negative performance trends; and opportunities with potential Maaden wide benefit."},{"id":"6.4.14","page":220,"heading":"Continual Improvement","text":"Relevant learning and effective practices shall be shared across affected Business Units, Sites and Corporate Functions."},{"id":"6.4.15","page":220,"heading":"Continual Improvement","text":"Changes arising from management review or continual improvement shall be implemented through the applicable MSHEM process, including Management of Change where required ."},{"id":"6.4.16","page":220,"heading":"Actions and Effectiveness","text":"Decisions requiring action shall define the accountable owner, required outcome and target completion date."},{"id":"6.4.17","page":220,"heading":"Actions and Effectiveness","text":"Actions shall be tracked to completion and escalated where overdue or where the related risk or performance gap has materially changed."},{"id":"6.4.18","page":220,"heading":"Actions and Effectiveness","text":"Action closure shall include verification of effectiveness where necessary to confirm that the required outcome was achieved."},{"id":"6.4.19","page":220,"heading":"Actions and Effectiveness","text":"An action found to be ineffective shall be revised, replaced or escalated and recurring or systemic action failures shall be evaluated for applicability across other Business Units, Sites or MSHEM processes."},{"id":"6.4.20","page":220,"heading":"Actions and Effectiveness","text":"Management review and supporting meeting records shall provide traceability between the matters reviewed, decisions, assigned actions and action outcomes."}],"area":"Performance and Assurance","part":"EHSS Performance, Assurance and Improvement","keywords":"Management Review and Continual Improvement; management review, improvement, corrective action, learning; Management Review Arrangements; Management Review Inputs; Management Review Evaluation and Outputs; Continual Improvement; Actions and Effectiveness; 7. DEFINITIONS; FINANCIAL, LEGAL & REGULATORY AND SOCIAL / COMMUNITIES AS PER ERM RISK MATRIX","trigger":"if leadership review, corrective action, lessons learned or continual improvement is required","refs":[]}],"activities":[{"id":"ACT-001","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Governance","title":"Define BU/site EHSS scope and applicability","scope":"Determine controlled entities, activities, workers, contractors, interfaces and exclusions.","keywords":"Define BU/site EHSS scope and applicability; Governance; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Determine controlled entities, activities, workers, contractors, interfaces and exclusions.; risk register, HIRA, TRA, bowtie, risk assessment, critical control, MUE, material unwanted event, control verification, KPI, monitoring, performance, measurement, reporting, management review, improvement, corrective action, learning","links":[["2.1","Primary",""],["2.2","Direct",""],["2.4","Direct",""],["2.5","Direct",""],["6.2","Direct",""],["6.4","Direct",""],["2.6","Conditional","if a fatal or material unwanted event or associated critical control is relevant"]]},{"id":"ACT-002","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Governance","title":"Assign EHSS roles, authorities and technical appointments","scope":"Executive, line, competent-person and technical-authority appointments and escalation levels.","keywords":"Assign EHSS roles, authorities and technical appointments; Governance; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Executive, line, competent-person and technical-authority appointments and escalation levels.; audit, assurance, inspection, verification","links":[["2.2","Primary",""],["2.4","Direct",""],["2.7","Direct",""],["6.3","Direct",""]]},{"id":"ACT-003","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Compliance","title":"Identify and evaluate legal, permit and other requirements","scope":"Legal registers, permits, licences, approved studies, reporting conditions and compliance evaluation.","keywords":"Identify and evaluate legal, permit and other requirements; Compliance; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Legal registers, permits, licences, approved studies, reporting conditions and compliance evaluation.; risk register, HIRA, TRA, bowtie, risk assessment, critical control, MUE, material unwanted event, control verification, KPI, monitoring, performance, measurement, reporting, audit, assurance, inspection, verification","links":[["2.4","Primary",""],["2.5","Direct",""],["6.2","Direct",""],["6.3","Direct",""],["2.6","Conditional","if a fatal or material unwanted event or associated critical control is relevant"]]},{"id":"ACT-004","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Risk Management","title":"Develop or review an EHSS risk register and bowties","scope":"Material unwanted events, critical controls, performance standards and verification plans.","keywords":"Develop or review an EHSS risk register and bowties; Risk Management; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Material unwanted events, critical controls, performance standards and verification plans.; risk register, HIRA, TRA, bowtie, risk assessment, critical control, MUE, material unwanted event, control verification, KPI, monitoring, performance, measurement, reporting, audit, assurance, inspection, verification","links":[["2.5","Primary",""],["2.6","Primary",""],["2.7","Direct",""],["6.2","Direct",""],["6.3","Direct",""]]},{"id":"ACT-005","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Planning","title":"Set EHSS objectives, plans and resources","scope":"Annual objectives, improvement plans, owners, milestones, budget and resources.","keywords":"Set EHSS objectives, plans and resources; Planning; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Annual objectives, improvement plans, owners, milestones, budget and resources.; KPI, monitoring, performance, measurement, reporting, management review, improvement, corrective action, learning","links":[["2.3","Primary",""],["2.2","Direct",""],["6.2","Direct",""],["6.4","Direct",""]]},{"id":"ACT-006","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Competency","title":"Develop competency profiles and training matrices","scope":"Role competencies, training needs, authorization, refresher and assessment requirements.","keywords":"Develop competency profiles and training matrices; Competency; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Role competencies, training needs, authorization, refresher and assessment requirements.; risk register, HIRA, TRA, bowtie, risk assessment, critical control, MUE, material unwanted event, control verification, KPI, monitoring, performance, measurement, reporting","links":[["2.7","Primary",""],["2.5","Direct",""],["6.2","Direct",""],["2.6","Conditional","if a fatal or material unwanted event or associated critical control is relevant"]]},{"id":"ACT-007","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Communication","title":"Conduct toolbox talks, pre-job briefs and EHSS communications","scope":"Risk communication, lessons learned, alerts, worker consultation and shift communication.","keywords":"Conduct toolbox talks, pre-job briefs and EHSS communications; Communication; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Risk communication, lessons learned, alerts, worker consultation and shift communication.; risk register, HIRA, TRA, bowtie, risk assessment, critical control, MUE, material unwanted event, control verification, incident, near miss, investigation, reporting","links":[["2.7","Primary",""],["2.5","Direct",""],["2.6","Conditional","if a fatal or material unwanted event or associated critical control is relevant"],["6.1","Conditional","if an incident, near miss, occupational illness, security event or environmental event occurs"]]},{"id":"ACT-008","area":"Corporate / All Lifecycle","category":"Contractor Management","title":"Prequalify and appoint a contractor","scope":"Risk classification, capability assessment, contractual requirements and approval.","keywords":"Prequalify and appoint a contractor; Contractor Management; Corporate / All Lifecycle; Risk classification, capability assessment, contractual requirements and approval.; risk register,